HT_DATA.load("detail", {"QNznD9hMEh0":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the best readily available interview with Simons, notable for his emphasis on luck and a humble approach to success.","q":{"x":"the full length one is very good. it gives an understanding as to why simons has been so successful.","i":21506006,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":40323065,"author":"bmitc","month":"2024-05","story":"Jim Simons has died","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent interview emphasizing luck in success and a humble approach.","quote":"in the Numberphile interview, which is excellent, he really emphasized the notion of luck in success.","text":"While I'm not one to fawn after billionaires, I found his life story and personality really fascinating. He really seemed to maintain a humble approach, and in the Numberphile interview, which is excellent, he really emphasized the notion of luck in success. He donated a ton of money in very targeted ways that have been extremely successful. I think because of his humble approach, lack of self-promotion, etc., he's a bit unknown outside certain circles, but his impact in certain areas has been big. While I wish that our country didn't have to rely on billionaires spearheading initiatives, which often goes the wrong way, Simons was absolutely an example of one of the good ones. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21506006,"author":"nikofeyn","month":"2019-11","story":"Renaissance Technologies","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Full-length interview explains why Simons has been so successful.","quote":"the full length one is very good. it gives an understanding as to why simons has been so successful.","text":"the full length one is very good. it gives an understanding as to why simons has been so successful. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15029276,"author":"dogruck","month":"2017-08","story":"Renaissance’s Medallion hiked investment caps 50 percent after Trump’s election","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fantastic interview with Jim Simons.","quote":"This interview with Jim Simons is fantastic","text":"RenTech is unparalleled and Medallion has proven itself for decades. This interview with Jim Simons is fantastic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36697759,"author":"WestCoastJustin","month":"2023-07","story":"Ask HN: I am interested in reading about techniques HFT firms use","stance":"rec","why":"Really good interview with a Renaissance founder.","quote":"this was a really good interview with one of the founders","text":"You can troll through the job postings of most of these companies and learn about their org structures and tech stacks. When I looked almost nothing is public other than older documentaries. Nanex had some neat wiring diagrams and a few videos clips if you google them. Don't get too excited you're not going to learn about some super secret strategy or anything. This book was good [LINK] in that is sort of explains at a high level how you'd build a company to look at something like this (think it was one of the last chapters). You'll need extremely deep/skilled hardware/networking groups, data acquisitions (think packet capture to data) folks, people to clean and maintenance massive the data repos, internal tool teams, quantitative analysts, traders, management/admin, etc. At this level just getting the fiber to each exchange likely costs many many millions. When you go and look through those job postings you can sort of slot these into your mental model of this flow and start to see how it might be put together. That book is good but if you are new to the topic it will be almost unreadable. At least it was for me. I had to read it 3-4 times over the years and still find new things as my knowledge grows. I don't know if rentec is a HFT firm or what strategies they use but this was a really good interview with one of the founders [VIDEO] ."},{"id":34759598,"author":"bmitc","month":"2023-02","story":"A Jim Simons Market Mystery – Solved? Not:-)","stance":"rec","why":"Full-length Numberphile interview with Simons.","quote":"Full-length Numberphile interview","text":"It's because James Simons is a serious and actually intelligent person with depth of knowledge, and thus, he is one that doesn't need to opine on random things. He also makes a lot of money in a very secretive way. I'm not sure why anyone expects him to expound in detail on how Renaissance Technologies does it. Simons also spends more money than some governments on very serious and advanced scientific research. I suppose he's rather busy with all his investments and philanthropy. Musk is busy selling himself and filling his unfillable ego. You can see in Simons' TED interview with Chris Anderson how he gets rather annoyed with Chris at times, particularly when Chris mentions Simons' \"amazing mind\" at around 8:30. Simons seems, to me, to be a rather pragmatic and humble person who doesn't particularly enjoy flattery. Chris even throws out a quote by Musk at the very end of the interview, fishing for some similar platitude from Simons, but Simons deftly avoids giving out such an empty quote. [LINK] Most interestingly, you can tell Simons' like of science and mathematics and alludes to his interest when he mentions his investment into the origins of life towards the end. There are two interesting talks or interviews I know of with Simons: * Full-length Numberphile interview: [VIDEO] * His lecture on his life given at MIT: [LINK] Simons actually attributes a lot of his success to luck."},{"id":31721397,"author":"WestCoastJustin","month":"2022-06","story":"A chat with Ray Dalio","stance":"rec","why":"Really good interview with his story.","quote":"Worth a watch if you haven't seen it and are into RenTec.","text":"More of a Musk fan myself. Since you mentioned him, Jim Simons has a really good interview too and it was pretty cool to hear his story [1]. Worth a watch if you haven't seen it and are into RenTec. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31326759,"author":"erk__","month":"2022-05","story":"The 2022 Pulitzer Prize Winner in Explanatory Reporting","stance":"rec","why":"Pretty good full-length one-hour interview with Simons.","quote":"Numberphile have done a pretty good interview with him which is worth a watch.","text":"Numberphile have done a pretty good interview with him which is worth a watch. There is both a full length 1 hour interview [0] and a cut down interview running 18 minutes [1]. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":25891517,"author":"ljosifov","month":"2021-01","story":"Jim Simons proved the textbooks wrong, almost","stance":"rec","why":"A good interview with Simons.","quote":"A good Simons interview (by Numberphile) is","text":"Collected takeaways from “The Man Who Solved the Market” - a (good) book about Jim Simons and RenTec that ring plausible to me at [LINK] . A good Simons interview (by Numberphile) is [VIDEO] ."},{"id":22182800,"author":"leto_ii","month":"2020-01","story":"Medallion Fund “Stretches Explanation to the Limit,” Professor Claims","stance":"rec","why":"Simons says they mostly use ML and simple techniques, plus market impact math.","quote":"It's worth taking a look at the Numberphile interview with Simons","text":"> The Medallion fund has been closed to external capital since 1993, and analysis of the flagship fund’s annual returns suggests that significant distributions are made each year to keep the fund about the same size. For example, despite the fact that Medallion reported annual net returns above 29 percent every year between 2010 and 2018, the fund’s assets under management stayed at about $10 billion throughout that period. This suggests to me that whatever opportunities they're exploiting, they would vanish quickly if they would increase the fund's size (e.g. by compounding returns). It's worth taking a look at the Numberphile interview with Simons ( [VIDEO] ). There he suggests that they're mostly using ML and other relatively simple techniques, with a bit of more advanced math when it comes to predicting how a trade will move the market. This chimes in well with the idea that they have to keep their trades relatively small in order to remain this profitable."},{"id":21423872,"author":"melling","month":"2019-11","story":"How Jim Simons Built the World’s Most Lucrative Black Box","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting Numberphile interview with Simons.","quote":"This Numberphile interview with him is interesting","text":"Article is about this book that will be released on Tuesday. [LINK] This Numberphile interview with him is interesting: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20941488,"author":"melling","month":"2019-09","story":"Day Trading for a Living?","stance":"rec","why":"Story of a mathematician beating the market with algorithms.","quote":"For anyone interested in a great story about a world class mathematician who built a company that has used algorithms to beat the market","text":"For anyone interested in a great story about a world class mathematician who built a company that has used algorithms to beat the market for the last few decades, James Simons has an impressive record: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] One other thing, the Simons Foundation funds Quanta Magazine, whose articles frequently appear on HN: [LINK]"},{"id":11770761,"author":"xviia","month":"2016-05","story":"Mathematicians Bridge Finite-Infinite Divide","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent interview with Simons.","quote":"Excellent interview with Numberphile here","text":"Ah, now I understand. For others: Simons Foundations --> James Simons, who is a hedge fund mananger worth ~ $14billion. Excellent interview with Numberphile here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10355756,"author":"andrewtbham","month":"2015-10","story":"How a reclusive computer programmer became a GOP money powerhouse","stance":"rec","why":"Interview with Renaissance's founder.","quote":"Here is an interview with James Simmons, the founder of Renaissance, fascinating guy.","text":"The title makes it out like he is a reclusive lone wolf programmer. He is the CEO of renaissance technologies, a hedge fund with 275 employees. I really feel misled and like they trying to portray him as a mysterious, dangerous loner. As a computer programmer, I take offense. Here is an interview with James Simmons, the founder of Renaissance, fascinating guy. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10168934,"author":"adenadel","month":"2015-09","story":"A rare interview with the mathematician who cracked Wall Street","stance":"rec","why":"Longer interview covering most of the same topics and more.","quote":"there is a longer interview that goes into most of the same topics as well as several others here","text":"If you found this interview interesting, there is a longer interview that goes into most of the same topics as well as several others here [VIDEO]"},{"id":40324067,"author":"max_","month":"2024-05","story":"Jim Simons has died","stance":"reference","why":"Interview that began Simons Foundation sponsorship of Numberphile.","quote":"invited them to his New York Apartment for an interview.","text":"They have many sponsors. Jim Discovered their channel, liked it and invited them to his New York Apartment for an interview. [1] From that time forward, all Numberphile videos have \"Simons Foundation\" at the end listed as a sponsor. [1]: Here is the video [VIDEO]"},{"id":26316359,"author":"melling","month":"2021-03","story":"Statistics Postdoc Tames Decades-Old Geometry Problem","stance":"reference","why":"Background on the hedge fund founder funding the research.","quote":"Funded by billionaire mathematician and hedge fund founder, where the algorithms make all the decisions","text":"Funded by billionaire mathematician and hedge fund founder, where the algorithms make all the decisions [VIDEO] 1976 winner of the Oswald Veblen Prize in Geometry [LINK]"},{"id":21430645,"author":"mdonahoe","month":"2019-11","story":"The making of Jim Simons","stance":"reference","why":"Simons's earlier Numberphile appearance.","quote":"Simons was on Numberphile a while back.","text":"I don't subscribe to wsj, so I can't read the article, but Simons was on Numberphile a while back. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14945220,"author":"bitanarch","month":"2017-08","story":"Fitting to Noise or Nothing at All: Machine Learning in Markets","stance":"reference","why":"Simons discusses math models for detecting market anomalies and trends.","quote":"he explicitly talked about using math models to detect anomalies, e.g. trends.","text":"What's your view on cases like Renaissance Technologies? There's an interview online on James Simons ( [VIDEO] ) - and he explicitly talked about using math models to detect anomalies, e.g. trends. It's also known that they've used Hidden Markov Models, at least in the early days."},{"id":9902397,"author":"melling","month":"2015-07","story":"Bernoulli: Exposition of a New Theory on the Measurement of Risk (1738) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a mathematician succeeding with algorithms in finance.","quote":"You've got Gordon Gecko and then you have James Simons.","text":"It's great when you can use math, and remove humans from the process once the algorithms are written, to do something people thought was impossible. Was never trying to imply that's the pinnacle. You've got Gordon Gecko and then you have James Simons. [VIDEO] Maybe the U.S. wouldn't rank so low in math and science if kids saw a few more career possibilities. [LINK]"},{"id":9941683,"author":"melling","month":"2015-07","story":"At 78, Scientist Is Starting a Hedge Fund","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"We have all watched James Simons, right?","text":"Yes, I know that. We have all watched James Simons, right? [VIDEO] What does that have to do with being a more appropriate news headline? I was responding to the 'clickbait' comment."}],"r":[["9XaYdCdwiWU",1],["G4hL5Om4IJ4",1],["j-vJxez9UF8",1],["coGpmA4saEk",1],["0qKp-0h9P18",1],["F9cO3-MLHOM",1],["Nb2tebYAaOA",1],["2qv3fcXW1mg",1],["PDylgzybWAw",1],["Wp-WiNXH6hI",1],["ef2jpjTEB5U",1],["21EiKfQYZXc",1],["0QNiZfSsPc0",1],["pqzcCfUglws",1],["Wpv-Qb-dB6g",1],["pSHj5UKSylk",1],["GOGru_4z1Vc",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["wJyUtbn0O5Y",1],["L0KuAx1COEk",1]]},"-6BsiVyC1kM":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as the talk that convinced them of immutability and functional programming.","q":{"x":"is what finally sold me on the benefits of pure functional programming and immutable data structures.","i":19296780,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":29035199,"author":"elamje","month":"2021-10","story":"Ask HN: Programming talks that made something click for you?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Part of a trio of talks by the strongest programming speaker","quote":"strongest programming speaker I've found to date.","text":"Simple Made Easy ( [LINK] ) The Language of the System ( [LINK] ) The Value of Values ( [VIDEO] ) All by Rich Hickey (Clojure creator) - strongest programming speaker I've found to date."},{"id":24341042,"author":"josephg","month":"2020-09","story":"The database I wish I had","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Place versus value, and storing full history by default","quote":"Well worth a watch, especially if you disagree","text":"Most software isn’t FAANG. In the long tail of database size, I’d guess 95%+ of databases are smaller than a few hundred megs. And at that size, storage is cheap and storing a full historical record of changes should absolutely be the default. Rich Hickey has a great talk on this a few years ago talking about the difference between Place and Value. He says accountants learned this lesson hundreds of years ago with bookkeeping. Users just don’t generate much data relative to the size of our disks. Well worth a watch, especially if you disagree: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22773508,"author":"lukifer","month":"2020-04","story":"New WebKit Features in Safari 13.1","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Convincing argument for immutability by default","quote":"What sold me on the idea was Rich Hickey's talk","text":"Immutability has been pretty hot of late, though still controversial. There are several popular languages which encourage or even force immutability (Elixir, Clojure). What sold me on the idea was Rich Hickey's talk, \"The Value of Values\": [VIDEO] I look at it as mostly a safety net: I'd rather use const by default, unless I have a good reason to be able to mutate the variable. (To flip it around: what problem does being mutable-by-default solve?) 9 times out of 10, I don't need to change the value for the lifecycle of the function; and often if I do, the code is cleaner anyway if I use an immutable pattern (such as `const foo = bar.map()` rather than `for(x in bar) foo.push()`). Thanks to the magic of Merkle trees, we can generally make fresh pointers without it costing much memory overhead."},{"id":19296780,"author":"lukifer","month":"2019-03","story":"Ask HN: What made you change your mind about a programming language/paradigm?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Persuasive case for pure functional programming and immutable data","quote":"is what finally sold me on the benefits of pure functional programming and immutable data structures.","text":"Rich Hickey's \"Value of Values\"[0] is what finally sold me on the benefits of pure functional programming and immutable data structures. (It remains horrifying to continue working with MySQL in my day job, knowing that every UPDATE is potentially a destructive action with no history and no undo.) [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37757625,"author":"dmitry-vsl","month":"2023-10","story":"Show HN: Leporello.js – interactive functional programming IDE for JavaScript","stance":"rec","why":"Advantages of immutability in functional programming","quote":"You can watch Rich Hickey's insightful talk [0], where he discusses the advantages of immutability in functional programming.","text":"Leporello.js follows a pragmatic functional approach. You can use IO functions and throw exceptions as you typically would, without the need for an IO monad or an Option monad. However, there is one critical limitation – you cannot mutate your data structures. Mutations are not allowed because they would make time-travel debugging impossible. For instance, the following code is forbidden: const point = {x: 1, y: 2} point.x = 2 And instead you should use this: const point = {x: 1, y: 2} const another_point = {...point, x : 2} In Leporello.js, it's vital to avoid mutating data structures; instead, you create new ones. You can watch Rich Hickey's insightful talk [0], where he discusses the advantages of immutability in functional programming. In many ways, Leporello.js bears a resemblance to Clojure. Clojure served as a source of inspiration for Leporello.js, to the extent that I even contemplated building Leporello.js for Clojure first. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20429950,"author":"lvh","month":"2019-07","story":"Clojure’s Approach to Identity and State (2008)","stance":"rec","why":"Clear explanation of values by the creator of Clojure","quote":"to be a great 'splainer","text":"I think Rich Hickey's (the creator of Clojure) talk, The Value of Values, to be a great 'splainer: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15779150,"author":"tree_of_item","month":"2017-11","story":"Carp, a compiled Lisp with type inference and a borrow checker","stance":"rec","why":"Makes the case for immutability","quote":"lays out the case quite well","text":"Clojure's implementation did not have to be so complicated, there are simpler implementations of persistent data structures. Immutability is obviously the more elegant option as far as I'm concerned: Hickey's The Value of Values [0] lays out the case quite well. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13285874,"author":"JMStewy","month":"2016-12","story":"Why Clojure? (2010)","stance":"rec","why":"Half-hour version of the Value of Values talk","quote":"which I enjoyed as well.","text":"That talk was \"The Value of Values,\" which I enjoyed as well. Links for anyone interested: [LINK] (1 hour version) [VIDEO] (1/2 hour version)"},{"id":12361706,"author":"unlogic","month":"2016-08","story":"Arguments against JSON-driven development","stance":"rec","why":"Clarifies the difference between objects and values","quote":"this video may be of help","text":"It's funny how catnaroek was completely vendetta-downvoted in this thread for speaking valid things. For those who don't understand the difference between objects and values, this video may be of help: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9852201,"author":"escherize","month":"2015-07","story":"Some Functional Programmers Are Arrogant","stance":"rec","why":"Rich Hickey talk that answers the question best","quote":"started picking up clojure after watching a few of rich hickeys talks","text":"Hi! I have been a programmer for a couple years and started picking up clojure after watching a few of rich hickeys talks. This is the one that best satisfies your question, imho: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8803885,"author":"lkrubner","month":"2014-12","story":"Programming languages and Frameworks to learn in 2015","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of concepts important to functional programming","quote":"is also a good overview of some concepts that are important to Functional Programming","text":"If you have the slightest interest in the issue of concurrency then you should study Erlang and Clojure. Since Moore's Law has largely faded, and we now gain speed by putting more CPUs in each server (rather than having the individual CPUs be faster) concurrency is an increasingly unavoidable aspect of programming. Joe Armstrong (Erlang) and Rich Hickey (Clojure) have both spent an enormous amount of time thinking about how to support concurrency in a programming language. Armstrong talks about a paradigm that he calls \"Concurrency Oriented Programming\". Armstrong's thesis is surprisingly readable and I recommend it: [LINK] Rich Hickey's video presentation \"The value of values\" is also a good overview of some concepts that are important to Functional Programming: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32186609,"author":"johnthescott","month":"2022-07","story":"The data rules worth $40k a day","stance":"reference","why":"Supports immutable facts in data modeling","quote":"","text":"is caching the correct analogy? the stars you see died eons ago. special relativity is your friend. we witness \"immutability\" casually. nothing in the relational model that i am aware of says that a materialized view cannot be a simple immutable table of \"facts\". same input same output. unambiguous. simply use the dynamic queries to summarize the \"facts\" in, typically, a small mat view tables and your gui will never lie. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25135582,"author":"slifin","month":"2020-11","story":"Write Code Like You Write a Recipe","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Who knows what bowl.stir() actually does to the internal state of bowl, and what methods should have been run up until this point to get into that state, is the bowl ready to stir? What methods should have already run for that? So much of the article's code is crutched on good naming I like to think of this in terms of the Charizard Pokemon card For context in this example I have this card and I'm sensitive about damage to it so in this OO example I put the card in a box and allow you to interact with it in a very limited way, you cannot use anything you're used to to interact with it like your own gloves or hands etc Just my \"methods\" so I might give you a tiny hole to look at it, you could still damage it through the hole, so I have lots of logic to ensure you cannot poke it incorrectly hopefully the verbosity on both your and my side is/was worth it and bug free and not missing cases, hopefully my hole was in the right place for your uses Obviously I can't give you too many holes in the box otherwise what's the point in the box? I need the box to maintain my sanity The other alternative is I just give you the card, and take the risk that you might damage it, this is a disaster for my well being OR I duplicate the card perfectly and give you the duplicate in which case I don't care what happens to the duplicate, MUCH easier in my opinion, so please Stop creating hellish boxes with holes for other developers to peak through just choose a language with efficient immutability "}],"r":[["oyLBGkS5ICk",1],["d0vY0CKYhPY",1],["SWEts0rlezA",1],["ROor6_NGIWU",1],["LKtk3HCgTa8",1],["aMcjxSThD54",1],["cVaTc15plVs",1],["wAIP6fI0NAI",1],["2wZ1pCpJUIM",1],["JcJSW7Rprio",1],["bNfAAQUQ_54",1],["Y65FRxE7uMc",1],["v3XcQtoja_Y",1],["XZ3w_jec1v8",1],["OjMPJVmXxV8",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1],["tIuj-oMN-Fk",1],["pdmODVYPDLA",1],["VPBH1eW28mo",1],["LkH2r-sNjQs",1]]},"42QuXLucH3Q":{"wy":"HN commenters link it whenever a shaky study or the p<0.05 threshold comes up, as a short primer on false positives in science.","q":{"x":"This video is a good primer","i":18501978,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":46293125,"author":"KK7NIL","month":"2025-12","story":"I don't think Lindley's paradox supports p-circling","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clearly explains skewed priors and the paradox.","quote":"This Veritasium video does a great job at explaining how such skewed priors can easily appear","text":"Wikipedia is infamously bad at teaching math. This Veritasium video does a great job at explaining how such skewed priors can easily appear in our current academic system and the paradox in general: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28339201,"author":"scotty79","month":"2021-08","story":"The Coronavirus Is Here Forever","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Why to distrust small, less rigorous studies.","quote":"Please watch this video.","text":"Please watch this video. It neatly shows why should you treat small and less rigorous studies with suspicion, even if there are many giving some results: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24675508,"author":"sillysaurusx","month":"2020-10","story":"Actively speaking two languages protects against cognitive impairment","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Background on why p-values mislead and origin of 0.05.","quote":"For a background on why p-values are misleading, see","text":"Cue “p = 0.05 is a random choice” and a big thread about p-hacking. Any takers? It feels like everything to say has already been said about the bias of small sample sizes and the dangers of p-hacking. But it also seems like the discussion is inevitable here. I wonder if there’s a new insight to glean, somehow... For a background on why p-values are misleading, see [VIDEO] which is excellent. He points out that 0.05 was selected by Ronald Fischer in 1925, and people have gone with it since."},{"id":16419008,"author":"r_singh","month":"2018-02","story":"Neural nets more accurate than humans at detecting sexual orientation in images","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains skewed test data problems in research.","quote":"reminds me of this great video by Veritasium","text":"If the test data does not have a 50/50 split (or something around that ratio), the headline is straight up lies. Skewed test data is the most common problem with research, reminds me of this great video by Veritasium (Is most published research wrong?): [VIDEO]"},{"id":18501978,"author":"hannasanarion","month":"2018-11","story":"Dead sperm whale found in Indonesia had ingested '6kg of plastic'","stance":"rec","why":"Primer on false positives in the literature.","quote":"This video is a good primer","text":"My dude. Please go get yourself some education on how science works. I feel embarrassed for you. The reproducibility crisis is about the prevalence of false positive results in the scientific literature. This video is a good primer: [VIDEO] A false positive is when a paper says \"this works\" when it actually doesn't, because of a statistical fluke in their sample. Even if citing the reproducibility crisis as an excuse to believe something that science has repeatedly found to be false isn't a really bad reason for belief (which it definitely is)... it doesn't even apply because there can't be false positive results regarding neurolinguistic programming because there aren't any positive results regarding neurolinguistic programming. Even the paper put out by neurolinguistic programming practicioners themselves wasn't able to produce a detectable effect over a placebo. I'm sorry, my friend, but this is an intervention. You're in a cult. You're making the exact same arguments as homeopaths and faith healers and fortune tellers."},{"id":14681065,"author":"wolfram74","month":"2017-07","story":"The US government is removing scientific data from the Internet","stance":"rec","why":"Walks through example calculation and improvement steps.","quote":"This video walks through an example calculation and what kinds of steps are being taken to improve on things.","text":"It's not 'these days' it's the intrinsic nature of false hypotheses outnumbering true hypotheses by an enormous ratio. Even if everything were working perfectly you'd expect false results to outnumber true results. It's like the common example of disease screening, where even if you only get a false positive 1% of the time, but there are only 1% of true positives in your sample population, you're going get many more inaccurate results than accurate results. This video walks through an example calculation and what kinds of steps are being taken to improve on things. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14251872,"author":"ReligiousFlames","month":"2017-05","story":"Science to Retract Study on Fish and Microplastics","stance":"rec","why":"Shows p-value threshold lets many false positives be published.","quote":"There's an interesting video by Veratasium \"Is Most Published Research Wrong?","text":"There's an interesting video by Veratasium \"Is Most Published Research Wrong?\" (Clickbaity title to be sure.) It shows, on the overall statistics of valid/invalid vs. published/unpublished papers, the arbitrary p-value threshold is wholy inadequate to prevent a sizable fraction of published papers from being false positives (invalid but published). [VIDEO]"},{"id":14135256,"author":"burntrelish1273","month":"2017-04","story":"Receding glacier causes Canadian river to vanish in four days","stance":"rec","why":"Shows reality and data can differ significantly.","quote":"Videos demonstrating this with examples","text":"Definitely. Another good idea is to show the difference between reality and data can be overwhelmingly significant. Videos demonstrating this with examples: - \"The Bayesian Trap\" [LINK] - \"Is Most Research Wrong?\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":45083610,"author":"r_singh","month":"2025-08","story":"New research reveals longevity gains slowing, life expectancy of 100 unlikely","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I can understand the sarcasm and respect your take Science has helped reduce mortality at birth and manage or even eliminate a lot of diseases like malaria, HIV, small pox, polio and many more. Also for burns, broken bones and accidents. And continues to. I advocate vaccines. I myself took the Anti Covid shot 3 times. And use medicines and supplements regularly. There’s another side to it though. At what point do I just take an Adderall that’s prescribed to me? Or a pain killer? Rather than trying preventive lifestyle cures? We need a way to tell when to apply what but IMHO in practical life what gains ground is what serves the providers more rather than the beneficiaries. An interesting example is that Anaesthesia was a more quickly adopted invention than Anti-Septic. Even though the latter is more important for the safety of patients. But the former makes life easier for docs, hence it was adopted much faster. There is a place for both and knowing when to apply which is key. You cannot trust HCPs using allopathy aka science backed by academia in every case cause they’re motivated by self interest and aren’t perfect! Even people like Gabriel Weinberg have acknowledged this in his book Super Thinking when touching upon inefficiencies in healthcare and academia. I believe that to be able to trust science more we need to save science from p-value manipulation by self-interest groups… This video by Veritasium also talks about how the accuracy of most published research is contes"},{"id":41199541,"author":"authorfly","month":"2024-08","story":"GPT-4 LLM simulates people well enough to replicate social science experiments","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Fair enough, you might have indeed rejected those authors - however, vast swathes, for Baumeister the majority, did not at the time. It's almost certainly true now for existing authors we are yet to identify, or maybe never will. I admit the point on TESS, I didn't research that enough. I'll look into that at a later point as I have an interest in learning more. To address your studies regarding expert / study forecasting - thank you for sharing some papers. I had time and knew some papers in the area so I have formulated a response because, as you allude to later regarding cultural predictions, there is debate in the question of the usefulness of expert vs non-expert forecasts (and e.g. there is a wide base of research on recession/war predictions showing the error rate is essentially random at a certain number of years out). I have not fully comprehended the first paper but I understand the gift of it. Economics bridges sociology and the harder science of mathematics, and I do think it makes sense for it to be more predictable than psychology studies by experts(and note the studies being predicted were not survey-response like most are in psychology), but even this one paper does not particularly support your point. Critically, one conclusion in the paper you cite is that \"Forecasters with higher vertical, horizontal, or contextual expertise do not make more accurate forecasts.\", \"If forecasts are used just to rank treatments, non-experts, including even an easy-to-recruit "},{"id":39865327,"author":"iLoveOncall","month":"2024-03","story":"Too many labs run like this","stance":"reference","why":"Showed most published research is wrong.","quote":"7 years ago Veritasium published a video showing that most published research is wrong.","text":"It's really starting to get hard to believe ANYTHING that comes out in a research paper this days. 7 years ago Veritasium published a video showing that most published research is wrong. News since then has shown that it's not getting better. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38898723,"author":"d0mine","month":"2024-01","story":"Lancet journal retracts article on hearing aids and dementia after prodding","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for claim that most published research fails replication.","quote":"most published research is wrong.","text":"> most science withstands the scrutiny If \"most\" means \"most published papers\" and \"scrutiny\" means \"can replicate\" then no, it doesn't withstand scrutiny: most published research is wrong. [VIDEO] (2016) (the exact percentages of non-replicatable articles may be different in different fields)"},{"id":39050175,"author":"WhitneyLand","month":"2024-01","story":"The problem is not plagiarism, but cargo cult science","stance":"reference","why":"Related Veritasium video.","quote":"Vertasium has a related video","text":"Possibly you and the OP would be better served to say “ I wouldn’t be surprised if some claim about x%” is true. Or, “I would guess it’s true that”. Back to the main subject Vertasium has a related video: Is Most Published Research Wrong [VIDEO]"},{"id":38148397,"author":"dharmab","month":"2023-11","story":"Ask HN: Where do you get your health news?","stance":"reference","why":"Claims most published research is wrong.","quote":"Most published research is wrong","text":"Most published research is wrong: [VIDEO] I usually check the UK NHS website for general information that has been reproduced consistently, and talk to a doctor for anything more specific. This about how poorly technology and law news is reported. Health news is _even worse_."},{"id":25478900,"author":"sillysaurusx","month":"2020-12","story":"'I am mine': Alzheimer's at 41 (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yeah, sure. Just don't eat, and that'll prevent alzheimer's. If you study history, you have to reconcile these views with the fact that we've been wrong about every medical development until recent history. At one point we were killing cats to curtail the black plague, when in fact it was the fleas that were the cause. We just don't know. And it's fine not to know. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18919001,"author":"nukemandan","month":"2019-01","story":"Consumption of a dark roast coffee blend reduces DNA damage in humans","stance":"reference","why":"Statistics can be spun easily.","quote":"Spinning statistics is not that hard, even with the best of intentions.","text":"All I can say... \"Is Most Published Research Wrong?\" [VIDEO] TL;DR: Likely yes. Spinning statistics is not that hard, even with the best of intentions."},{"id":15431758,"author":"trapperkeeper74","month":"2017-10","story":"“Peer review” is younger than we think","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on bad papers and low p-value standards.","quote":"","text":"There are many, many BS papers because of the mass-production death-march researchers are told to produce, low p-value and sample size standards and ”Is Most Published Reseach Wrong?” (Veritasium, 2016) [VIDEO] Which is about an essay from 2005: [LINK] This doesn’t mean adversarial and critical feedback are bad, it means dis/incentives, politics and standards need adjustments."},{"id":15474036,"author":"ehsankia","month":"2017-10","story":"Fraud Scandals Sap China’s Dream of Becoming a Science Superpower","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"doesn't the west have the exact same issues? p-Hacking, lack of repeat studies","text":"I mean, doesn't the west have the exact same issues? p-Hacking, lack of repeat studies, focusing on experiments that will make the biggest headlines, etc. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":14873223,"author":"DiThi","month":"2017-07","story":"High refined sugar intake linked to a 23% higher risk of mental disorders","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The vast majority of the studies mentioned in those videos are old, where most researchers could get away with hacking the P values [0]. A lot of studies done in the last decade contradict the older ones (the article I linked earlier is full of sources). Also correlation is not causation. In how many of those studies controlled the amount of carbs? Because the combination is very important [1] [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] Edit: Looking at the last link of your first video, it acknowledges low carb diets are beneficial short and medium term, but there's very little literature for long term. It concludes low carbs are bad long term... but you must see the table on page 13 to see how they reached that conclusion. The \"low carb score\" group still had a crazily high 37-42% of carbs (compared to LCHF diets which are usually 5% carbs), it had more smokers and all of them had trans fats (very dangerous but can be easily avoided altogether)."}],"r":[["qS34oV-jv_A",1],["dNs_kNilSjk",1],["b0EF0VTs9Dc",1],["LnS0PfNyj4U",1],["_tpB-B8BXk0",1],["R13BD8qKeTg",1],["PYylPRX6z4Q",1],["Bd8_vO5zrjo",1],["NxvQPzrg2Wg",1],["YHLU0Uej1WA",1],["fCn8zs912OE",1],["Ggz_gd--UO0",1],["Ox5P7QyL774",1],["DpXy041BIlA",1],["S7TUe5w6RHo",1],["AnaASTBn_K4",1],["pIgMTsQXg3Q",1]]},"LA_DrBwkiJA":{"wy":"HN fans call it a favorite engineering video and a showcase of Tom7's masterpiece-level, playful hacking.","q":{"x":"His video about the printable x86[1] is my favorite engineering video of all time.","i":21546805,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":37843620,"author":"omgmajk","month":"2023-10","story":"The GlEnd() of Zelda: Automatic 3Dification of NES Games (2016)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Personal favorite among Tom7's interesting videos.","quote":"is my favorite but most of his stuff is super interesting.","text":"Love Tom7, his video \"Compiling C to printable x86, to make an executable research paper\" [0] is my favorite but most of his stuff is super interesting. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36845375,"author":"nextaccountic","month":"2023-07","story":"C89 compiler produces printable executables from C code [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Video explaining the code; the author's channel is all masterpieces.","quote":"Absolutely every video on his Youtube channel is a masterpiece","text":"The author is an absolute legend. Absolutely every video on his Youtube channel is a masterpiece [LINK] And this includes the video that explains this code (Compiling C to printable x86, to make an executable research paper) [VIDEO]"},{"id":21546805,"author":"badrequest","month":"2019-11","story":"NaN Gates and Flip FLOPS","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite engineering video of all time.","quote":"His video about the printable x86[1] is my favorite engineering video of all time.","text":"Every one of this guy's SIGBOVIK papers/videos are pure gold. His video about the printable x86[1] is my favorite engineering video of all time. ^1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20573773,"author":"pronoiac","month":"2019-07","story":"Survival in chessland [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Compiler producing executables from printable characters only.","quote":"I like his compiler that generates executables made up only of printable characters","text":"He's a mad genius! I like his compiler that generates executables made up only of printable characters: [VIDEO] / [LINK] He's super playful and awesomely smart!"},{"id":36821507,"author":"nick-of-time","month":"2023-07","story":"GradIEEEnt half decent: The hidden power of imprecise lines [video]","stance":"rec","why":"One of three favorite talks listed.","quote":"Mine are \"Harder Drives\"[1], \"ELO World\"[2], and \"Printable x86\"[3]","text":"Mine are \"Harder Drives\"[1], \"ELO World\"[2], and \"Printable x86\"[3] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35123528,"author":"x86x87","month":"2023-03","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the most interesting YouTube channels to follow?","stance":"rec","why":"Named as all-time favorite among suckerpinch videos.","quote":"Suckerpinch (all time favourite","text":"A mix of various things. Google to get to the channel: pbs spacetime stuff made here ben eater's 8 bit computer mike boyd nerdforge sabine hossenfelder gotham chess uthermal 3blue1brown veritasium Numberphile Suckerpinch (all time favourite: [VIDEO] ) Stevey's tech talk leonard susskind's stanford lectures"},{"id":27767982,"author":"SAI_Peregrinus","month":"2021-07","story":"Undocumented x86 instructions to control the CPU at the microarchitecture level [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Video demonstrates running the executable paper.","quote":"incomplete without the video where he demonstrates running the paper","text":"That last one (Tom 7's executable x86 paper) is incomplete without the video where he demonstrates running the paper (or running the paper yourself). [VIDEO]"},{"id":26937917,"author":"anitil","month":"2021-04","story":"Lipogram","stance":"rec","why":"Explains creating an executable using only printable characters.","quote":"tom7 made a while video explaining how to create an executable only with printable characters","text":"tom7 made a while video explaining how to create an executable only with printable characters [0]. He even write a compiler called 'ABC' for it. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36756789,"author":"colejohnson66","month":"2023-07","story":"Undocumented 8086 instructions, explained by the microcode","stance":"reference","why":"Partial C89 compiler outputting only printable characters, self-hosting.","quote":"It's a partial C89 compiler that outputs only printable characters.","text":"Tom Murphy VII (tom7) did such a thing. It's a partial C89 compiler that outputs only printable characters. In meta fashion, the compiler is compiled by itself to also contain only printable characters. The TXT and EXE files below are exactly the same. Video: [VIDEO] Paper (PDF): [LINK] Paper (TXT): [LINK] Compiler (EXE): [LINK]"},{"id":34374171,"author":"asalahli","month":"2023-01","story":"ABC: A C compiler for printable x86","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses a specific moment on expressing OR via AND and XOR.","quote":"he goes to great lengths to express (A or B) as (A and B) + (A xor B)","text":"In the accompanying video[0] at 7:36 mark, he goes to great lengths to express (A or B) as (A and B) + (A xor B). Can anybody explain why it can't simply be expressed as (A and B) xor (A xor B)? What am I missing here? 0. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16046222,"author":"floatboth","month":"2018-01","story":"Ask HN: What’s your favorite talk from 2017?","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among favorite 2017 talks.","quote":"Compiling C to printable x86, to make an executable research paper","text":"On The Turing Completeness of PowerPoint [LINK] Compiling C to printable x86, to make an executable research paper [VIDEO] HDR Photography in Microsoft Excel?! [LINK] Zebras All the Way Down [LINK] Solving Layout Problems with CSS Grid and Friends [LINK] Console Security - Switch [LINK] Let's move SMM out of firmware and into the kernel [LINK] Bringing Linux back to server boot ROMs with NERF and Heads [LINK] Sharing the Chisel [LINK] (looks like the actual conference video wasn't released)"}],"r":[["JcJSW7Rprio",2],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1],["wrwgIjBUYVc",1],["KVl5kMXz1vA",1],["doaQC-S8de8",1],["5_KcQt0z-eE",1],["sWblpsLZ-O8",1],["r3snVCRo_bI",1],["K4JhruinbWc",1],["Erp8IAUouus",1],["gNRnrn5DE58",1],["DpXy041BIlA",1],["jvPPXbo87ds",1]]},"OkmNXy7er84":{"wy":"HN fans list it among their favorite 3Blue1Brown videos for showing how mathematical problem solving actually unfolds.","q":{"x":"these videos give a sense of how someone might actually go about thinking through and solving these kinds of problems","i":44618691,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":28197612,"author":"JackC","month":"2021-08","story":"How to avoid being hit by a laser in a room of mirrors [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Walks through problem-solving process for a Putnam problem","quote":"I like the 3blue1brown youtube series for explaining mathematical problem solving","text":"I like the 3blue1brown youtube series for explaining mathematical problem solving, like this example explaining a difficult problem from the Putnam: [VIDEO] \"And those of you who follow the channel know that rather than just jumping straight to the solution, which in this case will be surprisingly short, when possible I prefer to take the time to walk through how you might stumble upon the solution yourself. That is, make the video more about the problem-solving process than the particular problem used to exemplify it.\" I bailed out of math around calculus, and these videos helped me at least appreciate what I was missing."},{"id":28245390,"author":"msdrigg","month":"2021-08","story":"Manim: An animation engine for explanatory math videos","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Thought process behind solving a level 6 Putnam problem","quote":"He has an excellent channel that I would recommend anything from.","text":"This is an extension of an animation library used by math YouTuber 3blue1brown [0]. He has an excellent channel that I would recommend anything from. One of my favorite videos of his is where he shows the thought process behind solving a level 6 Putnam competition problem and makes it seem almost doable [1]. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24864478,"author":"pjfin123","month":"2020-10","story":"A Programmer’s Intuition for Matrix Multiplication","stance":"strong_rec","why":"A cool explanation, not exactly a math tutorial","quote":"3Blue1Brown is really great for learning/refreshing math. He has great visualizations.","text":"3Blue1Brown is really great for learning/refreshing math. He has great visualizations. I especially enjoyed this video (not exactly a math tutorial more of a cool explanation): [VIDEO]"},{"id":20473102,"author":"naturlich","month":"2019-07","story":"Think in Math, Write in Code","stance":"strong_rec","why":"One of the interesting proof vignettes","quote":"These are my favorite videos of his","text":"Indeed, I'd recommend the \"interesting proof\" vignettes by 3Blue1Brown: [VIDEO] These are my favorite videos of his"},{"id":15931809,"author":"skadamat","month":"2017-12","story":"Communicating Advanced Mathematics to Kids","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Visual intuition behind a hard Putnam problem","quote":"Here are my favorite videos by Grant","text":"So many things I could say about this, but I\"ll let Alan Kay speak for me - [LINK] Alan talks about how ideas in Calculus could be manifested from an early age and a richness in understanding built up as they aged up: - at very young ages, kids really respond well through \"doing\" / the enactive channel. When asked to draw a circle, kids in Papert's group would first emulate what a LOGO turtle would do by rotating their body in a circle (making tiny increments in x and y). - as they got a bit older, the visual / iconic channel was more developed and they could understand the abstraction of a circle on pencil/paper and how the concepts carried over there - closer to early teens, symbols were much easier to grasp and relate to, etc. With this context in mind, there have been some cool efforts to mix the second and third channels I just mentioned to communicate advanced math concepts. Vi Hart and Grant Sanderson's youtube channels come to mind. Here are my favorite videos by Grant: - \"What does it feel like to invent math\": [LINK] - On the visual intuition behind a hard problem on the Putnam exam - \"The hardest problem on the hardest test\": [VIDEO] - From vectors to matrices to vector spaces to higher-level ideas like the link between linear algebra and calculus: \"Essence of linear algebra\" series: [LINK] What I especially like about Grant's videos, is that he often walks through what a mathematician would do, the questions she would ask, etc."},{"id":44618691,"author":"mauriziocalo","month":"2025-07","story":"OpenAI claims gold-medal performance at IMO 2025","stance":"rec","why":"Walkthrough of a hard Putnam problem showing how one thinks through such problems.","quote":"these videos give a sense of how someone might actually go about thinking through and solving these kinds of problems","text":"Related — these videos give a sense of how someone might actually go about thinking through and solving these kinds of problems: - A 3Blue1Brown video on a particularly nice and unexpectedly difficult IMO problem (2011 IMO, Q2): [LINK] -- And another similar one (though technically Putnam, not IMO): [VIDEO] - Timothy Gowers (Fields Medalist and IMO perfect scorer) solving this year’s IMO problems in “real time”: -- Q1: [LINK] -- Q4: [LINK]"},{"id":21511789,"author":"ncallaway","month":"2019-11","story":"Moving towards a faster web","stance":"rec","why":"Great video on empathy for new learners","quote":"See for a great video by 3blue1brown on this topic.","text":"I strongly disagree with this comment. I sincerely doubt 100% of the HN audience always considers those things. You might think some of those things are basic, but that doesn't make them useless. This kind of lack of empathy for new learners can make it incredibly difficult to teach concepts to those who don't have as much knowledge as you may have. See [VIDEO] for a great video by 3blue1brown on this topic."},{"id":21287025,"author":"tromp","month":"2019-10","story":"The “Windmill” Problem on the 2011 International Mathematical Olympiad [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Insightful video on the hardest Putnam problem","quote":"I enjoyed watching this similarly insightful video [1]","text":"I enjoyed watching this similarly insightful video [1] on \"The hardest problem on the hardest test\" of the Putnam Competition. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["9XkHBmE-N34",1],["AJdEqssNZ-U",1],["agOdP2Bmieg",1],["PFc379hu--8",1],["TH9VCN6UkyQ",1],["XFDM1ip5HdU",1],["If61baWF4GE",1],["klTjiXjqHrQ",1],["FvmTSpJU-Xc",1],["p2LZLYcu_JY",1],["N9c7_8Gp7gI",1],["kjBOesZCoqc",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["akVL7QY0S8A",1]]},"Sm5xF-UYgdg":{"wy":"Recommended as evidence that the world is improving despite negative news, and for how people misjudge global trends.","q":{"x":"I promise it is not a waste of your time.","i":26313529,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26313529,"author":"Baeocystin","month":"2021-03","story":"The Microwave Economy","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows life is much better than in the past","quote":"I promise it is not a waste of your time.","text":"I'm not sure if capitalism deserves the credit or not, but you are completely correct when you say that life is much, much better now than it was even in the recent past. To those who doubt this: Please watch Hans Rosling's talk first, which I will link below. I promise it is not a waste of your time. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24368409,"author":"Baeocystin","month":"2020-09","story":"Nothing can now be believed which is seen in a newspaper (1807)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Source of the commenter's understanding","quote":"It's a very good presentation.","text":"Not the person you were responding to, but I learned it from this TED talk: [VIDEO] (Not joking in the least. It's a very good presentation. Title is: How not to be ignorant about the world | Hans and Ola Rosling)"},{"id":10356953,"author":"ptaipale","month":"2015-10","story":"'Extreme poverty' to fall below 10% of world population for first time","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Data showing the world is improving","quote":"You really should watch this Hans Rosling's TED talk","text":"You really should watch this Hans Rosling's TED talk: [VIDEO] It's not misleading. Malnutrition is going down, lack of education is going down, lack of access to healthcare is going down. The more you read the Guardian, the more you have misconceptions about how bad the world is developing."},{"id":36273109,"author":"thomashop","month":"2023-06","story":"Meta releases New SOTA Text to Music generator better than MusicLM","stance":"rec","why":"Global stats on health, education and poverty improving","quote":"The brothers Rosling have a nice talk about how all the big stats are improving globally","text":"Can you provide some sources? Last time I looked into statistics on these topics I found the opposite to be true. The brothers Rosling have a nice talk about how all the big stats are improving globally (gender equality, education, health, extreme poverty, life expectation) [VIDEO]"},{"id":28343117,"author":"NalNezumi","month":"2021-08","story":"Mean World Syndrome","stance":"rec","why":"How people misjudge how bad the world is","quote":"how bad people thought the world was compared to how it actually is","text":"Hans Rosling had a talk about this topic[1] 6 years ago, albeit less about \"mean\" and more about how \"bad\" the world was, and more importantly, how bad people thought the world was compared to how it actually is. Not too surprisingly, even media and journalists scored worse than random guessing. So it's hard to point fingers to the viewer getting wrong impression of the world when the information senders themselves can't measure it (or send it) correctly [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15279963,"author":"sekou","month":"2017-09","story":"Is the World Falling Apart? Not at All","stance":"rec","why":"Shows the world improving despite perception","quote":"covers this topic well. Objectively the world is improving for humanity","text":"The \"How not to be ignorant about the world\" ( [VIDEO] ) TED talk by Hans (Rest In Peace) and Ola Rosling covers this topic well. Objectively the world is improving for humanity, however in the West and in \"developed\" nations it's harder to feel like things are going well."},{"id":24648252,"author":"NalNezumi","month":"2020-10","story":"Industrial Literacy","stance":"reference","why":"Rosling's message that things are not as bad as you think","quote":"Hey, it is not as bad as you think","text":"The core message I found from this article was that, to be a valuable participant on the discussion of improvement in society, when it comes to industry, is: based on Chesterton's Fence principle, we need to inform ourselves of the good industrialization have brought us (and accept it), and from there acknowledge their short-comings/side effects so that an proposed solution does not undermine the good that was added by it. This includes pointing out where industrialization is not doing much good (such as fashion and its CO2 emission as someone pointed out) Isn't being pedantic about each bullet point (that obviously can't go in to fine detail because of short summary) kind of ignoring the overall point? Is the core argument such an emotion inducing controversial point that justify almost straw-man-like criticism? edit: I now understand that it seems to be a bit of a politically heated topic. For me, a non-american, I saw similarity in \"Industrial Literacy\" and what Hans Rosling[1] told us a couple of years ago: \"Hey, it is not as bad as you think\" [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20173762,"author":"dexen","month":"2019-06","story":"There is nothing more depressing than “positive news”","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence of positive trends","quote":"","text":"While the parent comment is getting downvotes, probably due to the somewhat accusatory form, the facts behind it are universally true: our civilization is improving. If you took several metrics and weighted them by people's concern, you would see a positive trend [1]. Any \"good news\" is a statement of the fact, and should be a duh moment to everybody. Granted, we need the \"bad news\" too, as certain things still need fixing. However living with a negative impression of the world is living in denial. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["fCn8zs912OE",1],["kdsb2wwn-7g",1],["mXVGIb3bzHI",1],["8bT6txm4RpA",1]]},"hVimVzgtD6w":{"wy":"HN invokes it as the classic evidence that the world has improved and that people misjudge it, and as an example of great data presentation.","q":{"x":"the original 2007 Gapminder talk[1] still surprised and educates people today","i":6921233,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":27070850,"author":"sdht0","month":"2021-05","story":"Gapminder: A non-proﬁt ﬁghting global misconceptions","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Classic talk showing people misjudge the state of the world.","quote":"This classic TED talk [0] by Hans Rosling presents the same idea.","text":"The choices are deliberately wide. The idea is that people are unable to make even broad estimates of the state of the world correctly. This classic TED talk [0] by Hans Rosling presents the same idea. I recommend watching all his other talks as well [e.g., 1-2]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":23550895,"author":"mywacaday","month":"2020-06","story":"Fighting poverty accidentally stopped deforestation","stance":"strong_rec","why":"A talk worth watching repeatedly, from a great presenter.","quote":"always worth a watch.","text":"Probably been posted a lot before but always worth a watch. [VIDEO] The Great Hans Rosling. His book is well worth a read as well."},{"id":21015881,"author":"jsperson","month":"2019-09","story":"Economists don’t understand much about the macroeconomy","stance":"strong_rec","why":"One of the best TED talks, showing the world has improved.","quote":"Rosling also gave one of the best TED talks ever","text":"You should check out the book Factfulness by Hans Rosling. Rosling, a now deceased Swedish doctor, makes the case that the world has improved immensely over the past two centuries. He backs it up with copious statistics from the UN and other international NPOs. Rosling also gave one of the best TED talks ever: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6921233,"author":"nl","month":"2013-12","story":"We Need to Talk About TED","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Classic Gapminder talk that still surprises and educates people.","quote":"the original 2007 Gapminder talk[1] still surprised and educates people today","text":"80% of everything is crap. And yet the original 2007 Gapminder talk[1] still surprised and educates people today [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":48050390,"author":"mzi","month":"2026-05","story":"Child marriages plunged when girls stayed in school in Nigeria","stance":"rec","why":"Data-driven talk by an excellent lecturer on similar subjects.","quote":"I find him an excellent lecturer.","text":"This kind of data was shown by late Hans Rosling and his foundation Gapminder¹. He gave a Ted talk² about similar subjects as well, and I find him an excellent lecturer. ¹ [LINK] ² [VIDEO]"},{"id":46252564,"author":"Gud","month":"2025-12","story":"Ensuring a National Policy Framework for Artificial Intelligence","stance":"reference","why":"Used to argue the poor have a decent life in most places.","quote":"","text":"This is completely wrong. Even “the poor” in most parts of the world has a pretty good life weight where they are. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40009833,"author":"vishalontheline","month":"2024-04","story":"Vietnamese property tycoon sentenced to death in $27B fraud case","stance":"reference","why":"Says urbanization and education reduce birth rates.","quote":"Urbanization and education are the two most effective ways of getting a population to have fewer children.","text":"Hans Rossling's talk, \"The best stats you've ever seen\" had something to say about this: [VIDEO] Urbanization and education are the two most effective ways of getting a population to have fewer children. Singapore is a highly educated and urbanized country."},{"id":39019721,"author":"yencabulator","month":"2024-01","story":"A woman bought a vintage dress. It had a secret pocket with a mysterious note","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how birth rates respond to child survival rates.","quote":"Having more children is near-universal reaction to poor child survival rate","text":"Having more children is near-universal reaction to poor child survival rate, and vice versa. (Though usually it's not about the offspring dying as adults .) [VIDEO]"},{"id":31226981,"author":"incomingpain","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: I am losing hope in humanity. What should I do?","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as supporting evidence in a discussion of doomerism and bubbles.","quote":"","text":">Is losing faith/hope in humanity something others experience as well? Generally considered the 'doomer' political circle. No proper political alignment, often seen as purple or populist. Also not necessarily a pessimistic world view. >s it due to the way i perceive things (i.e. my entourage)? Most likely yes, but can be independently derived. >Am I in a bubble and I don't see it? How do I get out? Should I be in a bubble to keep my sanity? How do I build those filters? Everyone is in a bubble. Imagine a venn diagram of things you know and things you dont know. That's your bubble. Being in a bubble isn't inherently bad. Being in an echo chamber is bad and the probability you are in an echo chamber is very high. To evaluate if you're in an echo chamber, pretend to say something you dont agree with inside your echo chamber just see how it's reacted to. \"climate change is false for X reason\" >- climate change or how society/corporations/governments are destroying the environment and/or are barely doing anything. All I think about sometimes is how we'll begin to see famines spreading across poor countries until it's all too late... Yet Elon buying Twitter is still in the news till this day. Climate change is often the first one in the doomer field lately. Take extinction rebellion for example, or the maya calendar or y2k doomers. They are all climate change now. Their climate change echo chamber is quite problematic. All this 10 years left or various ultimatums are absurd. They'v"},{"id":29417713,"author":"cowl","month":"2021-12","story":"100 years of whatever this will be","stance":"reference","why":"Supports the claim that humanity has improved on measurable metrics.","quote":"","text":"By every measurable metric, Humanity has gotten better. Longer lifespan, lower child mortality, better education, easier access to basic life neccessities and goods, (ironic to say at this time but yes even) better health, etc etc etc. We live in an age where every problem is weaponised and we are hyperaware of the problems now so that we don't see all the progrees that is done. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27591295,"author":"rocknor","month":"2021-06","story":"Australia opposes Great Barrier Reef ′in danger′ listing","stance":"reference","why":"One of several links showing global birth rates have fallen.","quote":"","text":"> if the 1 billion \"rich people\" drop their emissions down to below average then the majority of the problem will still be here. True. But the \"majority\" is not as big as you think. > there will be 1 billion less wealthy people Just wrong. There are lots of ways to reduce emissions without any effect in wealth. Eating less meat is one example. > for no particular net gain. Very wrong, there are huge gains: [LINK] > I advocate the radical approach of encouraging people to have less children. I knew you would say this, I was just waiting for it. Global birth rates have been in freefall for decades now, it's tragic that people don't know about it. You'd assume that supposedly \"better\" education in western countries would make people more aware, but western governments are only concerned with maintaining global western hegemony which requires falsely accusing the rest of the world and keeping their citizens ignorant. Also, you can't force people to have less children, that's inhumane. I suggest you go through the following links. [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":23450454,"author":"asjw","month":"2020-06","story":"A New Cold War Has Begun (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how national wealth leads to smaller families and longer lives.","quote":"Hans Rosling explained very well why advancements in National wealth leads to smaller families","text":"Hans Rosling explained very well why advancements in National wealth leads to smaller families (less children per family) and longer lives It's linked to becoming richer countries, which China has become [VIDEO]"},{"id":22226698,"author":"keanzu","month":"2020-02","story":"James Lovelock: 'I Would Not Waste My Fortune on Colonizing Mars'","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as showing the world has already improved dramatically.","quote":"According to Hans Rosling the world has already changed dramatically for the better","text":"> if you try to objectively make life better for the billions According to Hans Rosling the world has already changed dramatically for the better, you just didn't notice. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11796842,"author":"oconnor663","month":"2016-05","story":"Challenging the myth that the rich are specially-talented wealth creators","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as an economic play-by-play of the last 50 years.","quote":"Obligatory link to Hans Rosling's economic play-by-play of the last 50 years","text":"> If you buy some shares in M&S or BP on the stock market, the money you pay goes to the previous owner, not the company. You are what Keynes called a ‘functionless investor.’ When such so-called ‘investments’ pay off they extract wealth from the economy without creating anything in return. This doesn't seem very coherent. How could a company ever sell stock to raise capital, if the people who bought that stock didn't have a functioning market to resell it on? Obligatory link to Hans Rosling's economic play-by-play of the last 50 years: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6959840,"author":"trevorstrohman","month":"2013-12","story":"The real use of money is to buy freedom","stance":"reference","why":"Shows most global indicators like child mortality and GDP improving.","quote":"shows how most indicators are improving worldwide","text":"This Hans Rosling talk from a few years ago shows how most indicators are improving worldwide (e.g. reductions in child mortality, GDP per capita). [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ImoQqNyRL8Y",1],["5MxH1sfJLBQ",1]]},"qI_g07C_Q5I":{"wy":"HN repeatedly recommends it as a balanced, beginner-friendly overview of SQL versus NoSQL, and some teachers assign it to students.","q":{"x":"one of the rare videos I've required students in my database classes to watch.","i":12643863,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":15429433,"author":"WoodenChair","month":"2017-10","story":"Ask HN: Learning NoSQL, papers and books","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Strong overview of the NoSQL paradigms for beginners.","quote":"I strongly recommend this 1 hour talk by Martin Fowler","text":"As a starting point, if you have little background in NoSQL, I strongly recommend this 1 hour talk by Martin Fowler: [VIDEO] It's slightly dated, but it still gives a strong overview of the different paradigms. The truth is what you want to learn probably differs greatly depending on the paradigm that fits your application. NoSQL databases can broadly be categorized into document-oriented, key-value store, columnar, and graph. This video will help you understand what (at least three) of those are. Then you can focus in on books/articles about the paradigm that makes the most sense for you."},{"id":12643863,"author":"joeclark77","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great overview of why and when NoSQL is valuable; required viewing.","quote":"one of the rare videos I've required students in my database classes to watch.","text":"First, the \"Mother of all Demos\" by Doug Engelbart: [LINK] This was in 1968, at a time when most people thought about computers as being machines for solving computation problems, like processing payrolls or calculating rocket trajectories. Engelbart and his students had the radical idea that computers could be used for human \"knowledge worker\" productivity. In one 90 minute presentation, he introduces everything from the idea of a GUI, to the mouse, to word processing, hypertext, computer graphics, and (simulated) videoconferencing. You have to be able to put yourself in the shoes of the audience that has never seen this stuff before, and it'll blow you away. Something more recent: Martin Fowler's great introduction to NoSQL: [VIDEO] Not so technical, this is a great overview of the reasons why (and when) NoSQL is valuable. He crams a lot into a short speech, so it's one of the rare videos I've required students in my database classes to watch. Now, really getting away from the technical, I have to recommend watching the IDEO shopping cart video: [LINK] This is the classic introduction of Design Thinking to the world, in 1999. If you're using the Lean Startup or an Agile method, but have never heard of IDEO's shopping cart, you may be able to get along fine at work, but you should be kind of embarrassed like a physicist who's never read Newton."},{"id":26192401,"author":"databrecht","month":"2021-02","story":"Ask HN: SQL or NoSQL?","stance":"rec","why":"Very good introduction to the term NoSQL.","quote":"This is a very good introduction to the term","text":"Exactly, but it goes further than that. The mentality never made sense since the term NoSQL never made sense to start. It's amazing how many people use a term that just originated from a meeting to talk about alternative databases. How we keep using it, although it's practically impossible to say what NoSQL is. Depending on whom you ask that term means different things. This is a very good introduction to the term: [VIDEO] Graph databases are considered 'NoSQL' yet they have relations and transactions. Schemaless is often also one of the properties give to NoSQL, but it's also a bit strange to consider that a NoSQL attribute. Some traditional databases offer schemaless options and databases like Cassandra has schema yet is considered NoSQL. I work at Fauna which has relations, stronger consistency than many traditional databases. It is schemaless at this point but that might change in the future. Since it doesn't offer SQL it's thrown into the NoSQL bucket with the assumptions that come along with it. None of these one-liners in computer science make sense IMHO and we listen way too often to colleagues who use them. Similarly \"Use SQL for enforced schema\" might be accurate in many cases but in essence it depends on your situation, and we need to do research about what we use instead of following one-liners ;)"},{"id":15428267,"author":"rolandm","month":"2017-10","story":"Ask HN: Learning NoSQL, papers and books","stance":"rec","why":"Intro to NoSQL as learning resource.","quote":"Video from Martin Fowler about Introduction to NoSQL","text":"Video from Martin Fowler about Introduction to NoSQL: [VIDEO] Tutorial from Felix Gessert about NoSQL [LINK] and Slides [LINK]"},{"id":10683040,"author":"mrwilliamchang","month":"2015-12","story":"I Dreamed of a Perfect Database","stance":"rec","why":"More balanced view of SQL versus NoSQL.","quote":"This video presents a much more balanced view of SQL versus NoSQL, in my opinion.","text":"\"There is as yet no absolute challenger to the relational model. When people think database, they still think SQL. But if there is a true challenger, it is in the graph model.\" This article is quite biased towards graph databases with regards to the SQL versus NoSQL tension. This video presents a much more balanced view of SQL versus NoSQL, in my opinion. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10478844,"author":"joeclark77","month":"2015-10","story":"Why some NoSQL DBs only let you perform transactions on a single data item","stance":"rec","why":"Good introduction shown to students.","quote":"Martin Fowler has a good Youtube on \"Introduction to NoSQL\" that I sometimes show to my students","text":"The idea is that you won't need database-wide transactions if your data is structured into \"natural aggregates\" such as orders, articles, profiles, etc. In a relational database, something as simple as an \"order\" has components in lots of different tables -- customer, product, order, line_item, shipping_method, etc -- so you have to lock all of those tables when doing an INSERT or UPDATE. In the ideal MongoDB implementation, an \"order\" would be a document of its own, and all the other parts would be attributes or nested structures. So you only really need a transaction for the document itself. Other documents could be inserted/updated at the same time without creating a conflict. That's the theory, at least. Martin Fowler has a good Youtube on \"Introduction to NoSQL\" that I sometimes show to my students: [VIDEO] The other thing about NoSQL is that it's often used for write-once, then read-only applications. Twitter, Facebook, or Instagram would be good examples. When 1% of your queries are INSERTs and 99% are SELECTS (with UPDATE and DELETE almost never occurring), you really don't run as much risk of anomalies. I would not recommend NoSQL for something like a banking application where you're doing lots of updates and need to guarantee consistency."},{"id":9698248,"author":"alfonsodev","month":"2015-06","story":"Ask HN: When should i use a NoSql database","stance":"rec","why":"Fowler's recommendations on choosing relational, graph or NoSQL.","quote":"In this video Martin Fowler have some recommendations of when to choose a relational, graph or noSql database.","text":"[VIDEO] In this video Martin Fowler have some recommendations of when to choose a relational, graph or noSql database."},{"id":28362412,"author":"shaicoleman","month":"2021-08","story":"What I'm Up to Now","stance":"reference","why":"Talk briefly touches on downsides but aims to excite.","quote":"He briefly touches on the downsides during the talk, but the purpose of a talk is to excite and pique somebody's interest.","text":"> It turned out that they [The Guardian] were burnt, and wrote about their painful migration to a RDBMS and to safety It's not really fair to judge technical decisions retroactively. PostgreSQL in 2012 wasn't the same as in 2018. PostgreSQL was uncommon back then, there was a limited talent pool, it was painful to operate/failover/scale/shard, and it wasn't yet available on RDS. JSONB support only arrived to Postgres in Dec/2014. The alternatives (e.g. MySQL) were also problematic, e.g. locking tables every time a new column was added was likely a deal-breaker. They only migrated to PostgreSQL because they didn't want the pain and downtime of self-hosting MongoDB, but there didn't seem to be any major architectural issues. In this case it seemed like specific issues with MongoDB in production rather than NoSQL in general. He briefly touches on the downsides during the talk, but the purpose of a talk is to excite and pique somebody's interest. [VIDEO] In the book he uses more nuanced language and warns that it's not something to be used on every occasion. * \"It’s essential to test your expectations about programmer productivity and/or performance before committing to using a NoSQL technology.\" * \"Most applications, particularly nonstrategic ones, should stick with relational technology—at least until the NoSQL ecosystem becomes more mature.\" * \"But we also realize that there are many cases, indeed the majority of cases, where you’re better off sticking with the default option "},{"id":27932840,"author":"node-bayarea","month":"2021-07","story":"The Impedance Mismatch Test: Is Your Data Layer a Complex Mess?","stance":"reference","why":"Links Fowler as someone who uses the impedance mismatch term.","quote":"","text":"\"Object-Relational Impedance Mismatch\"[1] is been around for decades. Martin Fowler[2] and others who are deeply technical (think Sr. architects, CTOs) use this all the time. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28361124,"author":"charlysl","month":"2021-08","story":"What I'm Up to Now","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"his presentations are still very unbalanced, hyping what is still inmature and extremelly risky.","text":"My main point was that we should focus first on learning and applying well ageless fundamentals and mature solutions, rather than on shoehorning the latest fad. Only in extreme cases should that new idea from a FAANG be adopted. Martin Fowler is dangerous in this regard, there isn't a bandwagon he doesn't jump on making it sound like it is the new normal, and, although, as you say, he does mention counterindications, his presentations are still very unbalanced, hyping what is still inmature and extremelly risky. He is an excellent popularizer, I actually enjoy listening to him, but too many buy it uncritically. I meantioned CQRS, the wider context is microservices. Thank you for presenting so much evidence, you are right, but the thing is, the wider context where it was said, all the hype, is still missing. Here is one example from a few years ago in youtube [1], at the height of the NoSQL craze this time. He proudly mentioned how his mates at The Guardian adopted it, because \"a news article is a natural aggregate\", or something like that. He does indeed add later, in passing, that \"some NoSQL databases are immature , we don't have the tools, the experience, the knowledge to work with them well; we've got decades of experience with sql databases\". It turned out that they were burnt at The Guardian, and wrote about their painful migration to a RDBMS and to safety [2]. Now, one can argue, rightly, that this is just anecdotal evidence against NoSQL. The problem is that he used i"}],"r":[["WYPNjSoDrqw",1]]},"ynbgMKclWWc":{"wy":"HN commenters share it as an accessible case for worker cooperatives and workplace democracy as a model for a modern left.","q":{"x":"I recently came across an interesting talk by Richard Wolff at Google [1]","i":19435968,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":15943928,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2017-12","story":"An Anarchist Critique of Democracy (2005)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Wolff on what economics education omits; worker-run company example.","quote":"Fascinating talk.","text":"In this video[0], economist Richard Wolff talks about all the things he didn't learn studying economics at the US's most prestigious universities, because they were never mentioned. Fascinating talk. Towards the end he talks of a Spanish company run by its workers, which sounds like it has successfully transformed work for those people to something democratic, and, well, something free from what anarchists (and we all..uh 99% of us anyway) want to be free from. It sounds doable to extend that to other areas of life. Watch it! Highly enlightening ...and 'hope you can believe in' isn't that common nowadays. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15165783,"author":"TheDongIsYou","month":"2017-09","story":"To Understand Rising Inequality, Consider Janitors","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Wolff's practical critique of capitalism.","quote":"I'm currently very impressed by the work of Prof. Richard Wolff","text":"I used to be inspired by the Venus Project years ago. Unfortunately I realized it is extremely utopian and things needs to change in a more practical sense if we want a good chance of succeding. I'm currently very impressed by the work of Prof. Richard Wolff, an economist that heavily criticizes Capitalism and tries to make actual practical changes in the lives of people starting from their workplaces. Here is a talk he made in front of Google employees (the irony): [VIDEO]"},{"id":28822081,"author":"zja","month":"2021-10","story":"My Emotions as a CEO","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on democracy at work as a new flavor of socialism.","quote":"You might find this talk interesting, it’s about “democracy at work” as a new-ish flavor of socialism.","text":"Very well said! You might find this talk interesting, it’s about “democracy at work” as a new-ish flavor of socialism. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26781308,"author":"mempko","month":"2021-04","story":"How People Get Rich Now","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what a modern far left looks like.","quote":"I recommend anyone interested in what a modern \"far left\" looks like learn about Richard Wolff and the Democracy at Work movement.","text":"Mr Graham writes \"You would think, after having been on the side of labor in its fight with capital for almost two centuries, that the far left would be happy that labor has finally prevailed. \" Unfortunately Mr. Graham has an outdated view about the \"far left\". I recommend anyone interested in what a modern \"far left\" looks like learn about Richard Wolff and the Democracy at Work movement. A startup in a garage where everyone is an owner building great stuff is pretty much it. He did a Google Talk here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19435968,"author":"ace_of_spades","month":"2019-03","story":"Kickstarter’s staff is unionizing","stance":"rec","why":"Workplace democratization via worker coops as a solution.","quote":"I recently came across an interesting talk by Richard Wolff at Google [1]","text":"To make this discussion maybe a little bit more solution oriented, what would be your solution to problems of labor mistreatment? Or do you suggest that nothing like that exists? I recently came across an interesting talk by Richard Wolff at Google [1] which suggests that a great solution would be the democratization of the work place in the form of worker coops. It’s actually a tried and true model that works well in a variety of contexts. It would probably solve the problem you outline as well as make labor mistreatment a lot less common. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14781718,"author":"Vektorweg","month":"2017-07","story":"How economics became a religion","stance":"rec","why":"Worker coops as a solution out of the economy.","quote":"a possible solution out of this economy is worker coops aka democracy at work.","text":"Suggested recently by Richard Wolff and many others before him: a possible solution out of this economy is worker coops aka democracy at work. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46499242,"author":"EGreg","month":"2026-01","story":"It's hard to justify Tahoe icons","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for employee-run corporations.","quote":"","text":"Seriously. People got canned for resisting the corporate overlords. That’s capitalism. Corporations run by their employees? Guilds? Cooperatives? Hah! [VIDEO] Just that usually the forcefed initiatives have to do with corporate profits for shareholders, or trends like shoving AI into everything. Imagine saying no to that! Even at the supra-corporate and supra-national level, if the organizing principle is competition, no actor not even a CEO or a corporate board or a government can afford to stop racing towards disaster. There is a simple mantra: “If we don’t achieve AGI first, China will and then they’ll dominate.” Once in a while, the world comes together to successfully ban eg chemical weapons or CFCs, and repair the hole in the ozone layer. Cooperation and restraint takes effort. Judging by the way we’ve drained all the aquifers, overfished the last fish, destroyed the kelp forests, cut down the rainforsts, bleached the corals, and polluted the world with plastic, I don’t think there is much hope of stopping. Insects and pollinators are way down, and many larger species are practically extinct, 95% of the world’s animal biomass is humans and their food, and people still pretend environmental catastrophe is all about a few degrees of temperature. PS: Yes, that escalated quickly. In the real world, it has taken only 80 years… :-/"},{"id":15888517,"author":"zackmorris","month":"2017-12","story":"Ask HN: What to do if you hate working for someone else and arent an entrepreneur?","stance":"reference","why":"Wolff on workplace fairness and unions.","quote":"Richard Wolff has talked at length about this","text":"I was going to say the same thing but you beat me to it. Without some form of profit sharing or stake holding by employees, there is simply no way to make the workplace fair. That fairness was traditionally sought by unions but they have lost much of their influence for political reasons because of their relationship with socialism. Richard Wolff has talked at length about this: [VIDEO] [LINK] Thom Hartmann's programs are another good source: [LINK] [LINK] Socialism gets a bad rap in the US because it has been abused in the past to give rise to things like fascism and communism. But many social democracies (much of Europe) today enjoy high standards of living and I would argue are focused more on culture and the pursuit of non-material goals than the US. We're at a crossroads where the middle class is on the brink of failure due to decades of trickle down economics (Reaganomics). Things like the upcoming tax cut will likely lead to stagflation (high inflation and flat wages, last felt in the 1970s) because there is no mathematical or economic basis for lowering taxes in boom times under a high national debt. So this will be the third bust I've seen (the dot bomb and housing bomb being the others) since the Glass-Steagall Act was repealed in 1999 by the Gramm–Leach–Bliley Act. Keep in mind that we had almost 70 years of relative stability since it was implemented: [LINK] The current trends are towards maximizing concentration of wealth, not just in the US but under multination"}],"r":[["ras_VYgA77Q",1],["ZK3O402wf1c",1],["G1r55efei5c",1],["AD4b-52jtos",1],["NP9AIUT9nos",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["e3msMuwqp-o",1],["h2cs8QLnxlU",1],["EY6q5dv_B-o",1],["oUaOucZRlmE",1],["agOdP2Bmieg",1],["443UNeGrFoM",1],["klTjiXjqHrQ",1],["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["MdmQUlD7P40",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["ef2jpjTEB5U",1],["XvDZLjaCJuw",1],["KJe9H6qS82I",1],["osSMJRyxG0k",1]]},"3Fx5Q8xGU8k":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a serious, influential talk on market size and monopoly, though some commenters criticize its philosophy as endorsing anti-competitive goals.","q":{"x":"this talk from YC's Startup School 2014 covering the ideas from Zero to One is excellent","i":28654469,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":40152567,"author":"WhatIsAModel","month":"2024-04","story":"Airlines required to refund passengers for canceled, delayed flights","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Thiel discusses the doom loop of competition.","quote":"Peter Thiel discusses this same doom loop and some of your points in this lecture that I highly recommend","text":"Peter Thiel discusses this same doom loop and some of your points in this lecture that I highly recommend: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28654469,"author":"gmays","month":"2021-09","story":"Peter Thiel became the most feared man in Silicon Valley","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent talk covering the ideas of Zero to One.","quote":"this talk from YC's Startup School 2014 covering the ideas from Zero to One is excellent","text":"For context, this article is about a new book, 'The Contrarian' about Thiel (I haven't read the book). I've long been a fan of Peter Thiel's thinking. If you're unfamiliar, this talk from YC's Startup School 2014 covering the ideas from Zero to One is excellent: [VIDEO] But back the book this article is based on, I found this review of the book on Amazon quite insightful: [LINK] I've never met Peter Thiel, don't know much about him personally, and don't agree with everything he says and does, but he's a complex and interesting character that I've learned a lot from over the years. I think we all can."},{"id":24603605,"author":"yyyuutt","month":"2020-09","story":"Ask HN: How to learn sales?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Classic Thiel talk on market size and monopoly.","quote":"Sounds like its definitely something you should watch.","text":"Whenever I hear someone saying the market is huge I think of this classic Thiel talk. Sounds like its definitely something you should watch. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38535638,"author":"narag","month":"2023-12","story":"Tampermonkey: Dev Mode will become mandatory for running userscripts in Chromium","stance":"rec","why":"Relevant to market-hostile businesses.","quote":"watch Peter Thiel's \"competition is for losers","text":"One phenomenon I've recently clued into is that of the \"market-hostile business\". Not only old businesses, watch Peter Thiel's \"competition is for losers\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":25094831,"author":"29athrowaway","month":"2020-11","story":"Having 170 competitors in not an obstacle","stance":"rec","why":"Serious talk despite clickbaity title.","quote":"It's a clickbaity title but it's a serious talk.","text":"Watch this talk, \"Competition is for losers\" (by Peter Thiel) [VIDEO] It's a clickbaity title but it's a serious talk."},{"id":34796526,"author":"ly3xqhl8g9","month":"2023-02","story":"Health concerns grow in East Palestine, Ohio, after train derailment","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Thiel's competition is for losers idea.","quote":"any company worth their capital knows very well that \"competition is for losers","text":"By control point you can understand a corporation or a government, no need for false dichotomies: in the age of capital there is no beyond. Enron was a weak company, nowhere near the escape reality velocity: at peak, in 2000, they had a market capitalization of $60 billion, at the same time Microsoft was at $600 billion [1]. Also, any company worth their capital knows very well that \"competition is for losers\" [2] and dreams of the ultimate consolidation [3]. [1] The Largest Public Companies by Market Cap (2000–2022), [LINK] [2] Competition Is for Losers, [LINK] , [VIDEO] [3] The Consolidation Curve, [LINK]"},{"id":30833038,"author":"peter_retief","month":"2022-03","story":"I wasted $40k on a fantastic startup idea (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that you need monopoly to make money.","quote":"I have realised that one needs some sort of monopoly to make any money.","text":"Interesting, fun also some of the reactions I got to my many ideas. I have realised that one needs some sort of monopoly to make any money. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23046011,"author":"nickff","month":"2020-05","story":"Why Restaurants Are So Fucked","stance":"reference","why":"Thiel on why commoditized restaurants lack pricing power.","quote":"you can work very hard to make the best restaurant in a city, but it doesn't give you much pricing power","text":"This post misses the forest for the trees. The reason for the low margins in the restaurant business is the lack of differentiation. Their products are largely commoditized, so they have no competitive advantage or market power. Peter Thiel discussed this in a lecture for YC, where he said that you can work very hard to make the best restaurant in a city, but it doesn't give you much pricing power, because the second best is still pretty good.[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14110432,"author":"mindcrime","month":"2017-04","story":"How United Airlines Went from “Friendly Skies” to Throwing People Off Flights","stance":"reference","why":"Thiel's airline example for why competition is for losers.","quote":"Peter Thiel uses airlines - and their razor thin profit margins - as the prototypical example of why \"competition is for losers","text":"I'm a little iffy on this idea that there is any monopoly on air travel in the US in any general sense. Maybe for specific routes, but if you look at the big picture, you have: 4 \"major\" carriers - American, Delta, United, Southwest 1 \"mid major\" / borderline major in Alaska Airlines 1 \"not a major, but not exactly a regional carrier\" in Jet Blue and a bunch of regional outfits like Frontier, Porter, Spirit, etc. That's not to say that more competition wouldn't be nice, but it's hardly the case that there is no competition. Heck, as far as that goes, Peter Thiel uses airlines - and their razor thin profit margins - as the prototypical example of why \"competition is for losers\"[1] in his talks. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43802745,"author":"AndrewKemendo","month":"2025-04","story":"Mark Zuckerberg personally lost the Facebook antitrust case","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Zuckerberg is doing precisely what Thiel taught him to and has done it with preternatural skill.","text":"“Competition is for losers” - A talk from Peter Thiel hosted by Y combinator ( [VIDEO] ) Timestamp 13:50: “If you’re a startup, and you want to get to a monopoly, start with a really small market and you take over that whole market and then you find ways to expand that market in concentric circles” Last I checked Zuckerberg is doing precisely what Thiel taught him to and has done it with preternatural skill. This is the standard for how you’re expected to act as a technology “leader” - which the majority of VC backed unicorn goal founders (like every FAANG founder) have been executing since day zero."},{"id":42414996,"author":"benreesman","month":"2024-12","story":"The entire healthcare system is broken, not just health insurance companies","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"go so far as to actively endorse induced market failure as the goal","text":"Markets are inevitable: create an arbitrarily oppressive prison and you will watch some “black” market emerge: markets grow like weeds: it’s just silly to argue that we should have markets or not. But markets are not a-priori useful to the broader society just by being markets! A necessary (but not sufficient) condition for a market to be useful is that it substantially avoids lasting and structural market failures. Market failures are almost as common and ubiquitous as markets! They vary in duration and consequence. The ultimate market failure is to have costs which include a profit margin but little or no competition to get the benefits of a market. Healthcare like other mandatory purchases (you can buy an ambulance ride and six figures of hospital while unconscious!) is especially prone to market failure. The United States especially (though late neoliberal “capitalism” to some degree generally) and HN/SV/EA especially now go so far as to actively endorse induced market failure as the goal . Fuck that. Fuck everyone who is on board with it. This is your goblin brainchild @pg: renounce it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42280303,"author":"benreesman","month":"2024-11","story":"Everything You Never Wanted to Know About Linker Script (2021)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"He leads off with making sure everyone is crystal clear that “attaining a monopoly” aka anti-trust fraud is his “idee fixé”.","text":"I mean, Unit 5 of Thiel’s startup 101 lectures at Stanford is titled “Competition is for Losers”. He leads off with making sure everyone is crystal clear that “attaining a monopoly” aka anti-trust fraud is his “idee fixé”. This isn’t hosted on InfoWars. It’s hosted on YC’s YouTube channel. It’s not a secret what these guys are doing and plan to do more of: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vgT4Y30DkaA",1],["RPdB_zdyMbM",1],["iD9xk3SDSYc",1],["7pSqk-XV2QM",1],["Xe5AE-qYan8",1],["8pBPZMUcsh0",1],["P4VBqTViEx4",1],["yFIa-Mc2KSk",1],["bpAi70WWBlw",1],["XXBxV6-zamM",1],["PdO3IP0Pro8",1]]},"4d0EGIt1SPc":{"wy":"It is the go-to explanation whenever a thread touches on imaging exoplanets or solar gravitational lens telescopes.","q":{"x":"Pretty good explanation of the technique and challenges involved","i":34306727,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36530962,"author":"lasc4r","month":"2023-06","story":"Monster gravitational waves spotted for first time","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mind-blowing Space Time episode","quote":"If you think LISA is mad this will blow your mind.","text":"I feel like you need to see _this_ spacetime video: [VIDEO] If you think LISA is mad this will blow your mind."},{"id":41865883,"author":"tzs","month":"2024-10","story":"A solar gravitational lens will be humanity's most powerful telescope (2022)","stance":"rec","why":"Different proposals for solar gravitational lens, specifics from 7 minutes","quote":"It starts getting into the specifics of those approaches at 7 minutes in","text":"Here's a PBS Space Time episode [1] from a couple years ago that describes a couple different proposals for how to do this that take quite different approaches. It starts getting into the specifics of those approaches at 7 minutes in if you don't need the introductory material. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39336626,"author":"tzs","month":"2024-02","story":"Are we visible to advanced alien civilizations?","stance":"rec","why":"Proposals for a Sun-based gravity lens telescope","quote":"Here's a PBS Space Time episode that talks about the proposals for building a Sun based gravity lens telescope [1].","text":"Yes, it is possible. Briefly, you use your solar system's star as a gravitational lens. There are currently a couple proposals working their way through the system to do that here in our solar system. It doesn't require technology much beyond what we already have, nor is it much more expensive than other unmanned deep space missions. We wouldn't be able to see pyramid sized objects on alien planets that way because the Sun is only about 1.4 million km diameter, and from other comments it would take a few million km. But for aliens with a bigger star? No problem. Here's a PBS Space Time episode that talks about the proposals for building a Sun based gravity lens telescope [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39056293,"author":"tzs","month":"2024-01","story":"Rumors Circulating That James Webb Has Discovered Life on Another World","stance":"rec","why":"Covers gravitational lens telescope and swarm of small light-sail telescopes","quote":"Here's a PBS Space Time episode [1] that talks about this general topic","text":"That's one approach to use the sun as a gravitational lens for looking at exoplanets. There's another approach that instead of one big telescope sends a series of much smaller telescopes, small enough that light sails would be effective for getting them to where they need to go. Here's a PBS Space Time episode [1] that talks about this general topic, and then about the series of smaller telescopes approach. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35345703,"author":"Galaxeblaffer","month":"2023-03","story":"JWST gets best view yet of planet in hotly pursued star system","stance":"rec","why":"Space Time video on gravitational lens telescopes","quote":"good old Space Time has a pretty good video on this","text":"good old Space Time has a pretty good video on this [VIDEO] and it might even be possible and more feasible to use planets [LINK] and even earth [LINK] as lenses"},{"id":34306727,"author":"nkrisc","month":"2023-01","story":"Wolf 1069B: Earth-mass planet in the habitable zone of a nearby, low-mass star","stance":"rec","why":"Explains imaging exoplanets with solar gravitational lens and its challenges","quote":"Pretty good explanation of the technique and challenges involved","text":"Pretty good explanation of the technique and challenges involved: [VIDEO] One figure that stands out, the probe would need to be about 550 AU from the sun. Voyager 1 is about 150 AU away from the sun right now, for comparison."},{"id":38921577,"author":"hackerlight","month":"2024-01","story":"Are we stuck with the Zoo Hypothesis?","stance":"reference","why":"Advanced technology could detect planets from farther away","quote":"presumably advanced alien technology can go further. See for example[2].","text":"Right, human technology can detect up to hundreds of light years[1], but presumably advanced alien technology can go further. See for example[2]. Hard to say how far. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["d4EgbgTm0Bg",1],["TLm7Q92xMjQ",1],["94ed4v_T6YM",1],["K4JhruinbWc",1],["YycAzdtUIko",1],["gJ5KV3rzuag",1],["jyQwgBAaBag",1],["RkTvDjhImwo",1],["DovunOxlY1k",1],["5uiroWBkdFY",1],["p45T7U5oi9Q",1],["tiKCORN-6m8",1],["HF-9Dy6iB_4",1],["gwf5mAlI7Ug",1],["msVuCEs8Ydo",1],["r-A78RgMhZU",1],["04ERSb06dOg",1]]},"C2RO34b_oPM":{"wy":"Strongly recommended for anyone working with vectors for its clear take on how they should be treated in types.","q":{"x":"but seriously take the time to watch this, it's fantastic","i":24862320,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24862320,"author":"dnautics","month":"2020-10","story":"A Programmer’s Intuition for Matrix Multiplication","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Why treating vectors as matrices causes trouble in languages.","quote":"but seriously take the time to watch this, it's fantastic","text":"> Purist test The short answer is. Yes, but if your PL treats it that way you will wind up in trouble [0]. The long answer is: [VIDEO] (but seriously take the time to watch this, it's fantastic). [0] Separately C- and often C-derived languages/frameworks like numpy also treat them as 1xN matrices, but that's a nearly tabs-vs-spaces level religious war, with actual consequences. I think a lot of people got confused when translating andrew ng's early octave-based ML coursera course (Nx1) to python (1xN), and also in the back and forth between math ML papers which use Nx1 and python."},{"id":16634115,"author":"dnautics","month":"2018-03","story":"Types, and Why You Should Care [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"How vectors and matrices should be treated in type systems.","quote":"Seriously anyone who does anything with vectors should watch this.","text":"Addendum: Also really good is this video about how one should treat vectors and matrices in a type system. Has good lessons for python, R, and Matlab. Seriously anyone who does anything with vectors should watch this. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30913596,"author":"gugagore","month":"2022-04","story":"Eigen: A C++ template library for linear algebra","stance":"rec","why":"Design choices for vectors in linear algebra libraries.","quote":"If you want a great video on the topic, see","text":"If you want a great video on the topic, see [VIDEO] Or equivalently [LINK] There are basically two self-consistent choices for the design of linear algebra libraries."},{"id":27893260,"author":"freemint","month":"2021-07","story":"Julia Computing raises $24M Series A","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on treating vector transposes properly.","quote":"If you will do a lot of matrix stuff you might want to listen to","text":"If you will do a lot of matrix stuff you might want to listen to [VIDEO] taking Vector Transpose Seriously"},{"id":23595264,"author":"dnautics","month":"2020-06","story":"Why is it important for a matrix to be square? (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Historiography of linear algebra and vector versus matrix conventions.","quote":"there's a fantastic historiography of linear algebra","text":"I happened to be the other way, I learned first that matrices were linear transformations and, being thick-headed, it took me a really long time to understand that matrices could also be used as \"dumb excel spreadsheets\". there's a fantastic historiography of linear algebra and why we're stuck in this situation here: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["x1v2tX4_dkQ",1],["f61KMw5zVhg",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["aP0Y1uAA-2Y",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["yTkzNHF6rMs",1],["nLApFANtkHs",1],["HyznrdDSSGM",1],["2wZ1pCpJUIM",1],["MkJkyMuBm3g",1],["6qD9XElTpCE",1],["CUjt36SD3h8",1],["ho7oagHeqNc",1]]},"lMcRBdSdO_U":{"wy":"It is the standard recommended introduction to Shen, pointed to whenever Shen or its type system comes up.","q":{"x":"@deech's talks, though older now, are fantastic and relevant[1,2].","i":37480645,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37480645,"author":"eggy","month":"2023-09","story":"The Power of Prolog","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fantastic, still relevant talks on Shen","quote":"@deech's talks, though older now, are fantastic and relevant[1,2].","text":"I love Shen, but I have not taken it on. I subscribed to the professional version for half a year, and I like the amount of implementations of Shen in other PLs. The pattern matching, optional type checking, and the built-in Prolog is incredible. It's a case where the creator has been shunned by the wider community, partially due to his diplomacy, but he is brilliant, and the uptake has been niche and bleeding edge. If it had a Mozilla behind it or other, I think it would have been more universally appealing. @deech's talks, though older now, are fantastic and relevant[1,2]. But take what I have to say with a grain a salt: I am an avid APL/J/BQN fanboy ;) [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":11742724,"author":"eggy","month":"2016-05","story":"Stanza: A New Optionally-Typed General Purpose Language from UC Berkeley","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great introduction to Shen from Strange Loop 2014","quote":"Great talk by Aditya Siram Sept 2014 Strange Loop talk introducing Shen","text":"Stanza looks very interesting. I'll have to check it out. Thanks! Shen allows you to turn the type checker on or off: (tc +) (tc -) You can leave your type signatures in the code when you turn them off, and when tc is on you only need a type in the top level of your function. It has a fully functional Prolog engine built in, and it supports optional lazy evaluation too. Great talk by Aditya Siram Sept 2014 Strange Loop talk introducing Shen [2]. Shen has been ported to many languages, since it only requires porting the 46 primitive instruction set, called Klambda. Shen has been ported to CL (SBCL), Ruby, JavaScript, Haskell and even Emacs Lisp. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35553041,"author":"eggy","month":"2023-04","story":"Why Janet?","stance":"rec","why":"Videos on programming front and back end in Shen","quote":"Aditya Siram made two very good videos on Shen.","text":"I was considering Shen[1] for this type of use, since it is implemented in many languages. It is a Lisp with built-in Prolog. Feature list from the site's main page: pattern matching, lambda calculus consistency, macros for defining domain specific languages, optional lazy evaluation, static type checking based on sequent calculus, one of the most powerful systems for typing in functional programming, an integrated fully functional Prolog, an inbuilt compiler-compiler, a BSD kernel under 15 languages (Lisp, Python, Javascript, C ...) and operating systems (Windows, Linux, OS/X), is extensively documented in a book has nearly a decade of use. Aditya Siram made two very good videos on Shen. You can program your front and backend in Shen given Shen is implemented in JavaScript and other PLs. I know somebody did a port to C, but I am not sure if you can then make an exe as described for Janet. [VIDEO] [LINK] You implement Kλ in your PL of choice if it is not already available, and voila, you can program in Shen! [1] [LINK]"},{"id":10270180,"author":"eggy","month":"2015-09","story":"Show HN: Eslisp – Minimal JavaScript S-expression syntax and macro system","stance":"rec","why":"Shows writing Shen on JavaScript and Ruby, front and back end","quote":"this talk [2] shows how you can write Shen on JavaScript, Ruby, front an backend.","text":"I am learning Shen [1], and this talk [2] shows how you can write Shen on JavaScript, Ruby, front an backend. Shen is a lisp with pattern matching, optional lazy evaluation, optional static type checking, macros of course, and many other cool treats! It can run on top of CL (SBCL), Clojure, JavaScript, Python, and more. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9655243,"author":"delish","month":"2015-06","story":"Shen-js Browser REPL","stance":"rec","why":"A 40-minute feel for Shen","quote":"Looking for a feel for Shen? A Strange Loop talk by @deech (40minutes)","text":"Looking for a feel for Shen? A Strange Loop talk by @deech (40minutes): Shen: A Sufficiently Advanced Lisp. [VIDEO] An essay: [LINK] It's insightful and readable and it links to several articles new Shen users would be interested in[0]. It was written before Shen, so it talks about Shen's predecessor, Qi. This article and the Bipolar Lisp Programmer by Mark Tarver (creator of Shen) made me feel not so weird for not \"getting\" UNIX, and helped me get excited about lisp. I'm looking to use Shen or another highly cross-platform functional language for my next project. Application logic in Shen with type-checking. web code using some framework. [0] Wow I'm re-skimming it now: it has Stanislav, James Gosling, buncha Smalltalk, Rob Pike's \"Systems Research is Dead\" and a lot more. This is actually a good overview of ways to be disillusioned with programming. IMO disillusionment by knowing our historical failures is an antidote to the hype-machine."},{"id":42447938,"author":"cylinder714","month":"2024-12","story":"Crunch – a Scheme compiler with a minimal runtime","stance":"reference","why":"Covers Shen's types at 12:00","quote":"Aditya Siram addresses types at 12:00 in this video","text":"I'm not super familiar with the details but I've heard that Shen has a really good type system. Aditya Siram addresses types at 12:00 in this video: [VIDEO] Shen homepage: [LINK]"},{"id":37088856,"author":"bsima","month":"2023-08","story":"The Bipolar Lisp Programmer","stance":"reference","why":"Case study of Shen's type system used in the real world","quote":"there is at least one case study in the real world","text":"I love Shen and highly recommend the Book of Shen and Tarver's other book on Logic and Computation. The crazy thing about Shen is that its type system is sequent calculus, which means when you define your types, you are literally writing the same language that mathematicians use when they prove things about type theories. Why would you use it? Because you can leverage a crazy amount of power by writing your own type theories in your programs. Is it practical in today's corporate world? Probably not, but there is at least one case study in the real world [VIDEO]"},{"id":20615065,"author":"leanthonyrn","month":"2019-08","story":"Switching from Common Lisp to Julia (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I was thinking about this the other day. How can I expose my Common Lisp code to Julia. 1. You could take the Clasp path by exposing CL to C++. [LINK] This means taking / writing a CL in Julia. 2. Julia has an AST representation written in femtolisp. So it would be in interesting target for CL or some glue middle language like Shen. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9300485,"author":"deech","month":"2015-03","story":"Shen: A Sufficiently Advanced Lisp [video] (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Second half covers turning JSON into a type using macros","quote":"The second half of the talk ( ) is about turning a JSON object into a type using macros and meta-programming.","text":"The second half of the talk ( [VIDEO] ) is about turning a JSON object into a type using macros and meta-programming."}],"r":[["aNR4n0i2ZlM",1],["tQpqsmwlfY0",1],["z8MVKianh54",1]]},"l_aFHrzSBrM":{"wy":"HN points to it to debunk the stubborn olive-oil-at-high-heat myth, valued for its cited sources and food scientist interviews.","q":{"x":"Adam Ragusea did a great video on the topic, with cited sources. I highly recommend watching it","i":28000106,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28000106,"author":"KozmoNau7","month":"2021-07","story":"What I know about cleaning and seasoning cast-iron skillets","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Well-cited video on cooking with extra virgin olive oil.","quote":"Adam Ragusea did a great video on the topic, with cited sources. I highly recommend watching it","text":"The majority of chefs from countries bordering the Mediterranean would laugh at your claim that extra virgin olive oil is not suitable for frying. Obviously you shouldn't go using your fancy $100 bottle for deep frying, but mostly because it would be a waste of money, and because frying dulls the flavor. You want to save the really flavorful and expensive oils for salads and as a finishing touch. I've been pan frying eggs, onions, pork chops, chicken, potatoes, fish, basically anything interchangeably in extra virgin olive oil and butter/ghee for decades. There is certainly a flavor difference, but that's because the olive oil is much milder in flavor than butter, and the process of frying dulls the flavor of the olive oil, as mentioned. I - along with millions of people in countries bordering the Mediterranean - enjoy the flavor of food cooked in olive oil. Adam Ragusea did a great video on the topic, with cited sources. I highly recommend watching it: [VIDEO] Sources: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Including this famous chef using it for deep frying: [LINK] Additional sources: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] The bottom line is that extra virgin olive oil is more heat stable than other cooking oils with higher smoke points. Just don't use it to sear steaks or something. So much of the conventional wisdom around cooking is not based on scientific fact, but rather on unsubstantiated observations and gut feelings. The viability of extra virgin olive oil for cooking is one of those pieces of "},{"id":26753203,"author":"KozmoNau7","month":"2021-04","story":"How a carnivorous mushroom poisons its prey","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Debunks the myth that olive oil is unsafe at high heat.","quote":"That's a very common misconception and a stubborn myth","text":"That's a very common misconception and a stubborn myth: [VIDEO] I fry mushrooms (and many other things) in olive oil all the time. It works great, tastes great and as long as you use extra virgin oil, doesn't create any more harmful substances than using rapeseed or sunflower seed oil, probably a lot fewer actually. You can even deep fry in it, which some celebrated chefs do."},{"id":39584570,"author":"funklute","month":"2024-03","story":"Combating olive oil fraud with nuclear innovations","stance":"rec","why":"Explains oil stability below the smoke point.","quote":"There's a pretty decent video on this by Adam Ragusea","text":"That's not the whole story though. Often, what is more important is how stable the oil is below the smoke point. There's a pretty decent video on this by Adam Ragusea: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32820481,"author":"cthalupa","month":"2022-09","story":"Prenatal cannabis exposure associated with mental disorders in children","stance":"rec","why":"Well-researched, with food scientist interviews on cooking with olive oil past smoke point.","quote":"Some good interviews with food scientists, etc. Interesting for anyone wanting to learn a bit more on this subject.","text":"Adam Ragusea does a lot of well researched videos around food science, and did one around using olive oil, even past the smoke point, fairly recently - [VIDEO] Some good interviews with food scientists, etc. Interesting for anyone wanting to learn a bit more on this subject. I've swapped to using EVOO for most anything where it won't be too overpowering of a taste."},{"id":30457615,"author":"donatj","month":"2022-02","story":"Soybean oil affects hypothalamus, causes genetic changes in mice: study (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows olive oil is among the chemically safest oils to heat.","quote":"Olive oil is actually one of the chemically safest oils to heat.","text":"The olive oil bit is a myth - see: [VIDEO] Olive oil is actually one of the chemically safest oils to heat."},{"id":34285334,"author":"sambeau","month":"2023-01","story":"Death by Vegetable Oil","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"See also this video","text":"\"Monitoring of Quality and Stability Characteristics and Fatty Acid Compositions of Refined Olive and Seed Oils during Repeated Pan- and Deep-Frying Using GC, FT-NIRS, and Chemometrics\" [LINK] See also this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33334485,"author":"gilbetron","month":"2022-10","story":"Only virgin type of olive oil consumption reduces the risk of mortality: study","stance":"reference","why":"Smoke point concerns are overblown.","quote":"Smoke point\" concerns are usually overblown","text":"\"Smoke point\" concerns are usually overblown, it's really just the flavor that goes away making it pointless (and more expensive) too cook with EVO. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30466371,"author":"Pyramus","month":"2022-02","story":"Soybean oil affects hypothalamus, causes genetic changes in mice: study (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Found the video","text":"Yes, what I'm saying is I haven't seen evidence that change in the oil at smoke point is correlated with safety/toxicity. Found the video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27990326,"author":"KozmoNau7","month":"2021-07","story":"What I know about cleaning and seasoning cast-iron skillets","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The wisps you see from olive oil on a frying pan is steam, unless of course the pan is crazy 10/10 hot, at which point any cooking oil will burn. I fry most things in either extra virgin olive oil or butter. The olive oil stands up to the heat better than butter (the milk solids will burn). [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["cjriSNgFHsM",1],["rFejpH_tAHM",1],["msVuCEs8Ydo",1],["-suTmUX4Vbk",1]]},"GoW8Tf7hTGA":{"wy":"Shared as a go-to humbling look at cosmic scale, noted for its chilling audio and visuals pairing.","q":{"x":"The combination of audio and content of this video gives me chills.","i":31440797,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31440797,"author":"BLKNSLVR","month":"2022-05","story":"Vangelis has died","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Star scale visuals paired with a Vangelis score.","quote":"The combination of audio and content of this video gives me chills.","text":"This video introduced me to Vangelis' existence, and only after looking up the artist did I realise he did Blade Runner and Chariots of Fire. [VIDEO] The combination of audio and content of this video gives me chills."},{"id":23546811,"author":"terrycody","month":"2020-06","story":"As many as 6B Earth-like planets in our galaxy, according to new estimates","stance":"rec","why":"Widely viewed star size comparison.","quote":"for those never saw it","text":"I can't help but post this Youtube video, for those never saw it: Star Size Comparison 2 (watched 28M times) [VIDEO]"},{"id":20227890,"author":"13415","month":"2019-06","story":"Professional Decline Comes Sooner Than People Think","stance":"rec","why":"Conveys humanity's smallness in space.","quote":"Check out this video","text":">> We're on a rock flying through space. > I have never understood this phrase as anything more than a platitude to make people feel better. It's more than a platitude. Check out this video: [VIDEO] Still, nothing better than a rainy day on earth."},{"id":13128924,"author":"pmoriarty","month":"2016-12","story":"Cassini Beams Back First Images from New Orbit","stance":"rec","why":"Star size comparison, more impressive in VR.","quote":"though this sort of thing is far more impressive when seen in VR","text":"If you think Saturn's big: [VIDEO] (though this sort of thing is far more impressive when seen in VR) But even better than that, for me, is going somewhere with very little light pollution at night and simply looking up at the Milky Way. Nothing makes me feel smaller and more in awe."},{"id":12444699,"author":"no1youknowz","month":"2016-09","story":"How Much More Can We Learn About the Universe?","stance":"rec","why":"Star size comparison conveys the universe's vastness.","quote":"I am thankful for these types of videos [1].","text":"I think about it all the time as well and I am thankful for these types of videos [1]. What I think about most, is how awesome it is to be alive right now. Where this video (and others like it) tell me about the universe and what discoveries have been found. At the end of this video, I am always wondering that if the Universe is as big as they claim. There must be discoveries waiting to be found that we can't even comprehend! Sometimes I find myself wishing we had probes that could be much faster than they are now. Instead of taking decades to go to the edges of our solar system, it takes months. Maybe within 50 years, by the end of my lifetime. We'll start to see images from Alpha Centauri and beyond. That I am hoping for! [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["TTFoJQSd48c",1],["cjuGCJJUGsg",1],["15_-hgsX2V0",1],["HhGuXCuDb1U",1],["Ef-mxjYkllw",1],["hSrGfElyfVE",1],["S89BHnaxULo",1],["EY6q5dv_B-o",1],["Q3uvzcY2Xug",1],["3wfNl2L0Gf8",1],["uRGljemfwUE",1],["qbIk7-JPB2c",1]]},"LKnqECcg6Gw":{"wy":"HN cites it as the accessible go-to explanation of gamma and color blending errors in image programs.","q":{"x":"I found minutephysics' treatment of this subject to be really pleasant and easy to grok.","i":48550763,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48550763,"author":"joedrago","month":"2026-06","story":"What every coder should know about Gamma Correction","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Pleasant, easy-to-grasp treatment of EOTF; used in work talks.","quote":"I found minutephysics' treatment of this subject to be really pleasant and easy to grok.","text":"For those interested in this topic, I found minutephysics' treatment of this subject to be really pleasant and easy to grok. I had a talk I gave at work once or twice discussing color conversion and I would play a 90 second snippet from this (attributed appropriately) as it would very quickly crash course people on the reasoning behind an EOTF in a really cute and layman's terms kind of way: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47046052,"author":"quietbritishjim","month":"2026-02","story":"Rendering the Visible Spectrum","stance":"rec","why":"Classic video covering gamma averaging problems well.","quote":"This classic MinutePhysics video covers it well","text":"> The final piece of the puzzle is gamma correction. Applying gamma correction to these RGB coordinates produces a new set of values which we call (R', G', B') that are related to the original by a transfer function 6 ... The reason this is done is to account for how our eyes perceive brightness nonlinearly. We can distinguish changes in dark shades much more easily than light shades because a linear increase R in has much more of a relative effect when R is small. Switching to (R', G', B') therefore provides more resolution in dark regions of the image where the eye is more sensitive to variations in brightness. I'm surprised that this isn't mentioned much earlier and much more prominently. Instead, it's practically a footnote. Maybe I'm mistaken, but I would bet 90% of the awkwardness in the very first image is from averaging these values (R', G', B') for the gradients rather than switching to the true linear values, averaging, then converting back. This classic MinutePhysics video covers it well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25644151,"author":"fireattack","month":"2021-01","story":"Ditherpunk: The article I wish I had about monochrome image dithering","stance":"rec","why":"Good starting point on why image editing blends wrongly.","quote":"A good start point is [1][2].","text":"I misunderstood what you mean. Please ignore. On a side note, by colorspace I mainly meant that we can just stick with one with ultra-wide gamut. There are indeed other reasons to have different color spaces. (Below is my original comment for transparency.) ------------- Gamma isn't really about CRT; or I should say, they're two different things. The fact CRT has a somewhat physical \"gamma\" (light intensity varies nonlinearly with the voltage) is likely just a coincidence with the gamma we're talking here. The reason gamma is used in sRGB is because human eyes are more sensitive to changes in darker area, i.e. if the light intensity changes linearly, it feels more \"jumpy\" in darker end (which causes perceptible bandings). This is especially an issue with lower color depth. To solve this, we invented gamma space to give darker end more bits/intensity intervals to smooth the perceptive brightness. >it's not needed to understand the difference between linear and non-linear colorspaces It absolutely should, since any gamma space would have problem with \"averaging\", as explained by the GP. Actually, it's so bad that almost all the image editing/representing tasks we have today are doing it wrong (resizing, blurring, mixing..). This topic has been discussed extensively on Internet, so I'm not going to go into detail too much. A good start point is [1][2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":22325050,"author":"quietbritishjim","month":"2020-02","story":"Brown: color is weird [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why gradients have a dark band: nonlinear RGB averaging.","quote":"here's a great explanation of why gradients usually have a dark band in the middle, on the MinutePhysics channel","text":"If we're on the \"watch this YouTube video if you work with colours\" train, here's a great explanation of why gradients usually have a dark band in the middle, on the MinutePhysics channel: [VIDEO] In short: the RBG values in an image are not actually the intensity of light, but some nonlinear function of intensity. To do a weighted average of two colours you need to first undo that nonlinearity, then do the averaging, then reapply the function. Most software doesn't do that so the colours end up wrong. This also applies to resizing images."},{"id":12553042,"author":"mxfh","month":"2016-09","story":"What every coder should know about gamma","stance":"rec","why":"Well explained for non-coders in popular video.","quote":"which was well explained to non-coders as well here in a quite popular MinutePhysics video","text":"Good reminder about these persisting blending issues in the linear interpretation of RGB values, which was well explained to non-coders as well here in a quite popular MinutePhysics video: [VIDEO] As others commented the gamma scaling issues seem even more relevant. Just please, don't use RGB color space for generating gradients. In fact, it's ill fitted for most operations concerning the perception of colors as is. chroma.js : [LINK] D3 : [LINK] Interesting excursion: historically the default viewing gammas seem to have lowered, because broadcasting defaulted to dimly lit rooms, while today's ubiquitous displays are usually in brighter environments. [LINK]"},{"id":41007807,"author":"incrudible","month":"2024-07","story":"Polychromatic Pixels","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on color mixing in image programs being incorrect.","quote":"","text":"I am not saying you can use the exact same math as in an image manipulation program, these work with different assumptions. Mixing colors in those is usually not correct anyway. [VIDEO] I am saying you can think of subpixels, which already exist, as a form of dithering. Most displays use just three primaries for subpixels - red, green and blue. Their arrangement is fixed, but that is not a limitation of this new technology."},{"id":29903398,"author":"circlingthesun","month":"2022-01","story":"CSS Gradients that avoid the “gray dead zone”","stance":"reference","why":"Related video on RGB storing nonlinear brightness and bad blending.","quote":"There is a related video by minute physics","text":"There is a related video by minute physics about how the RGB color space stores the square root of the brightness of each color channel, because human color perception is roughly logarithmic. Bad color blending is therefore pretty commonplace because it uses the average of square roots instead of the square root of the average of the original brightness values. See: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27831409,"author":"Akronymus","month":"2021-07","story":"The worst volume control UI in the world (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Relevant to errors from blending raw sRGB data.","quote":"Relevant","text":">This is in the same category of error as most programmers thinking that sRGB uses a linear light model. They try to do arithmetic like blending or blurring on the raw image data this inevitably does the wrong thing. Relevant: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19626761,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2019-04","story":"Unveiling the first-ever image of a black hole [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Non-linear color space causes errors in manipulation.","quote":"","text":"Mind you, that's a JPG you are editing, so in the 8 bit sRGB color space. That means manipulations can lead to errors and illusions due to the color space being non-linear and clipped[0]. The original data was likely linear and at a much higher precision. If the source was a 16 bit linear grayscale PNG for example you could be much more assured you're not seeing the effects of JPG compression and things that were actually measured. EDIT: Found better sources: 16-bit sRGB PNG: [LINK] 180 MiB original TIFF: [LINK] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18820341,"author":"sometime","month":"2019-01","story":"Gamma error in picture scaling","stance":"reference","why":"Explains color blending errors in blur effects.","quote":"(Computer Color is Broken, MinutePhysics)","text":"The same phenomenon also applies to color blending. In a modern context this phenomenon is especially visible in UI elements that blur the background: Up until recently many UIs did not get the blurring right, resulting in greyish dark spots between different colors. I think CSS blurring in most browsers still gets it wrong. [VIDEO] (Computer Color is Broken, MinutePhysics)"},{"id":12553020,"author":"qwertyuiop924","month":"2016-09","story":"What every coder should know about gamma","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier explanation of the gamma issue.","quote":"I'd already seen most of this in a video","text":"I'd already seen most of this in a video (courtesy of Henry, aka MinutePhysics, [VIDEO] ), but it was nice to see a programmer-oriented explanation, nonetheless."},{"id":9872910,"author":"anon4","month":"2015-07","story":"Mandelbrot Set with SIMD Intrinsics","stance":"critical","why":"Example of Apple blur effects blending colors incorrectly.","quote":"Even Apple don't care to make their blur effects correct - see","text":"I'm calling it pedantic, because as far as I can see nobody cares outside of people doing movie VFX and the like, where it is actually really really important that you get it right. Your GUI for example doesn't care - elements are blended assuming a linear colourspace, which means that all font rendering is just slightly off. Even Apple don't care to make their blur effects correct - see [VIDEO] . Even photoshop doesn't do it correctly by default. Almost everybody is perfectly happy treating the values as linear colour intensity. The bottom line is that sRGB is hideously hard to work in. You really want to only use it for storage and only if you really must. It's an optimisation to allow you better range on the low end, at the expense of the high intensity end, to match human colour vision. However, that means using 48bit RGB and that's not a price everyone wants to pay. People who do professional graphics work simply use 32bit floats per colour channel - 128bit RGBA, or even 192bit RGBRaGaBa (separate alpha for each channel) and have workstations with 32 or 64GB of RAM. However, your normal everyday GUI application needs to run on a phone with about 1GB of RAM. Or going back farther, it has to run on an Intel 486 with 16MB of RAM. That kind of explains where the culture of \"just treat it as linear, it's not that wrong\" came from :) ."}],"r":[["BdHK_r9RXTc",1],["3GJUM6pCpew",1],["VkIJbpdTujE",1],["xAoljeRJ3lU",1],["J44svaQc5WY",1]]},"XRr1kaXKBsU":{"wy":"HN links it as a go-to intuitive explanation whenever gravity as spacetime curvature or free fall comes up.","q":{"x":"his videos on special [1] and general relativity [2] were real eye-openers for me","i":30560053,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30560053,"author":"fsh","month":"2022-03","story":"We're building computers wrong [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Eye-opening general relativity explanation by a brilliant educator.","quote":"his videos on special [1] and general relativity [2] were real eye-openers for me","text":"I think he is easily one of the smartest people on YouTube and is producing some of the very best videos. He is also a brilliant educator. For examples, his videos on special [1] and general relativity [2] were real eye-openers for me, even though I have studied physics and have seen this stuff explained a million times before. What I really don't like are the new video titles. At some point he started A/B testing them to maximize his revenue. He even made a video about it [3]. Of course, this selects for the most outrageous clickbait in order to get that sweet engagement. I am starting to wonder if he took into account, how much this is starting to alienate long-term followers. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":44018821,"author":"qnleigh","month":"2025-05","story":"Dead Stars Don’t Radiate","stance":"rec","why":"Explains inertial frames and general relativity.","quote":"Here's a slightly dorky but very good Veritasium video that explains this issue and general relativity","text":"Ah yeah there are multiple definitions of 'acceleration' here. Unruh radiation occurs when you're not 'in an inertial reference frame,' loosely meaning that you feel acceleration. So in a rocket in space or (presumably) standing on Earth's surface. What you say makes intuitive sense, but it was actually the opposite logic that lead Einstein to his general theory of relativity. Here's a slightly dorky but very good Veritasium video that explains this issue and general relativity [VIDEO]"},{"id":35732173,"author":"darken","month":"2023-04","story":"Why Gravity Is Not Like the Other Forces","stance":"rec","why":"Good video on free fall and why gravity is not a force.","quote":"Veritasium had a good video on this topic","text":"Respin of a comment I posted a while back: This is what helped me wrap ahead around the fact that gravity \"is not really a force\": when you are in free fall you feel 0 acceleration. You appear to be accelerating relative to the ground-- but you're actually motionless in an \"inertial reference frame\". (Similar to how the astronauts on the ISS don't \"feel\" acceleration despite accelerating rapidly relative to the earth.) The \"force\" of gravity is often modeled as \"gravity pulling you down\" and the ground \"pushing you back up\". This works mathematically, but isn't quite logically consistent. In reality, on the ground you're in a region of warped spacetime, so you feel constant upward acceleration despite not actually accelerating. (Thinking of this another way, standing on earth feels identical to being in a far away spaceship accelerating at 9.8 m/s².) This is also why time \"speeds up\" near more massive objects. (Separate from \"acceleration\".) We're so used to gravity this it doesn't seem weird. But when you consider the fact that free-fall is when you truly do not experience acceleration... well pondering that from many angles is what ultimately led Einstein to his model of relativity. (This is me trying to condense what could be a 10 minute explanation into a few sentences, so apologies if it's not particularly clear.) Veritasium had a good video on this topic: [VIDEO] (Thanks to u/badocr for commenting with this link last time.)"},{"id":30995931,"author":"metrognome","month":"2022-04","story":"Weightless cats – Do space cats land on their feet? (2011) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Intuitive explanation of why the ground accelerates upward.","quote":"This Veritasium video gives an intuitive explanation","text":"While falling, the cat is in an inertial reference frame, so it is not accelerating. The ground is actually accelerating upward at 9.8 m/s2, counteracting the flow of spacetime. This Veritasium video gives an intuitive explanation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24821704,"author":"doomrobo","month":"2020-10","story":"Gravity is not a force – free-fall parabolas are straight lines in spacetime","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why standing on Earth means accelerating.","quote":"I barely understand it but veritasium explains it in the newest vid","text":"When you are at rest on the surface of the earth, you’re actually accelerating. I barely understand it but veritasium explains it in the newest vid [VIDEO]"},{"id":24824807,"author":"vl","month":"2020-10","story":"Gravity is not a force – free-fall parabolas are straight lines in spacetime","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed popsci explanation of gravity as spacetime curvature.","quote":"This Veritasium video gives detailed “popsci” explanation","text":"Original page kinda doesn’t explain the idea in simple terms. This Veritasium video gives detailed “popsci” explanation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24832820,"author":"j1vms","month":"2020-10","story":"Gravity is not a force – free-fall parabolas are straight lines in spacetime","stance":"rec","why":"More thorough explanation of why gravity is not a force.","quote":"Here's [0] a bit more thorough explanation of this concept for those who are interested.","text":"Here's [0] a bit more thorough explanation of this concept for those who are interested. [0] [VIDEO] (Veritasium on Youtube: Why Gravity is NOT a Force @ 8:24)"},{"id":43758711,"author":"btilly","month":"2025-04","story":"Astronomers confirm the existence of a lone black hole","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the spacetime-accelerating visualization, admitted handwavy.","quote":"Yes I know it is handwavy and misleading.","text":"First, the idea of describing it that way comes from Veritasium. Take complaints to him. See [VIDEO] for the video where he does it. Yes I know it is handwavy and misleading. But I consider it less misleading than most attempts at visualizing it. > Ok, how does this sketch work for a low-ellipticity eccentric orbit? At what point in the orbit does it not work as a description of what's going on locally where the orbiting body is? > Sure, it's a set of events in a region of the whole spacetime. If we take \"Big Bang\" colloquially enough to include the inflationary epoch, always assuming GR is correct, then at every point in that \"Big Bang\" region of the whole spacetime there is a small patch -- a subregion -- of exactly flat spacetime. However, these small patches must be small because most choices of initially-close pairs of test objects can only couple to timelike curves that wildly spread in one direction (and focus in the other). No, there is no requirement of any region of locally flat spacetime existing. It is required (outside of singularities) that, when measuring things to first order, things are flat. However in curved space-time, the curvature can be theoretically revealed in any region, no matter how small, by measurements that are sufficiently precise to show the second order deviations from flatness that we call curvature. > I don't know how to understand your two final sentences: how do you connect the period just before the end of inflation and the expansion his"},{"id":28940564,"author":"rdtsc","month":"2021-10","story":"Greater than 99% consensus on human caused climate change in the literature","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as proof gravity is geodesic motion in curved spacetime.","quote":"Gravity is the effect of masses moving along geodesic lines in curved space-time.","text":"> Considering any disagreement with the idea that gravity is a force would end your academic career Gravity is not a force. So thinking it is a force would end one’s academic career. See, even scientists can live in their own “bubbles” once things get complicated enough :-) Gravity is the effect of masses moving along geodesic lines in curved space-time. Edit: Since you probably don’t believe me [VIDEO]"},{"id":28412422,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2021-09","story":"The New Thermodynamic Understanding of Clocks","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Light is not keeping the Earth and Moon \"in sync\". Gravitational force is.[1] That force propogates at the speed of light. It isn't light itself. Light does play a role in illuminating the system, rendering it observable. It's a signalling component. But again, that's exogenous to the system itself. (See discussion of Maxwell's Daemon and the resolution of that paradox for the role of light in that specific thermodynamic process.) But, and this is key: ANY clock needs some way of passing information on to the observer. From TFA, that might be light, or sound, or chemical potential (received as the smell of rotting garbage). But that is a common element, part of the signalling mechanism, but not of the timekeeping mechanism itself . For an astronomical clock, in most cases, the regular rotational or orbital motion is the timekeeping mechanism. The signalling mechanism is entirely separate. E.g., in the Earth-Moon system, timekeeping is noted by both the daily rotation of the Earth, and the monthly orbit of the Moon. The kinetic energy of both those regular motions is not driven in any sense, nor is it measuring a decay.[2] The signalling comes from an exogenous light source (the Sun) which illuminates both bodies. But the Sun isn't powering the timekeeping mechanism in question. And is equally significant in providing the light necessary for you to read a wristwatch or wall clock (of a non-illuminated variety). ________________________________ Notes: 1. I'm going to leave disc"},{"id":24823479,"author":"gilgoomesh","month":"2020-10","story":"Gravity is not a force – free-fall parabolas are straight lines in spacetime","stance":"reference","why":"Addresses curvature term balancing acceleration at rest.","quote":"See Veritasium's video at 10m17s","text":"Energy is Force times Distance. You're not moving (even though you're accelerating) so there's no energy expended. See Veritasium's video at 10m17s: [VIDEO] He addresses that basic science makes this confusing because there's a curvature term that is usually left out of acceleration equations which balances out when you're accelerating along your curvature (e.g. against gravity), instead of along your spatial coordinates."},{"id":32468086,"author":"O__________O","month":"2022-08","story":"Second-Order Thinking","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Albert Einstein said, “Intellectuals solve problems, geniuses prevent them”; he also likely was the main reason the US became aware of the need to enter the race to create nuclear weapons. [1] At some point problems become impossible to avoid, especially if you’re biased [2] — though in the end, at a given scale, most problems (and their solutions) become as predictable as watching a man falling through the air. [3] - Second-order thinking is not result of being smart, but not being lazy, biased, egotistical, etc. _________ [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43764531,"author":"raattgift","month":"2025-04","story":"Astronomers confirm the existence of a lone black hole","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I'd be grateful if you take me to the time stamp","text":"> See [VIDEO] for the video where he does it. I'd be grateful if you take me to the time stamp, because in a casual watch of the video there was nothing like your: Gravity pulls things in by causing space-time to accelerate in a particular direction. In other words we accelerate towards the Earth at 9.8 meters per second per second because that is what space-time itself does. The closest thing I noticed was just after the 10 minute mark, where he points at Christoffel symbols and essentially says that you can choose a set of accelerated coordinates such that to remain at the spatial origin you have to undergo proper acceleration. \"Your acceleration must be equal to this curvature term ... in curved spacetime you have to accelerate just to stand still\". Which is totally fine, and even finer if he made it clear that you are \"standing still\" at some spatial coordinate after an arbitrary splitting of spacetime into space + time. But I don't know how or if those approx 90 seconds connect to what I quoted from you above. (Even finer still if he removed the coordinates and completed the equation: see e.g. slide 20/50 at [LINK] - his whiteboard is the term labelled in cyan, adapted. approx 10m45s \"You don't have to worry about the details here. The point is...\". The point is that I'm not his intended audience, and his presentation is fine enough, so there's no good reason for me to take you up on your suggestion to contact him.) > At what point in the orbit does it not work as a desc"}],"r":[["lcjdwSY2AzM",1],["T71ibcZAX3I",1],["pTn6Ewhb27k",1],["XOR8mk0tLpc",1]]},"pkCLMl0e_0k":{"wy":"HN calls it a brilliant, mind-bending introduction to lambda calculus for programmers.","q":{"x":"David Beazley gives a brilliant introduction to lambda calculus here","i":27661600,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27661600,"author":"globular-toast","month":"2021-06","story":"Lambda Calculus Examples (2009) [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Brilliant intro to lambda calculus for Python users","quote":"David Beazley gives a brilliant introduction to lambda calculus here","text":"For someone fluent in Python, David Beazley gives a brilliant introduction to lambda calculus here: [VIDEO] This is great fun to follow through on a rainy afternoon!"},{"id":20768920,"author":"sethgecko","month":"2019-08","story":"One-lined Python","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mind-blowing lambda calculus PyCon talk","quote":"If you want your mind to blow up I recommend watching this year's","text":"If you want your mind to blow up I recommend watching this year's \"Lambda Calculus from the Ground Up\" PyCon talk by the wizard David Beazley [VIDEO]"},{"id":19978348,"author":"ehsankia","month":"2019-05","story":"Python built-ins worth learning","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Derives lambda calculus from scratch in Python","quote":"Great tutorial if you've never seen lambda calculus before, definitely brain twister.","text":"Just watched his PyCon 2019 where he derives lambda calculus from scratch in Python, and he had similar shell magics going on there too. Great tutorial if you've never seen lambda calculus before, definitely brain twister. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24194408,"author":"avador","month":"2020-08","story":"A circuit-like notation for lambda calculus (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains booleans and numerals in lambda calculus","quote":"This talk by David Beazley (Lambda Calculus from the Ground Up) might be useful","text":"This talk by David Beazley (Lambda Calculus from the Ground Up) might be useful: [VIDEO] Zero and one come in around the 35 minute mark (after booleans)."}],"r":[["X-iSQQgOd1A",1],["iphcyNWFD10",1],["1Jwo5qc78QU",1],["ObhK8lUfIlc",1],["JBgG_VSP7f8",1],["SxdOUGdseq4",1],["gSKTfG1GXYQ",1],["O61WihMRdjM",1],["EZ05e7EMOLM",1]]},"bkQJdaGGVM8":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a fun, short talk that shows how HDR photography actually works through an absurdly playful implementation.","q":{"x":"It's short and fun, I recommend it to all HN readers.","i":43985557,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43985557,"author":"asafira","month":"2025-05","story":"What is HDR, anyway?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Short, fun explanation of HDR photography.","quote":"It's short and fun, I recommend it to all HN readers.","text":"I did my PhD in Atomic, Molecular, and Optical (AMO) physics, and despite \"optical\" being part of that I realized midway that I didn't know enough about how regular cameras worked! It didn't take very long to learn, and it turned out to be extremely important in the work I did during the early days at Waymo and later at Motional. I wanted to pass along this fun video from several years ago that discusses HDR: [VIDEO] . It's short and fun, I recommend it to all HN readers. Separately, if you want a more serious introduction to digital photography, I recommend the lectures by Marc Levoy from his Stanford course: [LINK] . I believe he runs his own group at Adobe now after leading a successful effort at Google making their pixel cameras the best in the industry for a couple of years. (And then everyone more-or-less caught up, just like with most tech improvements in the history of smartphones)."},{"id":15076449,"author":"levthedev","month":"2017-08","story":"Employer gave me a conference budget for next year. Can I get suggestions?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk implementing HDR photography algorithm purely in Excel","quote":"My favorite talk from this year was about implementing an algorithm for HDR photography purely in Microsoft Excel","text":"You should definitely check out !!con. They have really fascinating and weird talks about all sorts of interesting CS and design topics. Plus, it's pay what you can and is located in New York, so there's lots of interesting stuff to do nearby. My favorite talk from this year was about implementing an algorithm for HDR photography purely in Microsoft Excel - [VIDEO]"},{"id":20284767,"author":"Mister_Snuggles","month":"2019-06","story":"3D engine on MS Excel without VBA (2018) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how HDR photography works by implementing it in Excel.","quote":"things like this are a really interesting way to show how things like HDR photography actually work","text":"My favourite (ab)use of Excel, which is already on your list, is using it for HDR photography[0]. While I'd never use it for day-to-day anything, things like this are a really interesting way to show how things like HDR photography actually work. Excel can be very approachable for stuff like this. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16046222,"author":"floatboth","month":"2018-01","story":"Ask HN: What’s your favorite talk from 2017?","stance":"rec","why":"Included in a list of favorite 2017 talks","quote":"","text":"On The Turing Completeness of PowerPoint [LINK] Compiling C to printable x86, to make an executable research paper [LINK] HDR Photography in Microsoft Excel?! [VIDEO] Zebras All the Way Down [LINK] Solving Layout Problems with CSS Grid and Friends [LINK] Console Security - Switch [LINK] Let's move SMM out of firmware and into the kernel [LINK] Bringing Linux back to server boot ROMs with NERF and Heads [LINK] Sharing the Chisel [LINK] (looks like the actual conference video wasn't released)"},{"id":16398953,"author":"EvanAnderson","month":"2018-02","story":"3D engine made of MS Excel formulae","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on high dynamic range photography in Excel","quote":"I just watched a talk this morning about doing high dynamic range photography in Excel","text":"I just watched a talk this morning about doing high dynamic range photography in Excel ( [VIDEO] ). Looks like now I've got something to watch tomorrow!"}],"r":[["y7HrM-fk_Rc",1],["uNjxe8ShM-8",1]]},"wsaufB5F8dk":{"wy":"Cited in vertical farming debates as a rigorous look at when indoor crops are economically viable.","q":{"x":"an extremely deep dive into the exact scientific measurements at which certain plant markets become viable at certain energy prices you will find this half hour very well spent","i":23630798,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23630798,"author":"cagenut","month":"2020-06","story":"What vertical farming and ag startups don't understand about agriculture","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deep dive on when indoor crops become economically viable.","quote":"an extremely deep dive into the exact scientific measurements at which certain plant markets become viable at certain energy prices you will find this half hour very well spent","text":"This has the infuriatingly common fatal logical flaw of wrapping \"farming\" in one giant layer of abstraction and comparing indoor vs outdoor at the broadest scale. Indoor farming, or greenhouse farming, or high-tunnel farming, or a zillion others are all incremental adaptations of particular plants and particular markets. You cannot compare the global corn and wheat markets to the nyc lunch salad market. \"Farming\" has always meant thousands of different things, and for some of those things there will be markets for indoor grow ops. This is not an assertion, we all know there's a very robust one right now. Debating indoor vs outdoor farming at this broad a level is like debating cars vs bicycles as if we have to pick one. If anyone would like to see an extremely deep dive into the exact scientific measurements at which certain plant markets become viable at certain energy prices you will find this half hour very well spent: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26188567,"author":"SomewhatLikely","month":"2021-02","story":"Vertical farming does not save space","stance":"rec","why":"Informative technical talk on crop lighting efficiency and viability.","quote":"here's an informative talk from Bruce Bugsbee of Apogee Instruments","text":"For anyone who wants to get into the technical details on efficiency of growing crops under lighting, here's an informative talk from Bruce Bugsbee of Apogee Instruments: [VIDEO] He shows that wheat, which was the example used in the article, would cost 100x more growing under lighting, but for greens and microgreens the lighting costs are only a few percent of the market value of the crop so those are very viable as indoor crops."},{"id":24095105,"author":"cagenut","month":"2020-08","story":"Wheat yield potential in controlled-environment vertical farms","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation by Bruce Bugbee, who grew wheat on the ISS.","quote":"That presentation is by one of the same scientists referenced in the linked paper","text":"> Now lets get the yield potentials for everything else under the sun so we can start optimizing better. [VIDEO] That presentation is by one of the same scientists referenced in the linked paper, Dr. Bruce Bugbee, the one who grew wheat on the ISS. He has a whole youtube channel that goes into more detail than you can imagine: [LINK]"},{"id":22004056,"author":"halbritt","month":"2020-01","story":"For tech-weary Midwest farmers, 40-year-old tractors now a hot commodity","stance":"rec","why":"Research on LED efficiency for growing lettuce indoors.","quote":"If you think LEDs have gotten epic, check out his research.","text":"You could grow salad in the cupboard. Lettuce is one of the few crops that would be economical to grow under indoor lighting. I believe that info comes from here: [VIDEO] If you think LEDs have gotten epic, check out his research. They're just getting to be more efficient than HPS."}],"r":[["m05abdGSOxY",1],["4PHT-zBxKQQ",1],["QEIUdMiColU",1],["Xe1g1JrRRkY",1]]},"9F9ahZQ7oP0":{"wy":"Recommended as a well-made documentary-style history that gives context around the game and inspires replays.","q":{"x":"Do yourself a favor and watch this wonderful video essay historique on The Monkey Island series from Ahoy","i":32901938,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32901938,"author":"FireInsight","month":"2022-09","story":"“Return to Monkey Island” is out today","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Wonderful video essay on the Monkey Island series.","quote":"Do yourself a favor and watch this wonderful video essay historique on The Monkey Island series from Ahoy","text":"Do yourself a favor and watch this wonderful video essay historique on The Monkey Island series from Ahoy; [VIDEO]"},{"id":30909136,"author":"nickt","month":"2022-04","story":"Return to Monkey Island – Coming 2022","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Inspired the commenter to replay the game.","quote":"highly recommended, as is the video.","text":"Really looking forward to this. Back in the day I played it on my Amiga 500, but last year, after watching Retro Ahoys video [1] I got my old 486 out of the loft and bought a Roland MT-32 just to play it - highly recommended, as is the video. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25254852,"author":"TimSchumann","month":"2020-11","story":"Guybrush Fact vs. Fiction","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary giving the story around the story.","quote":"For those of you who are looking for some context, and have an hour to spare","text":"For those of you who are looking for some context, and have an hour to spare, Ahoy made a great documentary on YouTube about ‘The Secret of Monkey Island’ that touches on this topic. [0] The source material linked in this thread looks to be more authoritative than the video, and if I remember correctly the video even gets this detail of the story wrong, but sometimes it’s nice to know the story around the story. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30322859,"author":"v-erne","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: What are your contrarian views?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows small, left-alone team made Monkey Island cheaply.","quote":"What struck me was how relatively cheap it was","text":">> tech companies in general should be doing significantly more to protect their engineers' time, and the ones that get good at this are going to have outsized impact I have just watched history of The Secret of Monkey Island ( [VIDEO] ). What struck me was how relatively cheap it was (if I remember correctly it costed 130000$ and main team was only 3 people). I now that it was a different time, but even if You take the inflation (of money and requirements) into consideration its still a miracle. And one of the reasons given for this project success was that Schafer and the rest was left alone to do their thing - I would not say they were protected, but at least nobody important enough paid attention to them to start messing with their creative process. This is somehow consistent with my general observation - that in knowledge based work only small subset of people do the real work."}],"r":[["_7X6Yeydgyg",1],["eLn6LC1RpAo",1],["jyQwgBAaBag",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["yCsgoLc_fzI",1]]},"RlXdsyctD50":{"wy":"HN commenters regularly send people to it as an accessible, friendly introduction whenever pilot wave theory comes up.","q":{"x":"Here's another great video from PBS Spacetime on this topic","i":13199636,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":25645785,"author":"treeman79","month":"2021-01","story":"Physics Without Probability (2015) [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Pilot wave removes messy probability issues.","quote":"PBS Spacetkme has a great video on pilot wave.","text":"[VIDEO] PBS Spacetkme has a great video on pilot wave. Getting rid of all the messy probability issues is amazing."},{"id":13199636,"author":"MichaelApproved","month":"2016-12","story":"Why don't more physicists subscribe to pilot wave theory?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great PBS Space Time video on pilot wave theory.","quote":"Here's another great video from PBS Spacetime on this topic","text":"Here's another great video from PBS Spacetime on this topic [VIDEO] This channel and Sixty Symbols are awesome for insight into physics/quantum mechanics without the math."},{"id":15106360,"author":"neom","month":"2017-08","story":"Rumours swell over new kind of gravitational-wave sighting","stance":"rec","why":"Part of a list of helpful PBS Space Time episodes.","quote":"These PBS Spacetime episodes should help","text":"These PBS Spacetime episodes should help: The Speed of Light is NOT About Light - [LINK] Is Quantum Tunneling Faster than Light? - [LINK] The Quantum Experiment that Broke Reality - [VIDEO] Pilot Wave Theory and Quantum Realism - [VIDEO] The Future of Gravitational Waves - [LINK]"},{"id":13198742,"author":"JshWright","month":"2016-12","story":"Why don't more physicists subscribe to pilot wave theory?","stance":"rec","why":"PBS Space Time episode on pilot wave theory for lay people.","quote":"I would recommend the PBS Space Time YouTube channel.","text":"If you're interested in this sort of thing as a lay person (as I am), I would recommend the PBS Space Time YouTube channel. Be sure to stick around for the end of each video, as he often calls out comments on earlier videos that contain corrections or clarifications (in many cases, from _very_ highly qualified physicists and other subject matter experts). Here's their recent episode on pilot wave theory: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32030522,"author":"tzs","month":"2022-07","story":"Notice of termination of Twitter merger agreement","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Have you spent much time on \"anything not illegal is allowed\" forums? Try to talk about popular movies are TV shows and you get things like this on such sites [1]. Want to discuss an episode of PBS Space Time such as this one [2]. Don't be surprised if this is the discussion you get [3]. What almost always happens on such forums is that the people there who aren't racists, sexists, antisemites, inane conspiracy theorists etc., leave, and the forum ends up being largely just such people. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":24171782,"author":"guerrilla","month":"2020-08","story":"A flutter in time: Quantum mechanics is immune to the butterfly effect","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as further material on pilot wave theory.","quote":"De Broglie–Bohm pilot wave theory [2] is a non-local hidden variable theory.","text":"> Hidden variable theories don't satisfy the bell equation. No, local hidden variable theories don't [1]. De Broglie–Bohm pilot wave theory [2] is a non-local hidden variable theory. [1]. [LINK] [2]. [LINK] [3]. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["9XkHBmE-N34",1],["2JAOTJxYqh8",1],["AZX6awZq5Z0",1],["YycAzdtUIko",1],["1YFrISfN7jo",1],["qbIk7-JPB2c",1],["mht-1c4wc0Q",1],["xRel1JKOEbI",1],["umatbZ0n4mE",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1],["G0Q4UAiKacw",1],["tafGL02EUOA",1],["KePNhUJ2reI",1],["msVuCEs8Ydo",1],["351JCOvKcYw",1],["RrHnLXMTOWM",1],["EACkiMRT0pc",1],["hT8GZlBBv5k",1],["hzyFZcuHmeI",1],["SFBBjynBpSw",1]]},"gwvA49ZXnf8":{"wy":"HN cites it as a striking confession obtained through caller ID spoofing and social engineering.","q":{"x":"But the best part is how he did it: caller ID spoof + social engineering.","i":25868124,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":39019747,"author":"cedws","month":"2024-01","story":"Bellingcat Open Technical Questions 2024","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Navalny calls an FSB officer and extracts a confession.","quote":"Even more incredible is that Alexei Navalny personally called an FSB officer and was able to extract a confession","text":"I highly recommend reading Bellingcat's investigation into the poisoning of Alexei Navalny and how they tracked down the individual FSB officers responsible: [LINK] It's the most impressive investigative work I have ever read. One can only imagine the capabilities of nation state actors if an NGO is capable of this. Even more incredible is that Alexei Navalny personally called an FSB officer and was able to extract a confession: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25495542,"author":"asats","month":"2020-12","story":"Russian opposition leader Navalny dupes spy into revealing how he was poisoned","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Full phone call where Navalny gets an FSB agent to describe his poisoning.","quote":"Also here's the full phone call with the FSB agent, with english transcript","text":"If you haven't seen the full investigation released last week I'd strongly recommend you check it out, top notch investigative journalism. The video has English subtitles: [LINK] Also here's the full phone call with the FSB agent, with english transcript: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25868124,"author":"wyldfire","month":"2021-01","story":"Novichok Nerve Agent Poisoning","stance":"rec","why":"Navalny spoofs caller ID and extracts confession from poisoning conspirator.","quote":"But the best part is how he did it: caller ID spoof + social engineering.","text":"In case you missed it: Navalny found conclusive evidence of a cover-up for his poisoning [1] [2] [3]. But the best part is how he did it: caller ID spoof + social engineering. IMO exploiting the authoritarian nature/culture was really clever. Every time Kudryavtsev demurs about revealing more details, Navalny (as Ustinov) calls him by his full name. To me, it sounds like he's threatening him or reminding him of their relative position in the hierarchy. Each time, Kudryavtsev continues to spill the beans and give up co-conspirators. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25871269,"author":"jannes","month":"2021-01","story":"Novichok Nerve Agent Poisoning","stance":"reference","why":"Agent admits the poisoning process on the phone.","quote":"Did you not see the YouTube video where one of the agents admits it on the phone and describes the process?","text":"Did you not see the YouTube video where one of the agents admits it on the phone and describes the process? I thought it was already established that Russia did this beyond reasonable doubt. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ibqiet6Bg38",1],["lkgszkPnV8g",1]]},"1W_mSOS1Qts":{"wy":"HN recommends it for concrete numbers on what tariffs do to small hardware businesses.","q":{"x":"Best part is having a product manager show a spreadsheet of costs and margins and explain in real terms ($$$) what tariffs do to their business.","i":43847486,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43847486,"author":"relwin","month":"2025-04","story":"Port of Los Angeles says shipping volume will plummet 35% next week","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Long documentary with a product manager showing costs and margins under tariffs","quote":"Best part is having a product manager show a spreadsheet of costs and margins and explain in real terms ($$$) what tariffs do to their business.","text":"Gamers Nexus essentially produced a 3-hr doc on how changing tariffs affect the US computer industry: \"The Death of Affordable Computing | Tariffs Impact & Investigation\" [VIDEO] . Best part is having a product manager show a spreadsheet of costs and margins and explain in real terms ($$$) what tariffs do to their business."},{"id":45942272,"author":"herdymerzbow","month":"2025-11","story":"When UPS charged me a $684 tariff on $355 of vintage computer parts","stance":"rec","why":"Manufacturers break down costs and tariff impacts; amounts now outdated","quote":"gamers nexus did a great (and very long) video on the impact of tariffs on US computer businesses.","text":"gamers nexus did a great (and very long) video on the impact of tariffs on US computer businesses. Some of the manufacturers went into quite a bit of detail breaking down their costs and how tariffs would render some products so unprofitable that they would cease to serve the US market. Not sure if it necessarily applies to a niche/low volume business, but the impacts on a larger business were eye opening: [VIDEO] tariffs have chopped and changed so much since this video that the specific tariff amounts mentioned are likely not accurate."},{"id":43854490,"author":"prof-dr-ir","month":"2025-05","story":"Wyze pays $255k of tariffs on $167k of floodlights","stance":"rec","why":"HYTE details cost structure, tariff impact and US production question","quote":"gives a remarkably detailed insight, both into their cost structure and how they are impacted by tariffs","text":"Similarly HYTE, mostly known for their PC cases, gives a remarkably detailed insight, both into their cost structure and how they are impacted by tariffs, in this video: [VIDEO] They also address the question of moving their production to the US."},{"id":43854802,"author":"Timshel","month":"2025-05","story":"Wyze pays $255k of tariffs on $167k of floodlights","stance":"reference","why":"Explores why small companies can't move manufacturing to the US","quote":"The GamerNexus video already mentioned elsewhere explore those issues","text":"I think the point is kind of moot. I doubt they own any part of their manufacturing at the moment. Unless manufacturing already exists in the US (which I doubt here), I don't believe company this size has any chance to move manufacturing to the US The GamerNexus video already mentioned elsewhere explore those issues: [VIDEO] ."}]},"86gyW0vUmVs":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a clear introduction to chinampas and an example of productive agroforestry.","q":{"x":"who is an amazingly clear presenter about permaculture","i":40240695,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40240695,"author":"jacobolus","month":"2024-05","story":"Gene banks aren’t enough to save the world’s food","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear explainer on chinampas by a horticulturalist","quote":"who is an amazingly clear presenter about permaculture","text":"I recently came across the videos of Oregon State horticulturalist Andrew Millison, who is an amazingly clear presenter about permaculture: [LINK] For example, he has nice videos about chinampas in Mexico [VIDEO] , a medieval Indian canal tunnel of a style found throughout Iran [LINK] , efforts to return a Hawaiian watershed to something like its historical state [LINK] , the Indian Paani Foundation's \"water cup\" to revitalize drought stricken villages [LINK] , and the difficulty of using ancient iron-saturated aquifers for irrigation in western Egypt [LINK]"},{"id":47882706,"author":"culi","month":"2026-04","story":"Girl, 10, finds rare Mexican axolotl under Welsh bridge","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth minidoc on chinampas","quote":"also made an in-depth minidoc on them","text":"This is awesome, thanks for sharing. Andrew Wilson, who works with the United Nations World Food Program, also made an in-depth minidoc on them: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37977066,"author":"datameta","month":"2023-10","story":"A Dutch graphic artist reconstructed Tenochtitlan in 3D","stance":"rec","why":"Chinampas as an example of productive agroforestry","quote":"The chinampas are a super neat example of agroforestry.","text":"The chinampas are a super neat example of agroforestry. The soil is extremely dark (like Ukrainian chernozem or better) and has a very low depletion per cycle. Some still exist and are being stewarded toward renewal and proliferation! [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["040ejWnFkj0",1],["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["S0dSm_ClcSw",1],["wVp5koAOu9M",1],["jN7mSXMruEo",1],["ZD_3_gsgsnk",1],["cidchWg74Y4",1],["KTzGBJPuJwM",1],["iph500cPK28",1],["Og6cTlwBTrk",1],["qhbuKbxJsk8",1],["n8m7lFQ3njk",1],["Ygty9HxhFK4",1],["-k3mVnRlQLU",1],["FJJTYQYB1JQ",1],["7OpM_zKGE4o",1],["wJWksPWDKOc",1]]},"8JrqH2oOTK4":{"wy":"HN points to it in threads on artificial sunlight, as a buildable and surprisingly convincing artificial window.","q":{"x":"The result is quite stunning. I've never seen anything like it.","i":32220738,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32220738,"author":"deltasevennine","month":"2022-07","story":"Ask HN: Why are there so few artificial sunlight or artificial window products?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Build guide for a stunning artificial window","quote":"you can build one following the video above. The result is quite stunning.","text":"[LINK] If you want one, you can build one following the video above. The result is quite stunning. I've never seen anything like it. [VIDEO] The one above is an easier build that takes up less space."},{"id":22168089,"author":"reeddavid","month":"2020-01","story":"Ask HN: What are some interesting projects to reuse your old devices?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why the Fresnel lens daylight panel works well","quote":"I got a great explanation of why this works well (fresnel lens)","text":"Not a company, but sharing a personal project: I got a free broken 55\" TV and turned it into a big daylight panel. I got a great explanation of why this works well (fresnel lens) from the DIY Perks channel on YouTube: Turning Smashed TVs into Realistic Artificial Daylight ( [VIDEO] ). The main board had failed so the screen was black and the backlight cycled on and off. After disconnecting the main board, the backlight stayed on. I am using the existing LED lights, but may replace them with the excellent color quality LEDs recommended by DIY Perks."},{"id":21663166,"author":"jsilence","month":"2019-11","story":"Rooms can be as bright as the outdoors","stance":"rec","why":"DIY directions for recycling LCD monitors into artificial windows","quote":"DIY directions on YT","text":"It is possible to recycle old LCD monitors into articficial windows that have a very real feel to them. The diffraction lenses for the backlight direct the light in a way that makes it appear sun like. Of course this is only about the direction and not about the spectrum, but it might be possible to find LED strips with a high CRI. Any feedback would be appreciated. DIY directions on YT: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42806135,"author":"nothrabannosir","month":"2025-01","story":"How to improve your WFH lighting to reduce eye strain","stance":"reference","why":"Light boxes from old monitors emulating sunlight","quote":"using decommissioned led monitors to create light boxes with parallel rays to emulate sunlight","text":"Reminds me of this old dyi video about using decommissioned led monitors to create light boxes with parallel rays to emulate sunlight: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37985212,"author":"yterdy","month":"2023-10","story":"Millions of smart meters will brick it when 2G and 3G turns off","stance":"reference","why":"Example of repurposing broken TVs as light panels","quote":"broken TVs could be repurposed as large light panels","text":"I disagree. The difficulty of replacing tech changes one's relationship to one's devices. Things that I see people throw away as \"e-waste\" are really, \"Things someone couldn't be assed to repair or repurpose.\" For example, broken TVs could be repurposed as large light panels ( [VIDEO] ), or their main boards could be hacked for fun and profit ( [LINK] ). When I see a broken TV, even one I don't own, that's where the pain comes from. Same with phones, laptops, etc. We throw away so mny perfectly good general-purpose computers, it's insane. When we run out of a necessary resource for their manufacture, will we just be scavenging them?"},{"id":33713427,"author":"rektide","month":"2022-11","story":"High CRI LED Lighting","stance":"reference","why":"Indoor sunlight builds using high-CRI LEDs","quote":"","text":"I really wish more led lighting was high CRI. We've suffered a huge degredation to our world vua this switch to efficiency & it's sad & unknown. Boo. These Yuji led folk notably appear in the DIY Perks videos on indoor sunlight! He uses a 500W watercooled setup & adapted parabolic satellite dish reflector in the second to make good parallel-rays sunlight. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":24986218,"author":"tootie","month":"2020-11","story":"Building an artificial sun that looks realistic [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier artificial daylight project, less good than newer version","quote":"It's not as good as this version but can look very nice.","text":"He has a video of that project as well. It's not as good as this version but can look very nice. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24988037,"author":"DanBC","month":"2020-11","story":"Building an artificial sun that looks realistic [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Shows turning smashed TVs into daylight panels","quote":"Turning Smashed TVs into realistic daylight panels","text":"DIY Perks has previously shown how to do that, and it looked pretty good. Turning Smashed TVs into realistic daylight panels: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21661252,"author":"muti","month":"2019-11","story":"Rooms can be as bright as the outdoors","stance":"reference","why":"Fresnel lenses from old TVs simulate distant daylight","quote":"a light panel that simulates light coming from very far away using large Fresnel lenses from old TVs","text":"I remember seeing a DIY video on how to make a light panel that simulates light coming from very far away using large Fresnel lenses from old TVs [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["5fG_lyNuEAw",1],["j5v8D-alAKE",1],["6bqBsHSwPgw",1],["ISAKc9gpGjw",1],["wlR5gYd6um0",1]]},"9c8an2XZ3MU":{"wy":"Cited as the origin of the 'culture/philosophy as operating system' metaphor and as a quick introduction to McKenna's ideas.","q":{"x":"If you want to know where the metaphor of philosophy as an operating system comes from","i":4651704,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":827400,"author":"Alex3917","month":"2009-09","story":"Apple brainwashing: How we do it","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Terence McKenna talks","quote":"the man was pretty much a living god","text":"If you are interested in learning more about them, this is pretty much the best podcast on the Internet: [LINK] And many of the talks on Pyschedelic Salon are also very good, especially the one by the Erowid founders which I submitted this morning, called The Role of Drug Geeks in Society, and also the talk by Dr. Andrew Weil. Also check out some of the talks by Terence McKenna, the man was pretty much a living god: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4651704,"author":"Alex3917","month":"2012-10","story":"How to Use Philosophy as a Personal Operating System: From Seneca to Musashi","stance":"rec","why":"Source of the philosophy-as-operating-system metaphor, about invisible cultural assumptions","quote":"If you want to know where the metaphor of philosophy as an operating system comes from","text":"If you want to know where the metaphor of philosophy as an operating system comes from: [VIDEO] (The video is called Culture Is Your Operating System, but what he's talking about is the philosophical assumptions that are completely invisible unless you find a way to step outside your culture.)"},{"id":2695046,"author":"Alex3917","month":"2011-06","story":"Research into hallucinogenic drugs begins to shake off decades of taboo","stance":"rec","why":"Basic idea of how psychedelics change thinking","quote":"here are a few YouTube videos to give you the basic idea","text":"\"How does making you see things that aren't real challenge any hierarchies?\" While LSD and psilocybin do sometimes cause people to hallucinate, this is only one small part of the psychedelic experience. The real value is in how the drugs change your thinking. It would take an entire book to explain this, but here are a few YouTube videos to give you the basic idea: [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] It's difficult to explain how exactly they challenge hierarchies, but the 35,000 foot overview would be that psychedelics tend to make one more prosocial, whereas hierarchies tend to be fueled by antisocial behaviors. They also tend to give one self-confidence and internalize one's locus of control, whereas again hierarchies can only exist when people lack self-confidence and have relatively external locii of control. Essentially hierarchies are built on the backs of those trying to fill some sort of void in their lives, whereas psychedelics undermine this by giving one perspective and making one whole. Here is the best description I've found: \"What is the psychedelic experience? For me it's the full and overwhelming realization of all the beauty, love, and pain, the wonder and possibility, of the human experience. For the first time you see your life truly objectively, what's going wrong, and what's going right. You grasp and grapple with how you can do better, and you are overcome by the newfound desire to strengthen your community, your relationships, and your karma. And so it proceeds for "},{"id":34564233,"author":"labrador","month":"2023-01","story":"Geek Mythology: The Religious, and Spiritual Folklore Surrounding Programming","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Bonus link: Terrance McKenna.","text":"Terry Davis and Temple OS. God communicated to him through the random number generator. This article is meant to be humorous, but I think it unintentionally hit on some important truths. Terry Davis Was Right Religion Is Computing, Computing Is Religion Terry’s big idea was that it is not possible to separate religion from computing. Since the beginning, every complex society has been based on a shared socio-cognitive operating system, a religion. Your religion says you must live a certain way. It tells you what is valuable and what is disgusting. It gives you your fundamental paradigm and ontology of belief through which you interpret the world. It tells you to read certain texts, listen to certain traditions of wise men, and meet with like-minded others at certain places and times. It tells you to avoid certain influences, and seek out others. It gives you techniques for structuring thought and mind and memory. When you have doubts, it gives you procedures to ask for and receive insight. It tells you who you are, and what your life is for. [LINK] I'm researching these ideas more since ChatGPT has shown me how to make certain religious and spiritual ideas more accessible. Levandowski shut down his AI church after he got in trouble with the law for something unrelated. He was too soon, so it's only a matter of time one starts up again. [LINK] Bonus link: Terrance McKenna. Culture is your operating system [VIDEO]"},{"id":1531622,"author":"wil2k","month":"2010-07","story":"How to Destroy Someone's Life or They called me a child pornographer","stance":"reference","why":"McKenna's idea that culture is your operating system","quote":"Stuff to ponder about!","text":"And as the late Terence McKenna put it: Culture is your Operating System [VIDEO] Stuff to ponder about! :O"},{"id":1261252,"author":"Alex3917","month":"2010-04","story":"Hallucinogens Have Doctors Tuning In Again","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Your operating system gets upgraded","text":"1. Your operating system gets upgraded: [VIDEO] 2. You lose your ability for self-deception, so when you come face to face with your problems you are forced to be completely honest about them. And once you're honest with yourself, it's very hard not to go through with changing your life for the better. 3. You get to diff your ordinary state of consciousness against your altered state of consciousness. This allows you to learn a ton about how your mind works, changes your theories about how lots of phenomena work, changes your assumptions about reality, etc. 4. If you take what Terence McKenna would call a 'heroic dosage', you might come up against 'the cosmic giggle'. That is, you come face to face with god or aliens or things that go completely against all of western science. And what's really interesting is that in double blind academic studies, multiple people have very similar encounters with alien entities even though they have no had no contact with each other. (C.f. Rick Strassman's book DMT: The Spirit Molecule.) (E.g. machine elves: [LINK] )"}],"r":[["KCkbp4wurW0",1]]},"IJcuQQ1eWWI":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as the key talk on why game AI is built to be fun rather than to win.","q":{"x":"Extremely enlightening, whether you are a gamer or not.","i":3368199,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":3368199,"author":"JonnieCache","month":"2011-12","story":"How games cheat to help players","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Civ lead designer on evolution of Civ AI, cheating, and fun versus good AI","quote":"Extremely enlightening, whether you are a gamer or not.","text":"Continuing my theme of posting relevant google tech talks, here is Soren Johnson, the lead designer and AI programmer on Sid Meier's Civilisation, discussing this very subject with specific reference to the evolution of civ's AI over the years. Extremely enlightening, whether you are a gamer or not. [VIDEO] \"Artificial intelligence is crucial to any strategy game, providing a compelling opponent for solo play. While many of the challenges of AI development are technical, significant design challenges exist as well. Can the AI behave like a human? Should it? Should the game design be adjusted to accommodate the limitations of the AI? How do we make the AI fun? Should the AI cheat? If so, how much? Do we even want the AI to win? This session suggests some possible answers to these questions using the \"Civilization\" series as a case study. Ultimately, developers must choose between a \"good\" AI and a \"fun\" one, with an understanding of the trade-offs inherent when deciding between the two.\""},{"id":4480393,"author":"aggronn","month":"2012-09","story":"Game AI vs Traditional AI","stance":"rec","why":"Talk by designer of much Civilization AI","quote":"Its a very good talk","text":"You might enjoy a talk by the designer of much Civilization AI: [VIDEO] Its a very good talk, but applied video game AI as a field is kind of a let down."},{"id":4103198,"author":"JonnieCache","month":"2012-06","story":"Civ II game a decade old","stance":"rec","why":"Civ developer talk on AI design and game AI generally","quote":"You may well enjoy this lecture at google from one of the civ5 devs talking about the AI design","text":"You may well enjoy this lecture at google from one of the civ5 devs talking about the AI design, and game AI design in general. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46920910,"author":"chongli","month":"2026-02","story":"OpenCiv3: Open-source, cross-platform reimagining of Civilization III","stance":"reference","why":"Civ AIs are supposed to play to lose","quote":"Civ AIs are supposed to \"play to lose\" [1].","text":"I would say diplomacy is the most misunderstood feature of the series. Players constantly say they want a stronger AI that's smarter at diplomacy. But whenever they have built an AI like that, their play testers complained that it doesn't behave like a real world leader (too ruthless). This experience led Soren Johnson (co-designer of Civ III and lead designer of Civ IV) to the realization that Civ AIs are supposed to \"play to lose\" [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20406587,"author":"6gvONxR4sf7o","month":"2019-07","story":"DeepMind’s StarCraft II agent will play anonymously on battle.net","stance":"reference","why":"Game AI is designed to be fun rather than strong","quote":"That sort of game AI isn't designed to be strong. It's designed to be fun.","text":"That sort of game AI isn't designed to be strong. It's designed to be fun. See e.g. this talk titled \"playing to lose\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":19324077,"author":"ggggtez","month":"2019-03","story":"Ask HN: Game devs, what is the hardest part about designing an AI for your game?","stance":"reference","why":"Explains making game AI fun, leader personalities, and why AIs ally with players","quote":"they specifically talk about how to make the game \"fun\".","text":"\"Playing to Lose\" : [VIDEO] Personal preferences aside, they specifically talk about how to make the game \"fun\". For example, they try to account for things like real-world leader's personalities. \"Gandhi\" shouldn't just attack his neighbors unprovoked, but maybe \"Caesar\" should. And why do the AIs even bother to ally/trade/help the player at all, since the player is the only enemy that really matters anyway? Because the player is also the only person that matters in terms of having fun, and making the game too hard isn't any fun."},{"id":14298748,"author":"ryzawy","month":"2017-05","story":"'Civilization' Creator Sid Meier: “I Didn't Really Expect to Be a Game Designer”","stance":"reference","why":"Civ lead designer explains AI design choices, rebutting lazy design claim","quote":"Here's a talk about AI in Civ from Soren Johnson, Lead Designer/AI Programmer on multiple Civ games","text":"Calling it \"lazy game design\" is not only offensive to me as a game designer, but it's plain wrong. Here's a talk about AI in Civ from Soren Johnson, Lead Designer/AI Programmer on multiple Civ games: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4480982,"author":"therealbatman","month":"2012-09","story":"Game AI vs Traditional AI","stance":"reference","why":"Splits AI into good AI and fun AI, using Civilization","quote":"Here he split AI into two groups, Good AI(Chess) and Fun AI(Tower Defense)","text":"Soren Johnson gave a similar talk on AI Playing to Lose: AI and \"Civilization\"[0]. Here he split AI into two groups, Good AI(Chess) and Fun AI(Tower Defense), While the good AI plays the same game as you with the same rules and tries to beat you,Fun AI is asymmetrical, playing a different game whose objective is not to beat you, but you might still be defeated. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["NHopJHSlVo4",1],["4XpnKHJAok8",1],["Z7Wl2FW2TcA",1],["x1bX3F7uTrg",1],["rOwMW6agpTI",1],["RUwS1uAdUcI",1],["njVv7J2azY8",1],["vAHXK2wut_I",1],["DlkMs4ZHHr8",1]]},"OqEeIG8aPPk":{"wy":"HN favors it as a compact, quotable management and systems-thinking talk, often cited for doing the right thing versus doing the wrong thing right.","q":{"x":"this is one of my favourite management talks by Russell Ackoff","i":30213919,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30213919,"author":"Litost","month":"2022-02","story":"Deming's Red Bead Experiment (2002)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Ackoff management talk mentioning Deming.","quote":"this is one of my favourite management talks by Russell Ackoff","text":"Thanks for the suggestion, how does what he says stack up against those who came after, I ask this because this is one of my favourite management talks by Russell Ackoff [1] and he mentions Dr Deming so assume he was influenced by/worked/studied with him and given their relative ages wondered if his work might be valuable to start with? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27041217,"author":"Litost","month":"2021-05","story":"Efficiency Is the Enemy","stance":"rec","why":"Ackoff on doing the right thing wrong versus wrong thing right.","quote":"The whole talk is quite interesting, but I've linked to the relevant segment","text":"This quote “You’re efficient when you do something with minimum waste. And you’re effective when you’re doing the right something.” reminds me of a Systems Thinking talk Russell Ackoff gave a long time ago [1] where he talks about the difference between doing \"the right thing wrong\" and the \"wrong thing right\" and getting more efficient at doing the wrong thing obviously might not be optimal :). This is actually a concept that Peter Drucker came up with, but I've not managed to find a good link to that, I'd be interested if anyone has? The whole talk is quite interesting, but I've linked to the relevant segment: [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23591098,"author":"Barrin92","month":"2020-06","story":"Scientists’ warning on affluence","stance":"rec","why":"Ackoff on systems thinking applied to environmental problems.","quote":"Russ Ackoff in a great talk about systems thinking[2]","text":"I think the article is right. Vaclav Smil has pointed out that today the share of fossil fuels, at about 90% of global energy consumed[1] is actually higher than in 2000, and falling at a snails pace. There is a worrying trend emerging, fuelled by the tech industry of performative environmentalism that seeks to promise absolution like the church did by buying Tesla's and biodegradable cups while the real-world impact is marginal while leaving the fundamental flaws of the system untouched. We won't consume ourselves out of the doghouse. Russ Ackoff in a great talk about systems thinking[2] once pointed out a few salient things 1. Counter to intuition one does not improve a system by merely removing deficiencies. Doing away with what you don't want doesn't get you what you want. 2. Incrementalism doesn't solve fundamentally new problems, rearranging deck chairs on the titanic is pointless, and people confuse efficiency with effectiveness. Doing the wrong thing right is worse than doing the right thing wrong. 3. One must recognise that like an architect, a designer does not design rooms independently of the house. One starts with the house, and only changes parts as to improve the overall functioning of the system, never the parts alone. This is what needs to be applied to modern environmentalism. Woke green consumerism and tech worship is useless. The entire global system needs to be redesigned, instead of trying to fix individual parts. We need to think in holistic terms rathe"},{"id":45424994,"author":"notpachet","month":"2025-09","story":"Comprehension debt: A ticking time bomb of LLM-generated code","stance":"reference","why":"Ackoff quote on knowing what you want before building.","quote":"To quote Russ Ackoff[1]","text":"> It's amazing how fast the code goes when you know exactly what you want. To quote Russ Ackoff[1]: > Improving a system requires knowing what you could do if you could do whatever you wanted to. Because if you don't know what you would do if you could do whatever you wanted to, how on earth are you going to know what you can do under constraints? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37120399,"author":"notpachet","month":"2023-08","story":"Risky Giant Steps Can Solve Optimization Problems Faster","stance":"reference","why":"Ackoff quote on discontinuous improvement and creativity.","quote":"One never becomes a leader by continuously improving -- that's imitation of the leader.","text":"\"Continuous improvement isn't nearly as important as discontinuous improvement. Creativity is a discontinuity. A creative act breaks with the chain that has come before it. It's not continuous. One never becomes a leader by continuously improving -- that's imitation of the leader. You never become a leader by imitating them and improving slightly. You only become a leader by leapfrogging those who are ahead of you. And that comes about through creativity.\" -- Russ Ackoff [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["jILgxeNBK_8",1],["xguam0TKMw8",1],["7vfqkvwW2fs",1],["A4UMyTnlaMY",1]]},"efPrtcLdcdM":{"wy":"HN points to it as the definitive account of the GPT-4chan experiment, used in debates on LLM bias, truthfulness and bots.","q":{"x":"might be the coolest thing an LLM has ever done on video","i":42280404,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42280404,"author":"benreesman","month":"2024-11","story":"Breaking the 4Chan CAPTCHA","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fine-tuned GPT-J on 4chan; coolest LLM video","quote":"might be the coolest thing an LLM has ever done on video","text":"In my opinion the granddaddy of all 4chan CAPTCHA busts is still Yannick Kilcher’s GPT-J tune on “Raiders of the Lost Kek” set, and might be the coolest thing an LLM has ever done on video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39667911,"author":"sigmoid10","month":"2024-03","story":"Reddit Launches Long-Awaited IPO with $748M Target","stance":"rec","why":"Experiment where commenters theorized about the bot's origins","quote":"I still love this experiment","text":"I still love this experiment: [VIDEO] The theories about the origins of these commenters were amazing. There's nothing to say that other people are not doing the exact same thing right now here on HN and other places. And very few people would even notice, because open LLMs have progressed significantly since then."},{"id":31614432,"author":"thegreatdukd","month":"2022-06","story":"GPT-4chan","stance":"rec","why":"Solid explanation of the GPT-4chan experiment","quote":"There is a solid video explanation","text":"There is a solid video explanation (though the author is undermining the toxicity of the average 4chan /pol/ posts): [VIDEO] . A really interesting experiment."},{"id":47023556,"author":"heliumtera","month":"2026-02","story":"4chan for Clankers","stance":"reference","why":"Small model that became 4chan folklore","quote":"there was already a MUCH, MUCH smaller model running loose on 4chan","text":">isn't it a matter of time before it succeeds in some way it is not a matter of time. given enough time, every `attempt` to emulate 4chan would succeed? initiatives to emulate 4chan already happened successfully, this is not close. this, is the same models over again. some people confuse asking standard models from standards providers to do something with an genuine attempt. this thing states it is 4chan for Clankers, but feels like Reddit for Redittors. there was already a MUCH, MUCH smaller model running loose on 4chan (before agents hiting mainstream) that became 4chan folklore. it was called Seychelles anon ( [VIDEO] , by the author YannicKilcher). but no, if you ask gpt or claude, all you get back is Reddit. and it is not a matter of time until this changes. if you are a redditor, you probably love llms."},{"id":47025497,"author":"ProllyInfamous","month":"2026-02","story":"4chan for Clankers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"GPT-4chan came out three summers ago","text":"GPT-4chan came out three summers ago , trained on /pol/ datasets: < [VIDEO] > In some ways, it was actually more helpful (\"more truthful \") than mainstream ChatGPT."},{"id":44985669,"author":"sunaookami","month":"2025-08","story":"4chan will refuse to pay daily online safety fines, lawyer tells BBC","stance":"reference","why":"Funny video of the bot experiment","quote":"Funny video btw","text":"This was one guy that did it for a few days (weeks?). Not some \"common occurence\". Funny video btw: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40128980,"author":"IvanAchlaqullah","month":"2024-04","story":"Phi-3 Technical Report","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> TruthfulQA Wait, people still use this benchmark? I hear there's a huge flaw on it. For examples, fine-tuning the model on 4chan make it scores better on TruthfulQA. It becomes very offensive afterwards though, for obvious reasons. See GPT-4chan [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39288711,"author":"ProllyInfamous","month":"2024-02","story":"ChatGPT Generating Racist Content","stance":"reference","why":"4chan-trained LLM was rated more truthful than ChatGPT","quote":"","text":"I just want to remind everybody, summer 2022 a jokester trained an LLM using 4chan as its source material... which ended up being \"more truthful\" than ChatGPT [1]. [1] \"GPT-4chan is worst AI ever!\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":34983731,"author":"flangola7","month":"2023-03","story":"Microsoft Kosmos-1: A Multimodal Large Language Model","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] The internet will be saturated with fake people soon. Someone already did a PoC of this on 4chan as a joke with just a small GPT2 model. In a few years you won't be able to tell if you're talking to a human unless they're physically in front of you."},{"id":35239813,"author":"EGreg","month":"2023-03","story":"The genie escapes: Stanford copies the ChatGPT AI for less than $600","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is different. It can act just like humans do for most people who skim comments won't be able to tell the difference. Note this was in 2020: [LINK] And here's 4chan bot: [VIDEO] I can tell you that HN is probably already being infiltrated as well. SPAM can't gang up on you in a forum and downvote you and turn your friends against you and destroy your reputation within 1 hour online. But soon, it will. The web as we know it is soon going to be over."},{"id":35260107,"author":"AlbertoGP","month":"2023-03","story":"Mozilla.ai: Investing in Trustworthy AI","stance":"reference","why":"Model trained on 4chan scored more truthful","quote":"","text":"GPT models have been shown to get significantly more truthful when trained on the right data set: [VIDEO] The 30 million they are investing should be enough to reproduce that. Looking forward to it!"},{"id":34618844,"author":"type0","month":"2023-02","story":"Ask HN: Does the HN commentariat have a reductive view of what a human being is?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm fairly certain you can create AI bot that would indistinguishably mimic your regular HN user, in the same style as GPT-4chan was [VIDEO] The only problem is that it might be banned for spewing too many falsehoods."},{"id":34415015,"author":"kcmastrpc","month":"2023-01","story":"Conservatives Are Panicking About AI Bias, Think ChatGPT Has Gone 'Woke'","stance":"reference","why":"Model trained on 4chan unleashed on 4chan","quote":"someone trained a giant model on 4chan then unleashed on 4chan... hilarious stuff.","text":"This has already happened, someone trained a giant model on 4chan then unleashed on 4chan... hilarious stuff. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33711459,"author":"visarga","month":"2022-11","story":"CICERO: An AI agent that negotiates, persuades, and cooperates with people","stance":"reference","why":"Model trained on 4chan posted for a day undetected","quote":"Someone trained a GPT-J 6B model on 4chan and then let the model loose on the forums for a day.","text":"That's nothing. Someone trained a GPT-J 6B model on 4chan and then let the model loose on the forums for a day. It took about 15k messages until people suspected something was off, and even that was because the country flags, Seychelles, a rare flag on 4chan, were a giveaway. video: [VIDEO] model: [LINK]"},{"id":32208959,"author":"benreesman","month":"2022-07","story":"Are language models deprived of electric sleep?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"So green text is easier to spoof than HN comments, but my, uh, friend tells me that the HN comment corpus is floating around. Someone is eventually going to speed run 10k karma on HN and YouTube it. This year? Next? Who knows. But not a decade. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31614448,"author":"albertzeyer","month":"2022-06","story":"GPT-4chan","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Check out the video","text":"I'm not the author. The author is Yannic Kilcher. Maybe he reads it here as well though. I don't know if he is active on HN. Check out the video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31887134,"author":"sitkack","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: GPT-3 reveals my full name – can I do anything?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"All the search engines are moving towards language models. I think language models are on a similar trajectory as sequencing the genome. The first sequences were massive undertakings, 15M USD in 2006, 100 USD now. [LINK] On the Dangers of Stochastic Parrots [LINK] \"Imagine you are remram and are looking to buy insurance, what are three things that could convince you to purchase ....\" You can take the generic \"trained on the world\" language model and filter it down to the language model of remram. GPT-4Chan [VIDEO]"},{"id":42869986,"author":"benreesman","month":"2025-01","story":"DeepSeek's Hidden Bias: How We Cut It by 76% Without Performance Loss","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Sometimes with hilarious consequences","text":"Operator-aligned models are believed by many to be more performant. [LINK] Sometimes with hilarious consequences: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["NH1Tta7purM",1],["HdHlfiOAJyE",1],["HJ-719EGIts",1],["YQ2QtKcK2dA",1]]},"gD_dZvPwAj0":{"wy":"HN users share it as a short, accessible explainer for land value tax whenever housing, rents or inequality come up.","q":{"x":"Land value tax explained in 10 minutes","i":21966231,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":21921737,"author":"amiga_500","month":"2019-12","story":"What Is the Most Valuable Thing You Can Learn in One Hour?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Short explainer for understanding real estate and land economics.","quote":"Watch this 5 times then spend ten minutes passing it on","text":"Watch this 5 times then spend ten minutes passing it on: [VIDEO] Questions in this thread about real estate, \"investing\" etc. Comprehend it with the above."},{"id":21966231,"author":"amiga_500","month":"2020-01","story":"How Much Land Does a Man Need? (1886)","stance":"rec","why":"Land value tax explained in ten minutes.","quote":"Land value tax explained in 10 minutes","text":"[LINK] > Tolstoy also became a dedicated advocate of Georgism, the economic philosophy of Henry George > towards the end of his life, Tolstoy become more and more occupied with the economic theory and social philosophy of Georgism. > He spoke of great admiration of Henry George, stating once that \"People do not argue with the teaching of George; they simply do not know it. And it is impossible to do otherwise with his teaching, for he who becomes acquainted with it cannot but agree.\" Land value tax explained in 10 minutes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21899605,"author":"amiga_500","month":"2019-12","story":"Is College Still Worth It? The New Calculus of Falling Returns [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Ten-minute explanation of land rents.","quote":"Have a look at this 10 minute video","text":"From your reply you are obviously not stuck at the \"rents are the cost to the landlord + 10%\". Good! Have a look at this 10 minute video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17875496,"author":"branchless","month":"2018-08","story":"Bullshit Jobs","stance":"rec","why":"Explains land value tax as fix for rentier economies.","quote":"Here is a video on land value tax, which would fix our messed up feudal economies","text":"Spot on. Spare time would allow us to conclude we can expunge rentiers. Here is a video on land value tax, which would fix our messed up feudal economies: [VIDEO] Posting here as shadow banned after two posts because Hacker News hates any kind of free speech."},{"id":10839902,"author":"ZenoArrow","month":"2016-01","story":"Economic Inequality: The Simplified Version","stance":"reference","why":"Land value tax as alternative to income tax.","quote":"","text":"Depends on how you tax them. We could get rid of income tax and still strive towards reducing some of the issues surrounding income inequality. [VIDEO]"}]},"j7zzQpvoYcQ":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as an emotionally powerful life-advice talk and revisit it as a reminder of mortality.","q":{"x":"I often times revisit Randy Pausch's The Last Lecture[1] to act as a memento mori[2].","i":28233013,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28151109,"author":"SEJeff","month":"2021-08","story":"Monday was my 46th birthday and likely my last (2013)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Emotionally powerful lecture.","quote":"It is a lecture that will really hit you in the feels","text":"This reminds me of the Last Lecture by Professor Andy Pausch. It is a lecture that will really hit you in the feels: [VIDEO] It is also an excellent book."},{"id":28233013,"author":"orbz","month":"2021-08","story":"Ask HN: What's the most life-changing blog post you've ever read?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Revisited regularly as a memento mori.","quote":"I often times revisit Randy Pausch's The Last Lecture[1] to act as a memento mori[2].","text":"While not a blog post really, I often times revisit Randy Pausch's The Last Lecture[1] to act as a memento mori[2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":19651032,"author":"jefflombardjr","month":"2019-04","story":"Ask HN: What are good resources for life advice?","stance":"rec","why":"Influential material: don't limit yourself.","quote":"Randy Pausch, The Last Lecture. My takeaway: don't limit yourself","text":"First and foremost, what you are describing is metacognition - thinking about the way you think. Hopefully that will aid you in your search for things! Secondly, here are some of my favorite influential material: - Li Ka-Shing, Tips on Life. My takeaway: invest in yourself. [LINK] - Randy Pausch, The Last Lecture. My takeaway: don't limit yourself [VIDEO] - Guy Kawasaki, Make Meaning. Aimed for startups, but equally applicable in life. My takeaway: Have the proper motivations for doing things. [LINK] - Admiral William H. McRaven, Make your bed. My takeaway: if you want to change the world, start by changing yourself. [LINK] - David Foster Wallace, This is Water. My takeaway: Take a big picture view, and don't sweat the small stuff. [LINK] - “Out beyond ideas of wrongdoing and rightdoing there is a field. I'll meet you there. When the soul lies down in that grass, the world is too full to talk about.”― Rumi - Man's Search for meaning. My takeaway: We make meaning. [LINK] - \"All the livin that you're saving, won't buy your dreams for you\" - Townes Van Zandt - Idiot wind by Bob Dylan My takeaway: Your reaction to things can be just as bad as the actual thing. [LINK] - Stoicism and Marcus Aurelius, Seneca. My takeaway: Focus on what matters. [LINK] (read this one first for a good foundation) [LINK] [LINK] - Buddhism. Our takeaway: Meditation is important, the doctrine of emptiness (not nihilism!) is super interesting. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] - Leonardo Da Vinci My take"},{"id":12336666,"author":"Create","month":"2016-08","story":"Fighting Cancer","stance":"rec","why":"Suggested as interesting viewing.","quote":"I am not sure you came across The Last Lecture, if not you may find it interesting","text":"I am not sure you came across The Last Lecture, if not you may find it interesting: [VIDEO] [LINK] I consider myself lucky to have had the opportunity to work with you and learn an awful lot along the way. Thanks!"}],"r":[["iffTJ1vPCSo",1],["pxBQLFLei70",1],["ksb3KD6DfSI",1]]},"yY1FSsUV-8c":{"wy":"HN commenters regard it as an inspiring, all-time favorite demonstration of live coding music.","q":{"x":"This video of one of his presentations on Impromptu is one of my all-time favorite programming talks","i":40512148,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40512148,"author":"epiccoleman","month":"2024-05","story":"Shadama: A particle simulation programming environment for everyone","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Sorensen's Impromptu presentation, an all-time favorite talk.","quote":"This video of one of his presentations on Impromptu is one of my all-time favorite programming talks","text":"Andrew Sorensen is awesome. This video of one of his presentations on Impromptu is one of my all-time favorite programming talks: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30628731,"author":"agentwiggles","month":"2022-03","story":"“Compiling” Music","stance":"rec","why":"Incredibly cool talk on live coding with Impromptu.","quote":"There's an incredibly cool talk by Andrew Sorensen using it","text":"That is really cool, I had a ton of fun playing with Overtone a few years ago (Clojure Supercollider wrapper). There's also Impromptu, which I think is Scheme... There's an incredibly cool talk by Andrew Sorensen using it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24469076,"author":"thdrdt","month":"2020-09","story":"Electronic music pioneer Simeon Coxe has died","stance":"rec","why":"Inspiring talk on live coding music.","quote":"Maybe you also like this talk of Andrew Sorensen on live coding music for inspiration","text":"[LINK] can also be added to the list. Visual programming audio and video. Maybe you also like this talk of Andrew Sorensen on live coding music for inspiration: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13947954,"author":"filleokus","month":"2017-03","story":"TidalCycles – A language for the Live Coding pattern","stance":"reference","why":"Example of live coding music performance.","quote":"","text":"Related question: Are there any nice videos demoing this kind of music artistry in a different genre? I'm thinking of stuff like [VIDEO] or maybe something with vocal sampling?"},{"id":13344309,"author":"kimburgess","month":"2017-01","story":"Ask HN: Does anyone here have an art degree?","stance":"reference","why":"Live coding happening at the commenter's university.","quote":"","text":"I studied a BFA in Sound Design. It was an incredible mix. Along with the base arts courses, it stepped across music, film, animation and comp sci. It taught me the basics of signal processing, encouraged 'play' and seemed to balance creative expression with the engineering skills to enable that to happen. After getting through all the courses that interested me I dropped out to travel and then fell into a few interesting jobs that kept me otherwise distracted from going back. I would 100% do it all again. The only thing that I'm super upset about is that I did't fall into the live coding community while I was there (this was literally happening at the same uni [VIDEO] ). One mindset that it solidified in me was when building things come up with the concept first, then figure out how to make that happen. That may be easy, or it may not. But at least what you're making will be worthwhile. I work in the audiovisual industry today running an R&D lab for a systems integrator. We create presentation spaces, high-end corporate facilities and VC suites (which are still apparently a thing), a little bit of broadcast work, some stadium projects and lots of large format displays. When I'm tied into projects I'm usually end up rolling with some systems engineering, DSP programming, a bit of embedded dev, UI work and network engineering. About to begin something new and rather exciting too."}],"r":[["U4FNBMZsqrY",2]]},"2A2NY29iQdI":{"wy":"HN people recommend it, along with the Lock Picking Lawyer's response, as an engaging look at lock design and how clever mechanisms get bypassed.","q":{"x":"Together they give a whole new understanding of lock design and picking methods.","i":31168122,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31880302,"author":"binarymax","month":"2022-06","story":"Enclave: An Unpickable Lock","stance":"rec","why":"Similar unpickable lock design with hardening suggestions.","quote":"an excellent set of videos.","text":"This looks very similar to the design[0] by Stuff Made Here (and collaborative hardening [1] suggestions by Lock Picking Lawyer)…an excellent set of videos. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":31168122,"author":"yccs27","month":"2022-04","story":"High-Security Mechanical Locks (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Novel anti-picking lock mechanisms and how they get bypassed.","quote":"Together they give a whole new understanding of lock design and picking methods.","text":"On the topic of \"novel, non-pin-tumbler mechanisms\": I can recommend the videos by Stuff Made Here [1][2], where he designs two novel anti-picking lock mechanisms, and the related video [3] where LockPickingLawyer tries to pick and bypass them. Together they give a whole new understanding of lock design and picking methods. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":28400776,"author":"infogulch","month":"2021-09","story":"O.mg Cable","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed design of novel unpickable locks.","quote":"Made for interesting videos on both sides","text":"Since this has generated some discussion on locks and picking, there's been some interesting developments on \"unpickable locks\" that sidestep the tolerance problem by decoupling setting the pins from testing them. I.e. pins are tested all at once after they are physically decoupled from the key & keyway, eliminating state space reduction attacks (aka picking one pin at a time) leaving only brute force. One such effort features locks made by Stuff Made Here sent to Lock Picking Lawyer. According to LPL the locks are theoretically sound and he did not attempt to pick them, but these particular implementations had a couple (easily fixable) bypasses. Made for interesting videos on both sides: Stuff Made Here describes the design in detail: TWO Unpickable (?) Locks for Lock Picking Lawyer! - [VIDEO] Lock Picking Lawyer reviews them and performs some bypasses: [1299] Unpickable Locks From Stuff Made Here - [LINK]"},{"id":29367539,"author":"ryzvonusef","month":"2021-11","story":"Saintcon: Lock Picking Lawyer Keynote","stance":"reference","why":"Part of a back-and-forth on unpickable locks.","quote":"","text":"are you perhaps talking about the forever lock? [LINK] while difficult, it can be undone ----- making a one-off \"unpickable\" lock is possible, here is some to-and fro between two youtube channels about such locks: Stuff Made Here: [LINK] [VIDEO] Lock Picking Lawyer: [LINK] But things from installation issues, to making sure tolerances are maintained while making the locks on a production line, mean that there are always some gaps left in a mass produced and installed lock."}],"r":[["Ecy1FBdCRbQ",3],["5MxH1sfJLBQ",1],["j5v8D-alAKE",1],["Dp-mQ3HxgDE",1],["p8u_k2LIZyo",1],["htYh-Tq7ZBI",1],["Y9FZ4igNxNA",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["ne99laPUxN4",1],["_7vPNcnYWQ4",1],["PmlRbfSavbI",1]]},"BiqDZlAZygU":{"wy":"Recommended as an eloquent defense of free speech against laws on offensive or insulting speech.","q":{"x":"Rowan Atkinson made an excellent speech on the matter back then [2]","i":30157833,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32135333,"author":"Jaruzel","month":"2022-07","story":"Rowan Atkinson: Visual Comedy (1992) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Atkinson defending free speech in comedy against UK legislation","quote":"he delivered this amazing speech","text":"Rowan is a staunch defender of Free Speech in Comedy. When it looked like the UK government were going to legislate against it, he delivered this amazing speech: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30157833,"author":"ninjin","month":"2022-02","story":"Man found guilty of ‘grossly offensive’ tweet","stance":"rec","why":"Atkinson's speech against criminalizing insulting words","quote":"Rowan Atkinson made an excellent speech on the matter back then [2]","text":"Sadly, this kind of behaviour is on brand when it comes to the UK state. It is less than ten years since Section 5 [1] was reformed to remove the display of “insulting” words and writing in public as an offence (Rowan Atkinson made an excellent speech on the matter back then [2]). However, the overall mentality and other laws are still very much at large. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] While I still do not claim to fully comprehend British culture, there is always an odd undercurrent of authoritarianism whose chill I can feel now and then – it is worth noting that it bridges the political divide. I have talked about this with my British colleagues and heard numerous explanations, but none that I feel satisfactory. At times when I feel particularly dark I joke about this with said colleagues and say: “You say that you went to war with the Germans on behalf of the free world. But at times I wonder: Was it not that they in a way were outdoing you and you were feeling a bit envious?” Now, clearly this is intended to be offensive and not something that I genuinely believe, but like many jokes, it somewhat works because there is some kernel of truth at its core."},{"id":24307636,"author":"eblanshey","month":"2020-08","story":"Google is apparently taking down all/most Fediverse apps from the Play Store","stance":"rec","why":"Rowan Atkinson speech on freedom of speech and protest","quote":"I recently watched an excellent speech concerning freedom of speech and freedom of protest by Rowan Atkinson","text":"I recently watched an excellent speech concerning freedom of speech and freedom of protest by Rowan Atkinson, and feel it's very appropriate to share here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35870900,"author":"sph","month":"2023-05","story":"Free speech under attack across Ireland, UK, Europe and globally","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in support of free speech against speech laws","quote":"","text":"\"So this is how liberty dies... with thundering applause.\" The amount of people defending these laws in this thread is a frightening proof of this. Everywhere these days you can find someone smart and utterly naive with their \"but actually...\" argument pushing the Overton window closer and closer to a censorship state, and there are not enough neutral people arguing for free speech itself. Only the weirdos and racist ones which are easy to hate and root against. So all this playing Devil's advocate is actually helping along the normalization of these free speech bans. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32564939,"author":"sph","month":"2022-08","story":"YouTube joins Facebook in banning Andrew Tate","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in argument against accepting censorship","quote":"","text":"> hasn't actually led to the terrible outcomes people predict I guess censorship is perfectly fine for most people, and that's the scariest outcome. \"Too bad for them, they should have been thinking like us.\" No wonder we descent into authoritarianism. Thanks to well meaning crowds of well mannered citizens cheering because the big bad got ousted. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30470281,"author":"thesz","month":"2022-02","story":"Ukraine calls on hacker underground to defend against Russia","stance":"reference","why":"Atkinson's point that the answer to speech is more speech","quote":"The answer to the speech is... more speech","text":"[VIDEO] \"The answer to the speech is... more speech\" And I agree that this is exactly the problem - winners are who have more energy and opportunity to speak. \"Drowning the voices\" as you pointed out. But, how to determine which voice is normal? Per your suspicion that fake news and misinformation make money, fact checking is alleged to make money to Facebook and fact-checking Facebook affiliates. The links on fact checking materials divert viewers from one arrticles to other and increase clicks and retention at Facebook. Yes, you are right again - misinformation may bring money to FB, at the very least, when FB tries to fight it. I guess it is a nature of capitalism, to try to make money out of everything."},{"id":26341945,"author":"lovelyviking","month":"2021-03","story":"Godot maintainer removes controversial satirical piece from documentation","stance":"reference","why":"Companion to Peterson talk on free speech and offense","quote":"Rowan Atkinson on free speech","text":"If that is the case then we should stick out more because later it will be even harder and hiding would not work anyway. Jordan Peterson: Free Speech & the Right to Offend: [LINK] Rowan Atkinson on free speech: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25313283,"author":"alexisread","month":"2020-12","story":"Maybe we shouldn't want a fully decentralized web","stance":"reference","why":"Supports argument that more discussion beats censorship","quote":"Rather than censoring people, we could take the approach that our worldview is robust enough to withstand competition / challenge.","text":"Rather than censoring people, we could take the approach that our worldview is robust enough to withstand competition / challenge. More discussion should in theory wither the fragile ones: [VIDEO] The real issue is echo chambers, and getting people to voluntarily cross- pollinate their bubbles. Censoring actually makes this issue worse though."}]},"Xe5AE-qYan8":{"wy":"Recommended as a documentary telling the early hacking and spy-hunting story, with the original people involved.","q":{"x":"It's quite amazing to see the original people involved recreating events just a few years after they took place","i":39257354,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":46426988,"author":"29athrowaway","month":"2025-12","story":"","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary adaptation of Stoll's Cuckoo's Egg spy-hunting story","quote":"and a documentary (\"The KGB, The computer and Me\")","text":"If you like this then you will probably like \"The Cuckoo's Egg: Tracking a Spy Through the Maze of Computer Espionage\", a 1989 book by Clifford Stoll. Also available as audiobook, and a documentary (\"The KGB, The computer and Me\"). [VIDEO]"},{"id":44351822,"author":"mrlatinos","month":"2025-06","story":"Klein Bottle Amazon Brand Hijacking (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"Clifford Stoll's NOVA episode on the Cuckoo's Egg story","quote":"His NOVA episode on the same subject is worth a watch as well","text":"His NOVA episode on the same subject is worth a watch as well [VIDEO] I think most younger readers will be familiar through his videos on the Numberphile YouTube channel."},{"id":39257354,"author":"dosman33","month":"2024-02","story":"Acme Klein Bottle","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary recreating the KGB hacker case with the original people","quote":"It's quite amazing to see the original people involved recreating events just a few years after they took place","text":"Here here. I love Cliff and have my own Acme Klein bottle, a wedding present from my wife. I read the book ages ago of course, but I just discovered the NOVA documentary last week. It's quite amazing to see the original people involved recreating events just a few years after they took place (docu was released in 1990). So... I can't believe Cliff's wife agreed to do a shower scene as part of a recreation for the documentary... Just sayin. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vgT4Y30DkaA",1],["RPdB_zdyMbM",1],["iD9xk3SDSYc",1],["3Fx5Q8xGU8k",1],["7pSqk-XV2QM",1],["8pBPZMUcsh0",1],["P4VBqTViEx4",1],["yFIa-Mc2KSk",1],["bpAi70WWBlw",1],["XXBxV6-zamM",1],["PdO3IP0Pro8",1],["Z-9rLlFgcm0",1]]},"ZRV3pBuPxEQ":{"wy":"HN commenters share it to show that modern PHP is much better than its reputation.","q":{"x":"I was quite surprised to see how far PHP has come since I used it many years ago","i":38597216,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38597216,"author":"xwowsersx","month":"2023-12","story":"Omg.lol: An Oasis on the Internet","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how far PHP has come.","quote":"I was quite surprised to see how far PHP has come since I used it many years ago","text":"Not related to security, but I was quite surprised to see how far PHP has come since I used it many years ago: [PHP doesn't suck (anymore)]( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":37446184,"author":"geek_at","month":"2023-09","story":"An Internet of PHP","stance":"rec","why":"Gave hope for the future of PHP.","quote":"I saw a video not long ago that made me have hope for the future of PHP again","text":"Many people who learned to code with PHP in the 2000s are picking it up again as first choice for web projects since they improved it so much and websites can be updated and running with a simple `git pull` without restarting any services. I saw a video not long ago that made me have hope for the future of PHP again -> [VIDEO]"},{"id":37294125,"author":"aarondf","month":"2023-08","story":"PHP-Tokio – Use any async Rust library from PHP","stance":"rec","why":"Covers how PHP changed from 2012 to now.","quote":"to try to cover some of the changes from 2012 to now","text":"I've been working with PHP since the early 2000s, and it has undergone massive changes. I made a video recently called \"PHP doesn't suck (anymore)\"[0] to try to cover some of the changes from 2012 to now. If you read through the comments you see a lot of people surprised at how much the language has evolved. And a lot of people saying \"PHP sucks lol,\" but it is YouTube afterall. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41762081,"author":"gjsman-1000","month":"2024-10","story":"React on the server is not PHP","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I’m a happy PHP developer","text":"If you need to clarify that something isn’t something else, because it isn’t obviously different, maybe the two are more alike than you’d like to admit. On that note, I’m a happy PHP developer, PHP doesn’t suck anymore ( [VIDEO] ), and there is no way to say JavaScript doesn’t have at least as many footguns now. (Personally, I think JavaScript’s footguns - like the ludicrous dependency trees and framework churn - are actually worse.)"},{"id":41140406,"author":"gjsman-1000","month":"2024-08","story":"Rails Is Good Enough","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"despite being way, way, better than it used to be","text":"What’s the alternative?! You can either have a massive Python project and struggle with a lack of types and a tenseness that becomes unreadable at huge sizes; A Laravel project that’s PHP, which despite being way, way, better than it used to be (types! traits! union type returns! faster than ruby! weak maps! [0]) still has a few footguns left; Or you can have a JavaScript monster. The worst of all worlds. Your complexity complaints times 10. A nightmare than in 10 years will make a 2008 PHP project look well-organized. A project that has thousands of packages you’ve never read. On the edges, you’ve got microservices (which turns into a mess); or you can go fancy with Go and Rust (have fun managing the budget). There’s also, of course, C# and Java, but come now, it’s 2024, you’re probably coupling them to a JavaScript monster anyway. TL;DR: Every large project becomes a mess. Pick your poison. One is hardly worse than another’s. Best to have the devil you know. Even if you do things perfectly with a FAANG level budget, AWS and Azure still have outages. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["T39QHprz-x8",1],["ZSPkcpGmflE",1],["QmOF0crdyRU",1]]},"c2lgEyNaop4":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a firsthand account from the creator, including the old tooling.","q":{"x":"There is a very nice presentation [1] in which Raynal shows some of the old tools that were used to create Alone in the Dark.","i":37967475,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37967475,"author":"smokel","month":"2023-10","story":"How the creator of Alone in the Dark came back to games","stance":"rec","why":"Raynal shows old tools used to create Alone in the Dark","quote":"There is a very nice presentation [1] in which Raynal shows some of the old tools that were used to create Alone in the Dark.","text":"Note that both Little Big Adventure (also called Twinsen's Adventure) and Alone in the Dark were created by the same person, Frédérick Raynal! There is a very nice presentation [1] in which Raynal shows some of the old tools that were used to create Alone in the Dark. I'm still not entirely sure what kind of rendering is being used, but the editor sure looks nice (in 640x350 EGA). Perhaps there is some more background information on LBA out there as well? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23667215,"author":"pengaru","month":"2020-06","story":"Alpha Waves was the first 3D platform game","stance":"rec","why":"Raynal's GDC postmortem covering Alone in the Dark and porting Alpha Waves to PC","quote":"Also worth a watch/listen is the talk by Frederick Raynal at GDC about Alone in the Dark.","text":"Also worth a watch/listen is the talk by Frederick Raynal at GDC about Alone in the Dark. He touches on converting Alpha Waves from Atari ST to PC: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20722210,"author":"nope96","month":"2019-08","story":"Alone in the Dark","stance":"rec","why":"Postmortem by Alone in the Dark creator Frederick Raynal","quote":"video from 2012 by Frederick Raynal","text":"There's also a great \"Classic Game Postmortem\" video from 2012 by Frederick Raynal, too: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["JFaOYYSxSEA",1],["X29lF43mUlo",1],["Ja5Q75hf6QI",1],["7xTGNNLPyMI",1],["jK0BkTPUGQY",1],["tzr7hRXcwkw",1],["VFFlO7yBIBM",1],["RKT-sKtR970",1],["Dw3BZ6O_8LY",1]]},"lnA9DMvHtfI":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a quick, non-dumbed-down way to get up to speed on how LLMs are built.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend this video by Steve Seitz","i":35704811,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35977022,"author":"peter_d_sherman","month":"2023-05","story":"Large Language Models from scratch (2022)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows neural nets approximate functions like Fourier and Taylor series","quote":"I had never made the connection between Fourier Series, Taylor Series, and Neural Networks","text":"Consider the following frame/time from the video: [VIDEO] At this point in time in the video, we see the following 3 things: 1) Fourier Series 2) Taylor Series 3) Neural Network The remainder of the video goes on to show how Neural Networks can approximate functions -- much like the Fourier Series and Taylor Series can... Now, I don't hold an advanced Ph.D. degree in Math, and prior to watching this video, I had never made the connection between Fourier Series, Taylor Series, and Neural Networks... I knew about each of those independently -- but prior to watching the video, I never made the connection that they can be used to approximate one another. And that's the one of the key words -- approximate . The other key word is Identity -- as in Mathematical Identity ... That's because IF two Mathematical Objects (functions, algorithms, call them what-you-will) are approximately equal -- then there should be a set of conditions (i.e., \"If and only if...\", etc.), a set of rigorously defined mathematical constraints which, if those conditions/constraints are met, then the two Mathematical Objects are exactly precisely equivalent -- under those rigorously defined conditions/constraints.... In other words, under those rigorously defined conditions/constraints -- one Mathematical Object could be substituted for another -- with no loss of precision or result. In other words, under those rigorously defined conditions/constraints -- those two completely apparently different Mathematical "},{"id":35704811,"author":"sainez","month":"2023-04","story":"A Completely Non-Technical Explanation of Deep Learning","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Quick overview of how LLMs are built without dumbing down","quote":"I highly recommend this video by Steve Seitz","text":"If anyone is looking for a quick overview of how LLMs are built, I highly recommend this video by Steve Seitz: [VIDEO] . It does an excellent job of taking you from 0 to a decent understanding without dumbing down the content or abusing analogies."},{"id":35981641,"author":"runetech","month":"2023-05","story":"Ask HN: Can someone ELI5 Transformers and the “Attention is all we need” paper","stance":"rec","why":"Short video that quickly gets you up to speed on LLMs","quote":"At least they helped me get up to speed fast.","text":"Not sure I can give an ELI5 explanation, but for me this the closest to what you are after in two short videos on the topic. At least they helped me get up to speed fast. Hope they will do the same for you ;-) Large Language Models from scratch [VIDEO] Large Language Models: Part 2 [LINK]"},{"id":39778114,"author":"mistermann","month":"2024-03","story":"The Starship or the Canoe: Where will our future adaptations be? (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"(Note: I am a different Human than the one you are replying to - apologies for interjecting.) > \"Physically preventable\" is a moving target. So are rockets and artillery shells, but check this out: [LINK] Humans can be very clever and generous ($60,000 USD per interceptor missile, hoo baby!! Is \"democracy\" not the greatest thing ever!!!???) when they set their minds to something. Unfortunately, delusion, untruthfulness, deceit, misdirection, etc are also somethings , and apparently very desirable somethings, seemingly irresistible to some. > The more resources are invested in preventing currently preventable deaths, the more farther away the target moves, because more and more causes of death become preventable. Something seems off here - when you say \"farther away\", what is the unit of measure? To make sense (if you can pardon the colloquialism) of this argument, I think it would help if you gave a quantitative example to prove that your fact is actually factual. My intuition is that you may be conflating [1] physical death (concrete) with causes of death (abstract). > So, this is a false argument. A problem for you - some of us know of this phenomenon: [LINK] ... and not just abstractly . There is a very interesting tangential conversation related to this seemingly [1] minor distinction - perhaps some day (some) Humans will have such conversations. I suspect this (\"this 'is' [1] a false argument\") may be another one of those not actually factual facts. [LINK] > There's no p"},{"id":34260212,"author":"mistermann","month":"2023-01","story":"Globalization Is Dead and No One Is Listening","stance":"reference","why":"Brief LLM explainer linked within a long unrelated discussion","quote":"","text":"> What is so unserious, disingenuous, and illegitimate about the “system”? Some examples (I could write indefinitely): [LINK] (section \"Bank Error In Our Favor\" is particularly offensive, to me) [LINK] (this sort of thing (which is fairly common) is offensive to me, as is our culture's complacency towards it) How it is (in part) perpetuated, in my estimation: - I’m sure whatever the system is it isn’t perfect. But here I am using the “system” and living my life. [rationalization] - I see so many strange comments on the internet that sound like “red-pilled” nonsense. [training-based heuristic pattern matching to ~bad/dumb/wrong] - Could it be better and is there room for improvement? Absolutely. But living in parallel red pill reality is an absolutely unhelpful endeavor . [training-based heuristic pattern matching to ~bad/dumb/wrong + cultural normalization of hallucination of reality (in this case: the future)] - After-all, you use the real system to have a life and so does everyone else. Might as well face that fact and truly engage with flesh and blood in the meat space rather than whatever or whoever is on red pill virtual land. [Stay within the Overton Window of ideas and behaviors one has been trained on] > Where would you rather be and what is your improvement on the “system”? a) How about something like \"The contents of the tin (upon substantial, fine-grained inspection) matches what's on the label\"? b) we shall see, maybe > If you have a good personal value propositio"}],"r":[["DovunOxlY1k",1],["Iuv6hY6zsd0",1]]},"ncPyMUfNyVM":{"wy":"HN commenters link it as a short, favorite explanation of Bitcoin's security and the sustainability of a 51% attack.","q":{"x":"Andreas Antonopoulos has a good take on this for Bitcoin","i":27413140,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":31826221,"author":"latchkey","month":"2022-06","story":"Are blockchains decentralized?","stance":"rec","why":"Short analysis of 51% attack cost","quote":"(one of my favorites and only a couple minutes long)","text":"> They don’t provide an analysis of the true cost of launching a 51% attack. Andreas Antonopoulos has done this many times already. [VIDEO] (one of my favorites and only a couple minutes long) [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":28126689,"author":"avnigo","month":"2021-08","story":"Bitcoin’s Energy Usage Isn’t a Problem. Here’s Why","stance":"rec","why":"Succinct explanation of Bitcoin security and energy","quote":"here's [0] a succinct explanation as to why.","text":"It should be noted that the bitcoin network does not need to consume more energy to scale up: - Using more energy does not translate to more transactions committed to the ledger. Second and third layer solutions can handle the world's transactional need with minimal energy usage, still using the security provided by the bitcoin network. - The security of the bitcoin network is not dependent on the amount of users, and mostly driven by financial incentives. However, the more energy it uses, the more secure it is against attacks. As it is, the bitcoin network does not need to use more energy, and here's [0] a succinct explanation as to why. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27413140,"author":"rattlesnakedave","month":"2021-06","story":"Cost of a 51% Attack for Different Cryptocurrencies","stance":"rec","why":"Antonopoulos on sustainability of a 51% attack","quote":"Andreas Antonopoulos has a good take on this for Bitcoin","text":"it’s basically impossible to sustainably 51% Bitcoin or ETH at this point. You could 51% for an hour (maybe, if you can get that hash rate from nicehash for that long, but I doubt you actually can), but what happens at the end of that hour? Would everyone simply throw their hands up, allow your double spend, and keep mining your chain? Likely not. Andreas Antonopoulos has a good take on this for Bitcoin: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40094529,"author":"latchkey","month":"2024-04","story":"Bitcoin Block 840000","stance":"reference","why":"Argument that a 51% attack would not matter","quote":"","text":"It will never be cheap enough to 51% attack either and even if it was attacked, it wouldn't matter. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":35545150,"author":"latchkey","month":"2023-04","story":"A systematic critique of Bitcoin's value proposition","stance":"reference","why":"Antonopoulos on government attacking Bitcoin","quote":"","text":"> I suppose if your worry is a government trying to destroy Bitcoin, then that’s exactly how to do it… Andreas Antonopoulos - 51% Bitcoin Attack [VIDEO]"},{"id":25667389,"author":"alwillis","month":"2021-01","story":"Nano: What Bitcoin Was Supposed to Be","stance":"reference","why":"Antonopoulos on why a 51% attack would not destroy Bitcoin","quote":"Bitcoin expert Andreas Antonopoulos said the same thing","text":"I don't think it's FUD. Of course you don't; you're here promoting Nano. If the Chinese government wanted to destroy BTC (because it violated their currency control laws), they could do it by coercing the owners of the mining pools located in China and those pools do do currently have over 51% of hash power. Again, this is FUD. It's not a good look to market Nano by trying to scare people into believing something like a 51% attack could happen tomorrow, when it's extremely unlikely to happen. Nano should be able to stand on its own without these scare tactics. You're assuming a double-spend attack on a block mined by Chinese miners would destroy Bitcoin when everyone knows such an attach is a theoretical possibility. As mentioned in the article I posted and you conveniently didn't respond to, there are many safeguards that would alert other miners. Bitcoin is about to pass $700 billion market cap—that's a lot of incentive to stop bitcoin from being destroyed. Even China knows it's better for them to have a non-sovereign store of value that's not the dollar. It would be pretty easy for the rest of bitcoin to see that the block is not legitimate and ignore it. Don't believe me? Bitcoin expert Andreas Antonopoulos said the same thing: [VIDEO] . It would take a lot of coercing to convince these miners to destroy their lucrative businesses for what would essentially be a one-time stunt that only effected a block these particular miners cared about. I suspect many of them would cho"}],"r":[["099cHWSbAL8",1]]},"tafGL02EUOA":{"wy":"HN points to it as a clear, approachable explanation of quantum entanglement.","q":{"x":"there is a great video by the PBS space time channel explaining quantum entanglement","i":12558600,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":32786747,"author":"shagie","month":"2022-09","story":"Two atomic clocks have been quantum entangled for the first time","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the weirdness of measurement and entanglement.","quote":"The issue is that the measurement of the particle changes it - and changes the entangled particle.","text":"Its... weirder than that. The issue is that the measurement of the particle changes it - and changes the entangled particle. PBS Space Time - Quantum Entanglement and the Great Bohr-Einstein Debate [VIDEO] (and then watch the referenced Veritasium video)"},{"id":15478222,"author":"neom","month":"2017-10","story":"A single photon reveals quantum entanglement of 16M atoms","stance":"rec","why":"Part of a recommended physics viewing order.","quote":"Would recommend this order.","text":"Some good related learnings: The Concept of Mass - with Jim Baggott (just watched this yesterday and it's reeeaalllyy good although basic): [LINK] Quantum Entanglement and the Great Bohr-Einstein Debate | Space Time: [VIDEO] Measure for Measure: Quantum Physics and Reality (kinda old but still good): [LINK] Would recommend this order."},{"id":12558600,"author":"beothorn","month":"2016-09","story":"Researchers quantum teleport particle of light six kilometres","stance":"rec","why":"Clear explanation of quantum entanglement.","quote":"there is a great video by the PBS space time channel explaining quantum entanglement","text":"For anyone interested, there is a great video by the PBS space time channel explaining quantum entanglement here [VIDEO] The thing is, although the quantum state of the photom collapses instantaneously on both entangled photons, we can't manipulate at which state the photon will collapse, and so, we can't really send information using quantum entanglement. We can't also confirm the entanglement until we can compare both measurement results or else we could send information from the future as in the quantum eraser experiment [LINK] Edit: formatting and typos"},{"id":26144102,"author":"runarberg","month":"2021-02","story":"I Really Blew It (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Covers the Bohr-Einstein debates.","quote":"Einstein was later proven to be wrong.","text":"Aside: Einstein (et. al.) wrote such a post (or a series of papers) as part of the Bohr–Einstein debates. Einstein was later proven to be wrong. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["jPhJbKBuNnA",2],["1YFrISfN7jo",2],["msVuCEs8Ydo",2],["4PaWFYm0kEw",1],["CbIZU8cQWXc",1],["yiw6_JakZFc",1],["9RUHJhskW00",1],["_gXlauRB1EQ",1],["klaJqofCsu4",1],["LxgMdjyw8uw",1],["rvskMHn0sqQ",1],["TwRjO3kHxU4",1],["XVmsfL5LG90",1],["HeQX2HjkcNo",1],["wO2lWmgEK1Y",1],["hyCIpKAIFyo",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["OHPkdMGI6D4",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1],["fD-R35DSSZY",1]]},"uDqjIdI4bF4":{"wy":"Recommended as a compact starting point for making animations feel more compelling, including for UI and app developers.","q":{"x":"The video I linked will help the animation more compelling for the viewer.","i":37445845,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":42266656,"author":"fewgrehrehre","month":"2024-11","story":"Ask HN: Things to do on my phone","stance":"rec","why":"Jumping-off point for learning animation principles","quote":"I'd recommend Alan Becker's series[1] on the 12 principles of animation","text":"If you want something a bit more out of the box than simply playing a game, give animating a spin! Just a simple flipbook-style animation app, there should be plenty out there. No need for pose-to-pose or anything at first, just try abstract, straight-ahead animation. If you find yourself gaining an interest, it's easy enough to pick up knowledge as you need it (if you do want a jumping-off point, I'd recommend Alan Becker's series[1] on the 12 principles of animation). It's incredibly fun bringing even the simplest of things to life, and really rewarding getting to look back and see how your understanding of motion and weight grow over time. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37445845,"author":"ttfkam","month":"2023-09","story":"Animations From First Principles (in 5 minutes)","stance":"rec","why":"Makes animations more compelling to viewers","quote":"The video I linked will help the animation more compelling for the viewer.","text":"Not to be confused with the 12 principles of animation. [VIDEO] mathspp.com will help with making an animation programmatically. The video I linked will help the animation more compelling for the viewer."},{"id":18771770,"author":"rayalez","month":"2018-12","story":"Foundations of Animation","stance":"rec","why":"Summary of the 12 principles of animation","quote":"I recommend watching this awesome video summarizing 12 principles of animation","text":"I recommend watching this awesome video summarizing 12 principles of animation: [VIDEO] (by the author of Animation vs Animator) I second Animator's Survival Kit by Richard Williams, it's great. And Keith Lango's video course for 3D animation. These two are absolute best for learning intermediate/advanced animation. Also, of course, Animation Mentor and 11secondclub competition."}],"r":[["sBzRwzY7G-k",1],["4aw9uyj9MAE",1],["NfizT369g60",1],["RxabLA7UQ9k",1],["KNWh6Kw3ejQ",1]]},"vtWp45Eewtw":{"wy":"Recommended in fluoride and fluorine threads as a short, fun demonstration of how nasty fluorine is.","q":{"x":"tooth chat at 5:10, but the whole video is interesting","i":27331433,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27331433,"author":"AndrewOMartin","month":"2021-05","story":"Trials begin on lozenge that rebuilds tooth enamel","stance":"rec","why":"A chemist explains fluoride and tooth enamel.","quote":"tooth chat at 5:10, but the whole video is interesting","text":"Just out of interest, and not detracting from what you're saying, but normal fluoride also forms a compound (flouro-apetite) more resistant to avoid than normal tooth material (appetite). At least according to this chemist: [VIDEO] (tooth chat at 5:10, but the whole video is interesting)"},{"id":16965265,"author":"CaliforniaKarl","month":"2018-05","story":"Hydrofluoric Acid: An Invisible Fire (1996)","stance":"rec","why":"Periodic Table of Videos episode on fluorine.","quote":"I also suggest watching the Periodic Table of Videos episode on Flourine","text":"Every time I hear anything about Flourine, I’m reminded of “Sand Won’t Save You This Time” [LINK] I also suggest watching the Periodic Table of Videos episode on Flourine: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16969392,"author":"toss1","month":"2018-05","story":"Hydrofluoric Acid: An Invisible Fire (1996)","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting fluorine video with a great explanation of tin fluoride for teeth.","quote":"well worth the ~6min","text":"This is a good little interesting video on fluorine here (previously linked by someone else), and the ending is the best description of tin fluoride for teeth I've ever seen -- well worth the ~6min [VIDEO] (edit: added link)"},{"id":9810096,"author":"gtrubetskoy","month":"2015-07","story":"Water Fluoridation May Not Prevent Cavities, Scientific Review Shows","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how nasty and fun fluorine is, plus tooth enamel.","quote":"if you don't know how nasty (and fun) Fluorine is","text":"An educational video from Univ. of Nottingham, if you don't know how nasty (and fun) Fluorine is: [VIDEO] (and they explain the tooth enamel theory towards the end as well)."}],"r":[["9KABcmczPdg",1],["q0RX59G2jaA",1],["03p2CADwGF8",1],["s_fkpZSnz2I",1],["p33CVV29OG8",1],["yReDbeY7ZMU",1],["dpw9EHDh2bM",1],["mPn2AdMH7UQ",1],["VscdPA6sUkc",1],["i70wkxmumAw",1],["HSxSgbNQi-g",1],["wROQUZDCIMI",1],["1bO8zEaSuWg",1],["wfm2xCKOCNk",1],["ras_VYgA77Q",1],["S4O5voOCqAQ",1]]},"y-uuk4Pr2i8":{"wy":"Shared as a vivid illustration of molecular machines at work inside cells.","q":{"x":"Take a look at dynein and kinesin walking along microtubules while dragging along their cargo. It's pretty neat.","i":21394315,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":28380982,"author":"dopylitty","month":"2021-09","story":"Tardigrades Walk","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how kinesin walks and carries cargo inside cells.","quote":"This shows how they look.","text":"If you think that’s cool you might also like to see the motor proteins that walk along inside our cells. There are a few types including the ones that make muscles work. Kinesin is fun because it has little legs made of proteins. It gloms onto big bubbles of stuff inside the cell and carries them along pathways to their destination. Chemical reactions cause the proteins to fold in the right way to take each step. This shows how they look. [VIDEO] This explains the mechanisms that they use to walk. [LINK]"},{"id":23870549,"author":"acqq","month":"2020-07","story":"Inside the Coronavirus","stance":"rec","why":"Cartoon-like animation that reflects real molecular behavior.","quote":"this looks like a cartoon but it really behaves so","text":"Then you have to watch these videos too: [LINK] For example, this looks like a cartoon but it really behaves so (except that the molecules are too small to have the colors, as in other visualizations): [VIDEO]"},{"id":21394315,"author":"droithomme","month":"2019-10","story":"Cell and Molecular Biology Animated Textbooks","stance":"rec","why":"Shows kinesin and dynein carrying cargo along microtubules.","quote":"Take a look at dynein and kinesin walking along microtubules while dragging along their cargo. It's pretty neat.","text":"> the molecules seem to have intent Take a look at dynein and kinesin walking along microtubules while dragging along their cargo. It's pretty neat. Unknown is how the really simple microtubules get to their destination while building themselves just ahead of the walkers or how the walkers know which turns to take. [VIDEO] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37077205,"author":"cosmojg","month":"2023-08","story":"After 118 days, GPT Unicorn has produced a unicorn","stance":"reference","why":"Footnote citing kinesin as an example in a metaphor discussion.","quote":"","text":"Honest question, why do you find it scary? I agree that some people take it too far, but most seem to be using metaphor to abstract away the underlying complexities and facilitate conversation. I did the same thing back in college, anthropomorphizing cells and even molecules when I was learning about microbiology and biophysics (e.g., kinesins[1] are a family of postal workers who work hard to deliver packages to their clients in a timely manner, and they spend their free time going for strolls and practicing on their favorite tightropes). I now do it in my day-to-day work at an AI/ML shop to communicate not just what the entire pipeline is doing, but what individuals layers or encoders are doing, or even what variables in an equation are doing. I find that my colleagues and I are better able to understand and remember concepts and ideas when they're communicated as part of an anthropomorphic story, but we scarcely forget that the things we're dealing with aren't human. But maybe I'm missing the point, and you're really worried about that first, smaller group of people who have really swallowed the Kool aid. To that, I can say that I don't think their behavior exceeds the baseline craziness/weirdness that I've come to expect from humanity. I'm sure there are far more people who believe in astrology than who believe that we've achieved true humanlike artificial general intelligence, for example. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32037465,"author":"s1artibartfast","month":"2022-07","story":"I should have loved biology","stance":"reference","why":"Shows kinesin walking, though not the molecular chaos around it.","quote":"What is not shown in the video is that this is all happening in a hurricane of molecules battering it from all sides.","text":"I had a similar revelation for structural biology, applying the physics I learned for bridges and buildings to microscopic proteins. They are structurally like a cathedral built by a blind and deranged architect. The fact that mechanically bend, pivot, and move like a complex machine at a micro scale to do real work is the most sci-fi thing I can conceive of. Think of a even a simple walking protein like Kinesin [1]. What is not shown in the video is that this is all happening in a hurricane of molecules battering it from all sides. Each part of the structure is being pushed, pulled, bent, robot made out of sticks and rubber bands. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31228239,"author":"tibbydudeza","month":"2022-05","story":"Ancient Genes for Symbiosis Hint at Mitochondria’s Origins","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates kinesin pulling cargo along cell highways.","quote":"They are like little stick men pulling stuff along the highways of the cell.","text":"You referring to the motor proteins Kinesin and dynein that uses ATP ???. They are like little stick men pulling stuff along the highways of the cell. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30357939,"author":"nosianu","month":"2022-02","story":"Citizen sleuths exposed pollution from a century-old Michigan factory (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Short movie of a protein machine moving cargo.","quote":"22 second movie - for a protein-machine whose function it is to move stuff around","text":"Chemicals don't need to chemically react. Especially for biology, merely existing is enough. Lots of biological regulation is done by influencing three-dimensional shapes of the huge highly complex structures called proteins. Organic chemistry especially is a science of complex three dimensional structures and its dynamic changes, at least as important as actually altering the chemical structure. Often they are not static but have moving parts, because not all bonds restrict movement of the atoms connected through that bond in relation to the other part. Many have what's call binding sites - other molecules fitting to those sites won't be altered or alter the chemical structure of the protein. They alter the shape . Proteins are machines, pretty much literally. For example, there are channel proteins, and a molecule docking on a binding site of such a protein might open or close that channel for certain molecules. The variety of those molecular machinery (really, literally) is huge. You can influence machines not just by breaking them down, you can just throw a spanner between their gears. Things that matter are not just shape and size, but also polarity of the parts. Polar components of a molecule influence polar components of proteins if they can get close enough, which influence the shape. Binding sites, as only one example: Description: [LINK] Images: [LINK] Example - 22 second movie - for a protein-machine whose function it is to move stuff around: [VIDEO] Example for io"},{"id":16236378,"author":"jfarlow","month":"2018-01","story":"DNA seen through the eyes of a coder (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Animation of a kinesin protein walking, illustrating protein machines.","quote":"you can get proteins that move or walk","text":"Proteins are 1-dimensional chains of 'lego-like' molecules that fold into atomically-precise 3D tools. There are ~20 types of legos that have properties that differ along a number of axis like hydrophobicity, charge, reactivity, size, flexibility, etc. And those 20, in combination, seem to be enough to build all of the tools life needs to interact with its environment. Most proteins are assemblies of a few hundred such 'legos' (again, of which there are 20 types). Those 3D structures then, because they're encoded in the 'tape-like' DNA, can actually themselves be modular, rearranged, and this chunk can be lopped off and plopped onto that chunk, or assembled with this chunk over here. When you string a 'firefly-shine' protein next to a 'in-the-nucleus' protein, the firefly-shine protein will now be physically connected to the 'in-the-nucleus' signal, so you get firefly-shine in the nucleus. Now encode all the things proteins can do: bind to things, enzymatically make things, go places, make structures, sense chemicals, light, force, electricity, other atoms, etc. - and you get little multi-part robots made of those various active 'chunks'. If you have a protein piece that itself assembles in a hexagonal-like shape, you could imagine assembling a number of them into a soccer ball - just as a virus does to protect its DNA. Or if there are small Brownian-aware 'ratchets' attached to stiff fiber-like proteins, you can get proteins that move or walk: [VIDEO] The microtubule's they'"}],"r":[["meiU6TxysCg",1],["-ga41edXw3A",1],["Q7jcBGLIpus",1],["GcJxRqTs5nk",1],["36GT2zI8lVA",1],["PLyEO0jNt6M",1],["kXpzp4RDGJI",1],["7J52mDjZzto",1],["9RUHJhskW00",1],["wJyUtbn0O5Y",1]]},"-J8YyfrSwTk":{"wy":"It is the go-to source for the idea of making illegal states unrepresentable through types, with the key moment at 28:02.","q":{"x":"Richard Feldman dives deeper into the topic with a lot more examples in","i":18371870,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18371870,"author":"jasim","month":"2018-11","story":"Book Review: A Philosophy of Software Design","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Minsky on making invalid states impossible","quote":"I've found this to be the single most valuable programming technique I've learnt.","text":"That'll be a great addition to the book. Can I also recommend Yaron Minsky's talk on OCaml, specifically the part where he talks about \"making invalid states impossible\": [VIDEO] I've found this to be the single most valuable programming technique I've learnt. Richard Feldman dives deeper into the topic with a lot more examples in [LINK]"},{"id":16422538,"author":"chillee","month":"2018-02","story":"What Made Lisp Different (2002)","stance":"rec","why":"Good example of encoding invariants in types","quote":"I thought this video had a good example.","text":"I guess the closest thing to what you want in Ocaml is modular implicits. [LINK] As far as the tradeoff, my view on types is a bit different than yours, I think. First, a quick note: > specify manually all the types of all the functions This is usually encouraged in OCaml, in the form of a .mli file. I think types can be a lot more than \"compiler checked documentation that can also work as basic tests\". They allow you to encode invariants that allow the compiler to check more than basic stuff. I thought this video had a good example. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29125113,"author":"marshray","month":"2021-11","story":"Why Co–Star Uses Haskell","stance":"reference","why":"Source of 'make illegal states unrepresentable' at 28:02","quote":"This video shows it at 28:02.","text":"\"Make illegal states unrepresentable\" is usually attributed to Yaron Minsky of Jane Street. This video shows it at 28:02. [LINK] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["baxtyeFVn3w",1],["IcgmSRJHu_8",1],["DgVS-zXgMTk",1]]},"7oWip00iXbo":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a credible, clearly explained alternative take on quantum foundations from a Harvard physicist.","q":{"x":"Barandes is excellent at clearly articulating complex ideas in very simple, concise terms.","i":45148064,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45148064,"author":"ilitirit","month":"2025-09","story":"Quantum Mechanics, Concise Book","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear history of QM and Barandes' own formulation.","quote":"Barandes is excellent at clearly articulating complex ideas in very simple, concise terms.","text":"I would recommend watching Curt Jaimungal's series of talks with Jacob Barandes. He gives a nice background history of various aspects of QM, including the formulation of Matrix and Wave mechanics (and loads of other ideas). Barandes is excellent at clearly articulating complex ideas in very simple, concise terms. He also has his own formulation of QM based on \"Indivisible non-Markovian Stochastic Processes\". Even if you disagree with his ideas, the interviews are quite fascinating. In this interview he goes over pretty much exactly what you mentioned (and a lot more): [VIDEO]"},{"id":42393413,"author":"slowmovintarget","month":"2024-12","story":"The Google Willow Thing","stance":"rec","why":"Barandes explains stochastic reformulation of quantum mechanics without wave functions.","quote":"Jacob Barandes is a professor of physics at Harvard (not a crank).","text":"Jacob Barandes is a professor of physics at Harvard (not a crank). He recently did a great podcast with Kurt Jaimungal [1]. In the interview he explains that every year he searches for a good way to introduce his students to quantum mechanics, and every year he's ended up being unsatisfied with the approach and started again. One year he decided to attempt describing systems with traditional mechanics, using probabilities (stochastic mechanics). He worked at it and worked at it, assuming that he would eventually have to take some leap to cross the chasm into the world of gauge theory, Lie Groups, and Hilbert spaces. What he found is, to his surprise, the math just seemed to fit together at some point. That is, he found a mapping, just as the path integral is a mapping into the math of the wave function, and it just kind of worked. He had been under the assumption that it shouldn't. It turns out, that in doing his stochastic mechanics, he had used mathematical descriptions that were indivisible. That is once a given process began, it could not be sliced into smaller and smaller time slices. It had to complete and yield a result. This was what he called a non-Markov stochastic process. Apparently all previous attempts at this used Markov processes, which are divisible like Hilbert vector calculations or the path integral. It turns out that things like \"collapse\" of the wave function, and all the quantum weirdness arose from how the math worked with the wave function and Hilbert"},{"id":46960133,"author":"phkahler","month":"2026-02","story":"Is Particle Physics Dead, Dying, or Just Hard?","stance":"reference","why":"Barandes argues QM computations do not need wave functions.","quote":"Jacob Barandes saying you don't even need wave functions to do the computations of QM","text":"I'm convinced there are some semi-classical explanations that just haven't been figured out. Electrons are helically moving photons: [LINK] That's some interesting/wacky stuff, but there has been more research to improve those calculations - like deriving the electron charge and magnetic moment. Personally I like the idea that a proton is somehow literally an electron and 3 up quarks (a neutron gets 2 electrons and 3 up quarks). I am not a physicist though, so I'm sure there are reasons they \"know\" this is not the case. I find it fascinating that some physicists say wave functions are somehow \"real\" and then we've got Jacob Barandes saying you don't even need wave functions to do the computations of QM: [VIDEO] IMHO there is a lot of exploration to be done without particle accelerators."}],"r":[["Cym4TZwTCNU",1],["5hVmeOCJjOU",1],["esQyYGezS7c",1],["xt1KNDmOYqA",1]]},"FkKPsLxgpuY":{"wy":"Recommended as a good primer on what IQ scores mean and their limited effect on life outcomes.","q":{"x":"Veritasium had a good primer on IQ scores and what they actually mean for your life","i":44195134,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":39538329,"author":"cdrini","month":"2024-02","story":"Ask HN: Slow thinkers, how do you compensate for your lack of quick-wittedness?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"History, controversy and real-world uses of IQ tests, such as the military","quote":"Would highly recommend a watch","text":"I recently shifted my opinion on IQ tests a bit after watching a recent Veritasium video. He goes into the background/history/controversy of the test as well as some of the concrete impacts of the test and places where it's used. For example did you know the US military has an IQ minimum cutoff? And furthermore they have a second 'soft' cutoff, where only 20% of the military can have an IQ under a certain value. In the past they tried removing this second restriction, but had to reinstate it after seeing increases in casualties/indicators of reduced efficiency! So are IQ tests everything? No. But do they have no merit? Also no. It's somewhere in between. Would highly recommend a watch [VIDEO]"},{"id":44195134,"author":"Balgair","month":"2025-06","story":"Longevity Is Now a Factor When Picking an Embryo for IVF","stance":"rec","why":"Good primer on IQ scores and their limited life impact","quote":"Veritasium had a good primer on IQ scores and what they actually mean for your life","text":"[VIDEO] Veritasium had a good primer on IQ scores and what they actually mean for your life TLDW: IQ score seems to really only correlates to being too dumb to be conscripted and effective in war. Going on Derek's work there, even if other countries do somehow manage to genetically engineer their children for higher IQ scores, and even if they manage to get all the environmental hazards (lead paint, nutrition, quality sleep, etc) out of the way for those children, and even if they don't then do some other draconian nonsense to those kids in the ~19 years until they become productive, then, even then, there seems to be absolutely no guarantee that those kids will go on to do any dominating of other countries in social, political, or military contexts. Outside of being too dumb to be trusted with a firearm, IQ seems to have no real bearing on measurable life outcomes. As the current science stands, from my understanding of it, it would be incredibly foolish to pursue genetic engineering/breeding as a method of international relations."},{"id":39538867,"author":"swframe2","month":"2024-02","story":"Ask HN: Slow thinkers, how do you compensate for your lack of quick-wittedness?","stance":"rec","why":"IQ stats and correlation with success","quote":"Pay attention to the stats on how IQ correlates to success (near the end).","text":"The Veritasium youtube channel has a few interesting videos related to this topic: This one is on IQ: [VIDEO] Pay attention to the stats on how IQ correlates to success (near the end). This one is about becoming an expert: [LINK] This one is about someone who just worked harder than everyone else: [LINK]"},{"id":39665163,"author":"mglikesbikes","month":"2024-03","story":"Lead in gasoline blunted IQ of half the U.S. population, study says","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for history of elite institutions and eugenics","quote":"","text":"If the US was an organism, what role would an under-educated X% play? You say it’s a “cost” but equal education obviously isn’t a priority in our country and I can’t help but wonder who benefits from such a high percentage of illiteracy (this is semi-rhetorical). I genuinely don’t think we’re tracking the cost appropriately nor implementing solutions with enough urgency; 10-15 years down the road this empire of ours is going to be far out-classed intellectually by countries that actually implement policy for the equitable benefit of everyone. If I’m off base I’d appreciate an education on this topic as it’s quite gross to think about our elite institutions being proponents of eugenics in the past[1] and how that likely spilled over into the world-building we did after WWII (Kissinger etc). Or put another way, how do we map “AI is going to save everyone” rhetoric to how history has actually gone, and what incentive do existing power structures have to change? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38180153,"author":"cosmojg","month":"2023-11","story":"Transformers struggle with generalizing tasks beyond pre-training data","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for training-data access affecting IQ performance","quote":"","text":"Has it even been shown that the average human can generalize beyond their training data? Isn't this the central thrust of the controversy around IQ tests? For example, some argue that access to relevant training data is a greater determinant of performance on IQ tests than genetics[1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37886340,"author":"ta_vf7xjd34cc","month":"2023-10","story":"The link between IQ and income is overrated","stance":"reference","why":"Covers IQ-income correlation","quote":"Veritasium did an episode on IQ a couple of month back where they cover the IQ-income correlation.","text":"Veritasium did an episode on IQ a couple of month back where they cover the IQ-income correlation.[1] One of the people he talked with said something that I find interesting: \"a lot of people who have very high intelligence scores don't have the same interest in accumulating money.\" [1] I Took an IQ Test to Find Out What it Actually Measures ( [VIDEO] )"}],"r":[["2JM2oImb9Qg",1],["k1kndKWJKB8",1],["EACkiMRT0pc",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["gB9n2gHsHN4",1],["-k3mVnRlQLU",1],["qJZ1Ez28C-A",1],["5eW6Eagr9XA",1],["AF8d72mA41M",1],["BCiZc0n6COY",1]]},"Q0yL2GezneU":{"wy":"HN points to it as an expert correction of myths about vocal fry, showing it is a normal speech pattern across groups.","q":{"x":"it's not just that. It's (also?) a normal speech pattern, used by different genders, ages, and social groups, in many languages.","i":47336872,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":41538984,"author":"qwery","month":"2024-09","story":"Who is responsible for the greatest change to our language? Teenage girls","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Linguist's video on vocal fry and gender.","quote":"Dr Lindsey's channel is totally bitchin' like you wouldn't believe. You'll love it!","text":"You might also be interested in Dr Geoff Lindsey's video on 'vocal fry'[0] which is a linguistic phenomenon also associated with young women (although it isn't that simple). Dr Lindsey's channel is totally bitchin' like you wouldn't believe. You'll love it! I'm sure. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47336872,"author":"ggus","month":"2026-03","story":"TADA: Fast, Reliable Speech Generation Through Text-Acoustic Synchronization","stance":"rec","why":"Shows vocal fry is normal speech across groups.","quote":"it's not just that. It's (also?) a normal speech pattern, used by different genders, ages, and social groups, in many languages.","text":"\"Vocal fry\", aka \"creaky voice\". It's stereotypically associated with irritating young women. I like me a good rabbit hole that's interesting and also digs into stereotypes. Turns out, like many memes, it's not just that. It's (also?) a normal speech pattern, used by different genders, ages, and social groups, in many languages. This doesn't mean that vocal fry isn't used as social signaling. But complaining about it, well, isn't that social signalling too? Geoff Lindsey - Vocal Fry: what it is, who does it, and why people hate it! - [VIDEO]"},{"id":40454622,"author":"queuebert","month":"2024-05","story":"OpenAI didn’t copy Scarlett Johansson’s voice for ChatGPT, records show","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for vocal fry in synthetic voices.","quote":"reek of Bay area vocal fry [1] and lilt","text":"I don't find any of the OpenAI voices sexy or deferential. They sound fake happy to me, like a customer service phone menu or an elementary school teacher, and reek of Bay area vocal fry [1] and lilt. I wish there was a greater diversity of accents and speaking patterns available, such as can be seen on the Speech Accent Archive [2]. 1. [VIDEO] 2. [LINK]"}]},"__wn9zXFiy8":{"wy":"Commenters call it an invaluable, approachable way to learn how optical networking actually works.","q":{"x":"proved invaluable to me in learning optical networking","i":18116975,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18116975,"author":"dvno42","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are the best textbooks in your field of expertise?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Invaluable talk for learning optical networking","quote":"proved invaluable to me in learning optical networking","text":"For Networking in the ISP space, for me the most helpful have been: - Interdomain Multicast Routing: Practical Juniper Networks and Cisco Systems Solutions - Inside Cisco IOS Software Architecture - MPLS-Enabled Applications: Emerging Developments and New Technologies This one's not a textbook but proved invaluable to me in learning optical networking: - New and Updated - Everything You Always Wanted to Know About Optical Networking - [VIDEO]"},{"id":15013500,"author":"madsushi","month":"2017-08","story":"Everything You Always Wanted to Know About Optical Networking [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Recent version of a recurring optical networking tutorial talk","quote":"learn something new every time","text":"YouTube link for the most-recent talk: [VIDEO] I've seen a couple versions of RAS' talk and learn something new every time."},{"id":18108978,"author":"mmt","month":"2018-09","story":"Everything You Always Wanted to Know About Optical Networking [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Updated video version of the optical networking slides","quote":"","text":"Looks like the full talk is on youtube at [LINK] [LINK] And an updated one: [VIDEO] [LINK] I'm not sure which version these slides correspond to."}]},"36qZYL5RlgY":{"wy":"HN points to it as the recorded talk behind the Netflix 800Gb/s slides and as a deep look at kernel-level optimizations for high throughput.","q":{"x":"this might be a little lower level that you may be interested in as it relates to kernel optimizations to jack bit throughput rates","i":37825491,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37825491,"author":"thomasjudge","month":"2023-10","story":"Video Streaming at Scale with Kubernetes and RabbitMQ","stance":"rec","why":"Netflix kernel-level optimizations for high throughput.","quote":"this might be a little lower level that you may be interested in as it relates to kernel optimizations to jack bit throughput rates","text":"OP mentions that \"I would love to be a little mouse and peek at YouTube’s complete architecture to see how far we are from them.\" You can occasionally find posts -often linked here- from another player in streaming video which you might have heard of, discussing technical architecture. For example, this might be a little lower level that you may be interested in as it relates to kernel optimizations to jack bit throughput rates, but I dig this sort of thing - [VIDEO]"},{"id":33452575,"author":"notaplumber1","month":"2022-11","story":"FreeBSD optimizations used by Netflix to serve video at 800Gb/s [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"EuroBSDcon talk recording on Netflix FreeBSD optimizations.","quote":"Some really great talks this year from all the *BSDs, highly recommend checking a look","text":"This talk was given at this years EuroBSDcon in Vienna, recording is up on YouTube. [LINK] [VIDEO] Some really great talks this year from all the *BSDs, highly recommend checking a look: [LINK]"},{"id":44726761,"author":"craftkiller","month":"2025-07","story":"Linux Performance Analysis (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Covers optimizations Netflix made to FreeBSD.","quote":"this video covers the optimizations Netflix has made to FreeBSD","text":"Netflix discusses their reasons starting at 18:20: [LINK] tl;dw: the performance, the efficiency of development, the community, FreeBSD is a complete operating system, the code base is smaller, the ports system, and the license. and this video covers the optimizations Netflix has made to FreeBSD: [VIDEO] Also potentially a reason: According to drewg123, Linux's kTLS was broken. Which I see drewg123 also commenting in this thread. Is he the \"Drew on my team\" mentioned in the first video? Is he the speaker in the 2nd video? Idk [LINK]"},{"id":36519367,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2023-06","story":"Happy 50th Birthday, Ethernet","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Netflix reaching encrypted 800 Gb/s.","quote":"Netflix is hitting (encrypted) 800 Gb/s","text":"> Ethernet is stuck on 1500 byte frames, with the result of limiting TCP as well. That's just nuts on 100G or 400G networks, even on 10G it's a bit of a stretch already. How limiting is 1500 for the end-nodes for most people? For access-, distribution-, and core-layer switches the ASICs process things at wire speed, so does it matter what the frame size is? I can see some server applications, but we've had offloading for years now: * [LINK] Netflix is hitting (encrypted) 800 Gb/s: * [VIDEO] * [LINK] > TCP is also sticking around for too long. Almost nothing wants a stream these days, lots and lots of applications reinvent datagrams on top of it. Tallk to the vendors of middleware boxes (e.g., (CPE) firewalls): we've had (say) SCTP and DCCP for twenty years that offer variations, but they're never allowed to pass through. No new Layer 4 can really be allowed through because of ossification, and so kludging everything to TCP/UDP port 80/443 is what we're left with. NAT and a lack of end-to-end also doesn't help: one has to futz around with things like STUN and TURN."},{"id":33450773,"author":"drewg123","month":"2022-11","story":"FreeBSD optimizations used by Netflix to serve video at 800Gb/s [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Talk behind the slides on Netflix FreeBSD optimizations.","quote":"The talk is on Youtube","text":"> How did you generate those flamegraphs and what other tools did you use to measure performance? We have an internal shell script that takes hwpmc output and generates flamegraphs from the stacks. It also works with dtrace. I'm a huge fan of dtrace. I also make heavy use of lockstat, AMD uProf, and Intel Vtune. > Did the graph show the bottleneck contention on aio queue? Did the graph show that \"a lot of time was spent accessing memory\"? See the graph on page 32 or so of the presentation. It shows huge plateaus in lock_delay called out of the aio code. Its also obvious from lockstat stacks (run as lockstat -x aggsize=4m -s 10 sleep 10 > results.txt) See the graph on page 38 or so. The plateaus are mostly memory copy functions (memcpy, copyin, copyout). We already use FreeBSD on our CDN, so it just made sense to do the work in FreeBSD. The talk is on Youtube [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["fE2KDzZaxvE",1]]},"4_qJptwikRc":{"wy":"Recommended as an accessible, curious-undergraduate-level overview of dark matter and its alternatives.","q":{"x":"Here’s Sabine Hossenfelder’s curious-undergraduate-level history of Dark Matter theories","i":34895856,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34895856,"author":"tlholaday","month":"2023-02","story":"Dark energy from supermassive black holes? Physicists spar over radical idea","stance":"rec","why":"Accessible history of dark matter theories.","quote":"Here’s Sabine Hossenfelder’s curious-undergraduate-level history of Dark Matter theories","text":"> correct me if I’m wrong … I think you’re oversimplifying, like seeing a forest and overlooking that it’s Prototaxites, not Malus domestica. Here’s Sabine Hossenfelder’s curious-undergraduate-level history of Dark Matter theories: [VIDEO] Proper nouns include Renzo’s Rule and Baryonic Tully Fisher Relation."},{"id":27308991,"author":"keithnz","month":"2021-05","story":"New dark matter map reveals cosmic mystery","stance":"rec","why":"Sabine's recent update on dark matter.","quote":"Sabine is a great science (physics) communicator to the \"masses\", she recently did an update on dark matter.","text":"Sabine is a great science (physics) communicator to the \"masses\", she recently did an update on dark matter. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30622277,"author":"astrange","month":"2022-03","story":"Four years on, new experiment sees no sign of ‘cosmic dawn’","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"They're \"actually\" what QFT says they are, but the rest isn't wrong either. It's not wrong to say \"a house exists\" because it's actually a pile of bricks in the shape of a house. Dark matter must be \"matter\" if it exists, because there's not a category of things that aren't matter. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27329831,"author":"raz32dust","month":"2021-05","story":"Astronomers have created the largest ever map of dark matter","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Also worth pointing out that dark matter is not the only explanation","text":"I am not an expert. My understanding - (1) The quantity of rocks that would be needed to explain such a gigantic gap in mass is bound to be observed - if they are dark, then at least we should be able to see them when they block light. You have to remember we need dark matter to be around 80% of total mass of the universe. So they either need to be super dense, like tiny black holes [1], or there needs to be so much of it that there is no way we won't see them. (2) Dark matter is too spread out. If they were rocks or any of the known matter types, they should have coalesced on their own. So they have to be such that dark matter doesn't interact with itself. Edit: This is a nice intro: [LINK] Edit 2: Also worth pointing out that dark matter is not the only explanation: [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"}],"r":[["au0QJYISe4c",1],["98LdFA-_zfA",1]]},"5q-BH-tvxEg":{"wy":"Shared whenever gear reductions, exponential scaling or playful kinetic art come up.","q":{"x":"I quite like how a lot of his work seems to have a punchline to it.","i":44746087,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44746087,"author":"Doxin","month":"2025-07","story":"Different Clocks","stance":"rec","why":"Playful kinetic sculpture with a punchline.","quote":"I quite like how a lot of his work seems to have a punchline to it.","text":"I think the artist put it as \"and then the last gear turns so slowly it doesn't really matter, so I cast it in concrete.\" Assuming we're talking about Arthur Gansons \"Machine with concrete\"[0] of course. I quite like how a lot of his work seems to have a punchline to it. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18156734,"author":"kybernetikos","month":"2018-10","story":"An artwork by Banksy shredded itself after selling for $1.3M at Sotheby’s","stance":"rec","why":"Motor with reduction gears ending in concrete.","quote":"I think this one is quite interesting","text":"I think this one is quite interesting: [VIDEO] A motor connected to a series of reduction gears, with the last part of the chain encased in concrete."},{"id":23857270,"author":"zimpenfish","month":"2020-07","story":"Making a Googol:1 Reduction with Lego Gears [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Gear reduction so large the last gear barely turns.","quote":"it will take well over two trillion years before the final gear makes but one turn.","text":"[VIDEO] \"With the motor turning around 200 revolutions per minute, it will take well over two trillion years before the final gear makes but one turn.\""},{"id":19052416,"author":"rzzzt","month":"2019-02","story":"A 500-Year-Long Science Experiment","stance":"reference","why":"Chain of 1:50 gears with the last embedded in concrete.","quote":"has 1:50 gears put in sequence, to the power of 12; the last section is embedded in concrete","text":"Arthur Ganson's \"Machine with Concrete\" is an installation that has 1:50 gears put in sequence, to the power of 12; the last section is embedded in concrete: [VIDEO] There's also the pitch drop experiment, which IMO rivals watching grass grow: [LINK]"},{"id":10257747,"author":"erre","month":"2015-09","story":"The 1,000-Year GIF","stance":"reference","why":"Twelve worm gear pairs ending in concrete.","quote":"it will take well over two trillion years before the final gear makes but one turn.","text":"This reminds me of \"Machine with Concrete\", by Arthur Ganson: [VIDEO] From the video description: Each worm/worm gear pair reduces the speed of the motor by 1/50th. Since there are 12 pairs of gears, the final speed reduction is calculated by (1/50)12. The implications are quite large. With the motor turning around 200 revolutions per minute, it will take well over two trillion years before the final gear makes but one turn. Given the truth of this situation, it is possible to do anything at all with the final gear, even embed it in concrete."},{"id":9859131,"author":"dominicgs","month":"2015-07","story":"Fist-sized 11M-to-1 gearbox","stance":"reference","why":"Ganson's gear reduction sculpture at MIT museum.","quote":"It's part of a great exhibit of fun/quirky machines","text":"There is one by Arthur Ganson in the MIT museum[1]. It's part of a great exhibit of fun/quirky machines and there's an interesting video about it on youtube[2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["KPUlgSRn6e0",1],["saRir95iIWk",1],["VZQoAKJPbh8",1],["M3BM9TB-8yA",1]]},"6JwEYamjXpA":{"wy":"Used as a funny, accessible reference for coincidences, spurious patterns and maths errors.","q":{"x":"It also comes with an entirely different set of interesting stories for anyone inclined to listen","i":40606796,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40606796,"author":"modderation","month":"2024-06","story":"Saint Michael Sword: Are the cathedrals really on a straight line?","stance":"rec","why":"Newer Matt Parker talk with better audio and different stories","quote":"It also comes with an entirely different set of interesting stories for anyone inclined to listen","text":"He also covered this in a more recent talk [1] which has some better audio and a direct feed of the slides. It also comes with an entirely different set of interesting stories for anyone inclined to listen :) 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25474545,"author":"db48x","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: Astronomical Coincidences. What does it mean?","stance":"rec","why":"Funny anecdote about spurious patterns and coincidence","quote":"Here's a funny anecdote about the same topic","text":"Nothing, as long as we learn the art of discerning coincidence from causation. An important part of that is deliberately noticing how many possibilities we're picking amongst when we single out a coincidence. Here's a funny anecdote about the same topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41483283,"author":"olejorgenb","month":"2024-09","story":"LibrePythonista allows running IPython code in a LibreOffice spreadsheet","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"most of the examples in Matt Parker's talk isn't exactly work of art","text":"> A real artist already putting this much effort into it, would never make this mistake I'm not so sure that's true. See [VIDEO] Granted - most of the examples in Matt Parker's talk isn't exactly work of art, but I'm sure effort were put into them. Edit: I agree the way the cogs don't work in the project's logo is of a different class, so maybe you have point."},{"id":31347778,"author":"codefreeordie","month":"2022-05","story":"When old historic maps overlap with modern political maps","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"when Matt Parker discovered ancient alien wisdom by plotting the lines between Woolworths stores","text":"Is this actually different from when Matt Parker discovered ancient alien wisdom by plotting the lines between Woolworths stores? ( [VIDEO] ) Some of these maps are for time period not that distant from each other where the correlation is pretty straightforward, and those are probably telling at-least-partly-true narratives. For others, it's less clear. How many different modern and ancient maps are there which could conceivably have interesting (spurious) correlations?"}],"r":[["YE8MVNMzpbo",1],["aeWyp2vXxqA",1],["FlQYU3m1e80",1],["wymmCdLdPvM",1],["c33AZBnRHks",1]]},"AchISJUKfH4":{"wy":"Cited to explain why poor people's seemingly irrational financial decisions can rationally protect their social safety net.","q":{"x":"poor people make seemingly dumb decisions because if they don't help their social network when it comes knocking","i":39221688,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39221688,"author":"e63f67dd-065b","month":"2024-02","story":"The business of check cashing","stance":"rec","why":"Poor people's seemingly dumb decisions preserve their social safety net","quote":"poor people make seemingly dumb decisions because if they don't help their social network when it comes knocking","text":"> This reflects a false belief that people who live in poor neighborhoods are less compassionate. In fact research suggests that people living in poverty tend to be more compassionate towards each other than rich people are toward the impoverished. It's comforting for wealthier people to hold this belief, because it makes us feel less bad about our own lack of compassion. From what I recall, its less that the poor are more compassionate, instead they participate in _reciprocal altruism_ as a form of insurance against further loss. I remember this talk [VIDEO] -- poor people make seemingly dumb decisions because if they don't help their social network when it comes knocking, they're in effect _cutting themselves off_ from said network. The middle class is more insulated from this effect because those with emergency savings don't often need to borrow $500 because of a broken car or need to house their recently homeless sister and children, so capital can accumulate more easily."},{"id":8792765,"author":"humanrebar","month":"2014-12","story":"We Are Built to Be Kind [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Social capital as rational choice for the poor","quote":"some people value social capital above other forms of capital and that can be a rational thing to do","text":"A different theory is that some people value social capital above other forms of capital and that can be a rational thing to do: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30239130,"author":"gruez","month":"2022-02","story":"On Being Broke","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> There's something I remember being told (or maybe read about too) I heard it from a youtube video [VIDEO]"},{"id":23984764,"author":"yvdriess","month":"2020-07","story":"Bread, How Did They Make It?","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on how social capital still matters in poor communities","quote":"The author links to a talk on how this still plays a role in poor communities today","text":"For readers jumping to the comments: Any surplus went into the social capital (dowry etc), distributing it inside the countryside as non-fungible untaxable wealth. Putting a year's saving or even surplus into easily transportable goods like money would be a really dumb thing to do, as it was incredibly risky (dumb) thing to do. The author links to a talk on how this still plays a role in poor communities today: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["nXaxk27zwlk",1],["fHNmRkzxHWs",1],["eR34r7HOU14",1]]},"F-yDKeya4SU":{"wy":"Cited as an accessible summary of evidence that corn ethanol is no better than gasoline for emissions.","q":{"x":"Corn-based ethanol is at best as bad a carbon emitter as gasoline, and it's most likely worse.","i":30949239,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":36233893,"author":"dmbche","month":"2023-06","story":"Net energy of cellulosic ethanol from switchgrass","stance":"rec","why":"Compares corn and switchgrass ethanol GHG emissions","quote":"This video is an interesting look at this whole issue","text":"This paper shows how much more efficient switchgrass is at producing ethanol than corn. This video is an interesting look at this whole issue:(Engineering Explained - America Was Wrong About Ethanol) [VIDEO] Especially his summary, where corn creates 20 percent less GHG than gasoline and ''breaks even'' after 28 years, while switchgrass creates 94% less GHG and breaks even in it's first year."},{"id":36233956,"author":"dmbche","month":"2023-06","story":"Grass Makes Better Ethanol Than Corn Does (2008)","stance":"rec","why":"Compares corn and switchgrass ethanol GHG emissions","quote":"This video is an interesting look at this whole issue","text":"This video is an interesting look at this whole issue:(Engineering Explained - America Was Wrong About Ethanol) [VIDEO] Especially his summary, where corn creates 20 percent less GHG than gasoline and ''breaks even'' after 28 years, while switchgrass creates 94% less GHG and breaks even in it's first year."},{"id":30949239,"author":"dreamcompiler","month":"2022-04","story":"The logistics behind corn farming [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Numbers showing corn ethanol is no better than gasoline","quote":"Corn-based ethanol is at best as bad a carbon emitter as gasoline, and it's most likely worse.","text":"Supports your point with numbers: [VIDEO] tl;dr: Corn-based ethanol is at best as bad a carbon emitter as gasoline, and it's most likely worse."},{"id":30595578,"author":"syshum","month":"2022-03","story":"UN Climate change is so severe we're running out of time to adapt","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"current research indicates has made things WORSE not better.","text":"Wait, so your solution to massive economic harm caused by government regulations, and money printing is to increase government regulations and print even more money... That's a bold strategy, Cotton. Let's see if it pays off for them last time government tried to solve climate change we got 10% corn based ethanol gas which current research indicates has made things WORSE not better.[1] [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["n02tImce3AE",1]]},"dGiQaabX3_o":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a compact, well-sourced intro to human evolution that even led one viewer to read Sapiens.","q":{"x":"there is a great 10 minute video from the Youtube Kurzgesagt channel that lead me to read Sapiens.","i":12286121,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":16235841,"author":"doctoboggan","month":"2018-01","story":"Jawbone of Earliest Modern Human Out of Africa Discovered in Israeli Cave","stance":"rec","why":"Well-sourced Kurzgesagt videos on human origins.","quote":"the YouTube channel Kurzgesagt has 2 very good videos on the topic","text":"It might not be what you were thinking, but the YouTube channel Kurzgesagt has 2 very good videos on the topic: [VIDEO] [LINK] His stuff is well sourced and generally reliable."},{"id":12286121,"author":"joshbaptiste","month":"2016-08","story":"Did Our Ancestors Become Bipedal So They Could Throw?","stance":"rec","why":"Short overview of human origins that prompted reading Sapiens.","quote":"there is a great 10 minute video from the Youtube Kurzgesagt channel that lead me to read Sapiens.","text":"I live in a world where 90% of my friends and family believe that they came from the sins of a woman who decided to eat an apple. I recently read a book called Sapiens, this book really drilled into my mind that we homo-sapiens are a derivative of a single great ape that had two daughters, one that became the ancestor of all chimpanzees and the other all humans. If human origin interests you there is a great 10 minute video from the Youtube Kurzgesagt channel that lead me to read Sapiens. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":30289325,"author":"sillysaurusx","month":"2022-02","story":"Modern human incursion into Neanderthal territories 54k years ago at Mandrin","stance":"reference","why":"Mentions uncertainty about Neanderthal extinction causes.","quote":"Even Kurzgesagt mentioned in passing that we're not sure if it was due to","text":"It's possible. It's just one of those explanations that we'd really love to believe, since the alternative is so unthinkable. It's been a hobby of mine to be skeptical of it. Even Kurzgesagt mentioned in passing that we're not sure if it was due to \"a series of minor genocides.\" - [VIDEO] - [LINK] If anyone knows of breadcrumbs to follow, please chime in. Genetic testing implies it should be possible to figure out how much interbreeding took place. We seem to be able to trace extinction events with some degree of accuracy, so it would be surprising if a genocide-type extinction event was easy to confuse with interbreeding."}],"r":[["czgOWmtGVGs",1],["gSKzgpt4HBU",1],["kixAljyfdqU",1],["UjtOGPJ0URM",1]]},"f4U8YbXKwog":{"wy":"Recommended as a great overview of Qubes OS and how its compartmentalization works in practice.","q":{"x":"This talk(ii) from Micah Lee when 4.0 launched is a great overview/preview.","i":32158096,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32158096,"author":"isolatte","month":"2022-07","story":"Ask HN: How do you develop locally with privacy/security in mind?","stance":"rec","why":"Great overview of Qubes OS around the 4.0 launch","quote":"This talk(ii) from Micah Lee when 4.0 launched is a great overview/preview.","text":"In order of decreasing isolation: For hardware and system's security QubesOS(i) is superb for isolation at the (virtual)machine layer. This talk(ii) from Micah Lee when 4.0 launched is a great overview/preview. It's my daily driver, and I find it indispensable. For software security any standard linux distro you can utilize encrypted chroots(iii). This is how things like crouton(iv) work on ChromiumOS. For library isolation you could utilise virtual environments like virtualenv for python. ( or nodenv(v) for 'Node libraries') (i) [LINK] (ii) [VIDEO] (iii) [LINK] (iv) [LINK] (v) [LINK]"},{"id":24069783,"author":"waldfee","month":"2020-08","story":"Usbkill – anti-forensic tool to halt computer when new USB device is connected","stance":"rec","why":"Overview talk on Qubes OS from HOPE 2018","quote":"Micah Lee held a great overview talk at HOPE 2018","text":"If you are paranoid about something like this happening, just use [LINK] . all usb devices are jailed in a non-networked vm by default. In general, if what you do warrants that level of paranoia, qubes will help you massively. Micah Lee held a great overview talk at HOPE 2018: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21668188,"author":"lokedhs","month":"2019-11","story":"How Is NordVPN Unblocking Disney+?","stance":"reference","why":"Micah Lee explains using Tor with a VPN and paying anonymously","quote":"Micah Lee did a talk about how he does it.","text":"Micah Lee did a talk about how he does it. In essence he uses Tor to connect to the VPN. He also explains how to pay for the VPN anonymously. I think it was part of his Qubes OS talk: [VIDEO]"}]},"gC295d3V9gE":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an approachable talk on a total functional generalization of Datalog, by the language's author.","q":{"x":"Also be sure to watch Datafun author Michael Arntzenius's Strangeloop talk.","i":44274287,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44274287,"author":"fogzen","month":"2025-06","story":"Implementing Logic Programming","stance":"rec","why":"Datafun author explains a total functional Datalog generalization","quote":"Also be sure to watch Datafun author Michael Arntzenius's Strangeloop talk.","text":"Yes, for some kinds of operations on some kinds of data structures. The keyword/property is \"monotonicity\". Monotonic functions are guaranteed to terminate under fixed-point semantics. Look into Datafun: A total functional language that generalizes Datalog. Also be sure to watch Datafun author Michael Arntzenius's Strangeloop talk. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17111905,"author":"a-saleh","month":"2018-05","story":"A relatively simple Datalog engine","stance":"rec","why":"Approachable Strange Loop talk on Datafun","quote":"for me it seemed quite approachable :)","text":"\"Streams of solution trees\" kinda reminds me of mikro-kanren [1]. When I was using Clojure roughly 3 years ago, kanren was quite popular embedded logic. W.r.t. Datafun, i learned about it from a talk its creator gave on strangeloop last year [2], for me it seemed quite approachable :) [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35010831,"author":"refset","month":"2023-03","story":"Deconstructing Datalog [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Early overview of Datafun research","quote":"with an early overview of this research","text":"Thanks for sharing the summary. There's also this Strange Loop 2017 talk \"Datafun: a functional query language\" with an early overview of this research: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31993058,"author":"tracnar","month":"2022-07","story":"Why Can't Database Tables Index Themselves? (2006)","stance":"reference","why":"Talk explores SQL-like language with automatic indexing","quote":"This talk explored a bit that idea","text":"This talk explored a bit that idea, you could have a (full) programming language which is more like SQL so you get good enough datastructures (and indexing- without manual tuning: [VIDEO] I think Prolog (another commenter pointed it out) and datalog do this to some extend."}],"r":[["gcUWTa16Jc0",1],["oYk8CKH7OhE",1]]},"nQHBAdShgYI":{"wy":"Pointed to in threads about mind uploading and teleportation as a good, accessible look at personal identity.","q":{"x":"CGP Grey has a good look at the question wrt Star Trek teleportation","i":16577967,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16577967,"author":"macintux","month":"2018-03","story":"A startup is pitching a mind-uploading service that is “100 percent fatal”","stance":"rec","why":"Good look at the Ship of Theseus applied to transporters.","quote":"CGP Grey has a good look at the question wrt Star Trek teleportation","text":"This question descends from an ancient philosophical question; see the Ship of Theseus[0]. CGP Grey has a good look at the question wrt Star Trek teleportation[1]. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11245499,"author":"rendezvouscp","month":"2016-03","story":"If you're alive in 30 years you might be in 1000 years too","stance":"rec","why":"Fun video on the philosophy of teleportation and identity.","quote":"","text":"That’s a great question that’s been pondered by philosophers for a while: [LINK] Here’s a fun video talking about the same thing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22945890,"author":"bmn__","month":"2020-04","story":"Conversations with a six-year-old on functional programming (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on transporter trouble among other sources.","quote":"CGP Grey: The Trouble with Transporters","text":"> a YouTube video CGP Grey: The Trouble with Transporters [VIDEO] Steve Shives: Is Star Trek's Transporter Actually a Murder Machine? [LINK] > why wouldn’t they just [LINK] For purpose of storytelling, these powerful side effects of transporters need to be restricted. Hence, as an in-universe explanation, a pattern buffer ∙ is a physical phenomenon that cannot be (fully) expressed as information ∙ deteriorates over time, so needs to be rematerialised soon. For the exception, see [LINK]"},{"id":19666342,"author":"martin-adams","month":"2019-04","story":"Travel through wormholes is possible, but slow","stance":"reference","why":"Poses what happens if you skip the vaporizing step.","quote":"","text":"Even if we are just mass and particles, then what happens if you just skip the vaporizing part. ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":13929543,"author":"sillysaurus3","month":"2017-03","story":"Your yearly dose of is-the-universe-a-simulation","stance":"reference","why":"Raises question of whether you are a single continuous person.","quote":"","text":"This runs into the question of whether you're a single person. [VIDEO] [LINK] If you transplant half your brain into another body, and both of you wake up, which one is you? If you start swapping your atoms one by one with someone else, and neither of you die, at what point do you become the other person? Unfortunately questions like that are necessary in order to address the idea of a simulation afterlife. If our bodies are literally who we are, and our bodies disperse, it may not make sense to say that we can still be simulated after our bodies disappear."},{"id":12548682,"author":"s_kilk","month":"2016-09","story":"Researchers quantum teleport particle of light six kilometres","stance":"reference","why":"Star Trek transporter destroys and recreates the original.","quote":"","text":"> The original photon was in fact destroyed, and only the information about its state was transmitted. Isn't that how teleportation works in, for example, Star Trek? [VIDEO]"}]},"nv7-YM4wno8":{"wy":"Recommended as a detailed, wonderful explanation of the physics of dynamic soaring and record-speed RC gliders.","q":{"x":"Here's a really wonderful talk by Spencer Lisenby about the basic physics","i":38672519,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38672519,"author":"jessriedel","month":"2023-12","story":"Dynamic Soaring","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on basic physics and pushing glider speed records","quote":"Here's a really wonderful talk by Spencer Lisenby about the basic physics","text":"Here's a really wonderful talk by Spencer Lisenby about the basic physics and Lisenby's work on pushing the speed [VIDEO] Some other interesting HN comments: [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":32739679,"author":"robomartin","month":"2022-09","story":"Create an Inexpensive Flying Wing / Drone (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Long, detailed technical explanation of dynamic soaring","quote":"This is a long video with a detailed technical explanation of dynamic soaring","text":"Adding to that, here's slope soaring, which is really cool. I haven't really done a lot of this due to having to travel far to have access to this type of geography. Beautiful silent flight, no motors required. They do add motors to some of the planes just so you have backup in case you lose lift. Here's an excellent intro to the sport: [LINK] And then there's the absolutely crazy/demented --in a good way-- dynamic slope soaring. I have yet to experience this. This is the domain of sophisticated carbon fiber and top-quality components. You can find videos of planes exploding in flight due to loads. This is a long video with a detailed technical explanation of dynamic soaring: [VIDEO] 548 mph (882 kph) run: [LINK]"},{"id":33888553,"author":"chrisdalke","month":"2022-12","story":"Dynamic soaring as a means to exceed the solar wind speed","stance":"reference","why":"Engineering of an RC glider flown at 519mph","quote":"In the video I linked he describes how they engineered and flew a RC glider at a speed of 519mph.","text":"Similar dynamic slope soaring has been researched and demonstrated to dramatic affect by Spencer Lisenby and others: [VIDEO] . In the video I linked he describes how they engineered and flew a RC glider at a speed of 519mph. Makes a lot of sense that this could be applied on a larger atmospheric scale for spacecraft."}],"r":[["sWFFiubtC3c",1],["-FENubmZGj8",1],["mf5otGNbkuc",1],["RuOvn4UqznU",1],["Unzc731iCUY",1],["iG9CE55wbtY",1]]},"pa5_tudyAF8":{"wy":"Cited as a vivid example of modern, highly automated manufacturing with few workers, and of carmakers as robotics companies.","q":{"x":"To me it looks like technology brought by aliens from the future.","i":11532982,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":15345424,"author":"retSava","month":"2017-09","story":"Dyson to make electric cars from 2020","stance":"rec","why":"Recommended factory tour of BMW i3 production.","quote":"I can recommend this video","text":"I can recommend this video: [LINK] \"Tearing Down the Model T of Our Time - Autoline After Hours 284\" and [VIDEO] \"BMW i3 Factory Production Tour\""},{"id":11532982,"author":"justsaysmthng","month":"2016-04","story":"BMW Loses Core Development Team of Its I3 and I8 Electric Vehicle Line","stance":"rec","why":"Shows highly automated BMW i3 production line.","quote":"To me it looks like technology brought by aliens from the future.","text":"Here's a video of the i3 production line: [VIDEO] As can be seen, they are not joking around. To me it looks like technology brought by aliens from the future."},{"id":14900405,"author":"delegate","month":"2017-08","story":"Mercedes kills their only electric car","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates car makers as robotics companies.","quote":"They build computers with wheels.","text":"Respectfully, I disagree. Their tech is not the cars themselves as much as the robots/software building those cars, so they have slowly moved from being car companies to robotics/tech companies. They build computers with wheels. [VIDEO] While the Americans do have a taste for disruption, these Germans have a taste for getting things done well. The wise thing to do is collaborate, which I hope we'll end up doing sooner or later despite all the barriers that some politicians are trying to raise between us."},{"id":12937562,"author":"tonyedgecombe","month":"2016-11","story":"Millions of ordinary Americans support Donald Trump. Here's why","stance":"reference","why":"Example of modern manufacturing needing few workers.","quote":"This is what modern manufacturing looks like, note there aren't many people working there.","text":"America is the 11th richest nation in the world (per-capita), if they wanted to find the money they could do it right now. I'm not saying America doesn't have a balance of trade problem, I just think people put too much faith in a resolution to that. This [VIDEO] is what modern manufacturing looks like, note there aren't many people working there."},{"id":11532785,"author":"justsaysmthng","month":"2016-04","story":"BMW Loses Core Development Team of Its I3 and I8 Electric Vehicle Line","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in an edit to the comment about BMW i3.","quote":"","text":"Sensationalist clickbait title. There's mention of 4 i-series executives leaving (BMW has over 100,000 employees), which is somewhat expected, given that the article mentions that i sales haven't been stellar. True, I haven't seen too many i models on the streets, but I haven't seen too many of competitor's electric models either. So the market is still not quite ready for them and the manufacturers, I guess, are wary of betting it all on the electric card. Of course there'll be people moving from company to company, just like people from Apple move to Google and vice-versa and it's normal, but the tone of this article is like \"BMW is collapsing and everyone is running for their lives... to China\". Why is this even in the news ? Press is so unreliable and sensationalist these days .. Or has it always been like this ? Edit: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["uDr4L6BzpP8",1]]},"sJFnWZH5FXc":{"wy":"Cited as the go-to demonstration of how hard and clever Atari 2600 graphics programming was.","q":{"x":"Here’s a detailed video by retro game mechanics that shows how the logo graphics of the infamous E.T. game is rendered","i":34384232,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34384232,"author":"ant6n","month":"2023-01","story":"Atari 2600 Hardware Design: Making Something Out of (Almost) Nothing","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed look at how E.T. logo graphics are rendered on Atari","quote":"Here’s a detailed video by retro game mechanics that shows how the logo graphics of the infamous E.T. game is rendered","text":"Here’s a detailed video by retro game mechanics that shows how the logo graphics of the infamous E.T. game is rendered: [VIDEO] It seems incredible, very difficult. And remember, back then cartridges only had 8KB of memory. This means even more hacks are required, if your game needs more than a few levels. Theres a recurring hn post how jungle hunt encoded levels in a single byte (bitfield), then used some sort of sequence generator to allow going back and forth among the 255 levels."},{"id":32260881,"author":"h3mb3","month":"2022-07","story":"The man who made the “worst” video game in history (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates how nightmarish Atari 2600 graphics programming was","quote":"has a good demonstration [2] on how the graphics programming seemed like a nightmare","text":"Keeping in mind how ridiculously primitive and cumbersome Atari 2600 was to program on, I think it's actually remarkable how functional E.T. is (adding in the time pressure and all). I never got around to read Racing the Beam [1] but Retro Game Mechanics Explained on YouTube has a good demonstration [2] on how the graphics programming seemed like a nightmare compared to later computers and game consoles. Just positioning things on screen required calculating machine cycles of other preceeding instructions. Refactoring must've been fun. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44015834,"author":"dejobaan","month":"2025-05","story":"Palette lighting tricks on the Nintendo 64","stance":"reference","why":"How much Atari 2600 games depended on racing the beam","quote":"What I didn't know until recently was how much Atari 2600's games owed to this kinda of craziness","text":"While I'm really happy we have faster systems now, there was something fun about about having to subvert constraints in games, and so satisfying and lovely when you did it right. HN folks are probably familiar with raster interrupts ( [LINK] ) and \"racing the beam.\" I always associated this with the Atari 800. You weren't \"supposed\" to be able to do stuff like [LINK] , but Display List Interrupts made that possible. What I didn't know until recently was how much Atari 2600's games owed to this kinda of craziness: [VIDEO] It's stuff like this that makes me think that if hardware stopped advancing, we'd still be able to figure out more and more interesting stuff for decades!"},{"id":42453765,"author":"zackmorris","month":"2024-12","story":"Lou's Pseudo 3D Page (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"Atari toggling colors as the beam sweeps to draw sprites","quote":"","text":"Kinda cool, I've never seen these techniques all in one place before. Growing up, I thought that SNES mode 7 scaling was so cool: [LINK] The article mentions about halfway down the page that what made the 80s road rendering technique possible was racing the beam. Where say an Atari 2600 would toggle the color at certain pixel counts as the TV's electron beam swept the screen, producing graphics that seemed otherwise impossible from such underpowered hardware: [LINK] [VIDEO] Some engines allowed for say 8 hardcoded sprites this way by toggling colors at each sprite's position, with various rules about overlapping, so sprites would flicker sometimes when they were next to each other."},{"id":39526312,"author":"derefr","month":"2024-02","story":"Insecure Features in PDFs (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Well, if you think it's possible, try coming up with even the core of an architectural basis for 1. a declarative language for describing the same things PostScript describes, 2. which allows the rasterization of arbitrary shapes at arbitrary DPI (PostScript is DPI-oblivious — it's up to the printer what DPI it's printing at!); 3. and which also works for vector plotters , that will never rasterize the data you're sending at all, but will actually follow the bezier curves, like the 2D version of 3D-printer GCODE; 4. and which enables the implementation of this rasterization and/or plotting on a variety of affordable hardware architectures in the 1980s — where the 1980s was a time where CPU power wasn't too expensive, but where memory prices were at an absolute premium. So your printer might have had a CPU as powerful as your computer's in it, to crunch PostScript — but definitely wouldn't have had the memory to buffer a full rasterized page. --- To put that last constraint another way: PostScript was designed to be rasterized in a way that enabled printers to do something much akin to \"Racing the Beam\" ( [VIDEO] ). In both the display-rendering and printing cases, this was done in 1980s hardware, because 1980s memory was too expensive for most systems to be dedicating it to hold a buffer to asynchronously pre-render into and then read from when drawing. So instead, in both cases, you must render+rasterize in one motion, programmatically and extremely efficiently. And the obvi"}],"r":[["Ho_CFfdsmc8",1],["roBkg-iPrbw",1]]},"x_yHtfGH0nI":{"wy":"Recommended as a great look at how thoroughly airliners are inspected and rebuilt, and cited in aviation maintenance discussions.","q":{"x":"The BBC has a great documentary on a BA 747-400 D-check that has been posted on Youtube","i":4605995,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":12477168,"author":"ubernostrum","month":"2016-09","story":"Rolls-Royce’s single-crystal turbine blade casting foundry","stance":"rec","why":"Pop-sci look at a 747 taken apart and reassembled in heavy maintenance.","quote":"Another favorite aviation-related one, though more pop-sci-oriented, is this look at a 747 going through a D (\"heavy maintenance\") check","text":"Another favorite aviation-related one, though more pop-sci-oriented, is this look at a 747 going through a D (\"heavy maintenance\") check that basically takes most of the plane apart and puts it back together, covering all the bits and how they need to work: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4605995,"author":"maximilianburke","month":"2012-10","story":"Discovery Channel's Inside a Jet Crash","stance":"rec","why":"BBC documentary on a 747-400 D-check, the heaviest maintenance inspection.","quote":"The BBC has a great documentary on a BA 747-400 D-check that has been posted on Youtube","text":"Other factors that make aircraft like this one dirt cheap are that older aircraft (especially the 727) are very loud which makes it difficult to use them in commercial service to areas with noise restrictions. Hush kits are available but they're expensive and don't quiet the aircraft enough to operate some sensitive jurisdictions. If the aircraft is also near the point where it has to undergo a D-check -- the most expensive kind of maintenance check where the aircraft is very nearly completely disassembled, inspected, and re-assembled -- it would make more economic sense to sell it for scrap than it would to continue operation. The BBC has a great documentary on a BA 747-400 D-check that has been posted on Youtube: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15936743,"author":"chiph","month":"2017-12","story":"German military kicks off heavy lift helicopter competition","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the thoroughness of 747 D-check inspections.","quote":"They inspect pretty near every part on a 747 during a D check.","text":"They inspect pretty near every part on a 747 during a D check. Including the toilets. [VIDEO] So far as the HH-53 (the USAF version), when I rode one in the 80's, the saying was that if it wasn't dripping hydraulic fluid, there was something wrong."}],"r":[["r8RxkpUvxK0",1]]},"zuKV2DI9-Jg":{"wy":"HN points to it as the short cliff's-notes version of the essay and as a charming introduction to The School of Life.","q":{"x":"If you want the cliff's notes, his School of Life channel has a shorter version [1].","i":43848842,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43848842,"author":"Nzen","month":"2025-04","story":"You Wouldn't Download a Hacker News","stance":"rec","why":"Four-minute short version of the essay.","quote":"If you want the cliff's notes, his School of Life channel has a shorter version [1].","text":"Alain de Botton also published this in video form, seven years ago [0]. If you want the cliff's notes, his School of Life channel has a shorter version [1]. [0] [LINK] 22 minutes [1] [VIDEO] 4 minutess"},{"id":11809997,"author":"sw00","month":"2016-05","story":"Why You Will Marry the Wrong Person","stance":"rec","why":"Short video version of the essay.","quote":"The author founded The School of Life - which I think is wonderful.","text":"It's the long form of this: [VIDEO] The author founded The School of Life - which I think is wonderful."},{"id":41051556,"author":"gabrielsroka","month":"2024-07","story":"Why You Will Marry the Wrong Person (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"School of Life video by the same author.","quote":"also 2014","text":"2017 Followup [LINK] EDIT: I thought his voice sounded familiar: > The School of Life was founded in 2008 by Alain De Botton... [LINK] also 2014 [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Dp-mQ3HxgDE",1],["KRvv0QdruMQ",1],["JTfhYyTuT44",1]]},"4To-F6W1NT0":{"wy":"HN uses it as the key reference in arguments about Micay's conduct and GrapheneOS leadership, with some defending it and some urging skepticism.","q":{"x":"You literally have the GOS developer messages coming in live while he rehashes frivolous accusations","i":44686878,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44686878,"author":"bernoufakis","month":"2025-07","story":"Graphene OS: a security-enhanced Android build","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Defends the video against claims it is baseless","quote":"You literally have the GOS developer messages coming in live while he rehashes frivolous accusations","text":"> It's objectively clear though, that this is a very low quality video full of baseless speculation, and severely lacking any technical understanding and knowledge. \"Baseless\" could not be further away from the truth. You literally have the GOS developer messages coming in live while he rehashes frivolous accusations and threatening to exposing him. To claim objectivity, when you seem to cherry pick the parts of the video that would (loosely) fit your narrative. Where is your evidence that Rossmann is in anyway associated to harassment campaign against the project ? > This is exactly the same as going to a restaurant, having an argument with the owner, and then claiming that they might be putting poison in the food, even though there's absolutely zero evidence or anything that might indicate that, solely because you had a disagreement with someone and now want to harm their reputation. Damn, so close, you were almost there. A more accurate analogy you could have come up with, had you actually critically listened to Rossmann's argument in his video. Yes, it's like going to a restaurant and having a disagreement with the cook, for the latter to explicitly threaten to harm onto you. At that point, is it that far fetched to think he might poison the food ? When you know he has full control over the kitchen ? You can disagree with Rossmann perception of the actual threat, but you should at least admit that it is not absurd for Rossmann to think that someone who demonstrated such i"},{"id":48608326,"author":"oceanhaiyang","month":"2026-06","story":"GrapheneOS has been ported to Android 17 and official releases are coming soon","stance":"reference","why":"Video discussing examples of Micay's conduct","quote":"I believe this video discusses some of these","text":"Apologies, I didn’t catch this reply. I believe this video discusses some of these, I’ll have to scroll through Mastodon to find more. [VIDEO] Edit examples: I’m referring to Daniel Micay using the official account to fight other people. Seems I’m not up to date, he supposedly “stepped down” from fighting others, [LINK] I firmly recall within the past two years witnessing similar actions but I’ll have to keep searching"},{"id":47851005,"author":"Springtime","month":"2026-04","story":"Original GrapheneOS responses to WIRED fact checker","stance":"reference","why":"Context on Micay's rant leading Rossmann to stop using it","quote":"Also went on long rant at Louis Rossmann[2] in an very knee-jerk tone","text":"More context on experiences with Micay[1]. Also went on long rant at Louis Rossmann[2] in an very knee-jerk tone, which led Rossmann to stop using it despite being a long-term advocate for GOS, due to trust issues. Likewise I don't doubt they're talented. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44363073,"author":"onli","month":"2025-06","story":"Fairphone 6 is switching to a new design that's even more sustainable","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as reason not to trust the developer","quote":"","text":"Why would you call the Fairphone an insecure device? It has long update support and can re-lock the bootloader, which is like the one criteria ROMs like that pick as the security feature. What is your attack scenario, what your security criteria here? microG is not a drawback, it's a proper FOSS implementation, which I vastly prefer to running Gapps in a sandbox. App compatibility has been perfect for me. The main criteria for it to be strictly better is that you do not give root to a dev that hallucinates enemies and then send their goons to attack them. See [VIDEO] . It's nice that Graphene has a hardened kernel, that helps nothing if you can't trust the developer - different attack scenarios."},{"id":44365722,"author":"mhitza","month":"2025-06","story":"Fairphone 6 is switching to a new design that's even more sustainable","stance":"reference","why":"Similar disagreement pattern with project owner","quote":"","text":"> They then started making up stories about me and throwing around unfounded accusations. That sounds oddly similar with Louis Rossmann's video when in disagreement with some (the?) project owner. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39672645,"author":"joemazerino","month":"2024-03","story":"GrapheneOS finds Bluetooth memory corruption via ARM MTE","stance":"reference","why":"Told to watch for reference on what Rossmann said","quote":"Watch his video for reference.","text":"Rossman did nothing of the sort. Watch his video for reference. [VIDEO] What Rossman referred to is easily revealed in this very thread from Daniel's comments. I refuse to use GrapheneOS because of Daniel's behavior. He has attacked me personally on this site multiple times."},{"id":39724752,"author":"onli","month":"2024-03","story":"Mozilla will be retiring the Mozilla Location Service","stance":"reference","why":"Reason to avoid the project and foundation","quote":"","text":"Fuck. I just last year integrated this in a project of mine. Thinking: \"Yeah, Google fucked me over here before when I used their, but it's Mozilla, it will be fine.\" It is not okay to stop providing a service like this. Not if you are Mozilla, with that mission and money, not something that is so deeply entrenched. Though, reading it more calmly, I can see how them being hit by a patent troll can make their investment in this project untenable. There is a comment about the Graphene Foundation maybe providing an alternative. That would be great - but having [VIDEO] in mind, with that maintainer still active the project and foundation is a complete no-go. The organic maps comment is the one to follow instead."},{"id":38563873,"author":"onli","month":"2023-12","story":"Fairphone 5 scores a perfect 10 from iFixit for repairability","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence of paranoid-seeming developer behavior","quote":"Behavior akin to a paranoid delusion.","text":"Behavior akin to a paranoid delusion. See [VIDEO] . The developer is gone, so no need to avoid the project completely, but CalyxOS is an interesting alternative anyway."},{"id":38128417,"author":"jchw","month":"2023-11","story":"Enable MTE on Pixel 8","stance":"reference","why":"Video felt damning about project leadership","quote":"I remember Louis Rossmann posting a video[1] that felt pretty damning regarding project leadership","text":"Speaking of which, what ever happened with all of the kerfuffle going on around GrapheneOS leadership, anyway? I remember Louis Rossmann posting a video[1] that felt pretty damning regarding project leadership, especially given that he seemed to be implicated in the mess for almost no reason at all. In general I wouldn't give a good god damn about drama, but I'll admit it: it's ALREADY hard to trust operating systems, and especially security software, that's effectively made by effectively random strangers on the Internet, but much worse so if it's unclear they wouldn't be willing to abuse their position. Daniel Micay (aka strcat on here I believe) apparently announced they were stepping down from leadership roles in the project shortly after, according to tweets that appear to now be deleted, but that Wikipedia has links to an archive of.[2] At least from the code side of things though, they are still working quite actively.[3] Which is not a bad thing, but it doesn't tell us much about what happened. What I'd hope to hear is that Daniel Micay is in better mental health, but they made the almost-certainly wise choice to stop engaging with social media, and I can't find much information about what actually went down, so I guess it's a question mark. This is unfortunate because as I understand it, the closest competitor to GrapheneOS, CalyxOS, is not nearly as sophisticated. I'd really prefer to just be able to trust GrapheneOS, but I'm left absolutely unsure about what even h"},{"id":38413583,"author":"summm","month":"2023-11","story":"DDG founder says Google's phone, manufacturing partnerships thwart competition","stance":"reference","why":"Example of similar pattern of behavior","quote":"","text":"The usual: Voice some opinion that doesn't exactly match theirs, Ina friendly way, get insulted, get accused to be part of another group that allegedly is conspiring to harm them... Later found this is apparently a pattern and happened to others in a very similar way, e.g. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37774831,"author":"ColoursofOSINT","month":"2023-10","story":"Pixel 8 Pro","stance":"reference","why":"Proof of Micay's inappropriate behavior toward Rossmann","quote":"Louis Rossman was a supporter of the project until he and Daniel had a failing out","text":"I use GrapheneOS, and I've donated to its development. To say that \"None of the claims are true\" and that they are \"baseless\" is incorrect. Louis Rossman was a supporter of the project until he and Daniel had a failing out in which Daniel behaved inappropriately [1], all over a Youtube comment. There's proof for that and other claims [2, 4, 5]. Daniel can be a talented developer, privacy advocate and asshole at the same time. It's sad to see him (and others, like yourself) say stuff like \"examine the baseless claims\", without providing any sources. After all the drama with Calyxos, Techlore, Louis Rossmann etc, at some point one has to notice a pattern of behavior. 1: [VIDEO] 2: [LINK] 3: [LINK] 4: [LINK] 5: [LINK]"},{"id":36148780,"author":"neom","month":"2023-06","story":"Lead Developer Leaves GrapheneOS Due to Harassment Including Swatting","stance":"reference","why":"Background found while researching the controversy","quote":"googling around a bit lead me to this Louis Rossmann video","text":"I'd never heard of GrapheneOS till 15 minutes ago. I couldn't understand why someone would randomly start swatting an OSS project maintainer.. googling around a bit lead me to this Louis Rossmann video: [VIDEO] Also this: [LINK] Yikes. Hope this Daniel guy can get some help and find some peace, poor dude seems incredibly unstable. :("},{"id":36150298,"author":"jacooper","month":"2023-06","story":"Lead Developer Leaves GrapheneOS Due to Harassment Including Swatting","stance":"reference","why":"Background on conflict with GrapheneOS lead","quote":"For a background on this, have a look in louis's latest video","text":"Finally. Daniel created a great project, then did everything to make people not use it. Any criticism, harassment You don't believe his story with copperhead?, Harassment. And so on. You can already see his community fans with new accounts on HN saying that Louis conspired against Daniel, he didn't need to do anything , Daniel did this to himself. For a background on this, have a look in louis's latest video [VIDEO]"},{"id":36089486,"author":"sremani","month":"2023-05","story":"Daniel Micay publicly steps down from GrapheneOS","stance":"reference","why":"Rossmann's account of his interaction with Micay","quote":"The following is link to Louis Rossmann's video about his interaction with Daniel published today.","text":"I am not sure, but some context about Daniel's failings in communication may have contributed. The following is link to Louis Rossmann's video about his interaction with Daniel published today. [VIDEO] This is speculation of course on my part and I do not have any evidence connecting the video with the event itself."},{"id":36090495,"author":"joemazerino","month":"2023-05","story":"Daniel Micay publicly steps down from GrapheneOS","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence of Micay's behavior","quote":"I completely understand where Louis Rossman is coming from.","text":"I completely understand where Louis Rossman is coming from. [1] Daniel does not apologize for anything he says or does and refuses to acknowledge any wrongdoing. He still refuses to acknowledge that how he responded to Louis was wrong. He hops on every thread of GrapheneOS and regularly accuses everyone of being sockpuppets and trolls. [2] He has accused me of being a Techlore and CalyxOS troll numerous times. Worst still, his community are full of psychophants that enable his abusive behavior. They refuse to see him or his outbursts as negative. They routinely collaborate to downvote comments and raid rooms to manage the narrative which I find to be underhanded. I was an early Copperhead customer who was very disappointed that he got up and destroyed the signing keys without providing any evidence to back his claims up. When I brought up the issue to him via email, his response was to throw his previous business partner under the bus without offering any apologies for damaging my business. I did use Android Hardening afterwards, used CopperheadOS for a bit and then moved back to LineageOS. I refuse to use any of his projects again. I wish the best for him and hope he gets well. He's not mentally stable enough to run a project and he has a long way to go for understanding the nuance of interpersonal relationships. 1 - [VIDEO] 2 - [LINK]"},{"id":36114911,"author":"theFrequency","month":"2023-05","story":"Daniel Micay publicly steps down from GrapheneOS","stance":"critical","why":"Linked as the video to rewatch skeptically","quote":"i urge you to rewatch the original rossmann video with a sober eye.","text":"It's pretty clear to me that Rossmann decided he would go after Micay to protect himself in some way. Louis has openly and proudly stated he has \"no patience\" for anyone with mental issues that aren't \"putting in enough effort to be normal\". he's made videos about this and commented to this effect. I believe Rossmann grew fustrated with Micay at some point and then decided to bait him into a reaction so he could take him out of context to.. i dont know get back at him? Its not very clear to me exactly why he would do this.. but i urge you to rewatch the original rossmann video with a sober eye. [VIDEO] then watch this one [LINK]"}]},"C6nAxiym9oc":{"wy":"After the FTX collapse, commenters cited it as an early red flag, though others disliked the YouTuber's style.","q":{"x":"The best video I've seen is SBF basically admitting it was a ponzi scheme","i":33635394,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33635394,"author":"dmix","month":"2022-11","story":"FTX's collapse strands scientists","stance":"strong_rec","why":"SBF effectively admitting a Ponzi structure on a podcast.","quote":"The best video I've seen is SBF basically admitting it was a ponzi scheme","text":"The best video I've seen is SBF basically admitting it was a ponzi scheme: [VIDEO] Selling something with no value other than the attention it gets on Twitter and the reputation it gets via reputable investors. What gets 'reputable investors' to invest in the scheme is the most interesting part of this story, if you at all care about stopping it. Something that has existed long before crypto."},{"id":38126437,"author":"olalonde","month":"2023-11","story":"Sam Bankman-Fried found guilty on all charges","stance":"reference","why":"Interview given months before FTX collapse.","quote":"Here's an interview he gave a few months before the FTX collapse","text":"Maybe not the mainstream media but I feel like a lot people, especially in the Bitcoin community, were suspicious of him. He just screamed grifter to me. I never really bought his arbitrage story either and wouldn't be surprised if that was not his first scam. Here's an interview he gave a few months before the FTX collapse: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33910842,"author":"TheLoafOfBread","month":"2022-12","story":"Everything SBF is doing is in singular pursuit of not going to jail","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier flag that SBF and FTX resembled a Ponzi scheme.","quote":"he actually pointed that SBF and FTX is doing Ponzi scheme 7 months ago.","text":"I did not know about him before this blow up, but looking into his history, he actually pointed that SBF and FTX is doing Ponzi scheme 7 months ago. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34097815,"author":"tibbydudeza","month":"2022-12","story":"FTX founder Sam Bankman-Fried to be released on $250M bail","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Did people actually listen to him whenever he spoke","text":"SBF just played along with the sheer insanity of the concept of crypto (nothing can make nothing) and all the fools who gave him money - it seems that common financial sense is not taught to people anymore or is really in short supply these days. Did people actually listen to him whenever he spoke - whenever did any of his BS make any financial sense ???. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33533291,"author":"lizknope","month":"2022-11","story":"In 2021, FTX had a funding round of $420.69M","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Just listen to Sam Bankman-Fraud on a financial podcast describe his magical ponzi box.","text":"Yes it is all made up. Or about 95% made up with a tiny amount of actual USD or Euros Just listen to Sam Bankman-Fraud on a financial podcast describe his magical ponzi box. Well now the thing anyone with a brain would have said sounds like a scam has been exposed as a scam. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33602849,"author":"mfkp","month":"2022-11","story":"FTX’s balance sheet was bad","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"there were plenty of red flags back then","text":"Matt Levine (article's author) did an interview with SBF about 6 months ago and there were plenty of red flags back then (mentioned in article in section about yield farming): [VIDEO]"},{"id":33618852,"author":"JimmyRuska","month":"2022-11","story":"The fall of Sam Bankman-Fried is crypto’s Enron moment","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"SBF describes coins like a ponzi with no shame","text":"Pretty unfair to CZ. FTX was using FTT as an infinite money glitch. Why would CZ hold a coin that has a strong likelihood of going to 0? [LINK] The balance sheet showed billions of dollars in assets for coins that had tens of millions in liquidity. There's an 8 billion dollar hole in the balance sheet. It's pretty clear they used customer deposits to pay back lenders. Why would CZ buy a company that's nearly an Enron level fraud, and due for a criminal investigation. [LINK] SBF describes coins like a ponzi with no shame [VIDEO] This was Alameda CEO [LINK] [LINK] Meanwhile, after 1 - 2bn in customer funds are missing. Everyone is asking Sam to say what happened. Sam proceeds to troll by writing the words \"What happened\" one letter at a time, with an Elizabeth Holmes-like legal disclaimer that he's not going to recall any of the details."},{"id":33619028,"author":"idatum","month":"2022-11","story":"The fall of Sam Bankman-Fried is crypto’s Enron moment","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I saw everything I hate about many youtubers' style, generally.","text":"> SBF describes coins like a ponzi with no shame [VIDEO] Just offering that just a few minutes into that video, I saw everything I hate about many youtubers' style, generally. I can't describe it, but there it is. I listen to Odd Lots. Generally, that podcast keeps things thoughtfully presented. I remember that episode and it reinforced distrust of crypto."},{"id":33625579,"author":"notch656a","month":"2022-11","story":"Genesis' Crypto-Lending Unit Is Suspending Withdrawals in Wake of FTX Collapse","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Never underestimate the number of stupid people out there","text":"Not really. It's been done time and time again. Never underestimate the number of stupid people out there, desperate to break even with inflation somehow while their banks pay them negative interest rates. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33689299,"author":"dotnet00","month":"2022-11","story":"While crypto bro scammed clients, reporters scammed readers","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"He behaves like a child when asked simple questions about crypto","text":"Based on this [VIDEO] it wasn't even anywhere near that well thought out with SBF. He behaves like a child when asked simple questions about crypto, trying to use double speak to weasel his way out but not even the way a typical CEO or politician might do it. I think SBF's trick was to just demonize regular finance and to treat all criticism as simply FUD. IIRC they also paid influencers a pretty penny to promote them. It's an extremely common tactic in crypto, basically every crypto scam covered on that channel involves a similar cult of personality being setup as a cover for the scam."},{"id":31738376,"author":"ethanbond","month":"2022-06","story":"Coinbase Announces 18% Layoffs","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"here’s fastest way to get to the relevant content","text":"What do you make of Sam Bankman-Fried describing his business as a literal Ponzi scheme on Odd Lots podcast? The commentary isn’t necessary to see the insanity but here’s fastest way to get to the relevant content: [VIDEO] Edit: To clarify, though not sure how much a mitigation this is, yes SBF is describing much of the underlying activity he takes commission on as a Ponzi scheme. Sure, he’s not operating the Ponzi itself, just keeping it operational and profiting from its continued operation. Mea culpa."},{"id":31467084,"author":"lizknope","month":"2022-05","story":"Sam Bankman-Fried Described Yield Farming and Left Matt Levine Stunned","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The way he describes the box and how it isn't a Ponzi scheme is just ridiculous","text":"This video was from a month ago and is about the same podcast with Sam Bankman-Fried. The way he describes the box and how it isn't a Ponzi scheme is just ridiculous Crypto CEO Accidentally Describes Ponzi Scheme [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["3CWCqJl0BEs",1],["rOwMW6agpTI",1],["YThelfB2fvg",1],["2Op3QLzMgSY",1],["NQFqDKRAROI",1],["O4Ko7NW2yQo",1],["f7MzFfuNOtY",1]]},"RVU62CQTXFI":{"wy":"HN cites it as a vivid record of how alien personal computing and hypertext sounded to a skeptical public, and as a favorite Nelson interview.","q":{"x":"My favorite Ted Nelson interview","i":16226304,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16226304,"author":"dangirsh","month":"2018-01","story":"Ted Nelson on What Modern Programmers Can Learn from the Past [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Nelson's prophetic vision met with intense skepticism","quote":"My favorite Ted Nelson interview","text":"My favorite Ted Nelson interview: [VIDEO] It gives a great perspective on Ted's prophetic vision for computers as well as the intense skepticism it raised in many."},{"id":40998473,"author":"superphunthyme","month":"2024-07","story":"The Objects of Our Life (1983)","stance":"reference","why":"Nelson discusses libraries, catalogs and tagging with skeptical interviewer","quote":"It's got the skeptical interviewer, and at 9:56 (with some lead-in before) there is a discussion of libraries, catalogs, tagging, etc.","text":"This one with Ted Nelson? [VIDEO] It's got the skeptical interviewer, and at 9:56 (with some lead-in before) there is a discussion of libraries, catalogs, tagging, etc. Edit: and then he goes on to mention Dewey Decimal as well."},{"id":22524066,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2020-03","story":"The Call of the Ocean: Hypertext, Universal and Open (1988)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Ted Nelson: Computer Lib / Dream Machines (1975) [pdf] (worrydream.com) [LINK] [LINK] For what it's worth, YC is helping Ted Nelson sell his \"Computer Lib / Dream Machines\" book: [LINK] This book is a truly unique and is worth owning in hardcopy format. [LINK] Ted versus The Media Lab [video] (youtube.com) [LINK] [LINK] Interview with Ted Nelson (notion.so) [LINK] [LINK] Ted Nelson on What Modern Programmers Can Learn from the Past [video] (ieee.org) [LINK] [LINK] Ted Nelson struggles with uncomprehending radio interviewer (1979) [audio] (youtube.com) [LINK] [VIDEO] Ted Nelson’s published papers on computers and interaction, 1965 to 1977 (archive.org) [LINK] [LINK] Ask HN: What is the best resource for understanding Ted Nelson's ZigZag? [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Alan Kay's tribute to Ted Nelson at \"Intertwingled\" Fest (how the script of Tron was the first movie script to ever be edited by a word processing program, on the Alto computer) [LINK] \"Silicon Valley Story\" — a Very Short Romantic Comedy by Ted Nelson A playful story about the microcircuitry of love, with Ted Nelson as an absentminded genius, featuring Doug Engelbart as Ted's father and Stewart Brand as the villainous CEO. Closing song: \"Information Flow\", sung by Donna Spitzer and the auteur. With Timothy Leary as the Good Venture Capitalist! [LINK] Ted Nelson's Channel [LINK]"},{"id":19951109,"author":"abtinf","month":"2019-05","story":"‘Knitting Is Coding’ and Yarn Is Programmable in This Physics Lab","stance":"reference","why":"Nelson remarks on knitting as a popular programming language","quote":"Ted Nelson pointed out that knitting (or was it weaving?) is one of the most popular programming languages back in 1979.","text":"Ted Nelson pointed out that knitting (or was it weaving?) is one of the most popular programming languages back in 1979. [VIDEO] Edit to add: I wouldn’t be surprised if there are more knitting programmers today than all computer programming languages combined. Just the size and pervasive presence of Walmart or Joann’s yarn and fabric sections makes me almost certain."},{"id":15258393,"author":"drtse4","month":"2017-09","story":"“There Is No Reason for Any Individual to Have a Computer in Their Home”","stance":"reference","why":"1979 radio interview where Nelson pitches home computing to skeptic","quote":"a radio interview where Ted Nelson tries to convince, unsuccessfully, a skeptical radio host that home computing has a future","text":"From the same period, a radio interview where Ted Nelson tries to convince, unsuccessfully, a skeptical radio host that home computing has a future: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15259573,"author":"tudorw","month":"2017-09","story":"“There Is No Reason for Any Individual to Have a Computer in Their Home”","stance":"reference","why":"Journalist struggling to grasp the computer as creative tool","quote":"here's a journalist in 1979 struggling to comprehend Ted Nelson's vision","text":"As an adjunct, here's a journalist in 1979 struggling to comprehend Ted Nelson's vision that the computer would become a creative tool for the masses, [VIDEO]"},{"id":28248239,"author":"shawnz","month":"2021-08","story":"Dumb Phone","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The author's arguments kind of remind me of those of the interviewer","text":"The author's arguments kind of remind me of those of the interviewer in this 1979 clip with Ted Nelson: [VIDEO]"}]},"i4pxw4tYeCU":{"wy":"HN links it as an entertaining, in-depth rebuttal to 5G health fears from an electrical engineer.","q":{"x":"ElectroBoom has a great video that goes in depth about the electromagnetic spectrum","i":23399463,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23399463,"author":"yabones","month":"2020-06","story":"Bell, Telus to use Nokia and Ericsson, not Huawei, in building their 5G networks","stance":"strong_rec","why":"In-depth, entertaining explanation of the EM spectrum and how it affects humans.","quote":"ElectroBoom has a great video that goes in depth about the electromagnetic spectrum","text":"ElectroBoom has a great video that goes in depth about the electromagnetic spectrum, what 'Ionizing Radiation' is, and how EM interacts with humans, and is entertaining enough to keep you watching. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39857005,"author":"1letterunixname","month":"2024-03","story":"We made a hot dog talk with RF [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among related classic electronics videos debunking 5G fears.","quote":"ElectroBOOM proves 5G isn't going to give you cancer or eat your children","text":"Related classics: (2019) BigClive overclocks a 120v Presto hot dogger to 250v [LINK] (2019) Veratasium grape microwave plasma [LINK] (2022) BigClive The dangers of microwave transformers [LINK] (2016) BigClive Electrocution risk of cheap USB chargers [LINK] (2020) ElectroBOOM proves 5G isn't going to give you cancer or eat your children [VIDEO]"},{"id":30445444,"author":"vkou","month":"2022-02","story":"FAA says 5G could impact radio altimeters on most Boeing 737s","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the well-understood risks of non-ionizing EM waves.","quote":"Because it consists of non-ionizing EM waves, the risks of which are very well understood.","text":"> Why do we even need 5G it seems like there’s always something, whether real or tinfoil, risks and unknown risks, so at one point do we decide that what we have is good enough? Because it consists of non-ionizing EM waves, the risks of which are very well understood. [VIDEO] And if you're concerned about the amount of non-ionizing EM waves in your environment from these 50 wi-fi networks, you should never go outside, since the sun is constantly blasting you with ~1,000,0000 GHZ-frequency electromagnetic waves, at an energy flux of ~1300 watts/square meter. It's half a dozen orders of magnitude more[1] energy than those fifty wi-fi networks are putting out. They aren't even a rounding error. The only thing the 5G 'controversy' reveals is that the public is happy to listen to people who don't know anything, as long as they are being told what they want to hear. [1] Not just more energy, more dangerous energy. Solar radiation, unlike 5G, is actually ionizing. Those radiation waves carry enough energy to break molecular bonds in your body."},{"id":23592771,"author":"the_pwner224","month":"2020-06","story":"Wireless Is a Trap","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as supporting evidence that WiFi power levels are harmless.","quote":"But BT/WiFi ain't gonna do shit to you.","text":"The sun beams down 1 KILOwatt of light (50% infrared, 40% visible, 10% other) to every square meter on the ground. Most of the infrared and much of the visible light is absorbed by your skin. Your WiFi router on the other hand has a power of 50-150 MILLIwatts, and won't let you set it higher because that's illegal. And your phone etc. run at around 15 milliwatts. I guess if you built a ridiculously high powered WiFi antenna (which would be illegal) and stood next to it for a while, you would cook yourself. But BT/WiFi ain't gonna do shit to you. [VIDEO]"}]},"mfv0V1SxbNA":{"wy":"HN mines it for Torvalds's opinions, such as ECC RAM, AI hype, and his desktop setup, and as a rare long informal interview.","q":{"x":"Great episode","i":46762099,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":46762099,"author":"andromaton","month":"2026-01","story":"What Happens If Torvalds' Git Repo Goes Away?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Torvalds on what happens to Linux if he dies.","quote":"Great episode","text":"Elijah Sandiford at Linus Tech Tips asking Linus Torvalds what happens to Linux if Linus dies. Recorded around September 2025. Aired in November. Plan made in the 2025 Maintainers Summit. Great episode: [VIDEO]"},{"id":49227009,"author":"ciupicri","month":"2026-08","story":"Monitors for Work","stance":"reference","why":"Torvalds's build uses a 6K 31.5 inch monitor.","quote":"And 6K 31.5\" is good for Linus / Linux [1] :-)","text":"And 6K 31.5\" is good for Linus / Linux [1] :-) More specifically the ASUS ProArt PA32QCV 31.5\" [2]. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":47723015,"author":"dataviz1000","month":"2026-04","story":"AI assistance when contributing to the Linux kernel","stance":"reference","why":"Torvalds discusses AI in kernel development.","quote":"This is discussed in the Linus vs Linus interview","text":"This is discussed in the Linus vs Linus interview, \"Building the PERFECT Linux PC with Linus Torvalds\". [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47532467,"author":"wyre","month":"2026-03","story":"Intel Announces Arc Pro B70 and Arc Pro B65 GPUs","stance":"reference","why":"Torvalds build included an Intel Arc card.","quote":"LTT built a computer for Linus Torvalds and they put an Intel Arc card inside","text":"There was the video a little while back where LTT built a computer for Linus Torvalds and they put an Intel Arc card inside, so I'd imagine Linux support is at the very least, acceptable. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46108909,"author":"hylaride","month":"2025-12","story":"1GB Raspberry Pi 5, and memory-driven price rises","stance":"reference","why":"Torvalds insists on ECC RAM for stability.","quote":"Torvalds was convinced that memory errors are a much greater problem for stability than otherwise posted","text":"Linus Torvalds was recently on Linux Tech Tips to build a new computer and he insisted on ECC RAM. Torvalds was convinced that memory errors are a much greater problem for stability than otherwise posted and he's spent an inordinate amount of time chasing phantom bugs because of it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46132790,"author":"littlecranky67","month":"2025-12","story":"IBM CEO says there is 'no way' spending on AI data centers will pay off","stance":"reference","why":"Torvalds comments on AI bubble.","quote":"My latest favorite \"We are in an AI bubble\" comment comes from Linus Torvalds himself","text":"There are so many CEOs, tech experts, financial analysts and famous investors who say we are in an AI bubble - even AI-invested companies say that about themselves. My latest favorite \"We are in an AI bubble\" comment comes from Linus Torvalds himself in the video with Linus from Linus Tech Tipps [0] [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46143086,"author":"komali2","month":"2025-12","story":"Valve reveals it’s the architect behind a push to bring Windows games to Arm","stance":"reference","why":"Torvalds on regretting nothing but not knowing the workload.","quote":"if I knew how much work this was going to take, I never would have started this project.","text":"> and that’s when the Fex compatibility layer was started, because we knew there was close to a decade of work needed before it would be robust enough people could rely on it for their libraries. In a recent Linus Tech Tips video, Linus Torvalds (original Linus) was asked, \"if you could go back in time and start the Linux project from scratch, what would you do differently?\" He had two answers, one was \"nothing,\" and the other was \"if I knew how much work this was going to take, I never would have started this project.\" It makes me wonder, is there some kind of blissful ignorance required to kick off a project that will take you years to see through? How many times have I self limited myself, stopped myself from starting something, because I put on my lead hat and did some estimations and thought eh, not worth it? [VIDEO]"},{"id":46177732,"author":"Retr0id","month":"2025-12","story":"Screenshots from developers: 2002 vs. 2015 (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Torvalds uses Fedora with GNOME.","quote":"Linus Torvalds currently uses Fedora with GNOME","text":"Linus Torvalds currently uses Fedora with GNOME, which was fun to learn because that's also been my personal choice for a while now. (source: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":46731271,"author":"squeefers","month":"2026-01","story":"30 Years of ReactOS","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"watch how far one lines tries to get his tongue up the other linuses backside.","text":"yeah im just not that impressed with him to put up with that. same with rms. they only act like that because of the pedastal people have put them on. watch how far one lines tries to get his tongue up the other linuses backside. [VIDEO] seeing people give linus the credit for the kernel whilst ignoring the millions of man hours of other peoples time that made also went it (the majority of linux dev effort actually) reminds me of the numpties that think elon musk is literally knocking out rocket ship schematics and then having the spacex engineers rush off to implement his genius insight"}]},"-oB6DN5dYWo":{"wy":"Mostly used as a joke reference whenever forklifts, safety or dying on the job come up, with one commenter calling it a funny and bloody case for warehouse safety.","q":{"x":"this video answered every question I’ve ever had about why warehouse safety is such a big deal.","i":17613347,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17613347,"author":"rm_-rf_slash","month":"2018-07","story":"Ask HN: Has anyone experienced workplace training that made a difference?","stance":"rec","why":"Campy, funny, bloody film showing why warehouse safety matters.","quote":"this video answered every question I’ve ever had about why warehouse safety is such a big deal.","text":"I’m neither German nor a forklift operator, but this video answered every question I’ve ever had about why warehouse safety is such a big deal. [VIDEO] (Warning: bloody. Campy, funny, but bloody)"},{"id":21428681,"author":"rm_-rf_slash","month":"2019-11","story":"Hailing a Driverless Ride in a Waymo","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Wouldn’t want to end up like Klaus","text":"Splitting hairs a bit: the qualifications and regulations for driving in ports, warehouses, docks, and factory floors are far more stringent than public roadways, and the conditions on roads are far less predictable and controllable. Wouldn’t want to end up like Klaus: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14458317,"author":"blorsh","month":"2017-06","story":"Ask HN: Is it normal for tech companies to test for drugs?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"drive like Klaus... Klaus with English subtitles","text":"I think this can become a matter of fairness or insurance demands. You have a forklift operator, so everybody must get tested. They are afraid you might do drugs, hop on the forklift, and drive like Klaus... Klaus with English subtitles: [VIDEO] Dubbed to English, as \"Chad\": [LINK]"},{"id":13781020,"author":"ptaipale","month":"2017-03","story":"Apple’s Devices Lose Luster in American Classrooms","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I cannot write about a \"forklift operator\" without asking if you have seen this German \"educational\" film.","text":"Yes. However, if your goal is to learn to lift up large masses, learning to operate a forklift is a good thing to do. (I cannot write about a \"forklift operator\" without asking if you have seen this German \"educational\" film. It starts slowly, but... [VIDEO] )"}]},"0tEnnvZbYek":{"wy":"Linked in Hacker Manifesto threads as the author's own account of the essay's origin and meaning.","q":{"x":"For the backstory by the author you're better off with the talk given at HOPE in 2002.","i":12321959,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12321959,"author":"uola","month":"2016-08","story":"The Hacker's Manifesto (1986)","stance":"rec","why":"The Mentor gives the backstory of the Hacker Manifesto at HOPE 2002","quote":"For the backstory by the author you're better off with the talk given at HOPE in 2002.","text":"It's slightly anticlimactic when the comment being responded to is a casual dismissal completely missing the context of the text. I've seen plenty of stories on HN where there's some random flamewar on top and the authors insightful comments are at the bottom. For the backstory by the author you're better off with the talk given at HOPE in 2002. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45822270,"author":"basilikum","month":"2025-11","story":"The Hacker’s Manifesto (1986)","stance":"reference","why":"The Mentor reflects on his manifesto 16 years later at H2K2","quote":"Loyd Blankenship (The Mentor) about the","text":"Loyd Blankenship (The Mentor) about the \"Conscious of a Hacker\" 16 years later in 2002 at H2K2: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30494997,"author":"catpea","month":"2022-02","story":"DaVinci's Gallery of Photoshop Graphics and Tutorials","stance":"reference","why":"Talk by Loyd Blankenship, author of the Hacker Manifesto","quote":"And here is Loyd Blankenship himself, with annoying microphone noise","text":"DaVinci's website is 25+ years old now. In some way the art is from the beginning of the earliest more creative websites. 1024x760, for some, is a statement that demands a decent computer. A lot of people had 800x600 resolution. And DaVinci Ice made it clear that his art required more. I hate to say, but by internet standards the website is an archaeological artifact. The web.archive.org link shows the last time the site was up. It is gone now. Goggle returns nothing. The subdomain return DNS errors. As to the UX. Back then we used to put up these dumb a$$ door pages, before you entered a website. It is like a splash screen, in many ways. And this was right after 9/11 so DaVinci Ice passionately, and angerly, put up the banner. This is the world of the old Hacker Manifesto, my friend. Which is extremely valid today due to the nuclear threat just made on the free world. I am sorry, but the past must not be forgotten. Even if it does not work well on modern gadgets. Here is another piece of history, from that era, The Hacker manifesto in text, not crazy HTML goofyness. [LINK] And here is Loyd Blankenship himself, with annoying microphone noise: [VIDEO] I never knew who DaVinci Ice was, I just came across his work on the internet, and loved the colors and art so much, that I remembered to look him up 20+ years after that. I also posted a link to Fravia, [LINK] which may even be more confusing. It breaks my heart to know he passed away in 2009: [LINK] (Ps, to me the modern web is"},{"id":30505711,"author":"catpea","month":"2022-02","story":"DaVinci's Gallery of Photoshop Graphics and Tutorials","stance":"reference","why":"One of several hacker-era videos, including Loyd Blankenship's talk","quote":"","text":"Maybe this imperfect selection of mostly off topic videos can help a tiny bit: Here is Eric's Freedom Downtime: [LINK] Captain Crunch, and Woz: [LINK] Evil Society Whose Plot Was To Control The Word: [LINK] Spot a fed 14: [LINK] And Loyd: [VIDEO] (Wasn't there a huge documentary about Legion Of Doom, or was that a book?)"}]},"1ZTRb-2DZGs":{"wy":"The author and others link it as the definitive explanation of redbean and the APE toolchain, including its performance claims.","q":{"x":"If you're interested in learning more, this talk from 2021 is fascinating","i":32115568,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32115568,"author":"TimTheTinker","month":"2022-07","story":"Unix didn't use to support '#!', a brief history","stance":"rec","why":"Fascinating talk on redbean and APE toolchain","quote":"If you're interested in learning more, this talk from 2021 is fascinating","text":"Justine Tunney's APE (actually portable executables) exploits, er, makes use of, the fact that a shebang isn't required to run a shell script. The single executable file generated by the Cosmopolitan linker creates an insane polyglot binary executable that runs on 6 operating systems as well as on bare metal (i.e. bootable as its own OS); in addition, it's a valid Zip archive, and you can modify files inside it using any zip utility... fascinating stuff. On Linux, it bootstraps execution initially as a shell script, but the shell script writes the \"ELF\" file header and executes it (I'm probably doing a horrible job of explaining though). [LINK] If you're interested in learning more, this talk from 2021 is fascinating: [VIDEO] . It covers Redbean, a portable, run-anywhere web server built with the APE toolchain; since then Redbean 2 has been released, which adds a lot more features. Here's a link to Redbean: [LINK]"},{"id":34267734,"author":"adolph","month":"2023-01","story":"LiteSync – Easy synchronization of SQLite databases","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on redbean, a portable web server embedding Lua, SQLite and MbedTLS","quote":"redbean embeds Lua, SQLite, and MbedTLS into a fork() driven application server that benchmarks at 1.1 million qps on a personal computer.","text":"Like running redbean on someone else's computer? redbean embeds Lua, SQLite, and MbedTLS into a fork() driven application server that benchmarks at 1.1 million qps on a personal computer. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29632268,"author":"jart","month":"2021-12","story":"Show HN: Lisp with GC in 436 Bytes","stance":"reference","why":"Author shares own talk about redbean","quote":"Here's a video of me giving a talk about it","text":"Author here. Thanks! The other projects I'm known for here on HN are: [LINK] Redbean (2011) is a single file distributable web server. Here's a video of me giving a talk about it [VIDEO] [LINK] αcτµαlly pδrταblε εxεcµταblε (2020) lets you build c/c++/fortran/etc. code so that it runs on seven operating systems [LINK] Operation Rosehub (2017) where I helped save a lot of people from an Apache security bug similar to Log4j issue today I like doing these projects because they all fit together so well into a consistent story. For example, a lot of these past projects were what helped to make SectorLISP better. Thanks to APE you now have a 20kb quickly deployable and embeddable LISP interpreter. Thanks to Redbean we're going to have an online pastebin service that'll let you publish SectorLISP gists in a few days, for example: [LINK] Thank you Hacker News community for being so supportive and encouraging."},{"id":28647728,"author":"jart","month":"2021-09","story":"Developers fix multitude of vulnerabilities in Apache HTTP Server","stance":"reference","why":"Author links talk on a fork-based web server's speed","quote":"redbean is a pure fork() web server and according to ab it is much faster than nginx.","text":"redbean is a pure fork() web server and according to ab it is much faster than nginx. I feel like we've come full circle with history. Apache started off with fork() since it offers the strongest security versus other i/o models. The problem is that code bloat makes fork() go slower so as Apache ballooned over the years there was this race to the bottom in terms of i/o models, caused by tragedy of the commons. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28024028,"author":"jart","month":"2021-08","story":"Show HN: Micro HTTP server in 22 lines of C","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for server performance metrics","quote":"Messages per second, latency, footprint, name it.","text":"Messages per second, latency, footprint, name it. Particularly if it's gzip encoded. See [VIDEO] I'm working on giving redbean the fastest https serving too. Recently I've been helping to make the strongest elliptic curves go 3x faster."},{"id":27517905,"author":"jart","month":"2021-06","story":"“Great resignation” wave coming for companies","stance":"reference","why":"Author links own interview about project","quote":"and interview","text":"Data control and privacy are two of the most important things. I wrote a popular open source project where I've sought to improve that. Would you be interested in taking a quick look and offering candid feedback? See [LINK] and discussion thread [LINK] and interview [VIDEO] and polyglot executable format [LINK] Someone like you could help me know if I'm doing it in the best possible way, since studying the influence of groups like cygnus was what helped me learn the hacking stunts that allowed me to pull it off in the first place."}]},"1eIxUuuJX7Y":{"wy":"Linked in housing and urbanism threads as a fun, deliberately provocative case that dense blocks are better than their reputation.","q":{"x":"Here is a fun and a bit provocative/exaggerated video","i":39419860,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39419860,"author":"bartwr","month":"2024-02","story":"The hottest trend in U.S. cities? Changing zoning rules to allow more housing","stance":"rec","why":"Fun, provocative defense of eastern european tenement blocks","quote":"Here is a fun and a bit provocative/exaggerated video","text":"I know this is an unpopular opinion in the US, but the tenements can be pretty great. I grew up in Eastern Europe (Warsaw) in \"commie\" blocks and there was a lot of valid criticisms and problems (like poor quality of buildings, small apartments, or thin walls - but consider that they rebuilt whole Warsaw after it was completely grounded in WW2 in a decade or two!), but also a lot to love. Extremely walkable, safe, all amenities (cinemas, stores, cultural centers, playgrounds) in the walking distance, lots of trees and green, easy access to public transit. As a kid or teenager they were great. I preferred it 100x over suburbs where my parents moved later, and to typical American cityscapes. (And this is why I moved to NYC and love it) Here is a fun and a bit provocative/exaggerated video [VIDEO] Everyone is different so I'm not forcing my perspective onto anyone, just worth considering - especially if you have not had such first hand experience (and the main objection to tenements comes from how depressing they look or American association of \"projects = crime\", which misses a lot of \"why\"). Feel free to disagree! And apart from that, I don't think \"more of small houses\" solves anything. It has to create more car dependence and social isolation. And it does not really scale, where would you fit more of smaller homes in SF?"},{"id":33120193,"author":"smusamashah","month":"2022-10","story":"Startup Builds Houses by Pumping Concrete into Inflatable Forms","stance":"reference","why":"Source for claims about tall buildings being unlivable","quote":"very tall buildings are cool only for the very rich but they are not very liveable","text":"From what I have found watching this [VIDEO] and [LINK] is that very tall buildings are cool only for the very rich but they are not very liveable. Burj Khalifa does not have a severage system. Trucks are used to take the human waste away every single day from burj khalifa."},{"id":33149566,"author":"Hard_Space","month":"2022-10","story":"Made in America is back, leaving US factories scrambling to find workers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"communist design principles designed a whole neighborhood, with schools and hospitals and doctors","text":"Higher density happens because investors want to maximize the use of land in desirable areas, not because land is unavailable or difficult to zone. I live in Bucharest, a city of incredibly high RE development, and most of the new buildings are more compressed and crowded in on each other than they were under communism, which is a common occurrence in the former Soviet satellite states [1]. From my window, I can see across the Delta Vacaresti, a wildlife reserve that was once a neighborhood that Ceausescu just had time to raze to the ground for some new insane scheme before his people shot him. Across the way, I see three massive multistory residential blocks going up, crowded together like pedestrians on a metro train at rush hour - only one side of the front-most block has any view of the Delta, while the others literally live in each other's shadows, lacking sunlight. By contrast, communist design principles designed a whole neighborhood, with schools and hospitals and doctors, with the 'megablocks' seen as small cities in themselves that needed dedicated support systems. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30044684,"author":"leto_ii","month":"2022-01","story":"Why skyscrapers are so short","stance":"reference","why":"Argues soviet-era blocks are efficient, pleasant housing","quote":"commie blocks [2] (surprisingly pleasant and efficient housing)","text":"I will contribute by adding a couple of references to AdamSomething's views on skyscrapers [1] (we shouldn't build them) and commie blocks [2] (surprisingly pleasant and efficient housing): [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] As an Eastern European who lately has developed a casual interest in urban planning, I wholeheartedly agree with a low to mid-rise approach to urban development. Both in my home town (Bucharest) as well as in my current place of residence (Amsterdam) by far the least pleasent areas are the places where high-rises concentrate (Pipera and Zuidas respectively)."}]},"4lmW2tZP2kU":{"wy":"Mostly a joke reference in threads about layoffs, idleness and wealth, with one commenter sincerely recommending the clip for its contrast of work attitudes.","q":{"x":"Highly recommend to watch the clip from the movie Office Space (only 54 sec long)","i":24139876,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24139876,"author":"sudeepj","month":"2020-08","story":"Bertrand Russell's argument for idleness is more relevant than ever","stance":"rec","why":"Short Office Space clip contrasting blue and white collar workers.","quote":"Highly recommend to watch the clip from the movie Office Space (only 54 sec long)","text":"Highly recommend to watch the clip from the movie Office Space (only 54 sec long) [1] The guy with the moustache is a blue collar worker and the other one is white collar worker (actually in Tech/IT) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41997164,"author":"computronus","month":"2024-10","story":"Dropbox announces 20% global workforce reduction","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Well you don’t need a million dollars to do nothing, man.","text":"Lawrence: Well, what about you now, what would you do [if you had a million dollars]? Peter: ... Nothing. Lawrence: Nothing, huh? Peter: I would relax, I would sit on my ass all day ... I would do nothing. Lawrence: Well you don’t need a million dollars to do nothing, man. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32317544,"author":"mabbo","month":"2022-08","story":"Understanding Jane Street","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory \"Office Space\" scene on \"what would you do with a million dollars\"","text":"> People who are driven and smart don't suddenly earn their first $5m go off and buy an annuity I guess I must not be driven, because I would. Or at least something close enough to that. $5m just earning interest at 5%ish is way more income than I need to live the lifestyle I want. I'd buy a few acres in the middle of no where, build a nice house, grow a big garden, raise my daughter as a nice family man and never work again. Obligatory \"Office Space\" scene on \"what would you do with a million dollars\": [VIDEO]"}]},"6SYHamnHqU8":{"wy":"Cited as an example of ocean rowing crossings and recommended by one commenter as really good.","m":[{"id":48792006,"author":"MrMember","month":"2026-07","story":"Record-breaking solo rower Kelsey Pfendler arrives in Hawaii","stance":"rec","why":"Four women rowing across the Atlantic","quote":"it's really good.","text":"I watched a YouTube video about four women who rowed across the Atlantic, it's really good. I couldn't imagine doing it solo. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45175080,"author":"foxyv","month":"2025-09","story":"Crossing the Atlantic Ocean. Alone. By Stand-Up-Paddleboard","stance":"reference","why":"Example of an Atlantic rowing crossing on a favorable route","quote":"","text":"Depends on the crossing. Usually they will choose a route that is favorable. This is probably the same as the ocean crossings by row boat. [VIDEO] Generally speaking there is a route called the \"Milk Run\" from Europe to the Caribbean that has tail winds and good currents in the right direction. Also much nicer weather."},{"id":33963174,"author":"mmanfrin","month":"2022-12","story":"The Longest Ever Flight Was over 64 Days in a Cessna 172 (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Four women rowing 3,000 miles across the Atlantic in shifts","quote":"a crew of 4 women rowing across the Atlantic in a tiny little boat","text":"Reminds me of a video I recently watched of a crew of 4 women rowing across the Atlantic in a tiny little boat, taking continual 4 hour shifts for 3,000 miles: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["UIKGV2cTgqA",1]]},"824yVKUPFjU":{"wy":"HN recommends it for its insight into Knuth's TeX algorithms and what changes when they are rewritten functionally.","q":{"x":"The video goes into this better than the InfoQ article.","i":8876040,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":8672876,"author":"gavinpc","month":"2014-11","story":"Ramda – A practical functional library for Javascript","stance":"rec","why":"Shows pains Knuth took avoiding subroutine calls in TeX.","quote":"You might also like the \"It's 1982 and I can't believe we're still...\" part of the talk","text":"TL;DR, stack frame all the things. I'm joking (kind of), but I was reminded of this talk about implementing TeX in Clojure [0], in which you are shown the pains Don Knuth took to avoid subroutines calls, ostensibly for performance reasons—such heavy macro usage, for example, that the speaker calls the language a \"dialect\" of Pascal. Now we have the maxim about programmer cycles versus machine cycles, which is itself getting a little dated because of scale. Anyway, fun times to be in this field. You might also like the \"It's 1982 and I can't believe we're still...\" part of the talk, (a.k.a. \"Everything's amazing and nobody's happy\") for putting things into perspective. [0] [VIDEO] EDIT Time is on their side though... with tail-call optimization coming to ES6 (just about the only \"good part,\" according to Crockford)."},{"id":13686458,"author":"rhythmvs","month":"2017-02","story":"SILE Typesetting System","stance":"reference","why":"Clojure TeX port mentioned as similar initiative.","quote":"","text":"SILE indeed looks great, promising to come on par with the typesetting quality we have come to expect since TeX: Knuth-Plass line breaking, Unicode and OpenType font features support, complete with contextual shaping, Cassowary constraint solver, parallel text, multiple apparati, foot and marginal notes, vertical typesetting… This is a typographer’s dream! But, like TeX and friends (LaTeX, ConTeXt, etc.), creating stylesheets and document templates still looks like a pain with SILE and alike. At least from the perspective of designers who have come to expect a strict separation of concerns between document structure/semantics and its styling, and who are used to work with a declarative stylesheet-based language like the prevalent CSS, as opposed to the macros of TeX, and its document model which conflates semantical markup with inline styling instructions. Similar initiatives like SILE, which attempt to port TeX to newer languages while untangling macro spaghetti, like Cló¹ and Rinohtype², didn’t consider CSS-based stylesheets either. Which is a pity, especially with highly developed W3C Working Draft open standard specifications³ for paged media being out quite some time now. That’s exactly what makes PDF formatters like Prince⁴, which accepts standard html and css as its input, so immensely attractive: users can continue to use the (Web) technologies they already know (html, javascript, css) and enjoy a strict separation of concerns between document contents, templates and "},{"id":8876040,"author":"dj-wonk","month":"2015-01","story":"Reimplementing TeX's Algorithms: Looking Back at Thirty Years of Programming","stance":"reference","why":"Talk explains educational goals of reimplementing TeX functionally.","quote":"The video goes into this better than the InfoQ article.","text":"Perhaps Glenn has different goals than you might expect? The video goes into this better than the InfoQ article. In short, I think Glenn embraced the differences and challenges of adapting TeX in Clojure from an educational perspective. I don't think he intended to port it quickly or verbatim. Glenn wanted to head-to-head comparison to promote education. To explain, in Glenn's talk around 11:36 ( [VIDEO] ) he says: > While I'm glad that the source code of TeX is still available to study, I sure wouldn't point a new programmer toward it because we should not be doing programs today like we did then. > But what might be really valuable is to have a modern reimplementation written in a modern functional style to study alongside [the original TeX] to see how much has changed and to appreciate what he have and the value of improved tools and techniques and the tradeoffs that have been made. His slide on his educational goals can be seen at 12:06: > Illustrate what's changed > Demonstrate values of expressive code > Provide real examples of expressing procedural algorithms in functional style > Show how functional programs are easier to reason about > Show different styles of optimization"}],"r":[["oyLBGkS5ICk",1]]},"BpT6MkCeP7Y":{"wy":"HN cites it as the example of real-time GPU path tracing, noting its noise problem.","q":{"x":"I think path tracing is even more impressive. For example this Brigade 3.0 demo","i":8211781,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":8211781,"author":"huuu","month":"2014-08","story":"Next-Gen Lighting Is Pushing the Limits of Realism","stance":"rec","why":"Impressive real-time photorealism.","quote":"I think path tracing is even more impressive. For example this Brigade 3.0 demo","text":"What UE4 can do is very impressive. But when it comes to real time photo realism I think path tracing is even more impressive. For example this Brigade 3.0 demo: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10892741,"author":"drojf","month":"2016-01","story":"Voxel Quest January 2016 Update﻿","stance":"reference","why":"Example of near-realtime GPU path tracing.","quote":"Here's an example of path tracing in \"realtime\" (ok, it's not quite there yet) on the GPU.","text":"well you COULD use OpenCL to \"control every pixel\". Here's an example of path tracing in \"realtime\" (ok, it's not quite there yet) on the GPU. [VIDEO] check out this blog too: [LINK]"},{"id":8911285,"author":"tpaksoy","month":"2015-01","story":"Path Tracing 3D Fractals","stance":"reference","why":"Brigade engine as a path tracing example.","quote":"There is the Brigade engine[1] that I know of.","text":"There is the Brigade engine[1] that I know of. It's as far as I know a one man project. I haven't heard of an update in a long time though. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8234137,"author":"fizixer","month":"2014-08","story":"Building 3D with Ikea","stance":"reference","why":"Real-time path tracing effort in an interesting videos list.","quote":"Brigade is an effort towards real-time path tracing","text":"In case anyone has about 30-60 minutes and is interested to get a quick glimpse of how easy (I guess meaning free and accessible at the least) it has become to do such graphics: - Download and install Blender 2.71 ( [LINK] ). On linux (Ubuntu) I did not even have to install it; I just extracted the tarball and ran the blender binary. - Go through this two part ceramic mug tutorial (30-60 minutes): [LINK] ... [LINK] As someone who does not have graphics training, I was blown away when I did this. Apparently there is this thing called 'path tracing' based rendering, that takes care of accurate lighting, as long as you give the correct specification of geometry and materials. Some interesting videos: - Octane 2.0 renderer: [LINK] - Lightwave: [LINK] - Brigade 3.0: [VIDEO] - Alex Roman, the third and the seventh: [LINK] Brigade is an effort towards real-time path tracing, and it's predicted that within 2-3 GPU generations, such graphics would be possible in games."},{"id":7537122,"author":"Geee","month":"2014-04","story":"Pyro from Team Fortress 2 raytraced in your browser","stance":"reference","why":"Realtime Brigade path tracing demo.","quote":"Have you seen what Octane/Brigade (not exactly same tech, but close) can do in realtime?","text":"Yup, both of them take at least 1-2 seconds to respond to mouse movements. Could you do WebGL and blend into ray-traced? Have you seen what Octane/Brigade (not exactly same tech, but close) can do in realtime? [VIDEO] (you probably have) Anyways, Brigade is coming to cloud as well on AWS GPU instances, so it would probably be easy for you to just use their solution integrated within your modeling tool then."},{"id":18714452,"author":"GolDDranks","month":"2018-12","story":"Path Tracing vs. Ray Tracing (2016)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This demo is 4 years old, but the noise still seems to be a problem","text":"I wonder what convolutional neural networks bring to the table. Maybe you could use them to get rid of the noise, or then you could use ray tracing for the base image and train a CNN to \"recolour\" that image based on a noisy path traced one of the same scene. This demo is 4 years old, but the noise still seems to be a problem: [VIDEO] If anybody's got more recent impressive demos to link, I'd like to see how things have been developing."}]},"D7GDTZ45TRw":{"wy":"Cited as the authoritative source on how Mill's VLIW-style compiler specializes code and differs from Itanium.","q":{"x":"have a design talk that covers these concepts wonderfully in the general form","i":34204572,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34204572,"author":"anotherhue","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Why did stack-based CPUs lose out?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how the Mill VLIW compiler specialises code for different hardware variants.","quote":"have a design talk that covers these concepts wonderfully in the general form","text":"The (disconcertingly silent) Mill CPU company have a design talk that covers these concepts wonderfully in the general form, while explaining their various innovations. e.g. this video describes how their VLIW compiler does partial compilation/'specialisation' to allow the same input to be consumed on machines with different variations of hardware features. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19212237,"author":"magicalhippo","month":"2019-02","story":"SIMD Instructions Considered Harmful (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Covers the Mill compiler aspects and how it differs from Itanium.","quote":"here's[1] the relevant one on the compiler aspect","text":"It's been too long since I watched the videos, so unfortunately I don't remember the specifics. For reference, here's[1] the relevant one on the compiler aspect. I do seem to recall that they seemingly had studied the failures of Itanium, and supposedly designed their architecture to not fall into the same pitfalls as with the EPIC/Itanium. One aspect I recall is that while they have VLIW, different operations within the (compound) instruction are issued in such a way that lets them be interdependent. Like, a single VLIW instruction could have an add and a multiply, where the result of the add is used as input for the multiplication. So while the operations are grouped in a single instruction, they're not executed strictly in parallel. There's a lot of other aspects too, that's just the one I remember. But yeah, really curious to know how that pudding will turn out. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9856726,"author":"fla","month":"2015-07","story":"The Mill CPU Architecture – The Compiler [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the Mill belt as a proxy for the bypass network.","quote":"about the belt being in fact a 'proxy' of the bypass network is very interesting","text":"The answer at 1:26:12 [1] about the belt being in fact a 'proxy' of the bypass network is very interesting. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["F8sZRBdmqc0",1]]},"E1AxVXt2Gv4":{"wy":"Referenced as an accessible explanation of Kolmogorov complexity and Hutter's view of intelligence as compression.","q":{"x":"it made sense to me","i":22432504,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22432504,"author":"jcims","month":"2020-02","story":"Machine Learning, Kolmogorov Complexity, and Squishy Bunnies (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Hutter explains Kolmogorov complexity in understandable terms.","quote":"it made sense to me","text":"New concepts to me coming from outside the field. Kolmogorov complexity was discussed just recently on Lex Fridman's podcast with Marcus Hutter - [VIDEO] I don't know enough to say how accurate Hutter's explanation is but it made sense to me."},{"id":43259485,"author":"AIorNot","month":"2025-03","story":"ARC-AGI without pretraining","stance":"reference","why":"Hutter discusses intelligence and compression.","quote":"","text":"I was thinking about this lex friedman podcast with Marcus Hutter. Also, Joshua Bach defined intelligence as the ability to accurately model reality.. is lossless compression itself intelligence or a best fit model- is there a difference? [VIDEO]"},{"id":33047652,"author":"WithinReason","month":"2022-10","story":"Hutter Prize","stance":"reference","why":"Interview with Hutter on intelligence as compression.","quote":"Then there is the interview with Hutter himself","text":"> Are they? They are actually. It's a kind of revelation when you realise this statement is true. There are people in AI who have thought long about the problem of intelligence and had this realisation. For example: [LINK] Then there is the interview with Hutter himself: [VIDEO] You can even semi-formally derive this from Bayes' rule. Let's consider intelligence to be the ability to predict future data from past data. This requires building a model for the past data that can make predictions. So intelligence produces the most likely model M given data D: argmax_M P(M|D) which from Bayes' rule is: argmax_M P(M|D) = P(D|M)*P(M)/P(D) You convert probabilities to information by taking the negative log: argmin_M -log(P(M|D)) = -log(P(D|M)) + -log(P(M)) We lost -log(P(D)) because we consider D constant so it can't influence the minimum. You can read the above informally as: The most likely model (the most general model) that can make predictions from data D is that where the (encoding of the model with the least information) plus (the encoding of the data using the model) is minimal. This is essentially a statement about compression. I'm sure an AI person could do this better. This is also known as: [LINK] or in philosophy as: [LINK]"}],"r":[["lY8Ol2n4o4A",1],["DXYje2B04xE",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["N3bJnKmeNJY",1],["aoczYXJeMY4",1],["bPhUhypV27w",1],["cdiD-9MMpb0",1],["krB0enBeSiE",1],["2hf9yN-oBV4",1],["7pSqk-XV2QM",1],["GhsZUZmJvaM",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["LQmTKxI4Wn4",1],["ta7nlkI5K5g",1],["UDtlvZGmHYk",1],["P-2P3MSZrBM",1],["TfYf_rPWUdY",1],["3dl9Ovwmnxw",1]]},"EWqThb9Z1jk":{"wy":"Cited in threads on flatness and precision as a glimpse into a niche metrology profession.","q":{"x":"I like the glimpse into the hobby/profession shown on channels like this","i":22898154,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22898154,"author":"205guy","month":"2020-04","story":"Hand Scraping a Truly Flat Plane (1908) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Glimpse of expert calibration and lapping of surface plates","quote":"I like the glimpse into the hobby/profession shown on channels like this","text":"The YouTube channel oxtoolco has a video showing lapping of surface plates. Not the same as hand scraping, but the principle of using a reference surface is similar, and the master plate looks like the illustrations in this 100-year old document. Plus I think with modern synthetic diamond abrasives, you can directly lap the plates against each other. Lapping starts at 17:40 (they do surface measurements first): [VIDEO] I am no machinist, but I like the glimpse into the hobby/profession shown on channels like this and others that comments have mentioned. In the video, the channel owner hires a company to service his stone regency plates, so there is an expert who comes and uses specialized tools to measure and then flatten the plates (and certify them too). You can see and experience the expertise that the guy has with these physical systems, yet it’s just one niche job in a huge engineering tool chain. It really made me think a lot about metal machining and fabrication, and how those rely on fundamental measurements and properties such as flatness."},{"id":18474185,"author":"blhack","month":"2018-11","story":"International System of Units overhauled in historic vote","stance":"reference","why":"Resurfacing a very flat plate in millionths of an inch","quote":"everything is being done in millionths of an inch.","text":"What applications are you talking about? Just for a general example of the sort of thing I'm talking about: [VIDEO] They're resurfacing a surface plate, which has to be very flat. If you listen to the exchanges between them, everything is being done in millionths of an inch."},{"id":13840670,"author":"NegativeK","month":"2017-03","story":"Mirror grinding","stance":"reference","why":"Hand lapping of granite surface plates","quote":"Granite surface plates (highly accurate planes) are hand lapped","text":"Hand techniques are still used for reference surfaces in machining. Granite surface plates (highly accurate planes) are hand lapped[1], and metal bearing surfaces on machine tools and cast iron reference tools are sometimes hand scraped[2]. [1] On location calibration and lapping of granite surface plates: [VIDEO] [2] Hand scraping (effectively chiseling) of reference cast iron surfaces: [LINK]"}],"r":[["wVp5koAOu9M",1],["fCbAafKLqC8",1]]},"IJNR2EpS0jw":{"wy":"HN mentions it mostly as a joke or a pop-culture reference, and as the campaign that titles play on.","m":[{"id":37166922,"author":"mytailorisrich","month":"2023-08","story":"Straightforward Thai Railway Safety Posters","stance":"rec","why":"Funny, memorable rail safety campaign.","quote":"was quite funny and memorable","text":"The 'Dumb Ways to Die' video from a few years ago, from Australia, was quite funny and memorable (which I think is the aim for these campaigns): [VIDEO] Be safe around trains."},{"id":48200054,"author":"chasil","month":"2026-05","story":"Dumb Ways for an Open Source Project to Die","stance":"reference","why":"Original campaign the post title plays on.","quote":"Is this a play on the rail safety videos?","text":"Is this a play on the rail safety videos? [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] Edit: Yes. \"The Melbourne Metro safety campaign this post is named after closes with “be safe around trains,” which is more actionable than anything I’ve got.\""},{"id":45585344,"author":"thelastgallon","month":"2025-10","story":"What Americans die from vs. what the news reports on","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Dumb Ways to Die, so many dumb ways to die","text":"Dumb Ways to Die, so many dumb ways to die: [VIDEO] I wonder why they didn't start with automobile accidents or driving drunk!"},{"id":16951744,"author":"majewsky","month":"2018-04","story":"How much time cutting corners actually saves (math)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> This is why my preferred method for crossing intersections with 4-way stop signs is to go straight through the middle. [VIDEO]"}]},"KkMP328eU5Q":{"wy":"HN points to it as an entertaining, approachable docu-series on how interconnected civilizations can collapse, often in discussions of societal fragility.","q":{"x":"entertaining docu-series[0] describing the collapse of Bronze Age civilizations around the eastern Mediterranean","i":32531740,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32531740,"author":"jason-phillips","month":"2022-08","story":"The Collapse of Complex Societies (Book Review)","stance":"rec","why":"Approachable docu-series on collapse of Bronze Age civilizations","quote":"entertaining docu-series[0] describing the collapse of Bronze Age civilizations around the eastern Mediterranean","text":"> First, there are way more examples of complex societies collapsing than I was aware of. To add to the discussion, I submit this entertaining docu-series[0] describing the collapse of Bronze Age civilizations around the eastern Mediterranean. The authors proffer that one possible cause for their near-total collapse could have been a breakdown within Bronze Age society's well-structured and complex systems from which recovery was simply not possible. Apologies to the history purists for the inevitable inaccuracies to be found herein but this was an approachable series for me as a codemonkey. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28603579,"author":"mkotowski","month":"2021-09","story":"A Tunguska size burst destroyed Tall el-Hammam, Bronze Age city in Jordan Valley","stance":"reference","why":"Series on weak points behind the Late Bronze Age collapse","quote":"series of videos describing various weak points that could lead to the Late Bronze Age collapse","text":"If I remember correctly, currently it is thought that the collapse was caused by many negative events happening almost at the same time. One of those are so-called Sea Peoples (seriously [0]). A single impact could explain a collapse of a city or a country, but not the fall of the whole region. Extra Credits once did a series of videos describing various weak points that could lead to the Late Bronze Age collapse: [VIDEO] [0]: [LINK]"},{"id":14738133,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2017-07","story":"The Uninhabitable Earth","stance":"reference","why":"Example of environmental change and misaligned incentives ending civilizations","quote":"Just one example, for which new media exists","text":"It's not the handful of climate change deniers that's the problem. It's the overwhelming number of people who want to say they're green, but still own two cars, never take public transport, eat meat daily, live in big houses, and buy buy buy. Given the evidence of all of recorded history and archaeological evidence, how is the denial of economics any better than climate change denial? We have many, many examples of environmental changes combined with misaligned incentives bringing about the end of many civilizations. (Just one example, for which new media exists: [VIDEO] ) As another HN commenter on this post noted, you can't overturn people's incentives through moralizing about some abstract fear. Even when pursuing an obvious course of justice, Gandhi knew that you must align with people's day to day incentives. It does no one good to rail against the stupidity of the masses and the greed of corporations. We need to figure out how to align everyone's incentives."}]},"LiWlvBro9eI":{"wy":"HN cites it as a favorite example of a famous prank or trolling stunt that fooled a major broadcaster.","q":{"x":"Perhaps my favourite","i":11959944,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11959944,"author":"e12e","month":"2016-06","story":"Lenin was a mushroom","stance":"rec","why":"Yes Men fooled the BBC into thinking they were Dow spokespeople","quote":"Perhaps my favourite","text":"Haha. For something equally funny, but more serious (in a way, although making fun of Lenin at the time is nothing to sneeze at), have a look at the work of \"the Yes Men\": [LINK] Perhaps my favourite: [VIDEO] \"Bhopal Disaster - BBC - The Yes Men\" - where they tricked media, including the BBC, into believing they were Dow Chemical spokespersons, and that Dow would take responsibility for the Bhopal chemical disaster, after Dow bought Union Carbide. [ed: some context: [LINK] ]"},{"id":31598101,"author":"jancsika","month":"2022-06","story":"Google contacted me directly to help restore FairEmail on the Play Store","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> An exception to normally bad behavior, and the first instance of a genuine change in approach, look identical. No, they probably don't. Take for example this example prank by the Yes Men : > [VIDEO] The beauty of the prank was that the company then had to send a real spokesperson out to explicitly state that \"Steve, yes,\" was the wrong answer to the question, \"Do you now accept responsibility for what happened?\" In fact I'll boldly claim you have never seen a genuine change in approach from a company of this size that wasn't forced by a settlement with the government or criminal prosecution(s). Edit: I'll go ahead and admit I'd love to read about the exceptions to my last paragraph, but only in terms of companies the size of Google."},{"id":21374769,"author":"reitzensteinm","month":"2019-10","story":"Bhopal disaster","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the Yes Men went on the BBC pretending to be representatives of Dow, taking full responsibility for the disaster","text":"Tangentially related, the Yes Men went on the BBC pretending to be representatives of Dow, taking full responsibility for the disaster, and temporarily wiping $2bn off the market cap. The man in the video, Jacques Servin, was fired from Maxis after adding an easter egg to SimCopter where groups of shirtless men appeared kissing each other on certain dates. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":11667435,"author":"feedjoelpie","month":"2016-05","story":"Aristotle, “On Trolling”","stance":"reference","why":"Yes Men activities as trolling","quote":"","text":"Would you consider, as I would, the Yes Men's activities as both trolling and more or less good? [VIDEO]"},{"id":1809881,"author":"sp332","month":"2010-10","story":"Elaborate fake Chevron PR campaign created by subversive ad group","stance":"reference","why":"Yes Men pranking a BBC interviewer","quote":"How about pranking a BBC interviewer?","text":"Spoofing emails? How about pranking a BBC interviewer? [VIDEO] or impersonating the U.S. Chamber of Commerce? [LINK]"}],"r":[["sWgDk-o-6ZE",1],["p6K4iIMlouI",1],["eX9m3g5J-XA",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1],["EKWGGDXe5MA",1],["_ahvzDzKdB0",1],["dK3zRXhrFZY",1],["oKg1hTOQXoY",1],["Av0PQDVTP4A",1],["6wZmYMWKLkY",1],["hEx5DNLWGgA",1],["4bM3Gut1hIk",1],["Eg_ToU7m1MI",1],["hOE6nVVr14c",1],["p2LZLYcu_JY",1],["30jNsCVLpAE",1]]},"SnRgW54EWwA":{"wy":"HN cites it as a source for how Valve uses machine learning on player behavior to detect cheating.","q":{"x":"Valve uses deep learning model called VACnet to \"watch\" every player on their servers","i":23790553,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23790553,"author":"cameronbrown","month":"2020-07","story":"Valve secrets spill over in new Steam documentary app","stance":"rec","why":"How Valve uses deep learning and community review against cheaters.","quote":"Valve uses deep learning model called VACnet to \"watch\" every player on their servers","text":"If you're interested, Valve uses deep learning model called VACnet to \"watch\" every player on their servers[0][1]. If any dodgy gameplay is detected, it gets submitted into a moderation queue called Overwatch[2], which is basically delegates the decisions to ban cheaters community. It's really cool how they've gamified this. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":35418587,"author":"judge2020","month":"2023-04","story":"The Day Windows Died","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source on Valve's automatic cheat detection.","quote":"","text":"That's not what I said - my point is that companies with a relaxed security baseline (ie. not having a dedicated team of people tasked with investigating cheats and improving their anti-cheat system) tend to invite an increased number of cheaters into their games, and I back this up with real events. - These companies will send cease-and-desist letters to cheat makers instead of working to detect their cheats (Joduska.me ceased operations due to a Riot C&D, and Bossland GmbH was actually sued and lost). If these cheats were instead detected and users banned on a continuous basis, no lawsuits would be needed. - CS:GO implemented (as in, almost a decade ago) an \"Overwatch\" review system to allow players to review potential cheaters and have a consensus on whether or not cheating is likely. Valve is knows to be very relaxed on VAC - and knowing the company culture @ Valve being what it is, these ban waves probably only happen when one of the seniors @ Valve gets killed by a cheater in-game themselves and tasks someone with implementing some detection mechanism into VAC. Only recently (~4 years ago) has valve supposedly started to incorporate more advanced automatic detection and punishment systems[0], but at the time it wasn't doing anything about wallhacks and at auto-headshot cheats tend to be pretty good at adding enough noise to make it look very close to what professionals can do and thus these cases still end up in the overwatch queue (and note that this system probably ha"},{"id":28634441,"author":"judge2020","month":"2021-09","story":"Epic launches anti-cheat support for Linux, Mac, and Steam Deck","stance":"reference","why":"Describes server-side VACnet feeding the Overwatch queue.","quote":"The server side of this is VACnet","text":"Worth noting that the VAC games on that list, CS:GO, TF2, and Dota 2, have effectively given up on actually detecting and preventing cheaters and now rely on server-based or community-consensus methods of finding and punishing cheaters (with TF2 being the exception with how little maintenance Valve performs on it). Both CS:GO and Dota 2 have a report+overwatch system where users watch a replay to determine if someone is exhibiting suspicious cheating behavior. The server side of this is VACnet where it uses heuristics like mouse movement or [in Dota] clicking out of regular camera bounds to detect these cheaters and expedite them to the Overwatch queue. You can readily download the biggest script client for Dota 2, and VAC ban waves for using it have been unheard of for years now (you only get an in-game ban when enough other players verify the cheating via Overwatch). [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":27852194,"author":"kllrnohj","month":"2021-07","story":"Valve Steam Deck","stance":"reference","why":"Valve uses ML on mouse movements to flag cheaters.","quote":"Valve is already using machine learning to analyze player mouse movements across all games","text":"[VIDEO] Valve is already using machine learning to analyze player mouse movements across all games to look for \"inhuman\" patterns and send those directly to the community-staffed manual review process called overwatch."},{"id":20456343,"author":"cameronbrown","month":"2019-07","story":"Respawn will deal with Apex Legends cheaters by making them fight each other","stance":"reference","why":"Explains Valve's VACNet neural network cheat detection.","quote":"it's a system called VACNet","text":"> They also have a neural network setup that constantly scans match logs for cheaters For those wondering what this is referring to, it's a system called VACNet[0] presented at GDC last year. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21948074,"author":"willis936","month":"2020-01","story":"EA is permanently banning Linux players on Battlefield V","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It’s security theatre a la malware.","text":"There exist good solutions, but none of these companies have the competence or will to actually solve the problem. It’s security theatre a la malware. “Hey everyone install this rootkit to pretend like we’re preventing cheaters. Don’t worry about the keylogging and screenshot uploading.” What’s most surprising is that the vast majority of gamers aren’t outraged by this at all, despite repeated demonstrations of untrustworthiness by anticheat companies. [VIDEO]"}]},"UacbJ72dluU":{"wy":"Linked as a source for Paul Graham's no-assholes rule and his comments on early YC funding.","q":{"x":"It's about Paul Graham's no asshole rule.","i":1866197,"y":"2010"},"m":[{"id":1866197,"author":"ashleyreddy","month":"2010-11","story":"My site looks like $hit any designers want to make a couple of hundred bucks","stance":"rec","why":"About Paul Graham's no asshole rule","quote":"It's about Paul Graham's no asshole rule.","text":"Your comment is mean spirited. It's meaningless and adds no value. Perhaps you should watch this video. [VIDEO] It's about Paul Graham's no asshole rule."},{"id":33128111,"author":"O__________O","month":"2022-10","story":"Ask HN: What Happened to YC?","stance":"reference","why":"Paul Graham on average funding per startup","quote":"PG 12 years ago saying average was $17,500 per startup","text":"Related links: PG 12 years ago saying average was $17,500 per startup [VIDEO] Last batch count was 240: [LINK] FAQ saying current standard offer is $500,000 [LINK]"},{"id":20361121,"author":"harryf","month":"2019-07","story":"Assholes: A Probing Examination","stance":"reference","why":"Paul Graham on removing assholes from a company","quote":"Paul Graham described this long ago here","text":"> Sorting people into bins: assholes and victims, is problematic. This is true of anything subjective. There are people (intentional assholes in this article) who absolutely _are_ assholes because it gives them pleasure - their ego or otherwise gains something from hurting others. Everyone in a company knows who they without needing to go through a labelling process. Ultimately it's a problem for leaders in a company, those that should be caring about the culture, to remove such people. Paul Graham described this long ago here - [VIDEO] (starts at 4m20s) - a leader in YCombinator he makes the judgement calls."},{"id":3092142,"author":"Hitchhiker","month":"2011-10","story":"Why female entrepreneurs should apply to YC","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This low rate may also have to do with biases extant in engineering / sciences etc. Couple that with YC's preference for hard-core tech founders ( a perfectly reasonable delimiter ), these results are not surprising. You can see similar composition in YC leadership where Jessica is the only female partner ( [LINK] ) and from my limited understanding, she brings in a much needed balance to their approach. A system that produces great results should perhaps focus on \".. but by the content of their character \" - Martin Luther King, Jr. This I think, pg emphasizes via [VIDEO]"}]},"cO1a1Ek-HD0":{"wy":"HN uses it as the canonical example that evolution is not optimal and can get stuck in local maxima.","q":{"x":"The bit about the recurrent laryngeal nerve of the giraffe is particulary memorable","i":41523035,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41523035,"author":"ufo","month":"2024-09","story":"If I could dissect a sauropod","stance":"rec","why":"Memorable giraffe recurrent laryngeal nerve segment from Inside Nature's Giants","quote":"The bit about the recurrent laryngeal nerve of the giraffe is particulary memorable","text":"The blog post recommended the TV show \"inside nature's giants\", and I second it. The bit about the recurrent laryngeal nerve of the giraffe is particulary memorable: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13770246,"author":"fhoffa","month":"2017-03","story":"Operation Rosehub – patching thousands of open-source projects","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Good catch. It seems like an artifact of working and editing a query until they got what they wanted - certainly not a BigQuery restriction. (like this [VIDEO] ) Note that the published query scans 2.25 TB of data. While impressive, for a better workflow and cost management I would split it into a 2 step process: - First extract all the files I'm interested in to a separate table (all pom.xmls?). - Then run whatever analysis you want over those files."},{"id":10884949,"author":"adevine","month":"2016-01","story":"New finding: 600M years ago, a single mutation changed everything","stance":"reference","why":"Giraffe nerve as example of evolution stuck in local maxima","quote":"Evolution most certainly DOES get stuck in local maxima","text":"Evolution most certainly DOES get stuck in local maxima: [VIDEO] . Also, it's quite easy for me to imagine an animal like an angel, with arms and wings. Tetrapods only have 4 limbs to work with though. For bats to gain wings they had to lose their front legs."},{"id":4972577,"author":"ChrisNorstrom","month":"2012-12","story":"Desktop App for All Your Email","stance":"reference","why":"Giraffe laryngeal nerve as example that form does not always follow function","quote":"Form doesn't always follow function even in nature and evolution","text":"A rule of thumb is not a law. Form doesn't always follow function even in nature and evolution: [VIDEO] Also styles and fads like \"minimalism\" and \"flat design\" are forms that do not follow function but are really forms that enforce function. Which is why they're fads and go away after enough people jump on the bandwagon and realize the fad's flaw and limitations."}]},"dqwpQarrDwk":{"wy":"Used as the accessible reference for skyhook and space tether ideas in HN discussions of Mars transit, launch and space elevators.","q":{"x":"I recently saw a very interesting video on a concept called a","i":21658594,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21658594,"author":"kibwen","month":"2019-11","story":"Domes are overrated","stance":"rec","why":"Skyhook concept might cut Earth-Mars transit time to about two months","quote":"I recently saw a very interesting video on a concept called a","text":"Regarding the time it could take to travel between Earth and Mars, I recently saw a very interesting video on a concept called a \"skyhook\" that suggests it might be able to economically reduce the transit time to \"just\" two months or so, though I assume you'd still want to wait for an appropriate launch window, and in addition it probably wouldn't be operational in time to benefit the first few generations of travelers. :P [VIDEO]"},{"id":42419629,"author":"ENGNR","month":"2024-12","story":"Elon Musk wanted an OpenAI for-profit","stance":"reference","why":"Martian moons as gravity wells for reaching asteroids","quote":"Kurzgesagt seem to think the Martian moons have particular value as central gravity wells","text":"I don’t know how deeply they analysed it, but Kurzgesagt seem to think the Martian moons have particular value as central gravity wells, to then reach further out to mine asteroids [VIDEO]"},{"id":35639085,"author":"limbicsystem","month":"2023-04","story":"Space Elevator","stance":"reference","why":"Skyhooks buildable now, perhaps best on Mars","quote":"we could build useful sky hooks right now","text":"Never mind space elevators - we could build useful sky hooks right now (and Mars might be the best place to do it...) [VIDEO]"},{"id":28129142,"author":"imtringued","month":"2021-08","story":"Bring back hydrogen lifting gas","stance":"reference","why":"Suggests combining skyhooks with balloons for launch","quote":"Combine balloons with skyhooks.","text":"Combine balloons with skyhooks. [VIDEO] Edit: According to proprietary imtringued numbers the hydrogen for a balloon capable of lifting 1kg to 50km would be $800 if you do not reuse the hydrogen. The skyhook would accept vehicles at an altitude of 100km so no, keep balloons out of the space launch industry."},{"id":24540361,"author":"kristianp","month":"2020-09","story":"Space Tethers: Stringing Up the Solar System","stance":"reference","why":"Video and sources referenced from the article","quote":"The video in the article is here","text":"Surprising that no one here has used the term \"skyhook\" in the comments here. Adding it to increase discoverability of this thread. Could SpaceX's starship reach the tether without the superheavy 1st stage? The video in the article is here: [1] and the sources to the video [2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":21558716,"author":"kempbellt","month":"2019-11","story":"Cable to the Stars – The Skyhook [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This part of the video alludes to what I'm getting at.","text":"[VIDEO] Fair point, that would indeed be a problem. This part of the video alludes to what I'm getting at. Granted, they are using a moon as the weighted object, so rotation wouldn't be affected by hook events. I wonder how massive the weighted object would have to be to make rotation changes negligible, or at least manageable."}],"r":[["wIC-iGDTU40",1],["QUcTsFe1PVs",1],["5TFDG-y-EHs",1],["s1i-dnAH9Y4",1],["hB6eY73sLV0",1],["GhsZUZmJvaM",1],["M4AhABManTw",1],["qL0BB2PRY7k",1],["uzkD5SeuwzM",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1]]},"hps-HfpL1vc":{"wy":"Recommended as an accessible layperson introduction to the Hubble tension when new expansion-rate results make the news.","q":{"x":"she introduces laymen to the problems well","i":39678307,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39673895,"author":"harywilke","month":"2024-03","story":"Webb and Hubble confirm Universe's expansion rate","stance":"rec","why":"Covers diverging CMB versus supernova measurements of the expansion rate","quote":"Dr.Becky goes over this in a video [0] from a year ago about the divergent results obtained by the two main ways we measure the rate of expansion.","text":"Dr.Becky goes over this in a video [0] from a year ago about the divergent results obtained by the two main ways we measure the rate of expansion. Cosmic Microwave Background Vs. Supernovae. As the accuracy of each method has improved, the end results have diverged. [0] 'theJWST just made the \"Crisis in Cosmology\" WORSE' [VIDEO]"},{"id":39678307,"author":"jug","month":"2024-03","story":"Webb and Hubble confirm Universe's expansion rate","stance":"rec","why":"Accessible introduction to the Hubble tension and JWST measurements for laymen","quote":"she introduces laymen to the problems well","text":"I can recommend Dr. Becky's introduction to the issues surrounding the Hubble Tension and recent JWST measurements in this 16 minute clip, as she introduces laymen to the problems well: [VIDEO] It's an older clip, but it checks out... ;) (still JWST discoveries and she most importantly introduces the viewer to the still remaining conflicts)"},{"id":34364863,"author":"SkyMarshal","month":"2023-01","story":"The James Webb Space Telescope is finding too many early galaxies","stance":"reference","why":"Supports that expansion-rate measurements disagree and the question is unsettled","quote":"the question seems unsettled at best","text":"Considering the two methods for measuring the rate of the universe’s expansion differ by about 10%, the question seems unsettled at best. You’re not wrong to question it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33951148,"author":"lovelearning","month":"2022-12","story":"Astronomers Just Confirmed the Most Ancient Galaxies Ever Observed","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the crisis in cosmology and doubts about the distance ladder","quote":"the distance ladder itself seems to have come under some doubt recently","text":"> \"we can be absolutely confident of their fantastic distances\" My current layperson's understanding is: 1) Redshift-to-distance conversion is a calibration based on the distance ladder. 2) And the distance ladder itself seems to have come under some doubt recently due to the \"cosmological crisis\" [1]. Can someone here ELI5 how they can be absolutely confident of these distances? [1] \"JWST just made the Crisis in Cosmology WORSE\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["fuFlMtZmvY0",1]]},"i3D7XYQExt0":{"wy":"Used as a short explainer when people need to illustrate why speed matters so much in braking and crash severity.","q":{"x":"There's also a great video I stumbled across yesterday","i":40606030,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40606030,"author":"nox101","month":"2024-06","story":"The right not to be subjected to AI profiling based on publicly available data","stance":"rec","why":"Explains kinetic energy scaling with speed squared for braking","quote":"There's also a great video I stumbled across yesterday","text":"Lol, I think they should be low and stay low and there should be more enforcement For me, I live in a quiet neighborhood. The residential street outside my apartment has speed limit signs of 20mph. People are trying to walk their dogs and kids. 9 out of 10 cars go 40-50mph. Part of that is road design. The road is new, straight, barrier and speed bump free. Worse, if you try to obey the law and go 20 the person behind you will get road rage and then illegally pass on the left (going into the opposite lane) or pass on the right and swerve around to get to the left turn lane. That said, a street a block over is 25mph with speed bump and at least 1 of 3 cars races to each bump. And 1 of 20 just flies over the bumps. This is a street with apartments and condos one one side and a park and elementary school on the other. There might be some roads that don't need enforcement (somewhere in Nevada) but even in outside of the city, say LA to Vegas or SF to LA there's enough traffic that speeders cause accidents. There's also a great video I stumbled across yesterday [VIDEO] Summary: Kinetic energy = 1/2 * mv^2 So if have a car going 70mph and they slam on their breaks to avoid an accident. When they stop they'll have expended 4900 units of kinetic energy. Another car, same weight, going 100mph slams on the breaks. When their car has expended 4900 units of kinetic energy they still have another 5100 units to get rid before their car will stop because 70^2 = 4900, and 100^2 = 10000. In o"},{"id":48693722,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2026-06","story":"Why does kinetic energy increase quadratically, not linearly, with speed? (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"Numberphile example of crash speeds and energy","quote":"","text":"Fun little anecdote: A blue care is travelling along at 70 units, and a red car (exact same make and model) is catching up to it going 100. When they're both right beside each other a bend in the road reveals an obstacle blocking both lanes, so both cars brake at the same intensity and deceleration. The blue care stops right before the obstacle. Since the red car was going at a faster speed, and braked at the same rate, it doesn't managae to stop: but what speed is it going when it hits the obstacle? The blue car, using ½mv², shed (~70²=) 4900 units of energy (we'll hand wave away the constants). So the red car, which had (100²=) 10000 units of kinetic energy to start, also shed 4900 units, which means it had 5100 units of energy when it collided, and so was going (√5100~) 71. * Numberphile: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25221921,"author":"leipert","month":"2020-11","story":"For the first time in Europe, registrations for EVs overtook diesel","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how velocity affects stopping distance","quote":"Also a lot of folks do not understand the relationship between velocity and the time you need to come to a stop.","text":"If it's your hobby and like driving 200 km/h, sure please do, on a separate track where it is as safe as possible. But driving it on _public_ roads is inherently dangerous to others. Our society has all kind of rules around protection of others. There are laws and rules around food safety, gun safety, distribution of drugs, hunting, using heavy equipment, operating boats and planes. Often you need a license to do these things. Noone trains for driving at high speeds in driving school. Also a lot of folks do not understand the relationship between velocity and the time you need to come to a stop. So 120km/h is way better than 200km/h. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16619554,"author":"Balgair","month":"2018-03","story":"Self-driving cars will cause sprawl","stance":"reference","why":"Explains kinetic energy and stopping distance in crashes","quote":"Numberphile had a recent video on car crashes here","text":"Numberphile had a recent video on car crashes here: [VIDEO] In short: Since kinetic energy goes as (velocity)^2, doubling the speed will quadruple the stopping distance (MANY other factors are significant, this is a rough estimate). 3X of the speed is ~9X the stopping distance, etc. NHTSB guidelines for 'proper' following distance are nearly never seen in LA and NYC, it's typically much shorter. If anything, L5 AVs will probably have longer gaps between AVs than what is currently on the road. Likely, the major improvement of L5 AVs will be in safety, not congestion. It turns out (I think), you can't technology your way out of a political problem. We need higher density housing, not magic carpets (effectively)."}]},"jeRgFqbBM5E":{"wy":"Pointed to as a clear treatment of the universe-in-a-black-hole idea whenever black hole cosmology comes up.","q":{"x":"was a huge relief and interesting dive into the q","i":33901859,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33901859,"author":"zoroaster","month":"2022-12","story":"Ask HN: Is it black holes all the way up?","stance":"rec","why":"A relieving and interesting dive into whether the universe is in a black hole.","quote":"was a huge relief and interesting dive into the q","text":"Your first link and one of the videos there ( [VIDEO] ) was a huge relief and interesting dive into the q - thanks again for sending it."},{"id":36530118,"author":"dotnet00","month":"2023-06","story":"Physicists Produce Neutrino Images of Milky Way Galaxy","stance":"reference","why":"Covers why the universe-in-a-black-hole idea lacks explanatory value.","quote":"PBS Spacetime covered this topic","text":"PBS Spacetime covered this topic: [VIDEO] Basically, with our current understanding, the only thing that makes the big bang and black holes seem connected is that they're both singularities. But, there's no explanatory value associated with the idea of the universe being the inside of a black hole, and thus there's no way to attempt to test it."},{"id":30872788,"author":"westurner","month":"2022-03","story":"Black Holes Shown to Act Like Quantum Particles","stance":"reference","why":"One of several PBS Space Time videos listed on related topics.","quote":"","text":"@PBSSpaceTime videos: \"Where Is The Center of The Universe?\" (2022) [LINK] \"Could The Universe Be Inside A Black Hole?\" (2022) [VIDEO] \"The Holographic Universe Explained\" (2019) [LINK] \"Are Black Holes Actually Fuzzballs?\" (2022) [LINK]"}]},"juWaO5TJS00":{"wy":"HN people link it as an example of a transformable small apartment in threads on micro-apartments and space-saving furniture.","q":{"x":"I like this guy's sliding lego house","i":3801676,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":3801676,"author":"zerostar07","month":"2012-04","story":"Inside Aaron’s Apartment: A Most Amazing Bedroom ","stance":"rec","why":"Sliding lego-style apartment tour.","quote":"I like this guy's sliding lego house","text":"What about bedsheets, pillows etc. Also it seems it is hygienic to let the mattress air dry. I like this guy's sliding lego house: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18070254,"author":"buboard","month":"2018-09","story":"Robot Furniture","stance":"reference","why":"Motorless transforming apartment as alternative to robotic furniture.","quote":"","text":"Polymorphic homes maybe, but what's robotic about these? A robot would self-clean for example. this accomplishes the same and doesnt require any motors: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4637927,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2012-10","story":"Show HN: UrbanDesk - Combination Full Size Bed and Desk","stance":"reference","why":"Similar transforming-space design.","quote":"Some similar designs","text":"Some similar designs: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] As others have noted, the inability to leave items (particularly monitors) on the desk is a major downside. I'm a fan of the collegiate solution: a loft bed. This is particularly suitable where high ceilings are available, especially cathedral or A-frame designs."},{"id":4248636,"author":"malandrew","month":"2012-07","story":"Micro-apartments next for S.F.?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a practical pull-out bed.","quote":"","text":"The only thing I found weird about the article was the developers product development decisions based on a single female MIT student as opposed to talking to lots of people who are the target for a product like this, especially since he plans on putting it in SoMa and targeting the tech crowd which is largely male. This isn't sexism, it's just knowing the audience. For example, the pull out bed in this apartment seems far more practical than a Murphy bed that really only works if you make your bed properly so it's not subject to the perils of gravity: [VIDEO] The other decision was the move to a tub style shower. The only people I know that that really take baths are women. Why? Because they fit in tubs comfortably. I can't think of any men who habitually take baths but know many women that do. Tub-size is probably a big contributing factor as to why. TBH, if someone is living in apartment like this, they value practicality over everything else. If that is the case, a bathroom space optimized for cleaning and maintenance is more ideal, such as the bathroom in this micro-apt, where the toilet, sink area and shower all share the same tiled floor [LINK] Anyways, really shortsighted product management in my view given the location and target market. If I were a single woman, SoMa is not a neighborhood I would live in. There is not enough foot-traffic and decent lighting at night to feel really safe."}]},"jxNM4DGBRMU":{"wy":"Pointed to as a detailed engineering walkthrough of a major dam incident, and as a parallel to software failures.","q":{"x":"Practical engineering has a good detailed walkthrough of what happened at Oroville dam from an engineering perspective","i":36530281,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36530281,"author":"trestacos","month":"2023-06","story":"What If California's Dams Fail?","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed engineering walkthrough of Oroville dam failure.","quote":"Practical engineering has a good detailed walkthrough of what happened at Oroville dam from an engineering perspective","text":"Practical engineering has a good detailed walkthrough of what happened at Oroville dam from an engineering perspective: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46836146,"author":"Mawr","month":"2026-01","story":"Automatic Programming","stance":"reference","why":"Example of civil engineers messing up, like software.","quote":"Also, civil engineers mess up in exactly the same ways.","text":"> making it default is often a huge waste of $ for problems you/most don’t have. It's the opposite — knowing the exact spec of your program up front is vanishingly rare, probably <1% of all projects. Usually you have no clue what you're doing, just a vague goal. The only way to find out what to build is to build something , toss it over to the users and see what happens. No developer or, dear god, \"stakeholder\" can possibly know what the users need. Asking the users up front is better, but still doesn't help much — they don't know what they want either. No plan survives first contact with the enemy and there's no substitute for testing — reality is far too complex for you to be able to model it up front. > You don’t iterate the foundation of a house. You do, actually. Or rather, we have — over thousands of years we've iterated and written up what we've learned so that nobody has to iterate from scratch for every new house anymore. It's just that our physics, environment, and requirements for \"a house\" doesn't change constantly, like it does for software and we've had thousands of years to perfect the craft, not some 50 years. Also, civil engineers mess up in exactly the same ways. Who needs testing? [1]. Who needs to iterate as they're building? [2]. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32527871,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2022-08","story":"Serving Netflix Video Traffic at 800Gb/s and Beyond [pdf]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I know how to operate a hose! How hard can it be to manage a large stream? It's \"just\" more water: [VIDEO]"}]},"mGQLXRTl3Z0":{"wy":"Offered as a well-known, accessible example of Bach's appeal.","q":{"x":"What's not to like in these e.g.?","i":16558619,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16558619,"author":"coldtea","month":"2018-03","story":"Can any composer equal Bach?","stance":"rec","why":"Bach cello suite offered as proof of Bach's appeal.","quote":"What's not to like in these e.g.?","text":"> but every time I get to Bach, I stop short. I just can't stand the music. What's not to like in these e.g.? [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] > I understand the music is important historically and that it has a technical beauty Historically? Technical beauty only? Huh?"},{"id":40475462,"author":"qwerty456127","month":"2024-05","story":"Show HN: MusicGPT – An Open Source App for Generating Music with Local LLMs","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm very glad this finally becomes available. I immediately asked it to do what I wanted for almost a decade: generate 30 minutes of Bach Cello Suite No.1 in G Prelude[1] (I want to listen to it for hours but don't want a loop neither do I want the whole original - only the prelude, subtle variations are welcome). The result was 9 seconds of ear horror. Do I need better hardware to get a listenable result (I ran it on a Ryzen laptop)? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11920315,"author":"bane","month":"2016-06","story":"Ten years on, revisiting YouTube’s first viral sensation","stance":"reference","why":"Bach cello suite as starting point for instrument covers.","quote":"here's a well known Bach Suite","text":"I agree in some sense, an astonishing number of Bach pieces have been rewritten for a bewildering array of instruments and sound absolutely amazing. But Bach is kind of like Math, it's true regardless if it's written in chalk on a chalk board or in mustard on an umbrella. Yet there's also plenty of great music that really only sounds good on the instrument, something about it is very \"subjective\" with respect to the particulars of the orchestration. I'll sometimes spend hours digging into covers of favorite songs on youtube, and it really is amazing how different kinds of music can survive this kind of instrument shifting. just some sharing here's a well known Bach Suite [VIDEO] now on guitar [LINK] on piano [LINK] flute [LINK] on Nyckelharpa [LINK] electric guitar [LINK] another electric guitar [LINK] Baritone Saxophone [LINK] Michael Hedges [LINK] Bass Guitar [LINK] Recorder [LINK] Tuba [LINK] Banjo [LINK] Rock version [LINK] Bobby McFerrin (not quite the same, but still...) [LINK] Pipe Organ [LINK] Marimba [LINK] Ukulele [LINK] Clarinet [LINK] Dubstep [LINK] Mandolin [LINK] Harp [LINK] Horn [LINK] Chapman Stick [LINK] Bassoon [LINK] Accordion [LINK] Trombone [LINK] It survives completely intact across instrument types, performance arenas, recording methods, synthesis, voice, octave...it's absolutely astonishing."}],"r":[["u404SLJj7ig",1],["Uo08uS904Rg",1],["S-Xm7s9eGxU",1]]},"mZBwsm6B280":{"wy":"Cited in threads on random sampling as a clear illustration of how reasonable-sounding random choices can give biased results.","q":{"x":"there's a Numberphile / 3B1B video about the pitfalls of plotting randomness in a circle","i":35957324,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":31513215,"author":"Dave_Rosenthal","month":"2022-05","story":"Uniformly Sample Points in a Disk","stance":"rec","why":"Bertrand's paradox on random chords of a circle.","quote":"it does remind me of a nice 3Blue1Brown video","text":"So points in a circle is pretty basic, but it does remind me of a nice 3Blue1Brown video ( [VIDEO] ) about random chords of a circle. Some of these 'generate a random sample' situations can be quite non-obvious. edit: The video is about 'Bertrand's Paradox' that another post references."},{"id":39607627,"author":"olliej","month":"2024-03","story":"How to generate uniformly random points on n-spheres and in n-balls","stance":"reference","why":"Bertrand's paradox illustrates biased results from reasonable random choices.","quote":"Bertrand's paradox which I was introduced to via numberphile/3blue1brown","text":"I was always surprised at how easily you get biased sampling when generating random points despite all input - something I often saw students do was essentially normalize({2 rand() - 1, 2 rand() - 1, 2 rand() - 1}) or variations of that (where rand() is \"good\" not the literal rand(3)), and there are numerous other ways that are more subtly wrong. IIRC the nominally correct way for a sphere specifically is something like a=rand() b=rand() and the random point is something* like { cos(a) sin(b), sin(b), cos(a) cos(b) }[1] I think the best illustration of \"reasonable choices of what random values should be used leading to biased results\" is Bertrand's paradox which I was introduced to via numberphile/3blue1brown: [VIDEO] and am just glad that nothing I have ever needed random sampling for has ever been important :D [1] please don't use this blindly, I'm really just going off very old recollection, if you need it google random sphere sampling :D"},{"id":35957324,"author":"zootboy","month":"2023-05","story":"Random(Random(Random(Random())))","stance":"reference","why":"Pitfalls of plotting randomness in a circle.","quote":"there's a Numberphile / 3B1B video about the pitfalls of plotting randomness in a circle","text":"In a related vein, there's a Numberphile / 3B1B video about the pitfalls of plotting randomness in a circle: [VIDEO]"}]},"1FwgCRIS0Wg":{"wy":"HN cites it as the source for the argument that safe withdrawal rates should be lower than 4%.","q":{"x":"For early retirees, Ben Felix has found the SWR to be closer to 2.7%.","i":34135448,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":43325225,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2025-03","story":"What We've Learned from 150 Years of Stock Market Crashes","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> What if your hypothesis is that the fundamentals have changed? There's been recent research on survivorship bias in market returns, especially with regards to the US: > Using international data, we quantify the magnitude of survivorship bias in U.S. equity market performance, and find that it explains about one third of the equity risk premium in the past century. We model the subjective crash belief of an investor who infers the crash risk in the U.S.~by cross learning from other countries. The U.S. crash probability shows a persistent and widening divergence from the implied global average. We attribute the upward bias in the measured equity premium to crashes that did not occur in-sample and to positive shocks to valuations resulting from learning about the probability. * [LINK] * [VIDEO] I'm Canadian, so I have a different home market, but that is correlated to the US economy (cf. tariffs), but my portfolio (TSX:VGRO) also has non-US/CA equities."},{"id":34765016,"author":"kwere","month":"2023-02","story":"My Fifth Year as a Bootstrapped Founder","stance":"reference","why":"Safer withdrawal rates than the 4% rule","quote":"a safer rate would be 2/2.5%[0]","text":"4% is specific to 60/40 US portfolio with 30 years horizon to not die poor at the end of it , a safer rate would be 2/2.5%[0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34135448,"author":"cko","month":"2022-12","story":"Money, Money, Money (and Investing) (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Safe withdrawal rate for early retirees","quote":"For early retirees, Ben Felix has found the SWR to be closer to 2.7%.","text":"For early retirees, Ben Felix has found the SWR to be closer to 2.7%. The oft-cited 4% depends on US data and studied 30-year time horizons. [VIDEO]"}]},"3kxG8uJUsWU":{"wy":"Cited as a primary source for what was actually said, in arguments about Russia and the 2016 election.","m":[{"id":29736297,"author":"throwawaylinux","month":"2021-12","story":"North Korea's Loch Ness Monster (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Available evidence strongly suggests Trump and his campaign coordinated with representatives of Russian government interests that involved both significant lawbreaking and a targeted media campaign that resulted in a victory for Trump that would not have occurred otherwise. Whether that's semantically equivalent to \"colluded with Putin to cheat in the election\" I don't know. The Mueller report disagrees. What available evidence exactly do you have which the special council investigation did not? Cite your sources please. > What you're saying, is itself an example of propaganda, in the sense that it's a simplification that while technically true leads a reader to reach a different conclusion that what actually occurred. And it's easy to do, because reality is complicated while disinformation can be simple. No it's not, what I am saying is almost verbatim from the Mueller report. No evidence was found implicating Trump or his campaign of colluding (conspiring) with Russians. I can dig out quotes from it verbatim or cite page numbers for you if you have not read it or disagree. EDIT: I have done so in another reply, if you are interested. > No, Donald Trump did not sit down in a hotel or pick up a phone and directly conspire with Vladimir Putin to scheme about how to change vote totals. Strawman, that is not what was being argued against. > Here's what is known to have happened: > Trump publicly asked Russia for help [LINK] ... Firstly, Rachel Maddow has a very poor and biased"},{"id":27170621,"author":"spankyspangler","month":"2021-05","story":"FBI Has Gained Access to Sci-Hub Founder's Apple Account, Email Claims","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Clearly a joke at the expense of the ineptitude of his opponent's campaign.","text":"This? [VIDEO] Clearly a joke at the expense of the ineptitude of his opponent's campaign. The fact people point to this as their \"evidence\" is pretty clearly desperation and self-delusion. There was no evidence. You were lied to."},{"id":22207637,"author":"dane-pgp","month":"2020-02","story":"Humans are hardwired to dismiss facts that don’t fit their worldview","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the media was also talking about Russia before the election too, and so was Trump himself","text":"To be fair, the media was also talking about Russia before the election too, and so was Trump himself: [VIDEO] Also, I'm not sure what you mean by \"didn't stick as planned\": [LINK] [LINK] but I'm glad we both agree about the correctness of the article title."},{"id":17751215,"author":"factshurtfeels","month":"2018-08","story":"US elections remain 'dangerously vulnerable' to cyber-attacks","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"that is precisely what he said, verbatim and clear as day on national television","text":"Ad hom nonsense. What's discrediting to journalistic integrity about stating the fact that he stated this? His \"intent\" doesn't matter, that is precisely what he said, verbatim and clear as day on national television: [VIDEO]"}]},"4pbAI40dK0A":{"wy":"Cited as the canonical visualization of signal-free intersections whenever HN discusses autonomous cars and road throughput.","q":{"x":"you can just have the traffic streams pass right through each other.","i":11988054,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":35843217,"author":"xnx","month":"2023-05","story":"Waymo doubles service area in Phoenix Metro","stance":"reference","why":"Extreme example of high throughput with better drivers.","quote":"The true throughput of roads is immense when utilized by better drivers","text":"Traffic isn't a function of road space. The true throughput of roads is immense when utilized by better drivers (one extreme example: [VIDEO] ). A lot of traffic is caused by bad human drivers: accidents (and associated rubbernecking), shockwaves, running red lights, double parking, circling around looking for parking. Additionally entire additional lanes of traffic (for cars or personal mobility vehicles) will be available when streetside parking isn't necessary. Personally I look forward to the possibilities of turning all that unecessary parking space into something useful (\"we’re the Saudi Arabia of developable land in cities\")."},{"id":13274516,"author":"beefield","month":"2016-12","story":"Ebikes: I Sing the Ride Electric","stance":"reference","why":"Cars communicating could raise intersection capacity.","quote":"by somehow communicating with each others like this","text":"> I can't help but think about how much this would alleviate traffic congestion Especially given that I do not see how self driving cars could decrease traffic congestion problems. A self driving car takes as much space as a \"regular car\", so only ways I can imagine autonomous cars reducing traffic are: 1. People ride less with cars. 2. People share rides more. 3. Autonomous cars are significantly more efficient finding free parking spaces, so they reduce the amount of cars looking for a free parking spot. 4. They do not need to be parked, so parking space is given to moving cars (and this actually helps, not increases, congestion). 5. Autonomous cars can drive in less space and/or are smaller. Of course, any of these claims being wrong attribute to increased congestion due to autonomous cars. And to be honest, I find it unlikely that any of those claims would be correct in the sense that it would materially reduce the congestion problems. (Well, maybe one more option came to my mind that might actually help. If the autonomous cars would be able to increase the capacity of crossroads by somehow communicating with each others like this: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":13274644,"author":"chris_7","month":"2016-12","story":"Ebikes: I Sing the Ride Electric","stance":"reference","why":"Asks about cyclists and pedestrians in such intersections.","quote":"How do cyclists and people walking handle that type of intersection?","text":"> (Well, maybe one more option came to my mind that might actually help. If the autonomous cars would be able to increase the capacity of crossroads by somehow communicating with each others like this: [VIDEO] ) How do cyclists and people walking handle that type of intersection?"},{"id":11988054,"author":"coryfklein","month":"2016-06","story":"New Cities","stance":"reference","why":"Autonomous intersection management eliminating stopping.","quote":"you can just have the traffic streams pass right through each other.","text":"> Self-driving cars ... could change this equation by intelligently routing cars around bottlenecks and avoiding the neighborhood entirely Self-driving cars can do even better than that - they can eliminate the \"stopping\" nature of the intersection entirely. If the self-driving cars are able to determine the position and speed of other cars approaching the intersection, you can just have the traffic streams pass right through each other. [1] [1] Autonomous Intersection Management: Traffic Control for the Future [VIDEO]"},{"id":10481157,"author":"omegaworks","month":"2015-10","story":"Tesla autopilot stops a collision [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Simulation of cars passing through intersections without stopping.","quote":"we'll see continuous-flow intersections where no human driver would dare try and cross.","text":"It's very likely that once self-driving cars reach a certain penetration, we'll see continuous-flow intersections where no human driver would dare try and cross. [VIDEO]"}]},"7xH7eGFuSYI":{"wy":"Linked in threads on pay and compensation as a quick explanation of why talking about salary helps employees.","m":[{"id":12864393,"author":"aq3cn","month":"2016-11","story":"LinkedIn launches Salary to chart what we earn","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why telling coworkers your salary helps.","quote":"Adam Ruins Everything - Why You Should Tell Coworkers Your Salary","text":"I read an article from Lifehacker about being transparent of your salary. Check out what are the benefits. [LINK] Adam Ruins Everything - Why You Should Tell Coworkers Your Salary [VIDEO]"},{"id":11327418,"author":"FloNeu","month":"2016-03","story":"My Passion Was My Weak Spot","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as support for fair compensation argument.","quote":"","text":"Sorry, but that's stupid... Being exploited and loving it - more sounds like an mental illness then passion. You can keep your passion - even when you get paid fairly... But enjoy your happiness in slavery, if you like. Bad thing is you hurt people who may need fair compensation (wife, kids etc. ) for their work, because you show the suits they can find people stupid enough to do so... [VIDEO]"},{"id":11154603,"author":"Eyes2design","month":"2016-02","story":"Yelp Fired an Employee After She Wrote a Post About Her Lousy Pay","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why telling coworkers your salary is legal.","quote":"SEE A Adam Ruins Everything (Why You Should Tell Coworkers Your Salary)","text":"Stating your pay is not illegal. In fact, Yelp Violates the State Employer laws by firing her on that account. No Terms of Service should ever interfere/break with state law or federal law. Yelp may be in trouble for that claim. SEE A Adam Ruins Everything (Why You Should Tell Coworkers Your Salary) [VIDEO]"}]},"BKm8FolQ7jw":{"wy":"HN commenters bring it up as proof that laser mosquito defense is technically feasible, in threads about DIY pest-zapping projects.","m":[{"id":29753999,"author":"smiley1437","month":"2022-01","story":"Predict 3D position of flying bugs and pest to shoot down them with a laser","stance":"reference","why":"Amazing slow-motion laser shootdowns of mosquitoes from Intellectual Ventures in 2010.","quote":"","text":"Intellectual Ventures made it seem promising enough in 2010, the slow-motion shootdowns are pretty amazing [VIDEO] Guess there were some obstacles that couldn't be overcome"},{"id":27552516,"author":"mLuby","month":"2021-06","story":"How many flies are in my apartment?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows laser mosquito-killing tech exists in theory but has no commercial product.","quote":"","text":"I want a mosquito-killing turret [1] that you could put in your bedroom and sleep in peace. Turns out one exists [2] (in theory—no commercial product a decade later), but the one I was thinking of would use microphones rather than cameras [3] to detect the bloody suckers. Thing is, I can't find any prior research or even hints that people have considered this approach. (The closest I can find is an app [4] that detects a mosquito from ≤10cm away.) Here are some of my guesses for why it's not been done: A. Maybe it's too difficult to distinguish mosquito buzz/whine from background noise, even when filtering in only known mosquito frequencies (~400Hz [5]). B. Maybe it's too slow to sweep a room with a directional mic that's focused enough to detect mosquito sounds? (Say the turret in one ceiling corner of a 12x12x8ft room.) C. Maybe directional mics don't work well for all sound in their cone and so need to be able to \"focus\" at different distances (by dynamically changing the parabolic dish's shape), thereby increasing the room sweep time? D. Maybe the parabolic dish needs to be so big that it just looks ridiculous? E. Maybe all of this is possible but the type of microphone is so expensive that it's worth pursuing commercially? I'm curious what you all think about this. It'd be an interesting thing to try making—who hasn't wanted to make a death ray while simultaneously furthering the fight against humanity's deadliest enemy? [6] See also this research paper: Detecting Insect"},{"id":26378218,"author":"janoc","month":"2021-03","story":"Raspberry Pi for Kill Mosquitoes by Laser","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Good luck detecting a mosquito optically from a distance of several meters using a cheap camera and Raspberry Pi. Oh and you want to do from a moving drone. That will certainly make it work! Just look at the images in the article - the guy's best result was detecting a black speck appearing on a nearby white wall with some 60-70% reliability (based on his own numbers). So you would be missing a lot of mosquitoes - but will be happy firing the laser at random shadows and what not. And that was in a completely stationary setup and controlled lab conditions, i.e. not at all something resembling a typical poorly lit room! This article is BS. Preprints are not peer reviewed (i.e. nobody has checked anything in it - so could even be a complete hoax), it is a pretty typical gadgetry style paper (we do it because we can, not because it makes sense) you do at when you need to fill up your resume with research papers (e.g. for keeping/obtaining a job reasons). The \"save the world\" (mosquito control, diseases, etc.) justification is also par for the course for this type of crappy paper. Anyone who seriously thinks that one could control mosquito problem by shooting them one by one by a laser is delusional. But neural networks and \"AI\" are being used, so it has to be cutting edge groundbreaking stuff, right? BTW, this nonsense idea has been floated as a publicity stunt a few years ago (including a slow motion video of a laser burning off wing of a mosquito in flight) and it seems that so"}]},"C_78DM8fG6E":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a primary source for Brockman's views, such as moving fast to reduce risk, and for capability anecdotes like GPT-4's arithmetic.","m":[{"id":38375767,"author":"upwardbound","month":"2023-11","story":"What is meant by AI \"safety\"?","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Greg Brockman's view on moving fast to reduce risk.","quote":"I've only heard it articulated by Greg Brockman so let's call it Brockism","text":"> is the “more safety” camp saying to slow down AI development? This is an EXCELLENT question and the answer is very very nuanced. Warning: the definitions of the terms I'm about to use are actively evolving in current discourse. The following taxonomy reflects my own views, specifically with the inclusion of faction 4: --- There are basically four competing factions. (1) The \"normal\" faction which includes Sam Altman and which basically says that we can deal with x-risk later and right now let's make money and continue life as normal. (2) The \"decel\" faction (short for \"decelerate\"), which includes most old-school AI safety folks such as Ilya, Helen, and Tasha. Sometimes you see these people with a \"pause button\" emoji or \"stop sign\" emoji in their Twitter name. (3) The \"e/acc\" faction (short for \"effective accelerationists\"). This faction is a mix of fanatical techno-utopians (like Yann LeCun and Andrew Ng), mixed with a bunch of Twitter people who post macho memes and have a \"lol let's watch the world burn\" sort of attitude. Those people are in my view very similar to the young people from 4chan who voted for Trump over Hillary in 2016 because they thought that a Trump presidency would be hilarious. (4) The newest faction doesn't even have a name. I've only heard it articulated by Greg Brockman so let's call it Brockism. This faction is very new and it actually has me reconsidering my own beliefs. Brockists believe that the safest way to reduce x-risk is to move as fast as"},{"id":35929163,"author":"dave84","month":"2023-05","story":"Ask HN: Why can GPT4 en/decode base64?","stance":"reference","why":"TED talk noting GPT-4 adds long numbers imperfectly.","quote":"GPT-4 can for example add 40 digit numbers perfectly, but makes mistakes on adding a 40 digit and a 35 digit number","text":"Based on my understanding from the recent TED talk [0] they mention that GPT-4 can for example add 40 digit numbers perfectly, but makes mistakes on adding a 40 digit and a 35 digit number. It hasn’t worked out a generic understanding of arithmetic but several smaller case specific ones which may not always be correct. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36003034,"author":"danesparza","month":"2023-05","story":"Here’s How AI Will Come for Your Job","stance":"reference","why":"Example of ChatGPT's ongoing iteration.","quote":"ChatGPT is already iterating","text":"What the author is describing is simply employers resetting their expectations of employable skills. I've personally seen this happen about 3-4 times over the course of my 30 year career as a software developer. Frankly, the author's tone strikes me as alarmist (and a bit whiny). Yes -- it's uncomfortable. But it's also necessary. Learn the tools well enough to be comfortable with them. And get ready for more change. ChatGPT is already iterating: [VIDEO] and it's not the only General AI tool widely available."},{"id":35749309,"author":"williamstein","month":"2023-04","story":"For accountants, AI is like spreadsheets in the 1980s","stance":"reference","why":"TED talk by OpenAI cofounder on ChatGPT querying data.","quote":"This was the subject of a PR talk last week be an openai cofounder","text":"Presumably ChatGPT is better at writing database queries or answering questions about a large Csv file than somebody who doesn’t know how to program. This was the subject of a PR talk last week be an openai cofounder - [VIDEO] - my guess is something like that is what inspired this article."}]},"CqiL5QQnN64":{"wy":"Cited as a source for the fact that OpenAI uses Ray for training, and for Brockman's views on developer speed.","q":{"x":"OpenAI fireside chat on the evolution of their infrastructure and usage of Ray for training","i":34758378,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37311273,"author":"waleedk","month":"2023-08","story":"Llama 2 is about as factually accurate as GPT-4 for summaries and is 30X cheaper","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence OpenAI uses Ray for training","quote":"OpenAI uses Ray, the open source version of Anyscale Platform, for training their models","text":"[Author] OpenAI uses Ray, the open source version of Anyscale Platform, for training their models (video evidence: [VIDEO] ). Ray ( [LINK] ) is available for anyone to use. You can also use Aviary ( [LINK] ) to serve any of those models yourself."},{"id":36316057,"author":"sashank_1509","month":"2023-06","story":"Speed matters: Why working quickly is more important than it seems","stance":"reference","why":"Brockman says developer speed matters most","quote":"Developer Speed is the most important thing I care about","text":"Consistently I see this advice from everyone who I consider has achieved outside success in their field. Greg Brockman (OpenAI Cofounder) : Developer Speed is the most important thing I care about: ( [VIDEO] ). I believe he said more about this topic but I can't find it now. Frank Slootman (Snowflake CEO): In his book Amp It Up, he stresses why pushing people to move fast is extremely important Elon Musk (Let's not get into a debate about Elon and just agree that he has helped setup 2 100bn+ companies) : \"I believe in maniacal sense of urgency, if you can implement something right after the meeting, do that\", upon his work philosophy when taking over twitter. In my personal experience one of the most impressive coders I met, worked extremely fast while also being very accurate, almost indicating that no tradeoff even exists."},{"id":34758378,"author":"robertnishihara","month":"2023-02","story":"How to Train Large Models on Many GPUs?","stance":"reference","why":"OpenAI fireside chat on infrastructure and Ray for training","quote":"OpenAI fireside chat on the evolution of their infrastructure and usage of Ray for training","text":"I'm one of the Ray developers, thanks for the shoutout :) If you're curious about how Ray is used for LLMs, here are some interesting examples of LLM projects using Ray! - Alpa does training and serving with 175B parameter models [LINK] - GPT-J [LINK] - Another HN thread on training LLMs with Ray (on TPUs in this case) [LINK] - OpenAI fireside chat on the evolution of their infrastructure and usage of Ray for training [VIDEO] - Cohere on their architecture for training LLMs [LINK] Some other thoughts 1. There is a lot more we want to do to make Ray better for working with large language models and for making training, serving, and batch inference work well out of the box. 2. The original post is about training, but we actually see even more interest in fine-tuning and serving with LLMs, in part because there are good pre-trained models. 3. For LLMs, we see a lot of interest in Ray + Jax or Ray + TPUs relative to what we see in other use cases."}]},"DbKNlFcg02c":{"wy":"Cited as a short explainer when Antarctic governance comes up, including as an analogy for governing Mars.","q":{"x":"Plenty of countries have claimed overlapping areas of Antarctica.","i":11392928,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":32377691,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2022-08","story":"Political Situation once Mars gets colonized","stance":"reference","why":"Antarctica's land management as analogy for Mars governance","quote":"","text":"In my opinion before we even get to that point Elon and others will first have to figure out how to include only people with psychological and physical profiles conducive to such a lifestyle. Even one unstable person could cause entire pods or groups of pods to be destroyed and mass evacuations, assuming evacuation is feasible. Limited versions of these experiments have occurred on earth with sub-optimal results. There are also some long term low-gravity induced health issues that will have to be addressed and that is work-in-progress. Here [1] was some recent discussion on this as well. I suspect that initially there will be teams of robots belonging to different governments and maybe a small handful of astronaut engineers managing them locally with bigger teams back here on earth. I envision robot-vs-robot sabotage leading to problems here on earth. The same may occur with asteroid mining despite there being vast numbers of asteroids to mine. At some point there will probably be more space-soldiers and defensive equipment than engineers. The folks that wrote the fictional series Star Trek had the optimistic view that humans would clash in a major world war in 2024 and then all get along swimmingly afterwards. I do not share their optimism but then my view would have entirely changed the Star Trek series into a different kind of show. To really answer your question I think we would have to fully understand how the land in Antarctica is managed and how we ended up with people"},{"id":11392928,"author":"nommm-nommm","month":"2016-03","story":"Climate Model Predicts West Antarctic Ice Sheet Could Melt Rapidly","stance":"reference","why":"Overlapping claims and a non-permanent Antarctic treaty","quote":"Plenty of countries have claimed overlapping areas of Antarctica.","text":"Plenty of countries have claimed overlapping areas of Antarctica. Currently an international treaty reserves Antarctica for mostly science and nature (no military, no mining) but the treaty isn't permanent. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10869081,"author":"jack9","month":"2016-01","story":"The Great Republican Land Heist","stance":"reference","why":"Example of treaties not always being followed","quote":"even after exploring existing treaties","text":"> The government owns all land until it is transferred/sold to a private owner. That's not a new thing: that precedent goes back to Day 1 of the United States, and maybe further I think you are talking about the fluidity of borders and lands worldwide. In reality, country borders are based on if land can be defended from other claimants ( [LINK] ). This is the final arbitration, even after exploring existing treaties (which arent always followed, [VIDEO] ). The idea that the US federal government owns land until it's disseminated is one of those american myths that the US Govt tries REALLY hard to let alone, despite most federal lands being originally acquired by fiat and in the last 200 years by eminent domain (sometimes a front for private parties like the railway barons). Almost any private vs federal case can raise issues that can be elevated to the supreme court where the USG has a lot more to lose, regardless of the outcome. So they make lots of small steps, wisely. When they run into a case, even if they lose, the USG will simply work around the small setback."}]},"Dp4dpeJVDxs":{"wy":"Cited whenever a discussion turns to how hard it is to answer \"why\" questions or what counts as an explanation.","q":{"x":"Feynman, of course, has a pretty definitive response on the difficulty of this problem","i":41612323,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41612323,"author":"deanCommie","month":"2024-09","story":"What Is a Particle? (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman's response on the difficulty of explaining why","quote":"Feynman, of course, has a pretty definitive response on the difficulty of this problem","text":"The same is true about the terms \"waves\" and \"fields\" when it comes to quantum mechanics. They're analogies. The concepts need names, but I think they do more harm than good because people then start with a mental model of a membrane or a surface - something they have experience seeing waves in. And then after 1 or 2 steps where the analogy helps, it breaks down, and people start being confused. Of course the alternative isn't any better. If they had named it a \"Wazoo function\" and a \"Quantum Flarg\" everyone would've just kept asking \"OK but what IS a Wazoo? What IS a Flarg\" and not been satisfied with a \"Yeah, it's a fundamental own thing\". Feynman, of course, has a pretty definitive response on the difficulty of this problem: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20523227,"author":"acqq","month":"2019-07","story":"What If Consciousness Comes First?","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on why questions and explanation","quote":"","text":"> Why is there subjective experience? It evolved in animals because it is advantageous. It is known that even the simplest worms can make a difference between the part of their own body and the body of the other worm or the rest of the environment: they would eat another worm but not their own tail. Worms eating their body are typically not having an advantage (but the local optimums of scarifying a part of the body can of course still develop). So the \"subjective experience\" of distinguishing between \"me\" and \"anything else\" allows the living things to protect themselves from the rest of the environment. Then if you question \"why are there living things\" the answer is again that they evolved exactly because the property of these units \"preserving\" themselves and duplicating that behavior -- these entities which don't have such properties remain lifeless and don't reproduce. In short, a \"subjective experience\" is still a \"simple\" emerging property of the complex enough living organisms. I intentionally quote \"simple\" as for us it is \"of course\" not simple, even if it can be reduced to such a simple explanation. The reason for that is again known today: the existence of life as we know it today is a result of the 14 billion years of the development of the environment during which effectively each moment and every detail happening just a bit different could have resulted in what we know as our environment not existing in this very form we know today and therefore \"we\" as we und"},{"id":10721051,"author":"tedsanders","month":"2015-12","story":"The Fed’s imminent interest-rate decision – Is America ready for lift-off?","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on difficulty of answering why questions","quote":"Feynman has a famous interview segment where he talks about the difficulty","text":"This is not a consensus view by macroeconomists. While fraud may have been important, and possibly more important than macroeconomists believe, I think you do a disservice to the HN reading community by not acknowledging the vast body of experts who have already tried to answer the question of what caused the Great Recession. It's a very difficult question to answer convincingly, given that we cannot run multiple trials to see any counterfactual scenarios. I think any attempt at an answer should be presented with humility and caution and nuance. Wikipedia discusses a bunch of factors related to the Great Recession, correctly mentioning that different economists place different weight on them: [LINK] Here's a video by Tyler Cowen that discussions the Great Recession in terms of four DIFFERENT macroeconomic models: [LINK] Lastly, I'll say that even talking about causation generally is difficult. If someone buys a gun and shoots me, what was the cause of me getting shot? If guns had been outlawed, I wouldn't have been shot. Is it Congress's fault? If the shooter hadn't pulled the trigger, I wouldn't have been shot. Is it the shooter's fault? If I hadn't failed to jump out of the way, I wouldn't have been shot. Is it my fault? Defining causality when there are many serially dependent steps is not easy. So when someone asks, why did the Great Recession happen, it's hard to even know what the question means. Feynman has a famous interview segment where he talks about the difficulty"}]},"IpMxOmUcfOI":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it as evidence that Hodgins built the glitter bomb Mark Rober presented as his own, and says he deserves credit.","m":[{"id":18742401,"author":"jtokoph","month":"2018-12","story":"What Is Glitter?","stance":"reference","why":"Sean Hodgins' making-of video of the glitter bomb Mark Rober presented as his own.","quote":"Its disappointing that Sean is getting absolutely zero credit.","text":"Link to the other video: [VIDEO] Its disappointing that Sean is getting absolutely zero credit. Mark Rober just keeps saying “I built”. There’s a link in the description but just suggests Sean has more build details. He could have at least brought Sean into the original video for some credit. Who knows, maybe this was the deal and Mark is giving Sean all of the ad revenue in exchange for the views/subs/credit...shrug"},{"id":41000659,"author":"ipsum2","month":"2024-07","story":"Jigsaw Puzzle Robot [pdf]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"he didn't create the glitter bomb, his most viewed video. That was done by Sean Hodgins","text":"Mark Rober doesn't do any engineering himself, he's the CEO who claims credit for what other people do. For example, he didn't create the glitter bomb, his most viewed video. That was done by Sean Hodgins (making-of video: [VIDEO] ). Sean's name was not mentioned at all in the glitter bomb video. Instead, Mark Rober claimed he built it by himself."},{"id":25510780,"author":"fxtentacle","month":"2020-12","story":"Mark Rober (former NASA) launches a 30 day Creative Engineering Class","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the famous glitterbomb was built by Sean Hodgins","text":"This is clearly marketing, and clearly good one. From what I can tell, it's a course that was mostly produced by someone else and will be delivered by someone else, but they recorded Mark Rober - the guy famous on YouTube for glitterbombing people - as the instructor. But their FAQ already states that he will \"be able to\" look at what students are doing and \"may occasionally [..] give feedback\", so most likely he won't be around to run the course. Also, from watching the videos, I had the impression that the technical work is mostly done by other people, e.g. the famous glitterbomb was built by Sean Hodgins: [VIDEO] So first and foremost, it appears that Mark Rober is a great showman and a great narrator. Plus he's collaborating with people like Mr Beast who have the power of a skilled agency behind them (nightmedia.co) to produce just the right narrative to go viral. And his first videos appear to be sponsored by a marketing agency. So I'm not surprised that this course is hyped up and not actually done by Mark Rober. I'd treat this more as a case study on how to perfect your social marketing ;) That said, their core promise of building 3 own projects within 30 days sounds reasonable if they have skilled instructors running the course. And the price seems reasonable for such a learning experience. When I was younger, my dad built some technical projects with me. Over the course of a weekend, I would learn more about electrics and physics than in a year in school. And once yo"},{"id":18741005,"author":"root_axis","month":"2018-12","story":"Video of Glitter Bomb for Package Thieves Exposed as Partial Fake","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"he didn't create it, someone else did","text":"I think he's lying and making up excuses because he got caught. He also misrepresented his role in the creation of the device (i.e. he didn't create it, someone else did, you can view their youtube channel here [VIDEO] ). IMO there is no reason to trust anything that guy says."}]},"L1N3WXZ_1LM":{"wy":"HN mostly uses it as a joke or cultural reference for rich Parisian stereotypes and subcultures, rather than recommending it.","m":[{"id":46468393,"author":"aebtebeten","month":"2026-01","story":"Straussian Memes: A Lens on Techniques for Mass Persuasion","stance":"reference","why":"Prep hop style comedy of subcultures","quote":"may get forwarded by members of different subcultures for two different reasons","text":"I've been trying to come up with \"self stabilizing\" examples since yesterday, but think I finally may have stumbled upon one: \"Prep hop\" videos, like [LINK] or [VIDEO] , may get forwarded by members of different subcultures for two different reasons: the first because they appreciate the comic satirisation of others, and the second because they appreciate how the comics have sweated the details of their own subculture — \"we are like that only\". Lagniappe: [LINK]"},{"id":39768545,"author":"bigmattystyles","month":"2024-03","story":"Paris preserves its mixed society by pouring billions into public housing","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Wowow, the rich have had it tough since the 90s","text":"Wowow, the rich have had it tough since the 90s (only the French born before 1988 or so will understand the video) [VIDEO]"},{"id":39776095,"author":"johnchristopher","month":"2024-03","story":"Paris preserves its mixed society by pouring billions into public housing","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"it was all over the place at that time","text":"> Wowow, the rich have had it tough since the 90s (only the French born before 1988 or so will understand the video) [VIDEO] Hahaha, it's not one of my favourites these days but it was all over the place at that time :D. edit: here's a more recent take [LINK]"},{"id":39498849,"author":"BrandoElFollito","month":"2024-02","story":"Ask HN: How to survive as a homeless programmer in the winter (AB, Canada)?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the canonical comedy sketch for these people","text":"Yes, this is helpful when you are outside by -10°C. You will die standing, literally. I would give my work on the spot if it helped me to survive and possibly get back on tracks. I don't know if you have fought like this, living in despair in the name of coding principles. If yes, good for you. If not then this is the kind of comment that reminds me of our French \"gauche caviar\", the people with position and money who discuss about how to fight for your rights before going golfing ( [VIDEO] is the canonical comedy sketch for these people)."}]},"NcZxaFfxloo":{"wy":"Cited as a source of examples such as California high-speed rail and rural broadband subsidies when HN discusses government dysfunction.","q":{"x":"Ezra Klien [1] recently highlighted two such examples: high speed rail in CA, and rural broadband subsidies.","i":44319349,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44319349,"author":"simpaticoder","month":"2025-06","story":"TI to invest $60B to manufacture foundational semiconductors in the U.S.","stance":"reference","why":"Klein on failed high speed rail and rural broadband subsidies","quote":"Ezra Klien [1] recently highlighted two such examples: high speed rail in CA, and rural broadband subsidies.","text":"The overall impression I get of such projects is that politicians celebrate the funding victory, and then forget about it. Then the project's funds get eaten up by administrative costs over time. Ezra Klien [1] recently highlighted two such examples: high speed rail in CA, and rural broadband subsidies. Personally, I haven't seen much, if anything, actually spent out of Biden's infrastructure bill. The money from these programs just seems to...vanish. Allowing this to happen undermines the idea that the USG is a trustworthy spender of our money. I would personally prefer it if political success wasn't just grabbing more budget, but actually overseeing successful execution of public projects. 1 - [VIDEO]"},{"id":43831191,"author":"mapt","month":"2025-04","story":"Reports of the death of California High-Speed Rail have been greatly exaggerated","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"California contracting in particular is built not of penurious individual corruption, but of systemic corruption. The lowest bid must win, and nobody has the political capital to establish a bidding system that works, so dramatic systematic underbidding + cost overruns is the rule, and is expected by all parties except the naive public. Couple that with the kind of \"Build nothing if any single problem can be detacted\", 'Cheems mindset' failures of liberalism, and high-pressure demands from a federal government whose legislators are eager to sabotage projects outside of purple states. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43512790,"author":"justanotheratom","month":"2025-03","story":"US Securities and Exchange Commission beginning to bring on DOGE staff","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"investments into significant infra programs might be another, even with long time horizons.\" - yes, really long horizons. case in point - [VIDEO] \"it arguably did not take any actions that directly make life worse for the average person.\" - I don't think you've talked to an average person. They are quite upset. \"you are rooting for people who are directly working against your own economic interest\" - No actually. They have a plan and they have articulated it. It may not work in the end, but it is not a forgone conclusion. And they are most definitely striving to help the average person. [LINK] , [LINK] ."},{"id":44805432,"author":"caseysoftware","month":"2025-08","story":"FCC abandons efforts to make U.S. broadband fast and affordable","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it was both comical and enraging to hear how much money and time was wasted","text":"We got an overview of those \"efforts\" a few months back and it was both comical and enraging to hear how much money and time was wasted. And this was a law+process crafted by Democrats, signed by a Democrat President, and presided over by a Democrat Administration.. they had all the pieces in place to accomplish their goals. Ref: [VIDEO] When you have tons of paperwork and ZERO deployments (not exaggerating), there's nothing to revert.. just a lot to be disappointed by."}]},"OqtNleXhTRE":{"wy":"HN cites it whenever someone asks whether USB-C holds up better than micro USB, as a small sample but concrete test.","q":{"x":"Linus Tech Tips actually tested 10000 USB-C insertions","i":21219789,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":29876980,"author":"FinnKuhn","month":"2022-01","story":"Ask HN: Are USB-C ports less durable than micro USB?","stance":"reference","why":"USB-C loosens faster but works longer overall.","quote":"USB C becomes loose faster, but continues to work for longer in total","text":"This honestly reminds me of this \"experiment\" in which the conclusion was, if I remember correctly, that USB C becomes loose faster, but continues to work for longer in total: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29210739,"author":"ccouzens","month":"2021-11","story":"World’s first USB-C iPhone sells on eBay for $86,001","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence USB-C outlasted microUSB insertions.","quote":"In this (sample size of 1!) experiment, microUSB failed at 8000 insertions device side.","text":"In this (sample size of 1!) experiment, microUSB failed at 8000 insertions device side. USB C lasted beyond 8000 insertions. [VIDEO] This isn't the first time I've heard someone suggest Lightning is more durable than USB-C, but I've not seen evidence of it."},{"id":21219789,"author":"zifutreiua","month":"2019-10","story":"USB-C Has Finally Come into Its Own","stance":"reference","why":"10000-insertion test: MicroB failed at 8000, USB-C kept working.","quote":"Linus Tech Tips actually tested 10000 USB-C insertions","text":"Did you actually manage to break that tiny wafer or it's more of a theoretical concern? I admit I had the same concern as you, but I'm not a mechanical engineer, and I'm sure a lot of thought was put in the mechanical design of the connector. It seems to me that the outer metal sheet on the connector will prevent a \"wrong\" angle on the wafer. Linus Tech Tips actually tested 10000 USB-C insertions: [VIDEO] Spoiler: USB MicroB failed at 8000, USB-C was still working at 10000"},{"id":20063276,"author":"opencl","month":"2019-05","story":"Why Was Mini USB Deprecated in Favor of Micro USB?","stance":"reference","why":"Test: microUSB socket failed at 8000 cycles, USB-C survived.","quote":"Actual testing[1] demonstrates that the microUSB socket on the device does not last the rated 10k cycles","text":"The cable plug is supposed to fail instead of the socket on the device. Actual testing[1] demonstrates that the microUSB socket on the device does not last the rated 10k cycles and also that the cable lasts longer than the socket on the device. [1] [VIDEO] TL;DW- Repeated insertion and removal led to the socket on the microUSB SSD failing after 8000 cycles, cable was still fine. USB-C still worked after 8000 cycles."}]},"SkH2iX0rx8U":{"wy":"HN cites it as a concise rundown of why the Mpemba effect likely does not hold up.","q":{"x":"walking through all the experiments that demonstrate the effect does not in fact exist.","i":31929288,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":47485544,"author":"chotmat","month":"2026-03","story":"LLMs Predict My Coffee","stance":"reference","why":"Mpemba effect not scientifically proven","quote":"The Mpemba Effect wasn't scientifically proven, and can be explained away with error in measurement.","text":"It's because you was wrong (or at least not correct). The Mpemba Effect wasn't scientifically proven, and can be explained away with error in measurement. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31929288,"author":"simonjgreen","month":"2022-06","story":"Controversy continues over whether hot water freezes faster than cold","stance":"reference","why":"Walks through experiments showing hot-freezes-faster effect doesn't exist","quote":"walking through all the experiments that demonstrate the effect does not in fact exist.","text":"There was a video by Derek/Veritasium as few years ago explaining this and also walking through all the experiments that demonstrate the effect does not in fact exist. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24157434,"author":"aaron695","month":"2020-08","story":"The physics of why hot water sometimes freezes faster than cold water","stance":"reference","why":"Run-down of why the Mpemba effect isn't real","quote":"Sciencium talks about it here if you want an ok run down and how it's not real","text":"We all get the Mpemba effect is totally fake right? You all own freezers so you can test it for yourself, it was found by a child making icecream so it will be easy! And if you're the !first! to put it on Youtube that'd be cool. Sciencium talks about it here if you want an ok run down and how it's not real - [VIDEO]"}]},"YWdD206eSv0":{"wy":"Used mostly as a joke or cartoon shorthand about misinformation online.","m":[{"id":31157432,"author":"TobyTheDog123","month":"2022-04","story":"Twitter set to accept Musk's $43B offer – sources","stance":"reference","why":"Offers a personal viewpoint on judging truth online.","quote":"I'll give my personal opinion, which you may want to treat as one of many possible viewpoints","text":"At the end of the day, it's your choice how you determine what's true, but I don't believe anyone has the right to tell you how to do that -- that the screening should happen between you and the content, not between the submitter and the platform -- kind of like XSS prevention (you must assume anything can get into the database, but all that matters is how it's executed by the end user). I'll give my personal opinion, which you may want to treat as one of many possible viewpoints: [VIDEO] [LINK] The internet has run on the concept of trust and reputation for a while now, but IMO, if you want anything close to the full story, you have to put in the work yourself: Compile your sources, evaluate your and their biases, and come to a conclusion. Ground News is a pretty neat tool to help me with this if it's a widely-reported story. Some examples: - Something from a reputable news source? Probably true, but not always. I try to wait for a decent handful of other, diverse outlets to report on it before believing it, sharing it, etc. - Something from a single account on Twitter on Hacker News? Probably not, but not always. I try to wait for other confirmations before believing it, sharing it, etc. I personally believe it to be a case where free speech wins, people raise the bar for what's worth their trust, and fake news begins to decline as a result -- the opposite being one where people lower their guard due to blind trust in other entities, and fake news can therefore take advanta"},{"id":22408228,"author":"bjt2n3904","month":"2020-02","story":"Twitter's community verification system will be a disaster","stance":"reference","why":"A cartoon take on false information on the internet.","quote":"","text":"I particularly enjoy Arthur the Aardvark's take on false information on the internet[1]. The author is right. Letting the community decide what is true will be a disaster. Really, truth (or lack there of) is outside Twitter's scope. Twitter exists to allow users to communicate. Not to educate users on critical thinking, or arbitrate truth. Even if they \"democratize\" truth. 1 - [VIDEO]"},{"id":42725178,"author":"hmcq6","month":"2025-01","story":"Israel, Hamas reach ceasefire deal to end 15 months of war in Gaza","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Asked if acts of violence against those who publish images of the Prophet Muhammad can \"never be justified\", 68% agreed that such violence was never justifiable. > But 24% disagreed with the statement, while the rest replied \"don't know\" or refused to answer. > Of those polled, 95% felt a loyalty to Britain, while 93% believed that Muslims in Britain should always obey British laws. 24%. 24% = 1/3. You seem like the kind of person who caused the McDonalds 1/3 pounder flop. [VIDEO] But the better argument is that 24% of people saying \"it could be justified\" is that it actually could be justified. Should I punch everyone who includes Nazi symbology in their art? No. Should I punch a Nazi who repeatedly promotes his ideology through his art? Yes."},{"id":12211905,"author":"justrossthings","month":"2016-08","story":"Tell your dentist to suck it: there’s little evidence flossing works","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"Anon, I suggest you start flossing more regularly to clean debris in-between your teeth\" \"No thanks Mr. Dentist, an article I read on the internet said that is dumb :-)\" [VIDEO]"}]},"_QvguJJNilA":{"wy":"Mentioned as a UK example of a whiteboard product that took off after a Dragons' Den appearance, in threads about whiteboard products.","q":{"x":"I remember seeing them on the BBC's Dragon's Den and winning over Deborah Meaden and Theo Paphitis","i":5819339,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":5819339,"author":"teh_klev","month":"2013-06","story":"IdeaPaint allows you to write all over the wall","stance":"reference","why":"Magic Whiteboard winning over Dragons' Den investors","quote":"I remember seeing them on the BBC's Dragon's Den and winning over Deborah Meaden and Theo Paphitis","text":"The folks at Magic Whiteboard in the UK have a good half way house product if you didn't fancy painting the walls: [LINK] They also do a clear version: [LINK] I remember seeing them on the BBC's Dragon's Den and winning over Deborah Meaden and Theo Paphitis: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4177441,"author":"miles","month":"2012-06","story":"Whiteboard Alternative – The Bamboo Board","stance":"reference","why":"Magic Whiteboard's Dragons' Den pitch for reusable sticky sheets","quote":"Took off after appearing on Dragon's Den","text":"Magic Whiteboard is pretty cool too: [LINK] It's basically a roll of big white sheets that stick to walls without adhesive. They're also erasable/reusable. Took off after appearing on Dragon's Den (a TV show where entrepreneurs pitch their ideas to investors): [VIDEO]"},{"id":1219763,"author":"singular","month":"2010-03","story":"WhiteyBoard: An Instant Whiteboard For your Instant Office","stance":"reference","why":"Magic Whiteboard's Dragons' Den appearance, similar to the product discussed","quote":"a UK product that was featured on the UK version of dragon's den","text":"Seems very similar/identical to Magic Whiteboard - [LINK] - a UK product that was featured on the UK version of dragon's den - [VIDEO] - is Whiteyboard a completely separate company or have Magic Whiteboard not yet got a US patent?"}]},"aaOB-ErYq6Y":{"wy":"Cited as evidence of Waymo's progress in Arizona and its lead over competitors.","m":[{"id":23489578,"author":"bhupy","month":"2020-06","story":"Tesla is close to becoming the most valuable car company","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to argue Waymo is closer to full self-driving.","quote":"","text":"> The point is that when it comes online, they will have a huge fleet. This might be correct in a world with full-self-driving, though as others might point out, we're quite far away from that. Waymo is probably closer to full-self-driving than Tesla is [1]. If you're talking about just raw Tesla car ownership, then ride-sharing operations still requires a human being to be sitting in the driver's seat that expects to be fairly compensated, at which point you have the same supply growth problem that Uber/Lyft have. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22232213,"author":"tim333","month":"2020-02","story":"Tesla's self driving algorithm's overlay [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence of self-driving cars operating in Arizona.","quote":"","text":"As to \"tech people\" falling for the hype, it all depends which particular hype but: \"surprised if we saw fully self driving cars any time in the next 50 years.\" a couple of years after there have been self driving cars driving around Arizona [VIDEO] reminds me of quotes like \"The aeroplane will never fly.\" — Lord Haldane, 1907 a while after the Wright brothers had done many flights. You could argue both the Wright brothers planes and the Arizona Waymos were a bit rubbish but these things tend to improve rapidly."},{"id":16513866,"author":"ehsankia","month":"2018-03","story":"Waymo released a 360-degree VR representation of what its robocar sees","stance":"reference","why":"Promo video shows the real in-car UI.","quote":"","text":"The same UI is shown in the Pheonix promotional video: [VIDEO] It seems like the it's the real UI currently used."},{"id":16594645,"author":"drawkbox","month":"2018-03","story":"Waymo shows off what it’s like to ride in a truly driverless self-driving car","stance":"reference","why":"Waymo rides on roads familiar to the commenter in Arizona.","quote":"","text":"Waymo cars are all over Chandler and Tempe in Arizona. I see many on a regular basis with a good portion self-driving. It is neat to see all the roads you normally drive in the self-driving videos and it is almost taken for granted that they are here [1][2]. Chandler has always been in early on technology due to many reasons but that lots of programmers/engineers work here due to intel historically, projects like Iridium started here, also one of the first places to get cable/broadband internet in the 90s. With that said, Chandler is very easy to drive, square roads, good weather, clean roads, clearly marked and kept up, minimal freeway needed etc. It is a great place to start but still lots for the cars/algos to learn, however it IS happening and I see it everyday. Many of the videos are around Chandler downtown that is a bit busy and has lots of movement/pedestrians[3], so that is good that it is able to navigate that, it is close to busy urban environments. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] (360 degree experience) [3] [LINK]"}]},"bZe5J8SVCYQ":{"wy":"HN commenters post it as an obligatory joke reply whenever threads touch on missiles, GPS, or circular reasoning.","m":[{"id":46015943,"author":"neuralkoi","month":"2025-11","story":"The Pentagon Can't Trust GPS Anymore","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I wonder if this changes the old adage, now that the missile knows where it is at all times. [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41187769,"author":"jazzyjackson","month":"2024-08","story":"Robot Dog with Gun Turret for Hunting Aerial Drones Being Tested by Army","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"predicting trajectory of a projectile is what computers were first made to do, at least if you count ENIAC as the first, and the Apollo Guidance Computer not long after (the missile knows where it is at all times, it knows this because it knows where it isn't... [0]) as it happens the projectiles have minds of their own now so to predict trajectory will require theory-of-mind shooting down fast moving drones might just be the path to self-awareness in the machine world, \"if I were a drone, what action would I take in that situation...\" [0] [VIDEO] [0.b remix] [LINK]"},{"id":39047990,"author":"bombcar","month":"2024-01","story":"The Miele Dialog: cook a fish in ice without melting the ice","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's a reference to [VIDEO] > The missile knows where it is at all times. It knows this because it knows where it isn't. By subtracting where it is from where it isn't, or where it isn't from where it is (whichever is greater), it obtains a difference, or deviation. The guidance subsystem uses deviations to generate corrective commands to drive the missile from a position where it is to a position where it isn't, and arriving at a position where it wasn't, it now is. Consequently, the position where it is, is now the position that it wasn't, and it follows that the position that it was, is now the position that it isn't."},{"id":38424415,"author":"H8crilA","month":"2023-11","story":"Rockwell Retro Encabulator [video]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's also important to know where you are (by knowing where you aren't): [VIDEO] There's also a version for when you need that little bit of extra motivation: [LINK]"},{"id":37082754,"author":"btown","month":"2023-08","story":"A Grumman F11 Tiger Shot Itself Down","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Ah, this happened with bullets, not missiles. Because the missile knows where it is at all times. It knows this because it knows where it isn't. By subtracting where it is from where it isn't, or where it isn't from where it is (whichever is greater), it obtains a difference, or deviation. The guidance subsystem uses deviations to generate corrective commands to drive the missile from a position where it is to a position where it isn't, and arriving at a position where it wasn't, it now is. Consequently, the position where it is, is now the position that it wasn't, and it follows that the position that it was, is now the position that it isn't. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33617786,"author":"dmix","month":"2022-11","story":"Galactica: an AI trained on humanity's scientific knowledge (by Meta)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Reminds me of this: [VIDEO] An algorithm being explained extremely literally whie failing to communicate anything."},{"id":33290762,"author":"outworlder","month":"2022-10","story":"Starlink signals can be reverse-engineered to work like GPS","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> The submarine needs to know where it is so it can tell the missile where to go. That reminds me of \"the missile knows where it is because it knows where it isn't\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":30444124,"author":"Nadya","month":"2022-02","story":"What makes writing more readable?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"That excerpt reads to me much like explaining how a guidance system works on a missile: [VIDEO] > The missile knows where it is at all times. It knows this because it knows where it isn't. By subtracting where it is from where it isn't, or where it isn't from where it is (whichever is greater), it obtains a difference, or deviation. The guidance subsystem uses deviations to generate corrective commands to drive the missile from a position where it is to a position where it isn't, and arriving at a position where it wasn't, it now is. Consequently, the position where it is, is now the position that it wasn't, and it follows that the position that it was, is now the position that it isn't. Very simple words, with 3 or 4 exceptions, but it short-circuits the brain a bit if you aren't following along carefully enough."},{"id":12960106,"author":"Z1nfandel","month":"2016-11","story":"Russia plans to install Pole-21 anti-missile jamming device on cell towers","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory","text":"My memory might be hazy, but I thought it was the other way around. INS as the primary navigation with periodic corrections from GPS. Obligatory: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10189893,"author":"mc32","month":"2015-09","story":"An Explanation of Cricket (2009)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"In the same vein: \"The missile knows where it is at all times. It knows this because it knows where it isn't. By subtracting where it is from where it isn't, or where it isn't from where it is (whichever is greater), it obtains a difference, or deviation. The guidance subsystem uses deviations to generate corrective commands to drive the missile from a position where it is to a position where it isn't, and arriving at a position where it wasn't, it now is. Consequently, the position where it is, is now the position that it wasn't, and it follows that the position that it was, is now the position that it isn't. In the event that the position that it is in is not the position that it wasn't, the system has acquired a variation, the variation being the difference between where the missile is, and where it wasn't. If variation is considered to be a significant factor, it too may be corrected by the GEA. However, the missile must also know where it was. The missile guidance computer scenario works as follows. Because a variation has modified some of the information the missile has obtained, it is not sure just where it is. However, it is sure where it isn't, within reason, and it knows where it was. It now subtracts where it should be from where it wasn't, or vice-versa, and by differentiating this from the algebraic sum of where it shouldn't be, and where it was, it is able to obtain the deviation and its variation, which is called error.\" Audio on yt[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9706893,"author":"IanCal","month":"2015-06","story":"Second-order logic explained in plain English","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> No matter what human action you consider, if everyone does it to everyone doing it to them, then everyone has it done to them by everyone to whom they do it I'm finding this incredibly hard to parse, yet feel like the point should be quite simple, and the paper seems to get harder from there. > if every given proposition bears it to every proposition bearing it to the given proposition, then every given proposition is borne it by every proposition the given proposition bears it to. I don't want to knock someone's efforts at this, but perhaps this is only a \"plain english explanation\" to someone who already understands the topic at hand? In all, it reminds me of \"the missile knows where it is\": [VIDEO]"}]},"dht_3NziwSw":{"wy":"Used purely as a joke reference to Harry Tuttle and the film's dystopian bureaucracy.","m":[{"id":37703054,"author":"javier_e06","month":"2023-09","story":"My Unhealthy Relationship with Keyboards ⌨","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"my desk right now resembles a wall panel from the movie Brazil.","text":"I was considering putting a light but my desk right now resembles a wall panel from the movie Brazil. [VIDEO] Is hard to find the middle ground between clutter and chaos."},{"id":28224581,"author":"mistersquid","month":"2021-08","story":"Guerrilla artist faked downtown LA freeway sign in 2001","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The name \"Harry Tuttle\" comes to mind.","text":"> Is artist the right term here? Seems more like guerrilla engineer or guerilla highway management person. The name \"Harry Tuttle\" comes to mind. [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8074163,"author":"sdenton4","month":"2014-07","story":"New broadband users shun manditory porn filters, (UK regulator) Ofcom finds","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Like this guy","text":"Wel they DO speculate that the low uptake is due to evil installation people who are maybe going around disabling the filter for everyone... Like this guy: [VIDEO]"}]},"mwdo17GT6sg":{"wy":"Used as a shorthand scene for conflicts of interest where auditors or raters are paid by the entities they judge.","q":{"x":"Memorable scene from The Big Short (2015)","i":20641844,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":47613774,"author":"sunir","month":"2026-04","story":"Mercor Hit by Cyberattack","stance":"reference","why":"Scene analogy for paid auditors passing clients.","quote":"It's like this scene from The Big Short.","text":"It doesn't force you go through risk modelling because by now most SOC2 platforms have templates you just fill in the blanks and sign off. Conversely, the auditors are paid by the company, so their incentive is to pass the audit so the client can get what it wants. Because there's no adversarial pressure as a check and balance to the security, and AICPA is clearly just happy to take the fees, it's a hollow shirt. It's like this scene from The Big Short. [VIDEO] As usual, it's only people that care that force positive change. The companies that want good security will have good security. Customers who want good security will demand good security."},{"id":20641844,"author":"Cactus2018","month":"2019-08","story":"Inflated Bond Ratings Helped Spur the Financial Crisis. They’re Back","stance":"reference","why":"Scene on ratings agencies selling good ratings.","quote":"Memorable scene from The Big Short (2015)","text":">Bank needs a rating on a asset, but their incentive is to get a \"better\" rating, not a more honest one. There are multiple agencies, so banks shop around for the most favorable one. So, as soon as one agency loosens their ratings, they all follow suit. They sell good ratings. That's the industry. Memorable scene from The Big Short (2015) - \"FrontPoint Partners confronts S&P\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":15214063,"author":"klenwell","month":"2017-09","story":"Why Sub-Prime Lenders Didn’t Cause the Housing Crash (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Scene where S&P rep cites competition to explain corrupt ratings.","quote":"In The Big Short movie, it's competition that the ratings agency rep played Melissa Leo cites","text":"Funny you suggest market competition. In The Big Short movie, it's competition that the ratings agency rep played Melissa Leo cites to explain their corrupt ratings: Georgia: If we don't give them the rating, they go to Moody's. Right down the street. If we don't work with them, they will go to our competitors. Not our fault. Simply the way the world works. Vinnie: Holy shit. Georgia: Yes, now you see. And I never said that. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33298455,"author":"bumpian","month":"2022-10","story":"Moody’s downgrade UK outlook to negative","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Did we forget what happened in 2008?","text":"Why people still trust these ratings from Moody's, S&P, etc? Did we forget what happened in 2008? [VIDEO]"}]},"ptUj8JRAYu8":{"wy":"HN references it as prior art whenever solar sintering, sand printing or sunlight-based manufacturing comes up.","m":[{"id":45087921,"author":"mitthrowaway2","month":"2025-08","story":"Lunar soil machine developed to build bricks using sunlight","stance":"reference","why":"Prior art for sunlight-based sintering","quote":"It's probably something similar in concept to Markus Kayser's solar sinterer[1]","text":"It's probably something similar in concept to Markus Kayser's solar sinterer[1], but with the light being focused onto an optical fiber bundle whose endpoint is moved around in X and Y, rather than moving a basket of regolith. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41393136,"author":"Log_out_","month":"2024-08","story":"Show HN: Homemade automated solar concentrator","stance":"reference","why":"Sintering sand into 3d structures with sunlight","quote":"You can also sinter sand into 3d structures.","text":"You can also sinter sand into 3d structures. [VIDEO] In theory if you could manufacture lenses and optical fibers, you could 3d print active solar powered structures anywhere where there is enough sun. Imagine a 3d printed little bottlegarden, with a water collector using a heated silicagel cycle printed in situ on mars. Still low atmospheric pressure, but warm and wetter."},{"id":33341335,"author":"qikInNdOutReply","month":"2022-10","story":"Solar Metal Smelter","stance":"reference","why":"Solar sintering of sand into structures","quote":"Solar Sintering by Markus Kayser","text":"In an old game of mine, there was a monastry driving on wheels through the dessert. The monks spend there days collecting sunlight, smelting sand into \"glasstrees\", which would then use daylight to collect water (silica gel), pump it up at night (using optic fibres for transport, salt water for storing heat, glass wool for isolation and distills to re-capture the nightly water vapors).. creating a ecosystem in its shade. Nearly all of it made from glas. While the premise might be idiotic scifantasy, the idea to terraform in situ with what you got, is quite neat. [LINK] [VIDEO] Solar Sintering by Markus Kayser Just imagine this driving around on mars, with huge halftransparent balloons over it, which like a parabolic mirror pre-collects the light, printing structures for future ecology with the little light that world offers from the material at hand."},{"id":24102525,"author":"avmich","month":"2020-08","story":"Australia fast-tracks plan to send solar power to Singapore","stance":"reference","why":"Popular video of 3D printing models from sand","quote":"A popular video a few years ago which demonstrate, uhm, a 3D printing of models out of sand.","text":"Like this? [VIDEO] A popular video a few years ago which demonstrate, uhm, a 3D printing of models out of sand."},{"id":15740757,"author":"mmjaa","month":"2017-11","story":"The World Is Running Out of Sand","stance":"reference","why":"Sand 3D printing with sunlight","quote":"Seems like an opportunity to use sun and sand to construct 3D-printing oases in the middle of nowhere.","text":"Seems like an opportunity to use sun and sand to construct 3D-printing oases in the middle of nowhere. [VIDEO] Wonder why this isn't working out as the \"new construction technique\" hit success that it seems, with a little optimisation, it could be?"}]},"yaXma6K9mzo":{"wy":"Cited as the source for Damon's explanation that the DVD market's collapse killed mid-budget movies, in threads on Hollywood economics.","q":{"x":"According to Matt Damon[1], it's the obsolescence of the DVD market that changed the calculus of mid-budget films.","i":35976631,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":48351439,"author":"hangsi","month":"2026-06","story":"'Backrooms' Stuns with $81M Debut","stance":"reference","why":"Matt Damon on Hot Ones explaining why DVD loss changed which movies get made.","quote":"This is from the interview on Hot Ones (released August 5, 2021)","text":"This is from the interview on Hot Ones (released August 5, 2021): [VIDEO] > Sean Evans: > I think a scenario lots of viewers can relate to is sitting on the couch on a Friday night, going through the streaming services, cycling through the movies and thinking to themselves \"they're not making movies for me anymore\". As somebody who's been intimately involved in movie making for 30 years, what are the macro Hollywood conditions behind that sentiment? > Matt Damon: > Well, so what happened was the DVD was a huge part of our business - of our revenue stream - and technology has just made that obsolete. And so, the movies that that we used to make: you could afford to not make all of your money when it played in the theater because you knew you had the DVD coming behind the release and 6 months later you'd get a whole other chunk - it would be like reopening the movie almost. > And when that went away, that changed the type of movies that we could make. I did this movie \"Behind the Candelabra\" and I talked to a studio executive who explained: it was a $25 million movie. I would have to put that much into print and advertising to market it - what we call P&A - so now I'm in $50 million. I have to split everything I get with the exhibitor, the people who own the movie theaters, so I would have to make $100 million before I got into profit. The idea of making $100 million on a story about this love affair between these two people... Yeah, love everyone in the movie, but that's sudde"},{"id":35976631,"author":"kelseyfrog","month":"2023-05","story":"A24 Took over Hollywood [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Matt Damon explains how DVD decline killed mid-budget movies.","quote":"According to Matt Damon[1], it's the obsolescence of the DVD market that changed the calculus of mid-budget films.","text":"It's the decline of DVD sales. Big budget Hollywood films are designed to appeal to the lowest common denominator. Why did the mid-budget sector (the one that can take risks with creativity) drop out leaving us with either indie films or big budget films? According to Matt Damon[1], it's the obsolescence of the DVD market that changed the calculus of mid-budget films. When a structural change affects the economics of content, it's difficult to localize the problem and envision a remedy. Unfortunately, these economic systemic problems tend to shut down our capabilities to affect change rather than enhance them. In some ways it's more convenient to envision the problem as being a problem of movie studio executives and their choices, than a media economy problem with a change in financial relations. 1. Matt Damon | Hot Ones [VIDEO]"},{"id":31256353,"author":"smnrchrds","month":"2022-05","story":"Pop culture has become an oligopoly","stance":"reference","why":"Matt Damon explains industry shift: streaming replaced DVDs.","quote":"Matt Damon agrees and gives some good insights about why the industry is moving in that direction.","text":"Matt Damon agrees and gives some good insights about why the industry is moving in that direction. Short answer: because streaming replaced DVDs. [VIDEO]"}]},"zn2Xf9hAFcE":{"wy":"Referenced as the canonical depiction of mass layoffs and canned HR scripts whenever layoffs come up.","q":{"x":"Reminded me of this scene where Eric Dale was getting let go and HR was regurgitating canned script.","i":36336434,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36336434,"author":"pcurve","month":"2023-06","story":"I’m an ER doctor. Here’s how I’m already using ChatGPT to help treat patients","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Reminded me of this scene where Eric Dale was getting let go and HR was regurgitating canned script.","text":"Reminded me of this scene where Eric Dale was getting let go and HR was regurgitating canned script. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33462224,"author":"greenyoda","month":"2022-11","story":"Twitter to start layoffs -internal email","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"in which most of a floor of employees in an investment bank is laid off","text":"There's a scene in the movie \"Margin Call\" (about the 2007-2008 financial crisis) in which most of a floor of employees in an investment bank is laid off: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31743659,"author":"Qub3d","month":"2022-06","story":"Coinbase Announces 18% Layoffs","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"That is a major plot point in Margin Call","text":"That is a major plot point in Margin Call , which is a dramatization of the initial housing bubble crash of 08: [VIDEO]"}]}});
